,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469092646,"data":"       | analysisb_ratio = sqrt(5) - 3/2 ≈ 2.236 - 1.5 = 0.73607. So AC length is about 0.736 * BC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469094035,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469095236,"data":"       | Thus side lengths: BC = 1, AB ≈ 1.236, AC ≈ 0.736. So triangle is scalene with AB \u003e BC \u003e AC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469096703,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469097858,"data":"       | Now we can compute geometry in this limiting shape. Then compute ratio α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469099175,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469100356,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute the ratio in the limit shape where BD = p = (3-√5)/2, CD = q = (√5-1)/2, AB = sqrt(5) - 1, AC = sqrt(5) - 3/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469101825,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469102995,"data":"       | Now we need to compute other points: incircle center I, radius r, contact points D, E, F, etc., K (A-mixtilinear touchpoint), K', N, T, etc. Then compute ratio.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469104461,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469105612,"data":"       | Thus we can treat this as a fixed triangle with these side lengths (scaled). Compute ratio exactly using symbolic algebra (with sqrt(5)). This will yield α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469107052,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469108335,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute ratio for the limiting triangle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469109787,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469110954,"data":"       | But we must verify that the condition KNK'B cyclic holds in the limit shape. Since for finite n, the condition holds exactly (by construction). As n→∞, the triangle tends to the limit shape, and the condition may hold in the limit as well (maybe the circle degenerates to a line). However the ratio limit may be well-defined.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469112755,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469113904,"data":"       | Thus we can compute ratio for the limiting shape (with exact values). That should give α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469115344,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469116491,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute ratio for the limiting shape.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469117714,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469119194,"data":"       | Now we need to compute geometry for triangle with side lengths expressed in terms of sqrt(5). Let's define BC = 1, B at (0,0), C at (1,0). D is at BD = p = (3-√5)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469121809,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469122947,"data":"       | Thus d = p.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.46912411,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469125252,"data":"       | Now we need to find coordinates of A such that side lengths AB = c = sqrt(5) - 1, AC = b = sqrt(5) - 3/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.46912671,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469127848,"data":"       | We can solve for A coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.469129028,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.46913017,"data":"       | Given B = (0,0), C = (1,0), unknown A = (x,y). Then:\n"}
