,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470493672,"data":"       | Goal: compute α. This is a geometry problem that likely reduces to some expression involving side lengths, maybe ratio of segments that tends to some constant as n large. The condition BD = F_n, CD = F_{n+1} suggests D is contact point of incircle with BC, so BD and CD are the tangential lengths from B and C to incircle: BD = s - b? Wait standard notation: In triangle ABC, with incircle tangent to BC at D, CA at E, AB at F. Let side lengths: a = BC, b = CA, c = AB. Semiperimeter s = (a+b+c)/2. Then BD = s - b, CD = s - c. Indeed, BD = s - b, CD = s - c. So we have s - b = F_n, s - c = F_{n+1}. Since AB ≠ AC, b ≠ c.\n"}
