,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470631745,"data":"       | Thus we can determine A given I and side lengths? Actually we can find coordinates of A such that distances from I to AB and AC equal r. Equivalent to I being on angle bisector of A: The distances to AB and AC are equal (both r). So I must lie on internal angle bisector of A. So line AI passes through I and is angle bisector.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470634515,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470636061,"data":"       | Given B and C fixed, we can find A such that incircle center I is at (F_n, r). The condition that I is equidistant from AB and AC is automatically satisfied if I lies on angle bisector of A. But we can enforce that by constructing A as intersection of lines through B and C that are tangent to incircle (i.e., lines AB and AC are tangents from B and C to incircle). Indeed, given a circle with center I and radius r, the lines from B and C that are tangent to the circle define points of tangency F and E, and intersection of those tangents is A. So we can construct A as external point from which two tangents to the incircle go to points B and C? Wait B and C are points on the circle's external lines? Actually B and C are points where the tangents from B and C to the incircle meet the circle at points F and E respectively. Since incircle touches BC at D, which is between B and C, B and C are external points to the incircle, and we can draw tangents from B to incircle: there are two tangents, one is BC (touches at D) but BC is not tangent to incircle (incircle touches BC at D but BC passes through D; it's not tangent). Actually BC is a line that passes through the point of tangency, but it's not a tangent line because incircle is tangent to BC at D, meaning BC is tangent to incircle at D. Wait incircle touches BC at D, so BC is tangent to incircle at D. So BC is tangent line from B and C to incircle? But B and C are points on BC, which is tangent at D. So B and C are points on the tangent line, but not necessarily points of tangency. So B and C are not external points with respect to incircle; they lie on the tangent line. So AB and AC are other tangents from B and C to incircle (i.e., lines through B and C that are tangent to incircle at F and E). Indeed, incircle is tangent to AB at F, to AC at E, and to BC at D. So B and C are points of tangency of lines BC? Wait B is endpoint of side AB and BC. The incircle is tangent to side AB at F somewhere between A and B, and to side BC at D somewhere between B and C. So B lies on two sides: AB and BC. So B is intersection of two lines that are tangent to incircle at two distinct points (F and D). Similarly C is intersection of lines BC and AC tangent at D and E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470641215,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470642566,"data":"       | Thus we can construct triangle ABC given incircle center I and radius r: draw two tangents from B to the incircle: one is BC (tangent at D) and other is BA (tangent at F). Similarly from C: BC (tangent at D) and CA (tangent at E). So B and C are points where two tangents meet.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470644238,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470645421,"data":"       | Thus given incircle center I = (F_n, r) and radius r, we can find B = (0,0) and C = (a,0) such that lines from B to I have angle with BC such that distance from B to D is F_n. But D is foot of perpendicular from I to BC (x-axis). Since BC is x-axis, D is (F_n,0). So B is at (0,0), C at (a,0) with a = F_{n+2}. So incircle touches BC at D = (F_n,0). So the geometry is set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470647246,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470648372,"data":"       | Now we need to find A such that lines AB and AC are tangent to incircle at points F and E respectively.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470649719,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470650918,"data":"       | Given a circle centered at (F_n, r) with radius r, and given external points B = (0,0) and C = (a,0), we can compute the points of tangency of tangents from B and C to the circle. For a point external to a circle, the distance from point to center is d = sqrt((F_n)^2 + r^2) for B. The length of tangent from B to circle is sqrt(d^2 - r^2) = sqrt((F_n)^2 + r^2 - r^2) = |F_n| = F_n. Indeed, the length from B to point of tangency F along the tangent line is F_n. That matches known property: length of tangent from B to incircle equals BD = s - b = F_n. Indeed, BD is the length along side BC from B to D, which equals the length of tangent from B to incircle along side BC (i.e., BC is tangent at D). But also the length of tangent from B to incircle along AB is also equal to BD = F_n (since tangents from a point to a circle have equal lengths). So AF? Wait the length from B to point of tangency on AB (i.e., BF) equals BD = F_n. Indeed property of incircle: tangents from B to incircle have equal lengths: BD = BF = s - b = F_n. Similarly from C: CD = CE = s - c = F_{n+1}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470655041,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470656204,"data":"       | Thus we have lengths of tangents from B to incircle: along BC to D is F_n, along BA to F is also F_n. Similarly from C, tangents lengths are F_{n+1}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470657641,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470659178,"data":"       | Thus we can compute coordinates of F: it's point of tangency of line from B to circle, other than D. Since B is at (0,0), the line from B to I is vector (F_n, r). The tangent point F is where line from B touches circle: The tangent point lies such that BF is length F_n and BF is perpendicular to radius IF. Actually radius IF is perpendicular to tangent line at F. The line from B to I is not tangent; it's the line connecting B to center. The tangent point is located at angle from B to I such that BF is tangent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470661288,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470662421,"data":"       | But we can compute coordinates of F using geometry: For a point B outside a circle, the tangency points satisfy that the line from B to the point of tangency is tangent, i.e., the segment BF is perpendicular to IF. Also BF length = sqrt(BI^2 - r^2) = F_n. Indeed BF = F_n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470664098,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470665235,"data":"       | Thus we can find coordinates of F as follows: Let vector u = I - B = (F_n, r). Its length d = sqrt(F_n^2 + r^2). The tangent points are at B + (r^2/d^2) * u ± (r/d^2) * u_perp * sqrt(d^2 - r^2)? Actually there is formula for foot of external tangents. Let's derive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470666945,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470668481,"data":"       | Let B = (0,0), I = (c0, r) where c0 = F_n. Let d = sqrt(c0^2 + r^2). The distance from B to I is d. The angle between BI and BF is α such that sin α = r/d, cos α = sqrt(d^2 - r^2)/d = F_n/d. Indeed length of tangent BF = sqrt(d^2 - r^2) = F_n. So the direction of BF is obtained by rotating vector BI by angle θ = arcsin(r/d) away from BI? Actually the tangent line from B to circle forms right triangle with BI as hypotenuse, BF as adjacent side? Wait right triangle: I is right angle at F, with IF = r (radius), BF = length of tangent = sqrt(d^2 - r^2) = F_n, and BI = d. So triangle BIF is right at F. So angle between BI and BF is angle at B, call β, such that sin β = r/d, cos β = BF/d = F_n/d. So direction of BF is rotated from BI towards the line perpendicular to IF? Actually we can compute coordinates of F using geometry: The point F is located at B + (BF/BI) * (unit vector along direction of BF). The direction of BF is obtained by rotating vector u by +θ where θ = arcsin(r/d) clockwise? Since we have two tangents from B: one is BC (horizontal) which is tangent at D. Indeed BC is tangent at D, and D is directly below I (vertical radius). So line BC is horizontal; vector from B to D is (F_n,0). This is the tangent from B that is horizontal. The other tangent BF goes upward.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470673144,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470674324,"data":"       | Thus we can find F coordinates: The tangent point is such that the radius IF is perpendicular to BF. So IF vector is from I to F. Since BF is tangent, IF is perpendicular to BF. Also F lies on the circle: |F - I| = r. Also F lies on line from B to F.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470676014,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470677192,"data":"       | Thus we can find F as intersection of line through B with direction unknown and circle such that distance from B to F = F_n and line BF is tangent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470678628,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470679778,"data":"       | But easier: Use geometry of right triangle: In triangle BIF, right angle at F, with BI known (d), BF = F_n, IF = r. Coordinates: Let angle at B between BI and horizontal (x-axis) be φ = arctan(r/F_n). Indeed tan φ = r / F_n (since vector from B to I is (F_n, r)). So BI makes angle φ above horizontal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470681586,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470683155,"data":"       | Now the tangent BF is at angle φ - θ where θ = arcsin(r/d) = arcsin(r/d). But note sin θ = r/d, cos θ = F_n/d. So tan θ = r / F_n. Wait that's same as φ. Indeed tan φ = r / F_n. So φ = arctan(r/F_n). Also sin θ = r/d, cos θ = F_n/d. Then tan θ = r / F_n = tan φ. So θ = φ. So angle between BI and BF is φ. So BF direction is along horizontal? Let's verify: Since φ is angle of BI above horizontal, subtract φ gives angle of BF relative to horizontal: angle of BF = φ - φ = 0 (horizontal). Indeed BF is horizontal line (BC). So that's the tangent BC. That matches: one tangent is horizontal (BC). The other tangent is symmetric about BI? Actually there are two tangents from B: one is BC (horizontal) at angle 0, the other is above horizontal at angle 2φ? Let's think: The angle between the two tangents from B is 2θ where θ is angle between BI and each tangent. Since one tangent is BC (horizontal) which is at angle 0, and BI is at angle φ above horizontal, then the other tangent must be at angle 2φ above horizontal? Actually the two tangents are symmetric about BI. So if one tangent is at angle -θ relative to BI, the other is at angle +θ relative to BI. Since BC is at angle -φ relative to BI (since BI is φ above horizontal, BC is horizontal which is φ below BI). So BC is at angle -φ relative to BI. So the other tangent must be at angle +φ relative to BI, i.e., angle = φ + φ = 2φ above horizontal. So BF (the other tangent) is at angle 2φ above horizontal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470687555,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470688738,"data":"       | Thus direction of BF is at angle 2φ. So slope = tan(2φ). So we can compute coordinates of F as intersection of line from B at angle 2φ with circle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470690235,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470691391,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can compute coordinates of F using formula for tangent point: The vector from I to F is obtained by rotating vector from I to B by 90 degrees and scaling appropriately? Let's derive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470692968,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470694131,"data":"       | Given B at origin, I at (c0, r). The line from B to F is tangent, so IF is perpendicular to BF. So vector IF is perpendicular to vector BF = F - B = F. So IF · F = 0. Also |F - I| = r. Also |F| = BF = F_n (since length from B to F is F_n). So we have unknown point F = (x_f, y_f) satisfying:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470697578,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470698751,"data":"       | 1) x_f^2 + y_f^2 = F_n^2 (distance from B).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470700021,"data":"       | 2) (x_f - c0)^2 + (y_f - r)^2 = r^2 (distance from I).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470701295,"data":"       | 3) (x_f - c0, y_f - r) · (x_f, y_f) = 0 (perpendicular).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470702622,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470703775,"data":"       | But note condition 2 can be simplified using 1: Since (x_f - c0)^2 + (y_f - r)^2 = r^2. Expand: x_f^2 - 2c0 x_f + c0^2 + y_f^2 - 2r y_f + r^2 = r^2 =\u003e (x_f^2 + y_f^2) - 2c0 x_f - 2r y_f + c0^2 = 0. Using x_f^2 + y_f^2 = F_n^2, we get:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470705432,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470706589,"data":"       | F_n^2 - 2c0 x_f - 2r y_f + c0^2 = 0 =\u003e 2c0 x_f + 2r y_f = F_n^2 + c0^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470707985,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470709135,"data":"       | Also condition 3: (x_f - c0) x_f + (y_f - r) y_f = 0 =\u003e x_f^2 - c0 x_f + y_f^2 - r y_f = 0 =\u003e (x_f^2 + y_f^2) - c0 x_f - r y_f = 0 =\u003e F_n^2 - c0 x_f - r y_f = 0 =\u003e c0 x_f + r y_f = F_n^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470710784,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470711948,"data":"       | Thus we have linear equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470713155,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470714316,"data":"       | (1) c0 x_f + r y_f = F_n^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470715522,"data":"       | (2) 2c0 x_f + 2r y_f = F_n^2 + c0^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470716739,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470718225,"data":"       | Multiply (1) by 2: 2c0 x_f + 2r y_f = 2F_n^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470719495,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470720684,"data":"       | Set equal to (2): 2F_n^2 = F_n^2 + c0^2 =\u003e F_n^2 = c0^2 =\u003e F_n = c0 (positive). Indeed c0 = F_n (since we set I's x-coordinate equal to BD = F_n). So indeed F_n = c0. So the equations are consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470722286,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470723415,"data":"       | Thus we have c0 = F_n. So we have x_f, y_f satisfying c0 x_f + r y_f = F_n^2 = c0^2. So c0 x_f + r y_f = c0^2. Since c0 \u003e 0, we can solve for x_f in terms of y_f: x_f = (c0^2 - r y_f)/c0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470725006,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470726159,"data":"       | Also we have x_f^2 + y_f^2 = c0^2 (since F_n^2 = c0^2). So we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470727481,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470728658,"data":"       | [(c0^2 - r y_f)/c0]^2 + y_f^2 = c0^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470729878,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470731035,"data":"       | Simplify: (c0^2 - r y_f)^2 / c0^2 + y_f^2 = c0^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470732302,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470733455,"data":"       | Multiply by c0^2: (c0^2 - r y_f)^2 + y_f^2 c0^2 = c0^4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470734741,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470735875,"data":"       | Expand: c0^4 - 2c0^2 r y_f + r^2 y_f^2 + c0^2 y_f^2 = c0^4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470737184,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470738371,"data":"       | Cancel c0^4: -2c0^2 r y_f + r^2 y_f^2 + c0^2 y_f^2 = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470740616,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470741812,"data":"       | Factor y_f: y_f (-2c0^2 r + y_f (r^2 + c0^2)) = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470743074,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470744235,"data":"       | Thus either y_f = 0 (which corresponds to D, the horizontal tangent) or y_f = 2c0^2 r / (r^2 + c0^2). So the non-zero y_f is:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470745636,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470746795,"data":"       | y_f = 2c0^2 r / (r^2 + c0^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470748005,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470749239,"data":"       | Plug back to find x_f: x_f = (c0^2 - r y_f) / c0 = (c0^2 - r * (2c0^2 r / (r^2 + c0^2))) / c0 = (c0^2 - 2c0^2 r^2 / (r^2 + c0^2)) / c0 = c0 * (1 - 2r^2 / (r^2 + c0^2)) = c0 * ((r^2 + c0^2 - 2r^2) / (r^2 + c0^2)) = c0 * ((c0^2 - r^2) / (r^2 + c0^2)).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470750848,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470752015,"data":"       | Thus coordinates of F: (x_f, y_f) = (c0 * (c0^2 - r^2) / (c0^2 + r^2), 2c0^2 r / (c0^2 + r^2)).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470753352,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470755025,"data":"       | Similarly, we can compute coordinates of E, the tangency from C to incircle. Since C is at (a,0) = (F_{n+2}, 0). The incircle center is at (c0, r). The distance from C to I is sqrt((a - c0)^2 + r^2). The tangent length from C to incircle is CD = F_{n+1}. Indeed sqrt((a - c0)^2 + r^2) =? Let's verify: Since CD = F_{n+1} is the length along BC from C to D: D is at (c0,0). So CD = a - c0 = F_{n+2} - F_n = F_{n+1}. Indeed a - c0 = F_{n+2} - F_n = F_{n+1}. So the horizontal distance from C to D is F_{n+1}. So indeed the tangent length along BC from C to D is F_{n+1}. And also the length of tangent from C to incircle along CA (i.e., CE) equals CD = F_{n+1}. So the distance from C to I is sqrt((F_{n+1})^2 + r^2). So indeed the tangent length from C to incircle is sqrt(CI^2 - r^2) = F_{n+1}. So consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470757646,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470758816,"data":"       | Thus we can compute coordinates of E similarly: treat point C as origin shifted: define coordinates relative to C: let x' = x - a. Then incircle center relative to C is (c0 - a, r) = (-F_{n+1}, r). The tangent from C to incircle at E is analogous to B case but mirrored. So coordinates of E relative to C: using same formula with c0 replaced by F_{n+1} (distance horizontally left). Since the geometry is symmetric horizontally, we can compute E coordinates:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470760799,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470762005,"data":"       | Let d0 = F_{n+1} (distance from C to I horizontally). Then incircle center relative to C is (-d0, r). Using same formula for tangency point from point at origin (C) to circle centered at (-d0, r) radius r, the tangency point other than D (which is at (-d0,0) relative to C) will be at:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470763679,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470764849,"data":"       | x_e_rel = -d0 * (d0^2 - r^2) / (d0^2 + r^2)   (since c0 replaced by -d0? Actually formula for point at (0,0) and circle centered at (c0, r) gave x_f = c0 (c0^2 - r^2)/(c0^2 + r^2). For point at origin and circle centered at (-d0, r), we replace c0 with -d0. Then x coordinate of tangency point relative to origin is (-d0) * ((-d0)^2 - r^2)/(((-d0)^2) + r^2) = -d0 * (d0^2 - r^2)/(d0^2 + r^2). So x_e_rel = -d0 * (d0^2 - r^2)/(d0^2 + r^2). y_e_rel = 2 d0^2 r / (d0^2 + r^2) (since formula uses c0^2). Actually y_f = 2 c0^2 r / (c0^2 + r^2). Since c0^2 = d0^2, same. So y_e_rel = 2 d0^2 r / (d0^2 + r^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470768028,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470769412,"data":"       | Thus absolute coordinates of E: x_e = a + x_e_rel = a - d0 * (d0^2 - r^2)/(d0^2 + r^2). Since a = c0 + d0 (since a = F_{n+2} = F_n + F_{n+1} = c0 + d0). So x_e = c0 + d0 - d0 * (d0^2 - r^2)/(d0^2 + r^2) = c0 + d0 * [1 - (d0^2 - r^2)/(d0^2 + r^2)] = c0 + d0 * [(d0^2 + r^2 - d0^2 + r^2)/(d0^2 + r^2)] = c0 + d0 * (2 r^2)/(d0^2 + r^2) = c0 + 2 d0 r^2/(d0^2 + r^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470771239,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470772415,"data":"       | Similarly, y_e = y_e_rel = 2 d0^2 r/(d0^2 + r^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470773655,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470774855,"data":"       | Thus we have coordinates for F and E:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470776069,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470777221,"data":"       | F: (x_f, y_f) = (c0 * (c0^2 - r^2)/(c0^2 + r^2), 2 c0^2 r/(c0^2 + r^2))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470778494,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470779625,"data":"       | E: (x_e, y_e) = (c0 + 2 d0 r^2/(d0^2 + r^2), 2 d0^2 r/(d0^2 + r^2))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470780936,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470782121,"data":"       | where c0 = F_n, d0 = F_{n+1}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470783484,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470784666,"data":"       | Now line EF: we can compute its equation. N is foot from D (c0,0) to line EF. So N is projection of D onto EF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470786001,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470787165,"data":"       | We also need K: intersection of circumcircle of AFE with circumcircle of ABC (Ω). We need to compute K coordinates perhaps. This is more involved.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470788631,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470789818,"data":"       | But maybe we can avoid explicit coordinates for K and K' by using known properties: Since K is second intersection of circumcircle of AFE and circumcircle of ABC, K lies on both circles. K' is reflection of K across EF. Condition that quadrilateral K N K' B is cyclic is equivalent to N lying on circle with B and K K'. Since K' is reflection across EF, the circle B K K' has center on EF. So condition is that N is intersection of EF with that circle. So N must be at distance from center O equal to radius.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470791869,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470793052,"data":"       | Thus we can perhaps derive condition in terms of distances: Let O be the center of circle passing through B, K, K'. Since O lies on EF, and OK = OK' = OB = R (radius). Then N is foot from D onto EF; we need N to lie on the circle: i.e., distance ON = R. So N is point on EF at distance R from O. So O is the midpoint of the segment between the two intersection points of EF with the circle. Since the circle intersects EF at two points symmetric about O. One of them is N; the other is the antipode N' (the other intersection). So O is midpoint of N N'.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470802631,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470803895,"data":"       | Thus condition reduces to: The distance from O to N equals distance from O to B (since OB = R). So O must satisfy OB = ON.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470805301,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470806768,"data":"       | Thus O is a point on EF such that its distance to B equals its distance to N (i.e., O is equidistant from B and N). Since O lies on EF, the locus of points equidistant from B and N is the perpendicular bisector of BN. So O is intersection of EF with perpendicular bisector of BN.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470809503,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470810741,"data":"       | Thus the condition is equivalent to EF intersecting the perpendicular bisector of BN at a point O such that O is also the circumcenter of triangle B K K'. But we don't need K: we just need that O is intersection of EF with perpendicular bisector of BN. However O is defined as circumcenter of B K K', which automatically lies on perpendicular bisector of BK. So we also need O to lie on perpendicular bisector of BK. But that is automatically satisfied for any K? Wait O is defined as circumcenter of triangle B K K', which must be intersection of perpendicular bisectors of BK and KK'. Since KK' is symmetric across EF, its perpendicular bisector is EF. So O is intersection of EF and perpendicular bisector of BK. So O is unique for each K. So condition that N lies on circle B K K' is equivalent to ON = OB (radius). But O is intersection of EF and perpendicular bisector of BK. So we need to find K such that the intersection point O satisfies ON = OB.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470813579,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470814769,"data":"       | Thus we can think of K as variable point on circumcircle Ω (or on AFE circle) such that the perpendicular bisector of BK meets EF at a point O equidistant from B and N.\n"}
