,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470820466,"data":"       | But perhaps we can find a simpler condition: Since O is on EF and is equidistant from B and N, O is the circumcenter of triangle B N ?. Actually if O is equidistant from B and N and lies on EF, then O is the circumcenter of triangle B N ?. Not exactly; we need also O equidistant from B and N, but not necessarily from any other point. However O also lies on EF, which is not necessarily perpendicular bisector of BN. The perpendicular bisector of BN passes through O, but O also lies on EF. So O is intersection of EF and perpendicular bisector of BN. So O is uniquely determined by BN and EF. So condition that O also lies on perpendicular bisector of BK (i.e., O is circumcenter of triangle B K K') is equivalent to that the perpendicular bisector of BK passes through O. So K must be such that its perpendicular bisector passes through O. That means K lies on a circle with center O and radius OB (since any point K on that circle will have perpendicular bisector passing through O). Indeed, the set of points K such that O is equidistant from B and K is the circle centered at O radius OB. So K must be any point on that circle (except B). So K is any point on circle centered at O radius OB, which is the same as the circle B K K' (since K' is reflection across EF, also lies on same circle). So condition reduces to: K must be a point on circumcircle Ω (circumcircle of ABC) that also lies on circle centered at O radius OB, where O is intersection of EF and perpendicular bisector of BN.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470824341,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470825881,"data":"       | Thus the condition that quadrilateral K N K' B is cyclic is equivalent to: The circle centered at O with radius OB (i.e., circle passing through B) intersects Ω at point K (other than B). This intersection exists if the distance from O to circumcenter of ABC (call O_Ω) is appropriate. But O is defined by geometry of triangle (EF, N). So condition imposes relation between triangle's geometry such that the circle centered at O (intersection of EF and perpendicular bisector of BN) passes through a point on Ω (other than B). Since O is on EF, which is known given triangle, we can compute O and then see if the circle with center O radius OB intersects Ω. For generic triangle, there will be two intersection points of circle centered at O radius OB with circumcircle Ω: one is B (since OB = radius). The other may be real or imaginary depending on distances. For intersection to be real, the distance between O and O_Ω must be ≤ R_Ω + OB and ≥ |R_Ω - OB|. Typically there will be a second intersection unless O is collinear with B and O_Ω? But perhaps for many triangles there is a second intersection. However the condition that K also lies on circumcircle of AFE (i.e., the Miquel point) might impose additional constraint linking O to A, E, F.\n"}
