,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.470981719,"data":"       | Circle tangent to line BN at B and passing through B and K. Since it's tangent at B to line BN, the radius at B is perpendicular to BN. So center O_T of that circle lies on line perpendicular to BN through B. Also O_T must be equidistant from B and K (since passes through both). So O_T is intersection of perpendicular to BN through B and perpendicular bisector of BK. So we can compute O_T as intersection of those two lines. Then radius R_T = distance O_T to B (or K). Then find intersection of this circle with line BC (x-axis) again (other than B). Solve for T.\n"}
