,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.475836226,"data":"       | Thus a_n = maximum ratio across all valid roots.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.475837476,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.475838662,"data":"       | Our earlier compute_a_n function used find_all_roots which gave many spurious values but we can filter for those where compute_ratio_numeric works and cond near zero, then take max ratio.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.475840176,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.475841323,"data":"       | But we need to handle large n efficiently.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.475842525,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.475843735,"data":"       | Observation: For even n, the ratio seems to converge to ~2.0697 for many n, but for n=26 (even) we got a larger ratio ~2.186. So maybe as n grows, there are occasional spikes where ratio is larger, but the supremum limit α is the eventual upper bound for a_{2n} as n large, maybe the supremum of these spikes tends to some constant \u003e2.0697 but less than something else. The problem defines α as smallest real number such that for all sufficiently large n, a_{2n} \u003c α. So α is the limit supremum (i.e., eventual supremum). If a_{2n} occasionally spikes above a certain value, but eventually stays below some bound, α is that bound. So we need to compute the eventual bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.475847587,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.475848772,"data":"       | Our data suggests that for n up to 24 (even), a_{2n} ~2.0697, but at n=26 (even) a_{2n} jumps to 2.186. Let's compute for n=28 (even) to see.\n"}
