,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477249839,"data":"       | But our algorithm may not capture spikes for larger n because they may occur at r values beyond our scanning range. Let's try to search for roots for n=50 (index 100). Use scanning factor range maybe up to factor 5 (i.e., r up to 5*c0). But c0 huge (~F_100 ~ 3.5e20). That's huge; scanning with logspace may be heavy but we can attempt using scaling: Since the geometry may be scale-invariant, the ratio may be independent of c0; the root r scales with c0. So we can consider normalized variables: let c0 = 1 (scale) and d0 = φ (approx). Then we can compute ratio for that normalized case. Indeed scaling all lengths by factor λ scales c0,d0,r, etc., but the ratio (CT*NB)/(BT*NE) is scale-invariant. So we can set c0=1, d0=φ (approx 1.618). Then compute ratio as function of r (scaled). That reduces to solving for r for generic c0=1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477252597,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477253801,"data":"       | Thus we can treat c0=1, d0=φ. Then find r that satisfies condition. Then compute ratio. That ratio will be same for all n (since geometry only depends on ratio of c0 to d0). However our earlier results show ratio varies with n, meaning scaling alone does not capture differences; there is also dependence on the integer nature of c0,d0 (maybe due to discrete tangency lengths). However as n large, the ratio of c0/d0 approaches φ, but the integer values may affect shape due to constraints of tangents lengths being integer? Actually the geometry only depends on the ratio c0/d0 (since scaling can be arbitrary). So for any given ratio, there may be a unique shape (or multiple). So for large n, ratio c0/d0 ≈ φ. So the geometry approximates the limiting case where c0/d0 = φ exactly. So the ratio a_n should converge to the value for the exact ratio φ. That limit is α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477257836,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477259017,"data":"       | Thus we can compute α directly by solving the problem for continuous parameters: let c0 = 1, d0 = φ (i.e., ratio φ). Then find r satisfying the cyclic condition and compute ratio. That will give α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477260606,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477269717,"data":"       | Thus we need to solve for c0=1, d0=φ, find r such that condition holds, then compute ratio. There may be multiple solutions; we need the maximum ratio.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47727136,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477272527,"data":"       | Let's attempt this numerically.\n"}
