,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477602437,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse problem. It's a complex geometry problem with many constructions. The problem defines n-tastic triangles: BD = F_n, CD = F_{n+1}, and quadrilateral KNK'B is cyclic. The triangle ABC must have incircle contact D on BC, etc. So BD and CD are distances from B and C to incircle touch point D on BC. So D divides BC into BD and CD. So BC = BD + CD = F_n + F_{n+1} = F_{n+2} (since Fibonacci recurrence). So BC length equals F_{n+2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477604431,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477605594,"data":"       | Given that, we consider other points: K is the second intersection of circumcircle of AFE with circumcircle of ABC (Ω). K' is reflection of K across EF. N is foot of perpendicular from D to EF. Circle tangent to line BN and passing through B and K intersects BC again at T (distinct from B). So T is second intersection of circle through B,K tangent to line BN at B? Actually \"circle tangent to line BN and passing through B and K\" means a circle that passes through B and K and is tangent to line BN at B. Since B is on both circle and line BN, tangency at B means BN is tangent to the circle at B. So the circle passes through B, K and has tangent BN at B. This uniquely determines a circle (except maybe two circles? but given B and K, there is unique circle tangent to a given line at B). So T is the second intersection of that circle with BC (other than B). So T lies on BC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477609787,"data":"       | \n"}
