,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47772013,"data":"       | Let's attempt to compute coordinates using analytic geometry to derive ratio.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477722658,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477723876,"data":"       | We can set up coordinate system: Let BC be on x-axis from B (0,0) to C (a,0) where a = BC = F_{n+2}. D is point on BC at distance BD = F_n from B, so D = (F_n, 0). CD = F_{n+1} from D to C: indeed a - F_n = F_{n+2} - F_n = F_{n+1} (since F_{n+2} = F_n + F_{n+1}). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477725527,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47772699,"data":"       | Let incircle ω have center I = (x0, r) where r is incircle radius (positive). Since incircle tangent to BC at D, its center I is vertically above D at distance r: I = (F_n, r). Indeed incircle touches BC at D, so center is directly above D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477728603,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477729767,"data":"       | Now incircle touches AB at F and AC at E. Since incircle is tangent to AB and AC, points F and E are foot of perpendiculars from I to AB and AC respectively.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477731204,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477732348,"data":"       | Let A be some point (x_A, y_A) with y_A \u003e 0 (above BC). Then line AB passes through B (0,0) and A; line AC passes through C (a,0) and A. The incircle being tangent to these lines means distances from I to these lines equal r.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477733981,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477735144,"data":"       | We can find coordinates of A in terms of r and maybe angle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477736421,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477737574,"data":"       | Let’s denote A = (u, v). Then line AB: passes through (0,0) and (u,v). Its equation: y = (v/u) x (if u ≠ 0). Distance from I (F_n, r) to line AB is |Ax0 + By0 + C| / sqrt(A^2 + B^2). For line AB: in standard form: v x - u y = 0 (since cross product). Indeed line AB: y = (v/u) x =\u003e v x - u y = 0. Distance from point (x0,y0) to this line: |v x0 - u y0| / sqrt(v^2 + u^2). Since I is above line (maybe), we have v x0 - u y0 = v F_n - u r. Its absolute value must equal r * sqrt(v^2 + u^2) (since distance = r). So:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477739744,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477740903,"data":"       | |v F_n - u r| = r sqrt(v^2 + u^2). Since v\u003e0, u may be positive or negative? Usually A is somewhere above BC, maybe between B and C horizontally? Not necessarily; but for acute triangle, u between 0 and a. We'll assume u between 0 and a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477742524,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477743677,"data":"       | Similarly, distance from I to line AC: line through (a,0) and (u,v). Equation: (v)(x - a) - (u - a) y = 0? Let's derive: slope = v/(u-a). So line AC: y = v/(u-a) (x - a). Multiply: (u-a) y = v (x - a) =\u003e v x - v a - (u-a) y = 0 =\u003e v x - (u-a) y - v a = 0. So standard form: v x - (u-a) y - v a = 0. Distance from I (F_n, r) to this line: |v F_n - (u-a) r - v a| / sqrt(v^2 + (u-a)^2) = r.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477745604,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477746767,"data":"       | Thus we have two equations for incircle tangency.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477747987,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477749147,"data":"       | We also have incircle radius r unknown.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477750347,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477751674,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can use known formula: In triangle with incircle radius r, distances from I to sides are r. The coordinates of I relative to B and C are (BD, r) = (F_n, r). So we can compute side lengths AB = c, AC = b, using geometry: c = length AB = sqrt( (u-0)^2 + (v-0)^2 ) = sqrt(u^2 + v^2). Similarly, b = length AC = sqrt( (u-a)^2 + v^2 ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477754556,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477755747,"data":"       | Also we have BD = s - b = F_n, CD = s - c = F_{n+1}. So s = b + F_n = c + F_{n+1}. So s = b + F_n = c + F_{n+1}. This gives relationship between b and c: b = c + (F_{n+1} - F_n) = c + F_{n-1}? Actually F_{n+1} - F_n = F_{n-1}? Let's compute: Fibonacci recurrence: F_{n+1} = F_n + F_{n-1}. So difference F_{n+1} - F_n = F_{n-1}. So b = c + F_{n-1}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47775753,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477758704,"data":"       | Thus side lengths difference b - c = F_{n-1}. So AC is longer than AB by F_{n-1} (since AB ≠ AC). Since AB ≠ AC, indeed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477760144,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477761287,"data":"       | Now we also have a = BC = F_{n+2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477762494,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47776369,"data":"       | Thus we have triangle side lengths (a,b,c) satisfying a = F_{n+2}, b - c = F_{n-1}. Also b + c = 2s - (BD + CD) = 2s - a? Wait s = (a+b+c)/2, so 2s = a+b+c. So b + c = 2s - a. But s is not fixed; we can compute s from b and c using BD = s - b = F_n =\u003e s = b + F_n. So b + c = (b + F_n) * 2 - a = 2b + 2F_n - a. But also we have c = b - F_{n-1}. So b + (b - F_{n-1}) = 2b - F_{n-1} = 2b + 2F_n - a =\u003e 2b - F_{n-1} = 2b + 2F_n - a =\u003e cancel 2b: -F_{n-1} = 2F_n - a =\u003e a = 2F_n + F_{n-1}. Let's verify if this holds given a = F_{n+2}. Compute RHS: 2F_n + F_{n-1} = 2F_n + F_{n-1} =? Use Fibonacci identities: F_{n+2} = F_{n+1} + F_n = (F_n + F_{n-1}) + F_n = 2F_n + F_{n-1}. Indeed matches. So identity holds automatically. So again, side lengths are consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477766377,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47776783,"data":"       | Thus the triangle is determined by side lengths (a,b,c) where b and c satisfy b - c = F_{n-1} and also b + c = something? Actually we can compute b and c individually using s = b + F_n = c + F_{n+1}. So s = b + F_n. Also s = (a+b+c)/2. So b + F_n = (a+b+c)/2 =\u003e 2b + 2F_n = a + b + c =\u003e b + 2F_n = a + c =\u003e b = a + c - 2F_n. But we also have b - c = F_{n-1}. So we have two equations for b and c:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47776982,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477771033,"data":"       | b - c = F_{n-1}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477772237,"data":"       | b = a + c - 2F_n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477773414,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477774584,"data":"       | Plug first into second: (c + F_{n-1}) = a + c - 2F_n =\u003e cancel c: F_{n-1} = a - 2F_n =\u003e a = 2F_n + F_{n-1}. Indeed holds. So again identity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477776064,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47777721,"data":"       | Thus b and c are not uniquely determined: there is one degree of freedom. Indeed we can choose any b (or c) satisfying b - c = F_{n-1} and triangle inequality with a. So there is a continuum of triangles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477778733,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47777988,"data":"       | Thus the cyclic condition may restrict b and c further.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477781181,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477782428,"data":"       | Now we need to compute K: intersection of circumcircle of AEF with circumcircle of ABC. There is known property: K is the exsimilicenter of incircle and circumcircle? Let's check: The circle through A,E,F is known as the \"A-mixtilinear incircle\"? Actually the A-mixtilinear incircle touches AB, AC, and circumcircle internally. Its touch point with circumcircle is known as the \"A-mixtilinear touch point\". However, the circle through A, E, F (where E and F are incircle touch points) is not the mixtilinear incircle. But there is known property: The circumcircle of AEF passes through the exsimilicenter of incircle and circumcircle? Let's recall: The exsimilicenter (external homothety center) of incircle ω and circumcircle Ω lies on the line connecting the centers I and O, and is the point where the external homothety sends ω to Ω. It lies on the line OI, outside the incircle. It is also the intersection of the external tangents to ω and Ω. I think the exsimilicenter lies on the line connecting the two centers, not necessarily on the circumcircle of AEF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47778735,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477788836,"data":"       | But there is known property: The circumcircle of triangle AEF passes through the exsimilicenter of ω and Ω. Let's verify: The exsimilicenter X of ω and Ω is the external homothety center mapping ω to Ω. Since ω is tangent to BC at D, and also tangent to AB at F and AC at E. The lines through X and the tangency points are collinear because of homothety: X, D, and the point where Ω touches BC (which is not defined) but maybe X, D, and the point where Ω is tangent to BC (the point where the circumcircle meets BC is B and C, not tangent). Actually the circumcircle is not tangent to BC; but the exsimilicenter lies on lines connecting the centers O and I. The homothety maps ω to Ω, sending each tangent point to a point on Ω where the corresponding side is tangent? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47779165,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477792824,"data":"       | But there is known property: The circumcircle of triangle AEF passes through the exsimilicenter of ω and Ω. Let me recall: The A-mixtilinear incircle touches AB, AC and Ω at a point T_A. The line AT_A passes through the exsimilicenter of ω and Ω. Also the circumcircle of AEF passes through the exsimilicenter? I'm not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47780346,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477804711,"data":"       | Let's search memory: In geometry, the A-mixtilinear incircle touches AB, AC at points that are not the incircle touch points. However, the circumcircle of triangle AEF (where E,F are incircle touch points) passes through the exsimilicenter of ω and Ω. I think that's true: The exsimilicenter is the Miquel point of quadrilateral BECF? Actually quadrilateral BECF has vertices B,E,C,F. The circumcircle of BECF is the circumcircle of ABC? Not necessarily. But the Miquel point of quadrilateral BECF is the point where the four circles (through each triple of adjacent vertices) intersect. The circles are: (B,E,F) which is the incircle? Actually B,E,F are not collinear; but circle through B,E,F is not incircle. The Miquel point of quadrilateral BECF is the point where circles (B,E,F), (E,C,F), (B,E,C), (B,F,C) intersect. Among these, (B,E,C) is just the circumcircle of ABC? Wait B,E,C are not collinear; they form a triangle; its circumcircle is not Ω (Ω passes through A,B,C). So not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477807764,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477809207,"data":"       | Thus maybe K is the exsimilicenter. Let's test: If K is exsimilicenter X, then K lies on line OI (centers). Does K lie on line OI? Not necessarily; but K is intersection of circumcircle of AEF with Ω. The exsimilicenter lies on line OI, which is not necessarily on Ω (though O is center of Ω, not on Ω). The exsimilicenter is outside Ω (if ω inside Ω). So exsimilicenter is not on Ω. So K cannot be exsimilicenter. So scrap that.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47781263,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477813821,"data":"       | Maybe K is the \"A-mixtilinear touch point\"? Actually the A-mixtilinear incircle touches Ω at a point T_A (call it M). The circle through A, E, F may intersect Ω at that point M? Let's verify: The A-mixtilinear incircle is tangent to AB and AC at points close to A, not at incircle touch points. So not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47781568,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477816847,"data":"       | Thus K is some point defined by geometry; we need to compute coordinates maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477818201,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477819367,"data":"       | Given we have coordinates for B (0,0), C (a,0), D (F_n,0), I (F_n, r). Points E and F are foot of perpendiculars from I to lines AC and AB respectively.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477820856,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47782201,"data":"       | We can compute coordinates of E and F in terms of A (u,v) and I. Then compute circumcircle of AEF, find its intersection with Ω (circumcircle of ABC) besides A, giving K. Then reflect K across line EF to get K'. Then condition that quadrilateral K,N,K',B is cyclic imposes equation.\n"}
