,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477824863,"data":"       | Then we need to compute N: foot of perpendicular from D to EF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477826137,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477827264,"data":"       | Then we need to compute circle tangent to BN at B passing through K; find its second intersection with BC, T. Then compute ratio.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477828667,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.4778298,"data":"       | Goal: find maximum of ratio over possible A (or equivalently over r) satisfying cyclic condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47783124,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477832377,"data":"       | This is a heavy analytic geometry problem but maybe we can compute using symbolic algebra and then analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47783375,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477834904,"data":"       | Given the problem's complexity, maybe there is a simplification: The ratio (CT * NB) / (BT * NE) may be independent of the shape (i.e., constant) under cyclic condition. Then a_n is that constant. Then α is that constant. Then p+sqrt(q) is constant. Could be something like 2+sqrt(3). Let's test with random numeric examples for moderate n to see if ratio seems constant.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477836743,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477838041,"data":"       | We can attempt to solve numerically for given n (like n=5) to find triangles satisfying cyclic condition and compute ratio. Then vary shape parameter to maximize ratio and see limit.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477839558,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477840748,"data":"       | But we need to find all triangles satisfying cyclic condition. That may be a one-parameter family; we need to find maximum ratio maybe occurs at boundary of triangle inequality (i.e., degenerate). Or maybe there is unique shape for which cyclic condition holds (maybe only one triangle for each n). Let's test numerically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477842661,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477843815,"data":"       | We can set up unknowns: variables u,v (coordinates of A) and r (incircle radius). But we have constraints: I = (F_n, r). Distance from I to line AB = r, to line AC = r (already satisfied automatically if we define A such that incircle is tangent). Actually we can derive A coordinates in terms of r and maybe angle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47784674,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47784791,"data":"       | Given incircle radius r and center I, the lines AB and AC are tangent to incircle at points F and E respectively. So AB is line through B (0,0) tangent to incircle at some point F. Since incircle is tangent to AB at F, we can find equation of AB as the line through B and tangent to circle centered at I. Similarly for AC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477849667,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477850834,"data":"       | Thus we can compute A as intersection of those two tangent lines.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477852135,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47785335,"data":"       | Thus we can parametrize triangle by r (radius). Indeed, given incircle center I = (F_n, r) and radius r, the tangents from B and C to the circle are unique (since B and C are external points). So we can compute lines BF and BC? Actually B is external point to incircle; there are two tangents from B to the incircle: one is AB (touching at F), the other is line through B tangent to incircle at some other point (maybe extension of side). But we know AB is one of these tangents. Similarly, line through C tangent to incircle at E is AC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477855481,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477856998,"data":"       | Thus we can compute the tangent lines from B and C to the circle (center I, radius r). Intersection of those tangents gives vertex A. So triangle is uniquely determined by r (given B and C positions). Indeed, for a given incircle (center I and radius r) with D at (F_n,0) (touch point on BC), the tangents from B and C to the incircle are uniquely defined; they intersect at A. So there is exactly one triangle for each r \u003e 0 (such that B and C are outside the incircle). So there is one-parameter family of triangles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47785919,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47786032,"data":"       | Thus the cyclic condition imposes a constraint on r (maybe yields specific value(s)). So for each n, there may be a discrete set of r values satisfying cyclic condition. Among them, we can compute ratio; perhaps there are two possibilities (maybe one yields maximum). So a_n may be the maximum ratio among those r values.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477862067,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477863187,"data":"       | Thus we can treat r as variable, compute ratio as function of r, and impose cyclic condition equation to solve for r.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477864564,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47786568,"data":"       | We can attempt to compute this numerically for some n (like n=5) and see ratio values.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477866977,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47786814,"data":"       | Let's implement in Python using sympy or numeric solving.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477869437,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477870571,"data":"       | Define parameters:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477871735,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477872883,"data":"       | n given, compute F_n, F_{n+1}, F_{n+2}. Use Fibonacci numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477874194,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477876335,"data":"       | Set B=(0,0), C=(a,0) where a = F_{n+2}. D = (F_n,0). I = (F_n, r). r \u003e 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47787774,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477878871,"data":"       | Now compute tangent lines from B to circle centered at I radius r.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477880174,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47788134,"data":"       | Given point B external to circle, we can find tangent points F (on incircle) and line equation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47788267,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477883838,"data":"       | Method: The line from B to tangent point F satisfies that BF is tangent to circle: the radius IF is perpendicular to BF. So vector BF dot IF - I? Actually IF is perpendicular to BF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477885351,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477886483,"data":"       | Let F = (x_f, y_f) on circle: (x_f - F_n)^2 + (y_f - r)^2 = r^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477887777,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477888928,"data":"       | Also BF line passes through B (0,0). So slope of BF = y_f / x_f.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477890214,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47789137,"data":"       | Condition: (F - I) dot (F - B) = 0 (perpendicular). So (x_f - F_n, y_f - r) dot (x_f, y_f) = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477892714,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477894095,"data":"       | Similarly for point C: tangent point E solves (x_e - F_n)^2 + (y_e - r)^2 = r^2, and (x_e - F_n, y_e - r) dot (x_e - a, y_e) = 0 (since vector from C to E is (x_e - a, y_e - 0) = (x_e - a, y_e). Dot product with radius vector (x_e - F_n, y_e - r) = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47789573,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477896874,"data":"       | We can solve these equations for F and E in terms of r and known constants.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477898165,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477899351,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can compute line equations directly using analytic geometry: The equation of tangent from external point P to circle (center I, radius r) can be expressed as: (P - I)·(X - I) = r^2, where X is any point on tangent line? Actually the equation of the polar line of P with respect to circle: (P - I)·(X - I) = r^2. Indeed, for a circle (X - I)·(X - I) = r^2, the polar line of point P (outside) is given by (P - I)·(X - I) = r^2. This line passes through the points of tangency from P. So we can compute line AB as the polar of B: (B - I)·(X - I) = r^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477901668,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47790281,"data":"       | Similarly, line AC is polar of C: (C - I)·(X - I) = r^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477904124,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477905258,"data":"       | Thus we can compute line equations directly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477906543,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477907671,"data":"       | Let B = (0,0), I = (d, r) where d = F_n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477908924,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477910043,"data":"       | Then vector B - I = (-d, -r). So line AB: (-d)*(x - d) + (-r)*(y - r) = r^2 =\u003e -d(x - d) - r(y - r) = r^2 =\u003e -dx + d^2 - ry + r^2 = r^2 =\u003e -dx - ry + d^2 = 0 =\u003e dx + ry = d^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477912858,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47791405,"data":"       | Thus line AB equation: d x + r y = d^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477915324,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477916487,"data":"       | Similarly, C = (a,0). Vector C - I = (a - d, -r). So line AC: (a - d)*(x - d) + (-r)*(y - r) = r^2 =\u003e (a - d)(x - d) - r(y - r) = r^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477917928,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477919063,"data":"       | Expand: (a - d) x - (a - d) d - r y + r^2 = r^2 =\u003e (a - d) x - r y - (a - d) d = 0 =\u003e (a - d) x - r y = (a - d) d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477920507,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47792165,"data":"       | Thus line AC equation: (a - d) x - r y = d (a - d).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477922944,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477924064,"data":"       | Thus we have linear equations for AB and AC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477925301,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477926444,"data":"       | Now find intersection point A = (u, v) solving both equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477927721,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47792889,"data":"       | d u + r v = d^2  (1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477930155,"data":"       | (a - d) u - r v = d (a - d)  (2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477931371,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.4779325,"data":"       | Add (1) and (2): d u + (a - d) u = u a = d^2 + d (a - d) = d^2 + d a - d^2 = d a =\u003e u a = d a =\u003e u = d = F_n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47793397,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477935403,"data":"       | Interesting! So u = d = F_n. So the x-coordinate of vertex A is exactly at the incircle touch point D's x-coordinate! That is surprising but maybe true: For any triangle with incircle touching BC at D, the intersection of tangents from B and C passes vertically above D. Indeed, the incircle center I is at (d, r). The tangents from B and C intersect at A directly above D. Let's verify: Since B and C are symmetric about vertical line through D? Not exactly unless B and C are symmetric distances from D? But B is at 0, C at a = d + (a - d). The equations gave u = d. So indeed A's x-coordinate equals D's x-coordinate, meaning AD is perpendicular to BC (vertical). So AD is altitude from A to BC, passing through D. So D is foot of altitude from A onto BC? Wait D is incircle touch point, not foot of altitude. But we derived that intersection of tangents from B and C to incircle yields A directly above D. Let's double-check the derivation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477938271,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477939894,"data":"       | We used line AB: d x + r y = d^2. This derived from polar condition: d x + r y = d^2. Let's test with known geometry: For incircle with center (d,r) and radius r, the line through B tangent to incircle should satisfy that B is external point. The line equation we derived: d x + r y = d^2. At B (0,0), left side 0, right side d^2 \u003e0, so B does not satisfy line; but line passes through B? Wait we derived line AB passes through B (0,0). Let's plug B: d*0 + r*0 = 0, but RHS d^2. So B does not satisfy equation. Something wrong: The line we derived is not passing through B. Indeed we used polar line of B w.r.t circle, which is line of points X such that (B - I)·(X - I) = r^2. But this line does not pass through B; it's the line connecting the points of tangency from B to the circle, but not through B. Wait the polar line of B is the line through the points of tangency from B to the circle, but not through B. Indeed, the tangents from B to the circle meet the circle at points F and some other point; the line joining those two points of tangency is the polar of B, which does not pass through B. So we incorrectly assumed that AB is the polar line. Actually AB is one of the tangents from B to the incircle, not the line joining the two tangency points. So we need to find the tangent line from B that passes through B and touches the incircle at point F. That line is not the polar; it's the line through B and tangent point F. So we need to compute that line differently.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477944924,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477946147,"data":"       | Thus our earlier approach using polar is not correct for AB. Let's compute AB directly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477947437,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47794862,"data":"       | Given point B (0,0) external to circle with center I (d,r) and radius r, there are two tangent lines from B to the circle. The line we need is the one that will be side AB of triangle; the other tangent from B would be extension of side? Actually there are two tangents: one touches incircle at point between B and D (i.e., near B) and the other touches incircle at point beyond D (maybe near A). The side AB is the tangent that lies on the same side of BC as A (above BC). Since A is above BC, the tangent from B to incircle that goes above BC will touch incircle at point F, with y-coordinate \u003e0. So we need to find equation of line through B and tangent to circle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477950985,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477952148,"data":"       | We can compute slope m_B of line BF such that distance from I to line equals r (tangency). For line through B with slope m: equation y = m x. Distance from I = (|m d - r|) / sqrt(m^2 + 1). This must equal r. So:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477953691,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477954843,"data":"       | |m d - r| / sqrt(m^2 + 1) = r.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477956023,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47795724,"data":"       | Since I is above line? For line above BC, we have I (d,r) above line y = m x? Actually for m positive small? Let's compute sign: For line y = m x, point I has y = r, x = d. The line's value at x=d is y=m d. Since I is above line (the incircle is above BC), we expect r \u003e m d. So m d - r \u003c 0, absolute value = r - m d. So equation: (r - m d) / sqrt(m^2 + 1) = r =\u003e r - m d = r sqrt(m^2 + 1) =\u003e bring terms: r - m d = r sqrt(m^2 + 1). Solve for m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477959354,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477960518,"data":"       | Similarly for line through C with slope m_C (or expressed as line equation y = m_C (x - a) maybe). For line through C (a,0) with slope m_C: equation y = m_C (x - a). Distance from I to this line is r. Compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477962053,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477963203,"data":"       | Let's solve for m_B and m_C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477964697,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47796584,"data":"       | From equation: r - m d = r sqrt(m^2 + 1) =\u003e divide both sides by r: 1 - (m d)/r = sqrt(m^2 + 1). Square both sides:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477967214,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477968375,"data":"       | (1 - (m d)/r)^2 = m^2 + 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47796957,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477970737,"data":"       | Expand left: 1 - 2 (m d)/r + (m^2 d^2)/r^2 = m^2 + 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477973014,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477974194,"data":"       | Cancel 1 both sides: - 2 (m d)/r + (m^2 d^2)/r^2 = m^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477975428,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477976577,"data":"       | Multiply both sides by r^2: -2 m d r + m^2 d^2 = m^2 r^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47797782,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477979001,"data":"       | Bring all to one side: m^2 d^2 - m^2 r^2 - 2 m d r = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477980234,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477981397,"data":"       | Factor m (since m may be zero? but not zero because then line is horizontal y=0 which is BC). Let's factor m:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47798273,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477983887,"data":"       | m (m (d^2 - r^2) - 2 d r) = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477985054,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477986198,"data":"       | m = 0 is not solution because then distance from I to line y=0 is r, which is true (since I is at (d,r)). But line y=0 is BC, not AB. So we need non-zero m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477987631,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477988784,"data":"       | Thus we have m (d^2 - r^2) - 2 d r = 0 =\u003e m = 2 d r / (d^2 - r^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477990078,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477991248,"data":"       | Thus slope of AB is m_B = 2 d r / (d^2 - r^2). Note sign: Since d\u003e0, r\u003e0, denominator d^2 - r^2 positive if d \u003e r. Usually incircle center is above BC, d is horizontal coordinate of D, not radius. Typically d may be larger than r? Not necessarily; but for typical triangle, incircle radius r is less than distance from I to B? Actually d is horizontal coordinate of incircle center relative to B. Since incircle touches BC at D, the radius is vertical, so r is vertical distance from BC to I. d is horizontal distance from B to D. Usually d can be less than r? Not typical; but for a tall narrow triangle with small base, r could be large relative to d. However, for typical triangle, r is less than d? Not sure. But denominator must be non-zero; if d = r, then slope infinite? Actually denominator zero leads to vertical line (infinite slope). That would correspond to AB being vertical line through B? Not possible because B is at (0,0). So we assume d != r.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.4779942,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477995665,"data":"       | Now compute slope of AC. For line through C (a,0) with slope m_C (so equation y = m_C (x - a)). Distance from I to line: formula for distance from point (x0,y0) to line y = m (x - a) =\u003e rewrite as m x - y - m a = 0. So line: m x - y - m a = 0. Distance from I = |m d - r - m a| / sqrt(m^2 + 1) = |m (d - a) - r| / sqrt(m^2 + 1). Since d \u003c a (since D between B and C), d - a is negative. For line above BC, we expect the point I above the line, so m (d - a) - r \u003c 0? Let's compute sign later.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477997844,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.477998982,"data":"       | Set distance = r: |m (d - a) - r| = r sqrt(m^2 + 1). Since d - a = - (a - d) negative, m positive? Let's solve similarly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478000354,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478001512,"data":"       | Let m = m_C. Let Δ = d - a = - (a - d) = -(a - d) \u003c 0. Write equation:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47800288,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478005055,"data":"       | |m Δ - r| = r sqrt(m^2 + 1). Since Δ negative, m positive maybe, m Δ negative, so m Δ - r is negative - r, so absolute value = -(m Δ - r) = r - m Δ (since r \u003e m Δ? Actually m Δ negative, so -m Δ positive; r - m Δ \u003e0). So we have r - m Δ = r sqrt(m^2 + 1). But Δ negative, so -m Δ = m (a - d) positive. So r + m (a - d) = r sqrt(m^2 + 1). Wait careful: Since Δ = d - a = -(a - d). So m Δ = - m (a - d). So r - m Δ = r + m (a - d). So equation becomes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478007487,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478008634,"data":"       | r + m (a - d) = r sqrt(m^2 + 1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478009811,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478010922,"data":"       | Solve for m:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478012067,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478013254,"data":"       | r + m (a - d) = r sqrt(m^2 + 1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478014444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47801561,"data":"       | Divide by r: 1 + m (a - d)/r = sqrt(m^2 + 1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478016811,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478017962,"data":"       | Square both sides:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478019112,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47802024,"data":"       | (1 + m (a - d)/r)^2 = m^2 + 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478021481,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478023411,"data":"       | Expand left: 1 + 2 m (a - d)/r + m^2 (a - d)^2 / r^2 = m^2 + 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478024672,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478025804,"data":"       | Cancel 1: 2 m (a - d)/r + m^2 (a - d)^2 / r^2 = m^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478027038,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47802816,"data":"       | Multiply by r^2:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47802933,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478030468,"data":"       | 2 m (a - d) r + m^2 (a - d)^2 = m^2 r^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478031667,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47803283,"data":"       | Bring to one side:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478034004,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478035164,"data":"       | m^2 (a - d)^2 - m^2 r^2 + 2 m (a - d) r = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478036371,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478037481,"data":"       | Factor m:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478038617,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478039738,"data":"       | m (m ((a - d)^2 - r^2) + 2 (a - d) r) = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478040934,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.4780421,"data":"       | Again m=0 is not solution (line y=0). So:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.4780433,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478044444,"data":"       | m ((a - d)^2 - r^2) + 2 (a - d) r = 0 =\u003e\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478045684,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478046814,"data":"       | m = - 2 (a - d) r / ((a - d)^2 - r^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478047992,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478049441,"data":"       | But note denominator (a - d)^2 - r^2. Since a - d = CD = F_{n+1} \u003e0. Usually r less than (a - d) maybe. So denominator positive. So m negative? Because numerator -2 (a - d) r negative, denominator positive =\u003e m negative. However, slope of line through C to A should be positive? Actually A is above BC, to the left of C, so line from C to A slopes upward leftwards, which is negative slope (since as x decreases from a to d, y increases). So slope negative is correct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478052744,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478053927,"data":"       | Thus m_C = - 2 (a - d) r / ((a - d)^2 - r^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478055141,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478056307,"data":"       | Now we have slopes m_B (positive) and m_C (negative). Intersection of lines yields point A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478057592,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478058717,"data":"       | Line AB: y = m_B x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478059864,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478060982,"data":"       | Line AC: y = m_C (x - a) = m_C x - m_C a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478062198,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478063378,"data":"       | Set equal: m_B x = m_C x - m_C a =\u003e (m_B - m_C) x = - m_C a =\u003e x = - m_C a / (m_B - m_C).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478064724,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478065835,"data":"       | Compute y = m_B x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478067002,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47806814,"data":"       | Now we can compute coordinates of A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478069322,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478070487,"data":"       | But earlier we derived that u = d; maybe that holds after solving? Let's test numerically for some values.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478071834,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478073001,"data":"       | Pick n=5: F_5 = 5? Let's compute Fibonacci: F_0=0, F_1=1, F_2=1, F_3=2, F_4=3, F_5=5, F_6=8, F_7=13. So n=5 =\u003e BD=5, CD=8, BC=13. So d=5, a=13. Choose some r value, say r=2. Compute slopes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478074497,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478075657,"data":"       | Let's test with python.assistantanalysis to=python codeimport math\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478076905,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478078047,"data":"       | def compute_A(d, a, r):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478079214,"data":"       |     # slopes\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47808039,"data":"       |     mB = 2*d*r / (d**2 - r**2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478081598,"data":"       |     mC = -2*(a-d)*r / ((a-d)**2 - r**2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478082851,"data":"       |     # intersection\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47808404,"data":"       |     x = -mC*a / (mB - mC)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478085248,"data":"       |     y = mB * x\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478086444,"data":"       |     return x, y, mB, mC\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478087608,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478088737,"data":"       | print(compute_A(5,13,2))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478089935,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478091064,"data":"     [Code]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478092274,"data":"       | import math\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478093424,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478095802,"data":"       | def compute_A(d, a, r):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478097,"data":"       |     # slopes\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478098171,"data":"       |     mB = 2*d*r / (d**2 - r**2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478099379,"data":"       |     mC = -2*(a-d)*r / ((a-d)**2 - r**2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478100607,"data":"       |     # intersection\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478101832,"data":"       |     x = -mC*a / (mB - mC)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478103078,"data":"       |     y = mB * x\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478104287,"data":"       |     return x, y, mB, mC\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478105458,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478106599,"data":"       | print(compute_A(5,13,2))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478107807,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478108981,"data":"     [Output]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478110484,"data":"       \u003e (4.666666666666667, 4.444444444444445, 0.9523809523809523, -0.5333333333333333)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478111797,"data":"       \u003e \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478113035,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478114144,"data":"     [Turn 2]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478115319,"data":"     [Reasoning] (9537 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478116527,"data":"       | analysisWe got x ≈ 4.6667, which is not equal to d=5. So earlier derived u = d is not correct; that was due to wrong assumption.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478117979,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478119142,"data":"       | Thus A is not directly above D generally; its x-coordinate depends on r.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478120405,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478121539,"data":"       | Now we can compute other points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478122798,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478123994,"data":"       | Given A, we can compute points E and F (touch points of incircle with AC and AB). However we could compute them directly as foot of perpendicular from I to lines AB and AC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47812547,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478126625,"data":"       | Given line AB: y = m_B x (through B). The foot of perpendicular from I (d,r) onto line AB is point F. Coordinates can be computed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47812801,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478129139,"data":"       | Similarly foot onto line AC is point E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478130344,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478131512,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can compute E and F using formula for foot of perpendicular.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47813284,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478133997,"data":"       | Given line AB: direction vector (1,m_B). Unit normal vector? The foot of perpendicular from point (x0,y0) to line y = m_B x is given by:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478135437,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478136635,"data":"       | Let line equation: m_B x - y = 0. Then foot coordinates (x_f, y_f) = ( (x0 + m_B y0) / (1 + m_B**2), (m_B (x0 + m_B y0) / (1 + m_B**2) ) ). Actually formula: projection onto line: For line through origin with direction vector v = (1,m), the orthogonal projection of point p onto line is ( (p·v) / (v·v) ) v. Since line passes through origin (B). Indeed AB passes through B (0,0). So foot of perpendicular from I to AB is projection onto line AB: v = (1,m_B). Compute dot product p·v = d*1 + r*m_B = d + r*m_B. v·v = 1 + m_B^2. So projection point coordinates: ((d + r*m_B) / (1 + m_B**2)) * (1, m_B). So F = ( (d + r*m_B) / (1 + m_B**2), m_B * (d + r*m_B) / (1 + m_B**2) ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478140429,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478141877,"data":"       | Similarly, line AC passes through C (a,0) with direction vector (1, m_C) but offset. Actually line equation: y = m_C (x - a). So direction vector also (1,m_C). To find foot from I to line AC, we can shift coordinates: subtract point C. Let p' = I - C = (d - a, r). Then projection onto direction vector v = (1,m_C): projection = ((p'·v)/(v·v)) v. Then foot E = C + projection.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478143825,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478144979,"data":"       | Thus compute E coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478146189,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478147327,"data":"       | Now compute EF line; N is foot of perpendicular from D (d,0) to line EF. Compute N coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478148677,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478149827,"data":"       | Compute K: intersection of circumcircle of AEF with circumcircle of ABC (Ω). We have coordinates of A, B, C, E, F. Compute circumcircle of ABC: center O (circumcenter) and radius R. Compute circumcircle of AEF: center O1 and radius R1. Intersection points: A is common; other intersection K can be found by solving intersection of two circles (two solutions). Compute using sympy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478151709,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478152837,"data":"       | Then K' is reflection of K across line EF: reflect point across line.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478154128,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478155554,"data":"       | Condition: quadrilateral K N K' B cyclic. That means points K, N, K', B lie on same circle. Equivalent to oriented angles? But we can enforce that distances satisfy: The four points are concyclic if and only if oriented angle KBN = K'BN? Actually B,N,K,K' cyclic means that the cross ratio of distances? Simpler: Points B, K, N, K' are concyclic if and only if ∠KBN = ∠K'BN? Wait B is common vertex. Actually for four points A,B,C,D to be cyclic, we need ∠ABC = ∠ADC (angles subtended by same chord). So we can test condition: ∠KBN = ∠K'BN? No, both angles share vertex B? Actually angle KBN uses points K-B-N; angle K'BN uses K'-B-N. For cyclic quadrilateral B K N K', we need ∠KBN = ∠K'KN? Let's recall: In cyclic quadrilateral, opposite angles sum to 180°, or angle between two chords equals angle between other two chords. For points B, K, N, K', the condition is that quadrilateral B K N K' is cyclic if and only if ∠KBN = ∠K'KN (angles subtended by same chord KN). Or equivalently, ∠KBN = ∠K'BN? Actually chord KN subtends angle KBN at point B and angle K' N K at point K'? Wait chord KN subtends angle KBN at point B (since endpoints K,N). At another point on the circle, say K', chord KN subtends angle K' N K? Actually angle K' N K uses points K', N, K (vertex N). So not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478159244,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478160404,"data":"       | Better: Use condition that oriented cross product of vectors? Or use that four points are concyclic if and only if the pairwise distances satisfy power of point: (BK)*(BK') = (BN)*(BN')? Wait N' not defined.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478161961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478165127,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can use condition that the points B, K, N, K' have equal power with respect to circle passing through B, K, N. So K' must satisfy that its power relative to circle through B, K, N is zero. So we can compute circle through B, K, N and check if K' lies on it.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478166828,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478168014,"data":"       | Thus we can compute circle through B, K, N (determine its center and radius) and then test if distance from K' to that center equals radius (within tolerance). This yields equation for r.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478169528,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478170664,"data":"       | Thus we can solve for r numerically for a given n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478171927,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478173071,"data":"       | Now compute T: Circle tangent to BN at B and passing through K. Determine its center: Intersection of line perpendicular to BN through B (call line L) and perpendicular bisector of BK (since center O2 must be equidistant from B and K). Solve for center O2. Then compute intersection of that circle with line BC (x-axis) other than B to get T. Then compute lengths: CT = distance between C (a,0) and T (x_T,0) = |a - x_T|. BT = distance between B (0,0) and T = |x_T|. NB = distance between N and B (distance from B to N). NE = distance between N and E (point E). Compute ratio.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478175284,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478176415,"data":"       | Now we need to find maximum ratio over possible r satisfying cyclic condition. Likely there are two possible r values (maybe one yields K,N,K',B cyclic). But maybe there is a continuum of r that satisfy cyclic condition? Let's test numerically for a given n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478178079,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47817921,"data":"       | We'll implement a numeric solver for r for n=5, maybe n=6, etc. Use sympy's nsolve.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478180542,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478181695,"data":"       | Let's code.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478182901,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478184029,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codeimport sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478185282,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478186422,"data":"       | def compute_ratio(n, r_val):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478187615,"data":"       |     # Fibonacci numbers\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478188814,"data":"       |     F = [0,1]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478190302,"data":"       |     for i in range(2, n+3):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478191537,"data":"       |         F.append(F[-1] + F[-2])\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478192937,"data":"       |     F_n = F[n]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478194112,"data":"       |     F_np1 = F[n+1]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478195288,"data":"       |     F_np2 = F[n+2]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47819645,"data":"       |     d = F_n\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478197575,"data":"       |     a = F_np2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478198751,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478199895,"data":"       |     # define symbols\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478201094,"data":"       |     r = sp.nsimplify(r_val) # may be rational or float\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478204091,"data":"       |     # slopes\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478205294,"data":"       |     mB = 2*d*r / (d**2 - r**2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478206509,"data":"       |     mC = -2*(a-d)*r / ((a-d)**2 - r**2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478207728,"data":"       |     # intersection A\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478208902,"data":"       |     xA = -mC*a / (mB - mC)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478210098,"data":"       |     yA = mB * xA\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478211265,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47821242,"data":"       |     # points B, C\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478213614,"data":"       |     B = sp.Point(0,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478214784,"data":"       |     C = sp.Point(a,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478215934,"data":"       |     # line AB, AC\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47821714,"data":"       |     lineAB = sp.Line(B, sp.Point(1, mB)) # direction (1,mB)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478218435,"data":"       |     lineAC = sp.Line(C, sp.Point(C.x+1, mC)) # direction (1,mC)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478219704,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47822086,"data":"       |     # foot F from I to AB\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478222034,"data":"       |     I = sp.Point(d, r)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478223257,"data":"       |     # projection onto line AB (through origin)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47822448,"data":"       |     # using formula\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478225668,"data":"       |     denom_AB = 1 + mB**2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47822687,"data":"       |     xF = (d + r*mB) / denom_AB\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478228051,"data":"       |     yF = mB * xF\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478229237,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478230391,"data":"       |     # foot E from I to AC\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478231577,"data":"       |     # shift coordinates: vector from C to I\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478232807,"data":"       |     dx = d - a\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478233977,"data":"       |     dy = r - 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478235147,"data":"       |     # projection onto direction (1,mC)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478236374,"data":"       |     denom_AC = 1 + mC**2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478237545,"data":"       |     proj = (dx + dy*mC) / denom_AC\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478238782,"data":"       |     xE = a + proj\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47823995,"data":"       |     yE = mC * proj\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47824117,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478242355,"data":"       |     # points\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478243554,"data":"       |     F_pt = sp.Point(xF, yF)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478244754,"data":"       |     E_pt = sp.Point(xE, yE)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478245922,"data":"       |     A_pt = sp.Point(xA, yA)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478247119,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478248226,"data":"       |     # circumcircle of ABC\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478249409,"data":"       |     circ_ABC = sp.Circle(B, C, A_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478250628,"data":"       |     # circumcircle of AEF\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478253165,"data":"       |     circ_AEF = sp.Circle(A_pt, E_pt, F_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478254444,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478255595,"data":"       |     # intersection points\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478256794,"data":"       |     inter = circ_ABC.intersection(circ_AEF)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478258017,"data":"       |     # there should be two points: A and K\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478259256,"data":"       |     # identify K as not equal to A\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478260476,"data":"       |     K_pt = None\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478265334,"data":"       |     for p in inter:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478266726,"data":"       |         if not p.equals(A_pt):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478267949,"data":"       |             K_pt = p\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47826917,"data":"       |     if K_pt is None:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478270687,"data":"       |         raise ValueError(\"K not found\")\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478271964,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478273071,"data":"       |     # line EF\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478274242,"data":"       |     lineEF = sp.Line(E_pt, F_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478275482,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478276687,"data":"       |     # reflect K across EF to get K'\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478277926,"data":"       |     Kprime_pt = K_pt.reflect(lineEF)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478279115,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478280264,"data":"       |     # N: foot from D to EF\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47828146,"data":"       |     D_pt = sp.Point(d,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478282628,"data":"       |     N_pt = lineEF.projection(D_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478283866,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478284964,"data":"       |     # Check cyclic condition: B, K, N, K' concyclic?\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478286279,"data":"       |     # Compute circle through B, K, N\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478287479,"data":"       |     circ_BKN = sp.Circle(B, K_pt, N_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478288674,"data":"       |     # distance from K' to center\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47828991,"data":"       |     cyclic = sp.simplify(circ_BKN.distance(Kprime_pt) - circ_BKN.radius)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478291175,"data":"       |     # cyclic should be zero (within tolerance)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478292454,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478293559,"data":"       |     # circle tangent to BN at B and passing through K\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478294801,"data":"       |     # line BN\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478295996,"data":"       |     lineBN = sp.Line(B, N_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478297181,"data":"       |     # direction of BN: vector from B to N\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478298371,"data":"       |     # center O2 lies on line perpendicular to BN at B\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478299608,"data":"       |     # slope of BN: compute direction vector\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478300796,"data":"       |     vBN = sp.Matrix([N_pt.x, N_pt.y])  # B is origin\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478303561,"data":"       |     # line perpendicular to BN through B: direction vector perpendicular to vBN\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478304885,"data":"       |     # param eq: O2 = t * (-vBN.y, vBN.x) (rotate 90°)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478306194,"data":"       |     t = sp.symbols('t', real=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478307428,"data":"       |     O2 = sp.Point(-vBN[1]*t, vBN[0]*t)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478308626,"data":"       |     # O2 must be equidistant to B and K\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478309831,"data":"       |     eq = O2.distance(B) - O2.distance(K_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47831105,"data":"       |     sol_t = sp.solve(eq, t)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47831229,"data":"       |     # there may be two solutions (positive/negative). Choose the one where O2 is on same side as K? We'll compute both.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478313668,"data":"       |     ratios = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478314834,"data":"       |     for sol in sol_t:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478316016,"data":"       |         O2_sol = sp.Point(-vBN[1]*sol, vBN[0]*sol)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478317299,"data":"       |         # radius\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478318477,"data":"       |         R2 = O2_sol.distance(B)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478319697,"data":"       |         # intersection of circle with BC (x-axis y=0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478320965,"data":"       |         # circle equation: (x - O2.x)^2 + (y - O2.y)^2 = R2^2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478322226,"data":"       |         # set y=0, solve for x\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478323406,"data":"       |         x = sp.symbols('x', real=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478324975,"data":"       |         eq_circle = (x - O2_sol.x)**2 + (0 - O2_sol.y)**2 - R2**2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478326282,"data":"       |         sol_x = sp.solve(eq_circle, x)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478327544,"data":"       |         # there will be two solutions: x=0 (B) and other T\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478328804,"data":"       |         for solx in sol_x:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478329994,"data":"       |             if not sp.simplify(solx) == 0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478331222,"data":"       |                 T_pt = sp.Point(solx, 0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478332442,"data":"       |                 # compute lengths\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478333634,"data":"       |                 CT = C.distance(T_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478334841,"data":"       |                 BT = B.distance(T_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478336058,"data":"       |                 NB = N_pt.distance(B)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478337282,"data":"       |                 NE = N_pt.distance(E_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478338506,"data":"       |                 ratio = sp.simplify(CT * NB / (BT * NE))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478339772,"data":"       |                 ratios.append(ratio)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478340954,"data":"       |     # return ratio(s) and cyclic condition value\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478342171,"data":"       |     return {\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478343322,"data":"       |         'ratio': ratios,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478344496,"data":"       |         'cyclic': sp.simplify(cyclic),\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478346882,"data":"       |         'points': {\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478348162,"data":"       |             'A': A_pt, 'B': B, 'C': C, 'D': D_pt, 'E': E_pt, 'F': F_pt,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478349455,"data":"       |             'K': K_pt, 'Kprime': Kprime_pt, 'N': N_pt\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478350717,"data":"       |         }\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478351902,"data":"       |     }\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478353094,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478354234,"data":"       | # Test for n=5, r=2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478355435,"data":"       | result = compute_ratio(5, 2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478356662,"data":"       | result['cyclic']\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478358631,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478359757,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478360885,"data":"     [Code] [ERROR]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478362045,"data":"       | import sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47836323,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478364374,"data":"       | def compute_ratio(n, r_val):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478365586,"data":"       |     # Fibonacci numbers\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478366767,"data":"       |     F = [0,1]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478367949,"data":"       |     for i in range(2, n+3):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478369149,"data":"       |         F.append(F[-1] + F[-2])\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478370343,"data":"       |     F_n = F[n]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478371541,"data":"       |     F_np1 = F[n+1]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478372695,"data":"       |     F_np2 = F[n+2]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478373865,"data":"       |     d = F_n\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478375018,"data":"       |     a = F_np2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478376164,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478377361,"data":"       |     # define symbols\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478378561,"data":"       |     r = sp.nsimplify(r_val) # may be rational or float\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478379797,"data":"       |     # slopes\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478380982,"data":"       |     mB = 2*d*r / (d**2 - r**2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478382194,"data":"       |     mC = -2*(a-d)*r / ((a-d)**2 - r**2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478383401,"data":"       |     # intersection A\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478384625,"data":"       |     xA = -mC*a / (mB - mC)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478385813,"data":"       |     yA = mB * xA\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478387045,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478388179,"data":"       |     # points B, C\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478389335,"data":"       |     B = sp.Point(0,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478390515,"data":"       |     C = sp.Point(a,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478391682,"data":"       |     # line AB, AC\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478392868,"data":"       |     lineAB = sp.Line(B, sp.Point(1, mB)) # direction (1,mB)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47839448,"data":"       |     lineAC = sp.Line(C, sp.Point(C.x+1, mC)) # direction (1,mC)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478396893,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478398072,"data":"       |     # foot F from I to AB\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478399257,"data":"       |     I = sp.Point(d, r)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478400449,"data":"       |     # projection onto line AB (through origin)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478401658,"data":"       |     # using formula\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478402879,"data":"       |     denom_AB = 1 + mB**2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478404081,"data":"       |     xF = (d + r*mB) / denom_AB\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478405275,"data":"       |     yF = mB * xF\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478406477,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478407621,"data":"       |     # foot E from I to AC\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478408824,"data":"       |     # shift coordinates: vector from C to I\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478410023,"data":"       |     dx = d - a\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478411179,"data":"       |     dy = r - 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478412359,"data":"       |     # projection onto direction (1,mC)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478413589,"data":"       |     denom_AC = 1 + mC**2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478414813,"data":"       |     proj = (dx + dy*mC) / denom_AC\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478416041,"data":"       |     xE = a + proj\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47841726,"data":"       |     yE = mC * proj\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47841844,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478419561,"data":"       |     # points\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478420728,"data":"       |     F_pt = sp.Point(xF, yF)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478421975,"data":"       |     E_pt = sp.Point(xE, yE)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47842321,"data":"       |     A_pt = sp.Point(xA, yA)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478424411,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478425557,"data":"       |     # circumcircle of ABC\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478426767,"data":"       |     circ_ABC = sp.Circle(B, C, A_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478428009,"data":"       |     # circumcircle of AEF\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478429226,"data":"       |     circ_AEF = sp.Circle(A_pt, E_pt, F_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478430454,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478431592,"data":"       |     # intersection points\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478432779,"data":"       |     inter = circ_ABC.intersection(circ_AEF)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478434013,"data":"       |     # there should be two points: A and K\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478435251,"data":"       |     # identify K as not equal to A\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478436502,"data":"       |     K_pt = None\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478437677,"data":"       |     for p in inter:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478438862,"data":"       |         if not p.equals(A_pt):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478440078,"data":"       |             K_pt = p\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478441269,"data":"       |     if K_pt is None:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478442436,"data":"       |         raise ValueError(\"K not found\")\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478443713,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478446023,"data":"       |     # line EF\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478447244,"data":"       |     lineEF = sp.Line(E_pt, F_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478448439,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478449591,"data":"       |     # reflect K across EF to get K'\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47845081,"data":"       |     Kprime_pt = K_pt.reflect(lineEF)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47845204,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478453187,"data":"       |     # N: foot from D to EF\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478454365,"data":"       |     D_pt = sp.Point(d,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478455559,"data":"       |     N_pt = lineEF.projection(D_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478456821,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478457942,"data":"       |     # Check cyclic condition: B, K, N, K' concyclic?\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478459181,"data":"       |     # Compute circle through B, K, N\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478460383,"data":"       |     circ_BKN = sp.Circle(B, K_pt, N_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478461595,"data":"       |     # distance from K' to center\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478462824,"data":"       |     cyclic = sp.simplify(circ_BKN.distance(Kprime_pt) - circ_BKN.radius)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478464102,"data":"       |     # cyclic should be zero (within tolerance)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478465342,"data":"       |     \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478466826,"data":"       |     # circle tangent to BN at B and passing through K\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478468106,"data":"       |     # line BN\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478469254,"data":"       |     lineBN = sp.Line(B, N_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478470468,"data":"       |     # direction of BN: vector from B to N\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478471694,"data":"       |     # center O2 lies on line perpendicular to BN at B\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478472978,"data":"       |     # slope of BN: compute direction vector\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478474188,"data":"       |     vBN = sp.Matrix([N_pt.x, N_pt.y])  # B is origin\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478475437,"data":"       |     # line perpendicular to BN through B: direction vector perpendicular to vBN\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478476756,"data":"       |     # param eq: O2 = t * (-vBN.y, vBN.x) (rotate 90°)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47847804,"data":"       |     t = sp.symbols('t', real=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478479256,"data":"       |     O2 = sp.Point(-vBN[1]*t, vBN[0]*t)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478480459,"data":"       |     # O2 must be equidistant to B and K\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478481659,"data":"       |     eq = O2.distance(B) - O2.distance(K_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47848287,"data":"       |     sol_t = sp.solve(eq, t)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478484122,"data":"       |     # there may be two solutions (positive/negative). Choose the one where O2 is on same side as K? We'll compute both.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478485517,"data":"       |     ratios = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47848673,"data":"       |     for sol in sol_t:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478487895,"data":"       |         O2_sol = sp.Point(-vBN[1]*sol, vBN[0]*sol)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47849034,"data":"       |         # radius\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478491515,"data":"       |         R2 = O2_sol.distance(B)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478492704,"data":"       |         # intersection of circle with BC (x-axis y=0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478493971,"data":"       |         # circle equation: (x - O2.x)^2 + (y - O2.y)^2 = R2^2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478495224,"data":"       |         # set y=0, solve for x\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478496426,"data":"       |         x = sp.symbols('x', real=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478497689,"data":"       |         eq_circle = (x - O2_sol.x)**2 + (0 - O2_sol.y)**2 - R2**2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478498948,"data":"       |         sol_x = sp.solve(eq_circle, x)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478500171,"data":"       |         # there will be two solutions: x=0 (B) and other T\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478501419,"data":"       |         for solx in sol_x:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478502611,"data":"       |             if not sp.simplify(solx) == 0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47850381,"data":"       |                 T_pt = sp.Point(solx, 0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478505021,"data":"       |                 # compute lengths\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478506206,"data":"       |                 CT = C.distance(T_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478507462,"data":"       |                 BT = B.distance(T_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478508679,"data":"       |                 NB = N_pt.distance(B)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478509872,"data":"       |                 NE = N_pt.distance(E_pt)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478511096,"data":"       |                 ratio = sp.simplify(CT * NB / (BT * NE))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478512343,"data":"       |                 ratios.append(ratio)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478513533,"data":"       |     # return ratio(s) and cyclic condition value\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478514768,"data":"       |     return {\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478515997,"data":"       |         'ratio': ratios,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478517482,"data":"       |         'cyclic': sp.simplify(cyclic),\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478518715,"data":"       |         'points': {\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478519936,"data":"       |             'A': A_pt, 'B': B, 'C': C, 'D': D_pt, 'E': E_pt, 'F': F_pt,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478521222,"data":"       |             'K': K_pt, 'Kprime': Kprime_pt, 'N': N_pt\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478522688,"data":"       |         }\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.478523857,"data":"       |     }\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.47852502,"data":"       | \n"}
