,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.4807832,"data":"       | We have BD = s - b, CD = s - c. Similarly, CE = s - c? Let's recall that the incircle tangency lengths from each vertex are equal to the semiperimeter minus the opposite side length. More precisely, the distance from vertex B to the touch point on side AB is s - b? Actually side AB opposite C. Let's check known formulas: In a triangle, the lengths from vertex A to the points of tangency on AB and AC are both equal to s - a. Similarly, from vertex B to tangency points on BA and BC are both s - b. And from vertex C to tangency points on CA and CB are both s - c. Yes, that's the standard property: The incircle tangency points partition each side such that each vertex's two adjacent tangency segments are equal. So:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480785685,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480786854,"data":"       | - On side AB, we have AF = s - a (distance from A to F) and BF = s - b (distance from B to F).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480788193,"data":"       | - On side AC, we have AE = s - a (distance from A to E) and CE = s - c (distance from C to E).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480789494,"data":"       | - On side BC, we have BD = s - b (distance from B to D) and CD = s - c (distance from C to D).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480790819,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48079195,"data":"       | Thus BD = s - b, CD = s - c, and also BF = s - b, AF = s - a, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480793266,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480802508,"data":"       | Thus BD and BF are equal. Similarly CD = CE? Wait CD = s - c, CE = s - c, yes CD = CE. So D and E are symmetric w.r.t. incircle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480804027,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480805187,"data":"       | Thus BD = BF = s - b = F_n, CD = CE = s - c = F_{n+1}. So we have BD = BF = F_n, CD = CE = F_{n+1}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480806551,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48080768,"data":"       | Thus we have side lengths expressed in terms of these tangency lengths. Let's denote x = F_n, y = F_{n+1}. Then s - b = x, s - c = y.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480809115,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480810279,"data":"       | We also have side lengths: a = BC = BD + CD = x + y. b = CA = CE + AE = y + (s - a). Wait AE = s - a. So b = CE + AE = y + (s - a). But s - a = ? Since a = BC = x + y. So s = (a + b + c)/2. But we have unknown b and c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480811851,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480813299,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can express b and c in terms of s and x, y: b = s - x (since s - b = x =\u003e b = s - x). Similarly, c = s - y (since s - c = y =\u003e c = s - y).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480815938,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480817129,"data":"       | Thus we have side lengths expressed in terms of s, x, y. So we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480818436,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480819574,"data":"       | - a = x + y (since BD + CD).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480820828,"data":"       | - b = s - x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480822011,"data":"       | - c = s - y.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480823215,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480824401,"data":"       | Now we can compute s = (a + b + c)/2 = (x + y + (s - x) + (s - y))/2 = (2s)/2 = s. So identity holds. So s is free variable; but we have constraints that side lengths must be positive: b = s - x \u003e 0 =\u003e s \u003e x; c = s - y \u003e 0 =\u003e s \u003e y. Also triangle inequality: a + b \u003e c, a + c \u003e b, b + c \u003e a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480826423,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480827571,"data":"       | Compute b + c = (s - x) + (s - y) = 2s - (x + y) = 2s - a. Since a = x + y. So b + c \u003e a =\u003e 2s - a \u003e a =\u003e 2s \u003e 2a =\u003e s \u003e a = x + y. So s \u003e x + y.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480829291,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480830439,"data":"       | Similarly a + b \u003e c =\u003e (x + y) + (s - x) \u003e s - y =\u003e x + y + s - x \u003e s - y =\u003e y + s \u003e s - y =\u003e y \u003e -y =\u003e always true (since y \u003e 0). So that inequality holds automatically. Similarly a + c \u003e b =\u003e x + y + s - y \u003e s - x =\u003e x + s \u003e s - x =\u003e x \u003e -x =\u003e always true. So only nontrivial condition is s \u003e a = x + y, and also s \u003e max(x, y). Since x,y positive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480832897,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480834044,"data":"       | Thus we can choose any s \u003e x + y. So there is a family of triangles with given incircle tangency lengths x, y (i.e., BD, CD). So there is a continuum of possible triangles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480835606,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480836729,"data":"       | Now we also have condition that quadrilateral KNK'B is cyclic.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480838014,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.4808392,"data":"       | We need to analyze geometry to find constraints on triangle shape (i.e., s). Then we need to compute ratio R = (CT * NB) / (BT * NE). We need to find maximum of R over allowed s (or over shape) for given x,y. Then a_n is maximum over all triangles for given n (i.e., given x=F_n, y=F_{n+1}) of R. Then as n grows large (even indices), we need limit supremum.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480841054,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480842413,"data":"       | Thus we need to derive expression of R in terms of x,y and perhaps s, and then maximize over s subject to cyclic condition. Then we need asymptotic behavior as n large (x,y large). Since Fibonacci numbers grow exponentially (phi^n / sqrt(5)). So ratio of successive terms tends to phi (golden ratio). So x = F_n, y = F_{n+1} ~ phi^n / sqrt(5), phi^{n+1} / sqrt(5) = phi * x. So y ≈ phi * x for large n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480844373,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480845537,"data":"       | Thus we can treat x and y as large numbers with ratio approx phi. So we need to find limit of maximum ratio R as n→∞ even (n large). Perhaps the maximum ratio tends to a constant independent of n, expressed as p + sqrt(q). So we need to find that constant.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480848375,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480849595,"data":"       | Thus we need to derive R expression as function of x,y and maybe s (or other parameters). Then find maximum over s satisfying cyclic condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480851043,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480852188,"data":"       | Thus we need to analyze geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480853395,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480854704,"data":"       | Let's set up coordinate geometry perhaps to compute lengths and conditions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480856156,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480857307,"data":"       | Given triangle ABC, incircle ω touches BC at D, CA at E, AB at F. Let’s place coordinates to simplify: Use incircle as unit circle? Or use coordinates with incircle center at origin O (0,0) and radius r. Then D, E, F are points of tangency of incircle with sides. Since incircle is tangent to each side, the sides are lines tangent to the incircle. So we can represent lines BC, CA, AB as tangents to the incircle at points D, E, F respectively.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480859351,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480860504,"data":"       | Thus we can set incircle ω as circle centered at O = (0,0) radius r. Let D, E, F be points on the circle at angles determined by triangle shape. Since incircle touches sides at D,E,F, we can treat D, E, F as points on circle such that the tangents at those points are the sides of triangle. Then triangle ABC is the triangle formed by the intersection of those tangents.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480862377,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480863519,"data":"       | Thus we can param param: Let D = (r cos θ_D, r sin θ_D), E = (r cos θ_E, r sin θ_E), F = (r cos θ_F, r sin θ_F). The tangents at those points have equations: line BC is tangent at D, line CA tangent at E, line AB tangent at F.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480865235,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480866686,"data":"       | We can compute lengths BD, CD etc. However BD is distance along side BC from B to D. B is intersection of tangents at D and F. C is intersection of tangents at D and E. So BD is segment from B to D along line BC. Since D is the point of tangency on BC, BD is the distance from B (intersection of tangents at D and F) to D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480868426,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48086958,"data":"       | We can compute BD in terms of r and the angle between tangents at D and F. There is known relationship: For a circle of radius r, the distance from point of tangency to intersection of two tangents is r * cot(θ/2) where θ is angle between the two radii to the tangency points? Let's derive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480871333,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480872515,"data":"       | Given circle of radius r, two tangents intersect at point B, tangency points are D and F. Then angle DBF (i.e., angle between lines BD and BF) equals angle between tangents = difference between angles of radii? Actually the angle between the two tangents at B equals the angle between the radii to the tangency points (i.e., angle DOF). More precisely, the angle between two tangents drawn from an external point B to a circle is equal to the difference of the arcs they intercept, i.e., angle DBF = angle DOF (the central angle between the two radii). Indeed, the angle between tangents is supplementary to angle D O B? Let's recall: The angle between two tangents from external point B to circle is equal to the difference of the arcs between the points of tangency, and also equal to the angle subtended by the segment connecting the points of tangency at the center. Actually property: If two tangents from B touch circle at D and F, then quadrilateral ODBF is kite with OD = OF = r and DB = FB (tangent lengths). The angle D O F is the central angle between radii to D and F. The angle DBF (at B) is equal to 180° - angle DOF? Let's verify.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480877646,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480879148,"data":"       | Consider external point B, tangents BD and BF. The radius OD is perpendicular to BD, and OF perpendicular to BF. So quadrilateral ODBF has right angles at D and F. So angle D O F is central angle. The angle DBF is external angle at B. Since ODBF is cyclic? Not necessarily. Let's compute using geometry: In triangle ODB, right angle at D. Similarly OFB right angle at F. The angle between the two lines BD and BF equals angle between the two lines perpendicular to OD and OF respectively. Since BD is perpendicular to OD, BF perpendicular to OF, the angle between BD and BF equals angle between the lines perpendicular to OD and OF, which equals angle DOF (since rotating a line by 90° preserves angle). Actually the angle between two lines is invariant under rotation. Rotating both lines by 90° yields angle between OD and OF, which is angle DOF. So angle DBF = angle DOF. Yes, angle between tangents equals central angle between points of tangency. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480882116,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480883286,"data":"       | Thus central angle between D and F is same as angle DBF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480884564,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480885735,"data":"       | Now length of tangent segment from B to D: BD = r * cot(θ/2), where θ is angle DOF? Let's derive: In triangle ODB, we have right angle at D, OD = r, angle OBD = (180° - angle DOF)/2? Actually triangle ODB: O-D-B are not collinear; O to D radius, D to B tangent. Since angle ODB = 90°, and angle DOF = θ. The external point B sees segment OD at angle? Let's compute using geometry: In triangle ODB, angle OBD is half of angle DOF? Wait there is known relation: The length of tangent from external point to circle is sqrt( (distance from external point to center)^2 - r^2 ). But we don't know distance OB.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480888093,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480889529,"data":"       | But we can compute BD in terms of angle between tangents: Let θ = angle DOF = angle DBF. Consider triangle OBD: we have right angle at D, OD = r, angle ODB = 90°, angle OBD = φ, angle BOD = 180° - (90°+ φ) = 90° - φ? Actually sum of angles in triangle OBD: angle O + angle B + angle D = 180°, where angle D = 90°, angle O = angle BOD? Wait O is vertex at O, B is at B, D at D. So angle O = ∠BOD (central angle between OB and OD). Not directly known. But maybe easier: Use known formula: length of tangent segment from B to point of tangency D equals r * cot(θ/2), where θ is angle between the two radii to the two tangency points (i.e., angle DOF). Let's verify with simple case: Suppose the two tangency points are symmetric about x-axis, D at (r,0), F at (r cosθ, r sinθ). Then central angle between them is θ. The external point B is intersection of tangents at D and F. For small θ, B far away, tangent length large. For θ small, cot(θ/2) large, matches. So formula plausible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480892807,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480893957,"data":"       | Thus BD = r * cot(θ/2). Similarly BF = r * cot(θ/2) (since B is same distance to each tangency point). So BD = BF = r * cot(θ/2). This matches earlier: BD = BF = s - b = x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480896652,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480897831,"data":"       | Thus we have x = r * cot(θ_{BF}/2) where θ_{BF} = angle DOF = angle DBF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480899195,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480900362,"data":"       | Similarly, CD = CE = r * cot(φ/2) where φ = angle between radii at D and E, i.e., angle DOE = angle CDE? Actually angle CDE is angle between tangents at D and E, which equals angle DOE. So y = r * cot(φ/2) where φ = angle DOE = angle CDE.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48090205,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480903582,"data":"       | Thus we have two adjacent central angles: between D and F (θ) and between D and E (φ). The third central angle between E and F (let's denote ψ) corresponds to angle at A? Actually A is intersection of tangents at E and F. So central angle between E and F is ψ = angle EOF = angle at A (i.e., angle EAF). So we have three central angles that sum to 360° (full circle) because the three tangents form a triangle circumscribing the incircle. Indeed, D, E, F are points of tangency of incircle with sides BC, CA, AB. The central angles between them sum to 360° (full circle). So θ + φ + ψ = 2π (or 360°). The triangle ABC is the excentral triangle of the contact triangle? Actually contact triangle is D,E,F. The triangle formed by tangents is ABC, and the incircle is the incircle of ABC. So central angles correspond to supplementary of angles of triangle? Let's recall: The angle at vertex A of triangle ABC equals 180° - angle EOF? Wait angle between sides AB and AC is angle BAC, which is angle between tangents at F and E. The angle between tangents equals the central angle between points of tangency (as we argued). So angle BAC = angle EOF = ψ. Similarly angle ABC = angle DOF = θ. Angle ACB = angle DOE = φ. So indeed the central angles equal the angles of triangle ABC. Wait earlier we said angle between tangents equals central angle. So yes, angle at vertex B (∠ABC) equals angle between tangents at B: lines BA (tangent at F) and BC (tangent at D). The tangents at F and D intersect at B. So angle ABC = angle between tangents at F and D = angle between radii OF and OD = central angle ∠FOD = θ. So θ = ∠B. Similarly φ = ∠C, ψ = ∠A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480908162,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480909354,"data":"       | Thus the triangle's angles are exactly the central angles between contact points. So we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480910681,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480911837,"data":"       | - ∠B = θ = angle DOF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480913135,"data":"       | - ∠C = φ = angle DOE.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48091441,"data":"       | - ∠A = ψ = angle EOF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480915595,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480917051,"data":"       | Thus the sum θ + φ + ψ = π (180°) because interior angles of triangle sum to 180°, not 360°. Wait earlier we said sum of central angles around incircle is 360°, but those central angles are measured around the circle, which is the same as the sum of the angles at the center between consecutive tangency points. Since D, E, F are three points on the circle, the arcs between them sum to full circle 360°. However, the central angles between them measured in the same orientation sum to 360°. But the triangle interior angles correspond to those central angles, not their supplements. Let's test with an equilateral triangle: incircle of equilateral triangle touches sides at points spaced 120° apart (since each side is tangent at 60° from each adjacent side?). Actually for equilateral triangle, each interior angle is 60°, and the central angle between tangency points is also 60°. Indeed, incircle is centered at centroid; points of tangency are at 120° arcs? Wait let's compute: In equilateral triangle, incircle touches each side at a point directly opposite the vertex. The central angles between points of tangency are 120°, not 60°, I think. Let's check: For equilateral triangle side length s, incircle radius r = s * sqrt(3) / 6. The points of tangency are at the midpoints of each side? Actually incircle touches each side at its midpoint. The three points are at 120° intervals around the circle (since they are equally spaced). The central angle between any two adjacent tangency points is 120°, not 60°. But the interior angle at each vertex is 60°. So angle between tangents at a vertex equals 180° - central angle? Let's compute: At vertex B, the two sides BA and BC are tangents at points F and D. The angle between these tangents (∠ABC) is interior angle of triangle (60°). The central angle between points of tangency D and F is 120°. So angle between tangents is not equal to central angle, but rather it's supplementary: angle between tangents = 180° - central angle? Let's verify: In equilateral case, central angle = 120°, angle between tangents = 60°, indeed 60° = 180° - 120°. So earlier claim that angle between tangents equals central angle is wrong. Let's correct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48092401,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480925481,"data":"       | Let's analyze geometry: At external point B, the two tangents BD and BF intersect. The angle between the tangents is ∠DBF. The central angle between the radii OD and OF is ∠DOF. Relationship: The angle between tangents equals the difference between 180° and central angle? Actually we derived earlier using rotation: Rotating both lines BD and BF by 90° (counterclockwise) yields lines perpendicular to them: OD and OF (since OD ⟂ BD, OF ⟂ BF). Rotating a line by 90° changes its direction by 90°, but angle between lines is preserved (since it's the absolute difference). However, if we rotate each line by 90° in the same direction, the angle between them stays the same. So angle between BD and BF equals angle between OD and OF. But that would give angle between tangents equals central angle, which contradicts equilateral case. Let's examine orientation: At point B, BD is tangent at D, which is perpendicular to OD, but direction of rotation may be opposite for each line: BD is perpendicular to OD, but orientation of the perpendicular might be outward vs inward? Actually there are two lines perpendicular to a given line at a point: one rotates +90°, other -90°. The tangent line BD is perpendicular to OD, but which side? Similarly BF is perpendicular to OF. The direction of rotation from OD to BD may be +90° (counterclockwise) or -90°, depending on orientation. If we rotate OD by +90° we get a line tangent at D that points outward from the circle on one side; rotating by -90° gives the opposite tangent direction. So the two tangents may be rotated in opposite directions relative to the radii, leading to angle between tangents = 180° - central angle. Indeed, if we rotate OD by +90° to get BD, and rotate OF by -90° to get BF (or vice versa), the angle between BD and BF equals 180° - angle between OD and OF. So the correct relationship is angle between tangents = 180° - central angle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480930404,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480931567,"data":"       | Thus ∠DBF = 180° - ∠DOF. In degrees, ∠B = 180° - θ where θ = central angle between D and F. So central angle = 180° - ∠B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480933222,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480934371,"data":"       | Similarly, central angle between D and E = 180° - ∠C. And between E and F = 180° - ∠A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480935832,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48093809,"data":"       | Thus the sum of central angles = (180° - A) + (180° - B) + (180° - C) = 540° - (A + B + C) = 540° - 180° = 360°, consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480939675,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480940839,"data":"       | Thus we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480942008,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480943181,"data":"       | Let angles of triangle ABC be A, B, C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480944438,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480945561,"data":"       | Then central angles:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480946729,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480947868,"data":"       | - α = ∠EOF = 180° - A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480949162,"data":"       | - β = ∠FOD = 180° - B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480950413,"data":"       | - γ = ∠DOE = 180° - C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480951586,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480952699,"data":"       | Thus we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480953908,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480955086,"data":"       | - BD = r * cot(β/2) (since β = 180° - B). But earlier we derived BD = r * cot(θ/2) where θ is central angle between D and F (i.e., β). So yes BD = r * cot(β/2). Similarly CD = r * cot(γ/2). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480956715,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480957884,"data":"       | Thus we have x = BD = r * cot(β/2) = r * cot((180° - B)/2) = r * cot(90° - B/2) = r * tan(B/2). Since cot(90° - B/2) = tan(B/2). So we get:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480959366,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480960484,"data":"       | x = r * tan(B/2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480961657,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480962835,"data":"       | Similarly, y = CD = r * tan(C/2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480964106,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480965481,"data":"       | Thus we have nice formulas: The distances from vertices to tangency points along sides equal r * tan of half the adjacent angle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480966899,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48096807,"data":"       | Specifically, BD = BF = r * tan(B/2). CD = CE = r * tan(C/2). And AF = AE = r * tan(A/2). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480969411,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480970533,"data":"       | Thus we have x = r tan(B/2), y = r tan(C/2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480971746,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480972902,"data":"       | Thus we have relationship between B and C given x and y (and r). Since r is unknown, we can express tan(B/2) = x/r, tan(C/2) = y/r.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480974332,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480975487,"data":"       | Thus we can compute B and C in terms of x,y,r: B = 2 arctan(x/r), C = 2 arctan(y/r). Then A = π - B - C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480976893,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480978039,"data":"       | Now we need to incorporate condition that quadrilateral KNK'B is cyclic.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480979356,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480980522,"data":"       | We need to find K: second intersection of circles (AFE) and circumcircle Ω of ABC. Since A, F, E are points on incircle tangency lines. Circle (AFE) passes through A, F, E. It intersects circumcircle Ω again at K.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480983266,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48098444,"data":"       | We also have K' reflection of K across line EF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480985716,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480986882,"data":"       | We need to consider condition that K, N, K', B are concyclic.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480988174,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480989314,"data":"       | Thus we need to find condition on triangle (i.e., on angles) that ensures this cyclic quadrilateral.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480990689,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480991853,"data":"       | Goal: find expression for ratio R = (CT * NB) / (BT * NE). We need to compute CT, NB, BT, NE lengths in terms of geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480993237,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48099438,"data":"       | Given the complexity, perhaps there is known property: For any triangle, the circle through B, K, N, K' being cyclic might be equivalent to some angle condition like B = 60°, etc. Let's explore.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480995985,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48099715,"data":"       | First, we need to compute N: foot of perpendicular from D onto EF. D is incircle tangency on BC, E on CA, F on AB. So EF is chord of incircle? Actually E and F are points of tangency on CA and AB, respectively. So EF is a line segment connecting two points on incircle (both on the incircle). Since incircle is a circle of radius r centered at O, points E and F lie on the incircle. So EF is a chord of incircle. D is another point on incircle. N is foot of perpendicular from D onto line EF. So N is the projection of D onto chord EF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.480999315,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481000815,"data":"       | Thus N is the foot of perpendicular from a point on a circle to a chord; this is related to geometry of circle: The line from center O to chord EF is perpendicular to EF at its midpoint. So O is the midpoint of the arc? Actually O to chord EF is perpendicular at midpoint of chord. So O is not necessarily foot from D onto EF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481002614,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481003765,"data":"       | Thus N is foot from D onto EF. So N lies on EF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481005031,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481006165,"data":"       | Thus NB is distance from B to point N on EF. B is intersection of tangents at D and F. So NB is some length.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481007539,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481008676,"data":"       | Similarly, NE is distance from N to E (since N lies on EF). So NE = length along EF from N to E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481010024,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481011144,"data":"       | CT: C is intersection of tangents at D and E. So CT is distance from C to T, where T is second intersection of BC with the circle through B and K tangent to BN at B. So CT is segment on BC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481012649,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481013759,"data":"       | BT: distance from B to T along BC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481014963,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481016106,"data":"       | Thus ratio involves lengths along BC (BT, CT) and lengths from N to B and N to E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481018481,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481019661,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute geometry of circle tangent to BN at B passing through K. That circle intersects BC again at T.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481021046,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481022197,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute T location along BC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481023445,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481024624,"data":"       | Thus overall we need to derive ratio R in terms of triangle parameters.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481025939,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481027111,"data":"       | Given the complexity, perhaps there is a known result: The maximum of (CT * NB) / (BT * NE) occurs when triangle is right-angled or something, and the value tends to constant p + sqrt(q). Might be something like (1 + sqrt(5))/2? But that's phi, not of form p + sqrt(q) with rational p,q. Could be something like 2 + sqrt(3) or 3 + sqrt(5), etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481028871,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481029999,"data":"       | We need to compute α = limsup_{n→∞} a_{2n}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481031313,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481032467,"data":"       | Given that x,y are large, ratio y/x tends to phi. So maybe as n large, the triangle shape tends to a limiting shape determined by ratio of tangency lengths (i.e., B and C angles). Since x = r tan(B/2), y = r tan(C/2). So tan(C/2)/tan(B/2) = y/x ≈ phi. So as n large, ratio of tan half-angles tends to phi.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481034251,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481035631,"data":"       | Thus as n→∞, we can treat ratio of tan half-angles as fixed constant phi, and r can be scaled arbitrarily (since scaling triangle uniformly scales all lengths, but ratio R might be independent of scale). Indeed, ratio R uses lengths along BC (CT, BT) and NB, NE (which are linear in scale). Since both numerator and denominator are products of lengths, scaling factor cancels? Let's check: Suppose we scale the whole triangle (including incircle) by factor λ. Then all lengths multiply by λ. Then CT, NB, BT, NE each scale by λ. So numerator scales by λ^2, denominator also λ^2, ratio unchanged. So ratio is scale-invariant. So we can set r = 1 for convenience (or any convenient value). Then x = tan(B/2), y = tan(C/2). So we have B and C determined by tan half-angle ratio.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481038328,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481039482,"data":"       | Thus the shape of triangle is determined by B and C (or equivalently, B and C angles). So we need to find condition for cyclic quadrilateral KNK'B, which will impose a relation between B and C (maybe a specific angle condition). Then we need to compute ratio R as function of B, C (or maybe just B, since C = π - A - B, but A expressed via B and C). Then we need to maximize R over possible B, C satisfying cyclic condition and maybe other constraints (like AB ≠ AC, which means B ≠ C). So we need to find maximum of R for given ratio y/x = tan(C/2)/tan(B/2) = F_{n+1}/F_n ≈ φ. As n large, this ratio tends to φ, but we also have condition that B ≠ C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481041991,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481043135,"data":"       | Thus as n large, we can treat ratio tan(C/2)/tan(B/2) = φ exactly? Not exactly, but tends to φ. So the shape of triangle tends to a limiting shape determined by ratio φ. So a_{2n} may approach some limit as n→∞, maybe the maximum ratio R for that limiting shape.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481046058,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481047549,"data":"       | Thus α is the supremum limit of a_{2n}. So we need to compute maximum possible ratio R for triangles where tan(C/2)/tan(B/2) = φ (or close). But perhaps the maximum is attained as n→∞, i.e., as ratio tends to φ exactly. So α = sup_{B,C\u003e0, B+C\u003cπ, B≠C, tan(C/2)/tan(B/2) = φ, and quadrilateral cyclic condition holds} R. But we also can vary r scaling? Not needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481049694,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481050846,"data":"       | But maybe the cyclic condition automatically forces a specific ratio of angles independent of B and C? Let's explore.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481052236,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481053419,"data":"       | We need to analyze condition that quadrilateral KNK'B is cyclic.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481055371,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481056522,"data":"       | Given the geometry, perhaps K is the Miquel point of triangle formed by lines? Or some known property: For triangle ABC with incircle, the circumcircle of AFE is the A-mixtilinear incircle? Wait there is concept of A-mixtilinear incircle: The circle tangent to circumcircle Ω and sides AB and AC internally. But here we have circle through A, F, E (touch points) which is not the mixtilinear incircle but the incircle of triangle AFE? Actually incircle of triangle AFE is the incircle ω itself (since F and E are points on ω). But the circumcircle of AFE passes through A and two points on ω (F and E). That circle is known as the A-excircle of the contact triangle? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481058995,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481060155,"data":"       | Let's examine: Points A, E, F: A is a vertex of triangle ABC, while E and F are points of tangency of incircle with CA and AB. So triangle AEF is formed by vertex A and two points of tangency on sides adjacent to A. Its circumcircle passes through A and those two points on incircle. This circle intersects the circumcircle of ABC again at K (other than A). So K is the second intersection of the circumcircle of triangle AEF with the circumcircle of ABC. This is reminiscent of the \"Miquel point\" of the quadrilateral formed by lines AB, AC, tangent lines at E and F? Actually the quadrilateral formed by lines AB, AC, EF, and BC? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481062559,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481064118,"data":"       | But there is known property: The intersection of the circumcircle of triangle AEF and circumcircle of ABC is the \"touchpoint of the A-mixtilinear incircle\"? Let's recall: The A-mixtilinear incircle is tangent to the circumcircle at a point T_A (often called the mixtilinear touchpoint) and tangent to sides AB and AC. The circle tangent to AB, AC, and Ω (circumcircle) is the A-mixtilinear incircle. Its tangency points with AB and AC are not the incircle tangency points E and F. So not that.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.4810663,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481067439,"data":"       | But there is also the \"A-excircle\" of the contact triangle? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481068823,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481069942,"data":"       | Nevertheless, we can compute K using coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481071179,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481072306,"data":"       | Given incircle centered at O (0,0) radius r = 1 (for convenience). Points D, E, F on circle at angles: Let’s set D at angle 0 (i.e., D = (1,0)). Then we can param B, C, A accordingly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481074848,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481076055,"data":"       | But we need to maintain general angles B and C. Let's define coordinates: Let incircle radius r = 1. Let D = (1,0). Let E = (cos θ_E, sin θ_E), F = (cos θ_F, sin θ_F). The angles measured from positive x-axis. The central angles between D and E is γ = angle DOE = 180° - C (in degrees) or π - C rad. Similarly, between D and F is β = π - B. So we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481078048,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481079209,"data":"       | - Angle from D to E measured counterclockwise (maybe) is γ = π - C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48108067,"data":"       | - Angle from D to F measured clockwise? Actually we need orientation. Let's choose orientation such that points around circle are D, E, F in order (counterclockwise). For triangle ABC, vertices are B at intersection of tangents at D and F, C at intersection of tangents at D and E, A at intersection of tangents at E and F. The incircle is inside triangle. The tangents at D and E intersect at C, which lies opposite side BC. So D is on side BC, E on CA, F on AB. So moving around triangle, we go from side BC (point D) to side CA (point E) to side AB (point F) back to BC (point D). So order around incircle is D -\u003e E -\u003e F -\u003e D (counterclockwise). So central angles: from D to E is angle γ = π - C (since angle at C is formed by tangents at D and E). Similarly, from E to F is angle α = π - A. From F to D is angle β = π - B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481083598,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481085031,"data":"       | Thus we can set D at angle 0. Then moving counterclockwise, E is at angle γ = π - C (i.e., angle measured from D). Then F is at angle γ + α = (π - C) + (π - A) = 2π - (A + C) = 2π - (π - B) = π + B. Wait compute: A + B + C = π. So γ + α = (π - C) + (π - A) = 2π - (A + C) = 2π - (π - B) = π + B. So F is at angle π + B (i.e., measured from D). Since B is less than π, π + B \u003e π, which is consistent: F lies after passing half circle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481087439,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481088576,"data":"       | Thus coordinates:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481089715,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481090881,"data":"       | - D: angle 0 =\u003e D = (1, 0).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481092185,"data":"       | - E: angle γ = π - C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481093501,"data":"       | - F: angle π + B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481094696,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481095857,"data":"       | Check that the angle from F back to D (counterclockwise) is β = π - B, as needed: angle from F (π + B) to D (2π) is 2π - (π + B) = π - B = β. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481097439,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481098601,"data":"       | Thus this parameterization works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481099782,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481100931,"data":"       | Now we have incircle radius r = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481102136,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481103325,"data":"       | Now we can compute points B, C, A as intersection of tangents at appropriate points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481104642,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481105814,"data":"       | Given a point on unit circle, tangent line at that point has equation x cos θ + y sin θ = 1 (since for unit circle x^2 + y^2 = 1, the tangent at (cosθ, sinθ) is x cosθ + y sinθ = 1). Indeed, because dot product of (x,y) with (cosθ, sinθ) = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481108582,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481109711,"data":"       | Thus:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481110855,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481112026,"data":"       | - Tangent at D (θ=0) is x*1 + y*0 = 1 =\u003e x = 1. So line BC (tangent at D) is vertical line x = 1. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481113472,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481114719,"data":"       | - Tangent at E (θ = γ = π - C) =\u003e cosγ = cos(π - C) = -cos C, sinγ = sin(π - C) = sin C. So line CA: x cosγ + y sinγ = 1 =\u003e -x cos C + y sin C = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481116377,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481117529,"data":"       | - Tangent at F (θ = π + B) =\u003e cos(π + B) = -cos B, sin(π + B) = -sin B. So line AB: -x cos B - y sin B = 1 =\u003e x cos B + y sin B = -1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481119071,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481120228,"data":"       | But careful: The tangent line equation is x cosθ + y sinθ = 1. So plugging θ = π + B gives x cos(π + B) + y sin(π + B) = 1 =\u003e x (-cos B) + y (-sin B) = 1 =\u003e -x cos B - y sin B = 1 =\u003e x cos B + y sin B = -1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481122003,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481123139,"data":"       | Thus line AB is x cos B + y sin B = -1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481124341,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481125528,"data":"       | Now we can find intersection points:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481126735,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481127908,"data":"       | - B = intersection of tangents at D (x=1) and F (x cos B + y sin B = -1). So set x = 1, then 1 * cos B + y sin B = -1 =\u003e y sin B = -1 - cos B =\u003e y = (-1 - cos B) / sin B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481129713,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481130866,"data":"       | Thus B = (1, y_B) where y_B = (-1 - cos B)/sin B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481132093,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481133244,"data":"       | - C = intersection of tangents at D (x=1) and E (-x cos C + y sin C = 1). So x=1 =\u003e -cos C + y sin C = 1 =\u003e y sin C = 1 + cos C =\u003e y = (1 + cos C)/sin C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481134771,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481135906,"data":"       | Thus C = (1, y_C) where y_C = (1 + cos C)/sin C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481137119,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481138285,"data":"       | - A = intersection of tangents at E and F. Solve system:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481139543,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481140702,"data":"       | Line E: -x cos C + y sin C = 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481141953,"data":"       | Line F: x cos B + y sin B = -1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481143134,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481144313,"data":"       | We can solve for A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481145528,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481146692,"data":"       | Now we have coordinates for D (1,0), E (cosγ, sinγ) = (cos(π - C), sin(π - C)) = (-cos C, sin C), and F (cos(π + B), sin(π + B)) = (-cos B, -sin B). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481149222,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481150397,"data":"       | Now we can compute geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481151568,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48115274,"data":"       | Now we need to compute K: second intersection of circumcircle of triangle AEF with circumcircle Ω of ABC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481154152,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481155433,"data":"       | Circumcircle Ω of ABC: we can compute its equation. Since we have coordinates for A, B, C, we can compute circumcenter and radius.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481156928,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481158084,"data":"       | But maybe there is known relationship: Since points A, E, F lie on incircle? Actually E and F are on incircle, but A is not. So circle (A,E,F) passes through A and two points on incircle. Intersection with circumcircle of ABC (which passes through A, B, C) is at A and K.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481159719,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481160882,"data":"       | Thus K is the Miquel point of quadrilateral formed by lines AB, AC, EF, and BC? Actually quadrilateral formed by lines AB, AC, EF, and BC has vertices A (intersection of AB and AC), B (intersection of AB and BC), C (intersection of AC and BC), and the intersection of EF with something? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481162546,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481163697,"data":"       | But we can compute K via solving intersection of circles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481164979,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481166146,"data":"       | Given coordinates, we can compute K as intersection of two circles (excluding A). Then we can compute K', reflection across EF (line through E and F). Then we need condition that quadrilateral K N K' B is cyclic. That is, points K, N, K', B lie on a circle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481167773,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481169171,"data":"       | We need to find condition on B, C such that this holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481170453,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481171634,"data":"       | We can try to compute this condition symbolically using sympy, perhaps solving for B and C (or maybe just for ratio tan(C/2)/tan(B/2) = φ). Then we can compute ratio R and maximize.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481173177,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481174335,"data":"       | Given the complexity, we can attempt to compute using symbolic algebra with unknown B, C (or maybe using variable t = tan(B/2) etc.) and impose condition that quadrilateral K,N,K',B is cyclic.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481175885,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481177062,"data":"       | But this condition may be equivalent to some simple angle condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481178368,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481179513,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can attempt to compute using numeric approach: for given B and C (with B != C), compute all points, compute condition for cyclic quadrilateral: points K, N, K', B are concyclic if and only if oriented angles satisfy something like ∠KBN = ∠K'BN or ∠KBN = ∠K'KN? Actually cyclic condition: quadrilateral K N K' B is cyclic iff ∠KBN = ∠K'KN (or any pair). Since B,N,K,K' are distinct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481182593,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481183739,"data":"       | Simpler: For points P,Q,R,S, they are concyclic iff cross ratio (P,Q;R,S) is real? Not needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481185088,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481186244,"data":"       | We can compute using condition that oriented angle between lines (K B) and (K N) equals angle between (K' B) and (K' N). Or equivalently, the directed angle ∠KBN = ∠K'BN? Wait B is common vertex for KBN and K'BN? Actually quadrilateral K N K' B: vertices maybe in order K-B-K'-N? Not sure. But cyclic condition is independent of order.\n"}
