,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481550216,"data":"       | Thus A = ( -cos((B-C)/2) / cos((B+C)/2), - sin((B-C)/2) / cos((B+C)/2) ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481551521,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481552916,"data":"       | So A = - (cos((B-C)/2), sin((B-C)/2) ) / cos((B+C)/2) ??? Actually A_y negative sign: - sin((B-C)/2) / cos((B+C)/2). So A = - ( cos((B-C)/2), sin((B-C)/2) ) / cos((B+C)/2). So A = -(1 / cos((B+C)/2)) * (cos((B-C)/2), sin((B-C)/2)). So A is a point on a line through origin? Indeed it's a scaled version of a unit vector at angle (B-C)/2, but scaled by -1/cos((B+C)/2). So A lies on line through origin opposite direction of angle (B-C)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481554884,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481556035,"data":"       | Thus A coordinates are:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481557242,"data":"       | \n"}
