,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481572565,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481573776,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can set A = ( -cot(A/2) * cos((B-C)/2), -cot(A/2) * sin((B-C)/2) ). Since 1 / sin(A/2) = csc(A/2). But we can keep as above.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481575225,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481576511,"data":"       | Now we have coordinates for all points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481577712,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481578856,"data":"       | Now we need to compute circumcircle of ABC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481580053,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481581244,"data":"       | Given points A, B, C, we can compute circumcenter O_ABC. However there may be a known relationship: For triangle with incircle radius r=1, the circumcenter O_ABC is at some coordinates. But we can compute using sympy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481582795,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481583979,"data":"       | But maybe we can avoid computing O_ABC; we just need intersection K of circles (A,E,F) and (A,B,C). We can compute K as second intersection of circles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481585419,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481586631,"data":"       | We can compute circle (A,E,F) easily: It's the circumcircle of triangle AEF. Since E and F are points on unit circle (incircle). So we can compute its center.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481588066,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481589222,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can use known property: The circumcircle of AEF is the A-mixtilinear incircle? Actually the circle through A and the two tangency points E and F is known as the A-Excentral circle? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481590782,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481592116,"data":"       | But we can compute directly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481593299,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481594456,"data":"       | Let's compute using sympy for generic B (with C defined via φ). However symbolic solving for K may be heavy. But we can compute numerically for many B values to see pattern.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481595953,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481597128,"data":"       | We'll treat B as numeric variable, compute C accordingly, then compute all points numeric, compute K as intersection of circles, compute K', N, T, ratio R, and check cyclic condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48159863,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481599785,"data":"       | First, we need to compute K: intersection of circles (A,E,F) and (A,B,C). Let's implement functions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481601144,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481602297,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codeimport sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481603555,"data":"       | def compute_geometry(B_val):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481604784,"data":"       |     # B_val numeric (float)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48160597,"data":"       |     phi = (1+sp.sqrt(5))/2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481607228,"data":"       |     B = sp.N(B_val)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481608394,"data":"       |     # compute C from tan(C/2) = phi * tan(B/2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481609606,"data":"       |     C = 2*sp.atan(phi*sp.tan(B/2))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481610824,"data":"       |     # define points\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481613368,"data":"       |     D = sp.Point(1, 0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481614571,"data":"       |     E = sp.Point(-sp.cos(C), sp.sin(C))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481615761,"data":"       |     F = sp.Point(-sp.cos(B), -sp.sin(B))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481616949,"data":"       |     yB = (-1 - sp.cos(B))/sp.sin(B)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481618156,"data":"       |     yC = (1 + sp.cos(C))/sp.sin(C)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481619367,"data":"       |     Bpt = sp.Point(1, yB)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481620553,"data":"       |     Cpt = sp.Point(1, yC)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481621728,"data":"       |     # compute A using formula derived\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481622999,"data":"       |     # A = - (cos((B-C)/2), sin((B-C)/2)) / sp.sin((sp.pi - (B+C))/2) ??? Actually sin(A/2) = sin((sp.pi - (B+C))/2) = sin((sp.pi - (B+C))/2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481624433,"data":"       |     # But we can compute directly using intersection of lines\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481625685,"data":"       |     # Use derived formula:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481626884,"data":"       |     denom = sp.sin(B + C)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481628095,"data":"       |     Ax = -(sp.sin(B) + sp.sin(C)) / denom\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48162937,"data":"       |     Ay = (sp.cos(B) - sp.cos(C)) / denom\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.481630582,"data":"       |     Apt = sp.Point(Ax, Ay)\n"}
