,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.482562477,"data":"       | But note B cannot be too small because tan(B/2) positive; B\u003e0. Also B cannot be too close to π because then tan(B/2) large. But we have C defined via φ tan(B/2). Need to ensure C \u003c π (since angle). Since tan(C/2) = φ tan(B/2). For B small, tan(B/2) small, then C small as well; B + C \u003c π (since A positive). So B can be up to maybe near π/2? Let's check.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.482564511,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.482565692,"data":"       | We need B + C \u003c π (since A = π - (B + C) \u003e 0). Let's compute for B near π: tan(B/2) large, tan(C/2) = φ tan(B/2) even larger, C may exceed π? Actually C = 2*atan(φ tan(B/2)). Since tan(B/2) can be arbitrarily large, φ tan(B/2) can be arbitrarily large, atan of large number approaches π/2, so C approaches π. So B + C can exceed π. So we need to restrict B such that B + C \u003c π. Let's find domain.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.482567895,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.482569031,"data":"       | Define function sum_angle = B + C. We'll need sum \u003c π.\n"}
