,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486949586,"data":"       | Points E,F lie on ω. Circle (AEF) passes through A and two points on ω. This is known as the \"A-mixtilinear incircle\"? Wait the A-mixtilinear incircle is tangent to circumcircle and sides AB, AC. Its tangency points with AB and AC are not E and F. However, the circle through A,E,F is known to be the \"A-excircle of the contact triangle\"? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486951803,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486953227,"data":"       | Nevertheless, we can consider the Miquel point of quadrilateral formed by lines AB, AC, and the tangents at D? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48695463,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486955798,"data":"       | Maybe we can use property: The circle (AEF) is the \"A-Apollonius circle\" of triangle ABC? Actually Apollonius circle of angle A is the locus of points X such that XB/XC = AB/AC. That circle passes through the midpoint of BC and the foot of internal angle bisector? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486957539,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486958741,"data":"       | But the circle through A, E, F is known to be the circle with diameter AI, where I is incenter? Let's test: Does I (incenter) lie on circle AEF? Since I is center of incircle, and E,F are on incircle, so IE = IF = r. So triangle IEF is isosceles with IE = IF = r. Does AI =? Not necessarily equal to IE. So I not on circle AEF generally.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486960521,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486961688,"data":"       | But maybe K is the exsimilicenter of incircle and circumcircle? Actually the exsimilicenter (external homothety center) lies on line OI and is the intersection of lines connecting corresponding points of tangency of incircle and circumcircle. For triangle, the exsimilicenter is known as the \"Gergonne point\"? Not.\n"}
