,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486979073,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486980227,"data":"       | Given constraints, perhaps we can approximate α numerically by solving for B that satisfies cyclic condition for ratio φ (or for large n). Since we have difficulty computing ratio due to degenerate geometry, but we can compute ratio directly using formula for ratio in terms of B and C perhaps derived from lengths.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486982018,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48698318,"data":"       | Let's attempt to compute ratio R using only B and C (angles) without needing T.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486984491,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486985656,"data":"       | We need CT, BT, NB, NE.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486986845,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486988003,"data":"       | - NB: distance from B to foot N on EF. Since N is foot from D onto EF, we can compute NB using geometry of incircle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486989365,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486990515,"data":"       | - NE: distance from N to E along chord EF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486991796,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486992932,"data":"       | - BT, CT: distances along BC from B and C to T, where T is intersection of BC with circle tangent to BN at B passing through K.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486994324,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48699547,"data":"       | But perhaps we can use power of point B with respect to circle (BKT). Since BN is tangent, NB^2 = power of N w.r.t. circle (BKT). Also, for point T on BC, we have BT * CT = (some expression). Actually for a circle intersecting line BC at B and T, the power of a point D on BC is DB * DT (if D lies on line). For point D (tangency point of incircle with BC), we have BD * CD? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486997327,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48699847,"data":"       | But we can compute BT * CT = (distance from B to T)*(distance from C to T) = (BT)*(BC - BT) = BT*(a - BT) where a = BC = BD + CD = x + y.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.486999903,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487001045,"data":"       | Thus ratio R = (CT * NB) / (BT * NE) = ((a - BT) * NB) / (BT * NE). So R = NB/NE * (a/BT - 1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487002422,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487003585,"data":"       | Thus if we can find BT in terms of NB, NE, and maybe other known lengths, we can compute R.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487004918,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487006371,"data":"       | But we also have relation NB^2 = power of N w.r.t. circle (BKT). Since the circle passes through B and K and is tangent to BN at B, the power of N is NB^2. Also N lies on line BC? No, N is on EF, not BC. So power of N with respect to circle is NB^2 = (distance from N to intersection points of line through N intersecting circle). The line through N and K? Actually K is on circle, so line NK intersects circle at K and another point (maybe K'?). Since K' is also on circle (by cyclic condition), line NK passes through K and K'? Wait K', N, K are points on the circle (since quadrilateral cyclic). So line NK intersects circle at K and K' (two points). So power of N = NK * NK'. So NB^2 = NK * NK'.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487010347,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487011534,"data":"       | Thus NK * NK' = NB^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487012721,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487013878,"data":"       | Similarly, power of N with respect to circumcircle Ω (ABC) maybe also expressed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487015213,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48701639,"data":"       | But perhaps we can use similar triangles to relate NB/NE.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487017669,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487018825,"data":"       | We can compute NB and NE in terms of B and C using incircle geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487020155,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487021314,"data":"       | Let's compute NB and NE analytically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487022538,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487023698,"data":"       | Coordinates: D = (r,0) (with r=1). E = (-cos C, sin C), F = (-cos B, -sin B). N is foot of perpendicular from D onto line EF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487025096,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487026287,"data":"       | We can compute vector EF and find projection of D onto EF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487027572,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487028731,"data":"       | Let’s compute using sympy symbolic in terms of B, C (or t). Then compute NB^2 = |B - N|^2, NE^2 = |N - E|^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487030107,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487031236,"data":"       | We can then compute ratio NB/NE.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487032458,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487033636,"data":"       | Similarly, we need BT. To find BT, we need circle tangent to BN at B passing through K. This circle is the circle with center O_T on line perpendicular to BN through B, and radius OB = distance O_T B = distance O_T K. So O_T is intersection of perpendicular to BN at B and perpendicular bisector of BK. So we can compute BT as distance from B to intersection of that circle with line BC (x=1). This can be expressed algebraically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487035581,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487036721,"data":"       | Thus we can compute R symbolically as rational function of t (tan(B/2)). Then impose cyclic condition (K,N,K',B cyclic) which yields equation maybe simplifies to something like t satisfies polynomial. Then compute R for that t, simplify to p + sqrt(q). Then we can compute p and q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487038413,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487039606,"data":"       | Thus we need to implement symbolic calculations using sympy rationally, but we need to manage complexity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487040969,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487042335,"data":"       | We can try to compute using sympy with rational sqrt(5) and treat t as a symbol. However solving for K (intersection of circles) symbolically may be heavy but perhaps manageable if we use algebraic elimination.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487043926,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487045077,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can use geometry to express K more simply: Since K is the second intersection of circles (A,E,F) and (A,B,C), perhaps K is the Miquel point of quadrilateral formed by lines AB, AC, and the tangents at D? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487048451,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487049609,"data":"       | But maybe K lies on the circumcircle of triangle formed by the excenters? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487050932,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487052109,"data":"       | Given time constraints, maybe we can attempt to compute symbolic expressions for K using coordinates and solve for intersection of circles analytically using algebraic elimination. The equations are quadratic, solving yields rational expressions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487053725,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487054905,"data":"       | We can set up equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487056143,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487057305,"data":"       | - (x - O1x)^2 + (y - O1y)^2 = r1^2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487058521,"data":"       | - (x - O2x)^2 + (y - O2y)^2 = r2^2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487059743,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487060909,"data":"       | Subtract to get linear equation: 2*(O2x - O1x)*x + 2*(O2y - O1y)*y = (O2x^2 + O2y^2 - r2^2) - (O1x^2 + O1y^2 - r1^2). This is line equation for radical axis, which passes through intersection points A and K. Since we know A coordinates, we can solve for K as second intersection of line (radical axis) with one of the circles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487062676,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487063905,"data":"       | Thus we can find K coordinates by solving linear equation (radical axis) and circle equation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48706526,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487066411,"data":"       | Thus we can compute K symbolically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487067653,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487068794,"data":"       | Let's attempt to implement symbolic steps with sympy using t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487070082,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487071217,"data":"       | Given the complexity, we can try to compute using sympy's rational simplification but with sqrt(5) as symbolic.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487072598,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487073843,"data":"       | We need to define t as a symbol, then define B = 2*atan(t). However using atan leads to transcendental functions, not algebraic. However we can use rational expressions for sinB, cosB in terms of t as we already have. So we can treat sinB, cosB as rational functions of t. Similarly for C with u = φ t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487075598,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487076737,"data":"       | Thus we can define sinB, cosB, sinC, cosC rationally.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487078394,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487079537,"data":"       | Now we can compute coordinates of points expressed rationally.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487080827,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487081976,"data":"       | Then compute O1 (center of circle AEF) and O2 (center of ABC) using circumcenter formulas (which involve rational functions of coordinates). Since coordinates are rational functions of t and sqrt(5), O1 and O2 will be rational functions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487083605,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487084823,"data":"       | Then compute radical axis line: L: (O2 - O1)·(X) = something.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487087942,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487089153,"data":"       | Then find K as intersection of line L with circle (A, O1, r1). Solve linear equation for x,y, substitute into circle equation, solve quadratic; one root corresponds to A (we know coordinates), the other is K.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487090675,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487091858,"data":"       | Thus we can get K coordinates rationally.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48709309,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487094258,"data":"       | Then compute K' as reflection across line EF (which can be expressed using vector formulas). Since EF line equation known, reflection formula can be expressed rationally.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487095715,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48709694,"data":"       | Then compute N as foot from D onto EF (also rational). Then compute cyclic condition: K,N,K',B concyclic. This condition can be expressed as determinant zero, which yields polynomial equation in t (maybe of low degree). Solve for t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487098483,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48709965,"data":"       | Then compute ratio R using NB, NE, BT, CT. BT and CT require T intersection with line x=1 (BC) of circle through B and K tangent to BN at B. However we can compute T using geometry: Since circle passes through B and K and tangent at B to BN, we can find its center O_T as intersection of perpendicular to BN at B and perpendicular bisector of BK (as before). This can be expressed algebraically using coordinates of B, K, N. Then compute intersection of that circle with line x=1, solving for y coordinate of T (the other intersection). Then compute BT and CT lengths (distance formula). All rational functions in t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487101911,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48710308,"data":"       | Thus we can compute R as rational expression in t (with sqrt(5) present). Then using the polynomial equation for t from cyclic condition, we can eliminate t to get R expressed as algebraic number (maybe p + sqrt(q)). Then evaluate.\n"}
