,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487325832,"data":"       | The circle tangent to line BN and passing through B and K intersects BC again at T ≠ B. So there is a circle through B and K tangent to BN at B? Wait: \"circle tangent to line BN and passing through B and K\" - ambiguous: a circle tangent to line BN (i.e., line BN is tangent to the circle) and also passes through B and K. So the circle is tangent to BN at some point? Usually \"tangent to line BN\" means the line BN is tangent to the circle at the point of tangency, which could be B (since B lies on BN). Indeed B lies on line BN, so the circle passing through B and tangent to BN at B would be a circle with B as a point of tangency, i.e., BN is tangent at B. But the problem says \"circle tangent to line BN and passing through B and K\". If the circle passes through B, and is tangent to BN, then B must be the point of tangency (since BN passes through B). So the circle is tangent to BN at B, passes through B and K. That seems plausible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487330569,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487331754,"data":"       | Thus we have a circle through B and K, tangent to BN at B. This circle intersects BC again at T ≠ B.\n"}
