,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487355643,"data":"       | The constraints BD = F_n, CD = F_{n+1} are lengths along side BC: D is incircle touchpoint, so BD and DC are segments on BC. So BC = BD + DC = F_n + F_{n+1} = F_{n+2} (since Fibonacci recurrence). So BC = F_{n+2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487357165,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487358317,"data":"       | Thus BC length is determined by n: BC = F_{n+2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487359552,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487360727,"data":"       | Given triangle with incircle touchpoint D dividing BC into those lengths. The incircle radius r is not given. But incircle tangency distances from B and C to D are BD, DC. So the semiperimeter s = (AB + AC + BC)/2 = (AB + AC + F_{n+2})/2. Also, BD = s - B? Actually standard incircle property: BD = s - b where b = AC (side opposite B), and CD = s - c where c = AB (side opposite C). Wait: In triangle ABC, sides: a = BC, b = CA, c = AB. Then incircle touchpoint D on BC yields BD = s - b, CD = s - c. So we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487364518,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487365647,"data":"       | s - b = F_n,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.4873668,"data":"       | s - c = F_{n+1}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48736796,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487369082,"data":"       | Thus b = s - F_n,\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487370265,"data":"       | c = s - F_{n+1}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487371438,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487372583,"data":"       | Also a = BC = F_{n+2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487373799,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487374946,"data":"       | We can express s in terms of a, b, c: s = (a + b + c)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487376189,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487377331,"data":"       | Plug b and c: b = s - F_n, c = s - F_{n+1}. Then:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487378555,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487379714,"data":"       | a + b + c = a + (s - F_n) + (s - F_{n+1}) = a + 2s - (F_n + F_{n+1}).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48738097,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487382103,"data":"       | But s = (a + b + c)/2, so:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487383312,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487384799,"data":"       | 2s = a + 2s - (F_n + F_{n+1}) =\u003e cancel 2s both sides? Wait: This leads to 0 = a - (F_n + F_{n+1}) = a - F_{n+2}. Since a = F_{n+2}, this holds identically. So consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48738631,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487387469,"data":"       | Thus any triangle with side BC = F_{n+2} and incircle touchpoint D dividing BC into F_n and F_{n+1} can be realized for some b, c satisfying b = s - F_n, c = s - F_{n+1} with s = (a + b + c)/2. This yields relationship between b and c: b - c = (s - F_n) - (s - F_{n+1}) = F_{n+1} - F_n = F_{n-1}? Actually F_{n+1} - F_n = F_{n-1}? Let's check: Fibonacci: F_{n+1} = F_n + F_{n-1}, so F_{n+1} - F_n = F_{n-1}. Yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487389361,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487390494,"data":"       | Thus b - c = F_{n-1}. So AB ≠ AC, consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487391777,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487392954,"data":"       | Thus we have side lengths expressed in terms of s. But we can solve for s: from b = s - F_n, c = s - F_{n+1}, a = F_{n+2}. Then s = (a + b + c)/2 = (F_{n+2} + (s - F_n) + (s - F_{n+1}))/2 = (F_{n+2} + 2s - (F_n + F_{n+1}))/2 = (F_{n+2} + 2s - F_{n+2})/2 = (2s)/2 = s. So identity; s remains free variable? Wait we have no equation to determine s; it's consistent for any s? That seems odd: side lengths b, c depend on s, but s also depends on b, c. The equations are identities, meaning that for any s, we can define b = s - F_n, c = s - F_{n+1}, and then s = (a + b + c)/2 holds automatically because a = F_{n+2} = F_n + F_{n+1}. Let's verify: (a + b + c)/2 = (F_{n+2} + s - F_n + s - F_{n+1})/2 = (F_{n+2} + 2s - (F_n + F_{n+1}))/2 = (F_{n+2} + 2s - F_{n+2})/2 = s. So identity holds for any s. So s is free parameter; we can vary triangle shape while preserving BD and CD lengths.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487395698,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48739684,"data":"       | Thus we have a family of triangles with same BC and incircle contact distances but varying b and c (i.e., varying AB and AC) as long as they satisfy b - c = F_{n-1} and both \u003e0. So s must be \u003e max(F_n, F_{n+1}) to keep b, c positive. So s \u003e F_{n+1}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48740048,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487401648,"data":"       | Thus there is a continuum of triangles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487402845,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487404021,"data":"       | Now additional condition: quadrilateral KNK'B is cyclic. This imposes a constraint linking geometry of incircle, circumcircle, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487405386,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487406555,"data":"       | Goal: maximize ratio CT * NB / (BT * NE) over triangles satisfying that cyclic condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487407903,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487409312,"data":"       | Thus we need to understand geometry of points K, K', N, B, T, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487410588,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487411774,"data":"       | Given the complexity, perhaps the ratio simplifies to something depending only on BD, CD, maybe independent of shape, and maximum occurs at some limit shape, maybe as triangle becomes degenerate (AB \u003e\u003e AC). The condition of cyclic quadrilateral may restrict shape to a specific family.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487413501,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487414652,"data":"       | We need to find α = supremum limit for even n large.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487415933,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487417082,"data":"       | Probably the ratio tends to a constant as n grows large, perhaps related to golden ratio φ = (1+√5)/2, because Fibonacci numbers appear. So α may be expressed as p + sqrt(q) where p, q rational.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487418662,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487419798,"data":"       | Given typical competition problems, α might be something like 3 + √5 or 2 + √3, etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487421192,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48742238,"data":"       | We need to compute p and q rational numbers, then compute floor(p^{q^p}) mod 99991. This suggests p and q are small rational numbers, maybe integers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487423815,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487424951,"data":"       | Let's attempt to derive geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487426148,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487427284,"data":"       | First, recall incircle contact points: D on BC, E on CA, F on AB. Points E, F are where incircle touches sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487428663,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487429787,"data":"       | Let’s define coordinates to model geometry. Use triangle with BC as base on x-axis: B = (0,0), C = (a,0) where a = BC = F_{n+2}. Let incircle have center I = (x0, r) where r is incircle radius, and D = (x0,0) because incircle tangent to BC at D (vertical radius). Indeed incircle touches BC at D directly below I.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487431565,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487432718,"data":"       | Given BD = x0 = F_n, CD = a - x0 = F_{n+1}. So x0 = F_n, a = F_{n+2}. So coordinates: B = (0,0), C = (F_{n+2},0), D = (F_n,0). I = (F_n, r).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487434131,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487436858,"data":"       | Now we need coordinates of A such that incircle touches AB at F and AC at E. Since incircle is tangent to sides AB and AC, lines AB and AC are tangent to the incircle at points F and E respectively.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48743844,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487439594,"data":"       | We can compute A coordinates using geometry: A is intersection of lines through B and C that are tangent to incircle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487440961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487442303,"data":"       | Given incircle centered at (F_n, r) radius r, lines from B = (0,0) tangent to incircle have distance from I equal to r. Similarly for C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487443709,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487444832,"data":"       | Thus AB and AC are tangents from B and C to incircle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487446096,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487447226,"data":"       | We can compute slopes of tangents from B to incircle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487448478,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487449624,"data":"       | Let line through B with slope m: equation y = m x. Distance from I (x0, r) to line y = m x is |m x0 - r| / sqrt(m^2 + 1). This must equal r (since tangent). So:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487451085,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487452217,"data":"       | |m x0 - r| = r sqrt(m^2 + 1). Since r\u003e0, we can square both sides:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487453536,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48745467,"data":"       | (m x0 - r)^2 = r^2 (m^2 + 1) =\u003e m^2 x0^2 - 2 m x0 r + r^2 = r^2 m^2 + r^2 =\u003e m^2 x0^2 - 2 m x0 r + r^2 = r^2 m^2 + r^2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487456134,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487457269,"data":"       | Cancel r^2 on both sides: m^2 x0^2 - 2 m x0 r = r^2 m^2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487458516,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487459648,"data":"       | =\u003e m^2 (x0^2 - r^2) - 2 m x0 r = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487460926,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48746207,"data":"       | =\u003e m [ m (x0^2 - r^2) - 2 x0 r ] = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487463334,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487464485,"data":"       | One solution m=0 (horizontal line) which is not tangent because incircle is above base; but B is at (0,0), incircle above; line y=0 touches incircle at D? Actually incircle touches BC at D, not at B. So m=0 line passes through B and D? D is at (x0,0) not B. So line y=0 passes through B and D, but incircle touches BC at D, not tangent at B. So not tangent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487466327,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487467445,"data":"       | Thus we need m ≠ 0, solve:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487468719,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487469863,"data":"       | m (x0^2 - r^2) - 2 x0 r = 0 =\u003e m = (2 x0 r) / (x0^2 - r^2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487471166,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487472327,"data":"       | Thus slope of tangent from B to incircle is m_B = 2 x0 r / (x0^2 - r^2). Similarly, slope from C = (F_{n+2},0) to incircle: line through C with slope m_C: equation y = m (x - a). Distance from I to this line: |m (x0 - a) - r| / sqrt(m^2 + 1) = r. Solve similarly: m = (2 (a - x0) r) / ((a - x0)^2 - r^2). Since a - x0 = CD = F_{n+1}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487475914,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487477069,"data":"       | Thus slopes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487478222,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487479358,"data":"       | m_B = 2 F_n r / (F_n^2 - r^2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487480582,"data":"       | m_C = 2 F_{n+1} r / (F_{n+1}^2 - r^2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487481815,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487483039,"data":"       | Now A is intersection of these two lines: line from B: y = m_B x, line from C: y = m_C (x - a). Solve for x coordinate of A:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487484409,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487485596,"data":"       | m_B x = m_C (x - a) =\u003e m_B x = m_C x - m_C a =\u003e (m_B - m_C) x = - m_C a =\u003e x = - m_C a / (m_B - m_C).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487487091,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487488437,"data":"       | Then y = m_B x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487489624,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487490785,"data":"       | Thus A coordinates expressed in terms of r and known lengths.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487492075,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48749394,"data":"       | Now we also have circumcircle Ω of triangle ABC. The circumcenter O is somewhere; but we might not need explicit coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487495348,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487496537,"data":"       | We need K: second intersection of circumcircle of AFE with Ω. Points A, F, E are on incircle tangency points? Actually F is tangency point on AB, E on AC. So points A, F, E define a triangle (AFE). Its circumcircle passes through A, F, E. Intersect with circumcircle of ABC again at K (other than A). So K is the Miquel point of quadrilateral formed by lines AB, AC, and incircle tangents? Might be known: The circumcircle of AFE is the A-excircle of triangle? Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487498567,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487499734,"data":"       | But there is known geometry: The circle through A, the touchpoints of incircle on AB and AC is called the A-mixtilinear incircle? Actually the mixtilinear incircle is tangent to circumcircle and to sides AB, AC. However, the circle through A, E, F is the A-excircle? Wait excenter touches AB, AC externally. The incircle touches AB at F, AC at E. So circle through A, E, F is not incircle; it's some circle passing through A and the two touchpoints. This circle is known as the A-Apollonius circle? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48750179,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487502971,"data":"       | Nevertheless, we need K, the second intersection with circumcircle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487504241,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487505413,"data":"       | Then K' is reflection of K across line EF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487506651,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487507783,"data":"       | Then N is foot of perpendicular from D to EF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48750899,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487510145,"data":"       | Then we have circle through B and K tangent to BN at B (i.e., B is point of tangency). This circle intersects BC again at T.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487511518,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487512677,"data":"       | Goal ratio: CT * NB / (BT * NE).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48751575,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487516978,"data":"       | Now we need to compute ratio in terms of geometry, perhaps simplifies to something like (CD * something) / (BD * something) etc.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487518386,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487519913,"data":"       | Given complexity, perhaps there is a known result: For any triangle, the circle through B and K tangent at B to BN intersects BC at T such that CT/BT = something like (CD/BD) * (some ratio of distances). And NB/NE maybe independent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487521492,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487522652,"data":"       | Alternatively, perhaps the ratio simplifies to (CD/BD) * (something) = (F_{n+1}/F_n) * constant, and maximum occurs as n large (so ratio tends to φ * constant). Then α = constant * φ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487524223,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487525386,"data":"       | But the problem asks for α as p + sqrt(q). φ = (1+√5)/2 = 0.5 + 0.5√5. So p = 0.5, sqrt(q) = 0.5√5 =\u003e q = 5/4? Actually sqrt(q) = 0.5√5 =\u003e q = (0.5√5)^2 = 5/4. So p = 1/2, q = 5/4. Then p + sqrt(q) = 0.5 + sqrt(5/4) = 0.5 + (√5)/2 = (1+√5)/2 = φ. So α could be φ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487527422,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487528624,"data":"       | But α is supposed to be p + sqrt(q) with p, q rational. φ indeed can be expressed as 1/2 + sqrt(5)/2 = 0.5 + sqrt(5)/2. That's p = 0.5, q = 5/4? Wait sqrt(q) = √(5/4) = √5 / 2. So p + √q = 0.5 + √5/2 = φ. So p = 1/2, q = 5/4. Both rational.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487530368,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487531544,"data":"       | Thus α could be φ. Then p = 1/2, q = 5/4. Then compute floor(p^{q^p}) = floor( (1/2)^{ (5/4)^{1/2} } ). Let's compute numeric: (5/4)^{1/2} = sqrt(1.25) ≈ 1.118... Then (1/2)^{1.118...} = 2^{-1.118...} ≈ 0.46? Actually 2^{-1.118} = e^{-1.118 ln 2} = e^{-0.774} ≈ 0.461. So floor = 0. Then remainder mod 99991 is 0. That seems trivial, unlikely.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487533463,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487534638,"data":"       | Thus maybe α is larger than 1, maybe p + sqrt(q) \u003e 1. If p=1/2, sqrt(q)=√5/2 ≈ 1.118, sum ≈ 1.618 \u003e1. So floor of p^{q^p} with p=1/2 yields less than 1, floor 0. That yields remainder 0. Could be answer 0? But seems too trivial for such a complex problem.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487536422,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487537576,"data":"       | But maybe p is \u003e1, e.g., p=2, sqrt(q)=something, leading to p^{q^p} huge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487538892,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48754006,"data":"       | Let's compute p^{q^p} with p rational maybe \u003e1. The floor may be huge, then we need mod 99991.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487541416,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487542573,"data":"       | Thus we need to find actual α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487543797,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487544945,"data":"       | Let's attempt to derive geometry more concretely.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487546172,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487547343,"data":"       | Given coordinates, we can compute everything in terms of r (incircle radius) and known lengths F_n, F_{n+1}. Then we can compute K, K', N, T, etc., and ratio.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487550218,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487551609,"data":"       | But there is also cyclic condition: quadrilateral KNK'B cyclic. This imposes a relationship between K and K' relative to B and N.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487553003,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487554168,"data":"       | Since K' is reflection of K across EF, the condition that B, K, K', N are concyclic may impose a specific value of r relative to side lengths.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487555573,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487556758,"data":"       | Thus we can solve for r that satisfies cyclic condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487558017,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487559178,"data":"       | Then compute ratio expression in terms of r and lengths, then maximize over possible triangles (i.e., over r). The maximum across all triangles for given n is a_n. Then as n large (even), a_{2n} tends to some limit α (maybe independent of n). So we need to compute limit of maximum ratio as n→∞.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487560966,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487562136,"data":"       | Thus we need to find r that maximizes ratio given constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48756341,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487564548,"data":"       | We can attempt to compute ratio symbolic as function of n and r, then find maximum, then limit as n→∞.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487565954,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487567123,"data":"       | Given the complexity, we may attempt to use sympy to derive formulas.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487568392,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487569541,"data":"       | But we need to compute K: intersection of circumcircle of AFE with circumcircle of ABC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487570837,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487572001,"data":"       | Given coordinates, we can compute circles and intersection.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48757328,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487574427,"data":"       | But the expression may be messy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.48757563,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487576785,"data":"       | Alternatively, perhaps there is known geometry: For any triangle, the reflection of K across EF lies on the circumcircle of B,N,K? Actually the cyclic condition may hold for all triangles? Let's test numerically for random triangle.\n"}
