,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487818726,"data":"       | But line EF is known to be perpendicular to AI? Let's compute: Since AI is angle bisector of angle A (incenter property). The incircle center I lies on angle bisector of A. So AI is internal angle bisector. The line EF is the chord of contact of incircle with sides AB and AC. There is known property: EF is perpendicular to AI. Indeed, the chord of contact of a circle with respect to two tangents from a point on the circle's external? Actually for incircle, the line connecting points of tangency of incircle with AB and AC is known to be perpendicular to the internal angle bisector AI. Let's verify: Since IF ⟂ AB and IE ⟂ AC, then the angle between IF and IE equals angle A. But AI bisects angle A, so AI is at half-angle between AB and AC. The line perpendicular to AI would be at 90° - A/2 from AB? Not obviously same as line through points where incircle touches AB and AC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487821594,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487823027,"data":"       | Let's compute using geometry: Let AB and AC be lines forming angle A at A. Let I be interior point on angle bisector. The foot of perpendicular from I to AB is F, to AC is E. So quadrilateral AIFE is cyclic? Actually ∠AFI = 90°, ∠AEI = 90°, so A, F, I, E are concyclic (since both subtend right angles). Indeed, points A, F, I, E lie on a circle with diameter AI. Because ∠AFI = 90°, ∠AEI = 90°, so both subtend AI. So A, F, I, E are concyclic with AI as diameter. Therefore EF is a chord of that circle, and AI is the diameter, so AI is perpendicular to EF (since the perpendicular from the center of a circle to a chord bisects the chord). Wait, AI is diameter, not center. The center of the circle is midpoint of AI, call O_A. The line O_A to chord EF is perpendicular to EF, but O_A lies on AI. So AI is not necessarily perpendicular to EF; but the line through midpoint of AI is perpendicular to EF. However, chord EF is symmetric w.r.t AI? Actually because the circle with diameter AI is symmetric about AI (the line AI is a diameter, so it's a line of symmetry). Since points F and E are symmetric with respect to AI (since they are reflections across AI). Indeed, the incircle's tangency points on AB and AC are symmetric across the angle bisector AI. So EF is perpendicular to AI? Let's think: If you have a circle with diameter AI, then any point on the circle has symmetric counterpart across AI. The chord connecting symmetric points is perpendicular to AI. Indeed, if you take two points symmetric across a line, the segment connecting them is perpendicular to that line. So EF is perpendicular to AI. So yes, EF ⟂ AI.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487827351,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487828513,"data":"       | Thus line EF is perpendicular to AI.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487829741,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.487830891,"data":"       | Thus N is foot of perpendicular from D to EF. Since EF ⟂ AI, N is projection of D onto EF.\n"}
