,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49223083,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492231972,"data":"       | Alternatively, maybe α = (p + sqrt(q))/r? But problem says α = p + sqrt(q) directly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49223332,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492234489,"data":"       | Thus p may be rational not integer; maybe p = 11 + 1/2? Actually p could be 11 + 1/2? That would be 11.5 plus sqrt(q) small negative? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492235864,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492238685,"data":"       | Let's solve for p and q rational such that α = p + sqrt(q). We have α approx 11.177816972660636.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.4922401,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492241275,"data":"       | Let p = a/b rational. Let sqrt(q) = α - p.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492242547,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492243706,"data":"       | Thus q = (α - p)^2 must be rational.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492244945,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492246113,"data":"       | Thus we need to find rational a/b such that (α - a/b)^2 is rational.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492247402,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492248546,"data":"       | Thus we need to find rational approximations to α such that the square of the difference is rational.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492249913,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492251118,"data":"       | Thus we need to solve for rational a/b such that (α - a/b)^2 ∈ Q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492252434,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492253561,"data":"       | But α is algebraic number (maybe of degree 4). So there may be representation with rational p and q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492254903,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49225606,"data":"       | We can attempt to find exact expression for α using symbolic solving of the geometry with p=1, q=φ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49225742,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492258558,"data":"       | Thus we need to solve for r analytically, then compute ratio expression, and simplify to p + sqrt(q).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492259902,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492261072,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute ratio expression symbolically and simplify.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492266167,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492267449,"data":"       | Given the difficulty of deriving K analytically, maybe we can find ratio directly using known geometry relationships.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492268853,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492270283,"data":"       | Alternative approach: Derive ratio formula in terms of side lengths and r using known formulas for distances.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492271651,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492272796,"data":"       | Let's attempt to express ratio CT*NB/(BT*NE) in terms of p,q,r.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492274053,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492275209,"data":"       | We have already derived formulas for T: T is intersection of circle tangent to BN at B passing through K with BC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492276542,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492277693,"data":"       | We derived that T's x-coordinate is 2*Ox where Ox is x-coordinate of center O_T.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492279007,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492280131,"data":"       | But O_T is intersection of line L1 (through B perpendicular to BN) and perpendicular bisector of BK.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492281441,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492282578,"data":"       | Thus we can compute ratio using vector formulas.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492283809,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49228662,"data":"       | But perhaps we can derive a simpler expression for ratio using power of point B? Let's analyze geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492287997,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492289173,"data":"       | Circle tangent to BN at B and passing through K: Since BN is tangent at B, the radius at B is perpendicular to BN. So center O_T lies on line through B perpendicular to BN.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492290669,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492291825,"data":"       | Also O_T is equidistant from B and K, so O_T lies on perpendicular bisector of BK.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492293129,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492294289,"data":"       | Thus O_T is intersection of these two lines.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492295506,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492296647,"data":"       | Thus O_T is determined uniquely (unless lines parallel). Then radius R_T = distance O_T B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492297946,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492299141,"data":"       | Now T is second intersection of this circle with BC (line y=0). Since B is on circle, the other intersection point T satisfies that the chord BT is a diameter? Actually not; but we can compute using geometry: The line BC is horizontal; the circle equation is (x - Ox)^2 + (y - Oy)^2 = R_T^2. With y=0, we solve for x: (x - Ox)^2 + Oy^2 = R_T^2 = Ox^2 + Oy^2 (since B at (0,0)). So (x - Ox)^2 + Oy^2 = Ox^2 + Oy^2 =\u003e (x - Ox)^2 = Ox^2 =\u003e x - Ox = ± Ox =\u003e x = 0 (B) or x = 2 Ox (T). So indeed T's x-coordinate = 2 Ox.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492301375,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492302554,"data":"       | Thus CT = (p+q) - 2 Ox, BT = 2 Ox.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492303775,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492304928,"data":"       | Thus ratio = ((p+q) - 2 Ox) * NB / (2 Ox * NE).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492306139,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492307293,"data":"       | Thus ratio = ((p+q)/(2 Ox) - 1) * NB / NE.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49230849,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492309657,"data":"       | Thus ratio depends on Ox.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492310831,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492312183,"data":"       | Now Ox is x-coordinate of O_T, which is intersection of line L1 (perpendicular to BN through B) and perpendicular bisector of BK.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492313564,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492314715,"data":"       | Thus we can compute Ox analytically using vector formulas.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492315969,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492317128,"data":"       | Let BN direction vector = v = N - B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492318331,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492319453,"data":"       | Line L1: passes through B (0,0) and direction w = perpendicular to v: w = (-v_y, v_x). So any point on L1 is s * w.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492320787,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492321942,"data":"       | Perpendicular bisector of BK: midpoint M = (K)/2 (since B is origin). Direction is perpendicular to BK vector u = K - B = K. So perpendicular bisector direction is perpendicular to K: w2 = (-K_y, K_x). So line L2: M + t * w2 = (K/2) + t * w2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492325154,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492326351,"data":"       | Set s * w = (K/2) + t * w2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492327524,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492328679,"data":"       | We can solve for s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492329864,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492331012,"data":"       | Thus Ox = x-coordinate of intersection point = s * w_x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492332243,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492333384,"data":"       | Thus we can compute Ox in terms of K coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492334603,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492335697,"data":"       | Let's derive formula.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492336842,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492338011,"data":"       | Let v = (v_x, v_y) = N.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492339169,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492340275,"data":"       | Then w = (-v_y, v_x).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492341446,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492342569,"data":"       | Let K = (k_x, k_y).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492343735,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492344846,"data":"       | Then w2 = (-k_y, k_x).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49234604,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492347337,"data":"       | We need to solve s * w = (k_x/2, k_y/2) + t * w2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492348562,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492349837,"data":"       | Thus we have two equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492351019,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492352162,"data":"       | s * (-v_y) = k_x/2 + t * (-k_y)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492353386,"data":"       | s * v_x = k_y/2 + t * k_x\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492354646,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492355751,"data":"       | Solve for s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492356964,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492358096,"data":"       | We can solve linear system for s and t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492359449,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492360563,"data":"       | Write matrix:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492361709,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492362853,"data":"       | [ -v_y   -k_y ] [s] = [k_x/2]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492364245,"data":"       | [  v_x    k_x ] [t]   [k_y/2]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492365435,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492366615,"data":"       | But careful: The second column corresponds to t times w2 components: w2 = (-k_y, k_x). So the system:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492367955,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492369224,"data":"       | s * (-v_y) + t * (-k_y) = k_x/2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492370444,"data":"       | s * v_x + t * k_x = k_y/2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492371622,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492372735,"data":"       | Thus solve for s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492373892,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492375057,"data":"       | We can solve using linear algebra.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492377887,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492379106,"data":"       | Compute determinant D = (-v_y) * k_x - v_x * (-k_y) = -v_y * k_x + v_x * k_y = v_x * k_y - v_y * k_x = cross product of v and K (i.e., v × K).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492380553,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492381686,"data":"       | Thus D = cross(v, K) = v_x * k_y - v_y * k_x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.4923829,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49238405,"data":"       | If D ≠ 0, we can solve.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492385282,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492386466,"data":"       | Solve for s using Cramer's rule:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492387738,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492389182,"data":"       | s = ( (k_x/2) * k_x - (k_y/2) * (-k_y) ) / D? Wait compute:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492390445,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492391592,"data":"       | We have matrix M = [[-v_y, -k_y], [v_x, k_x]].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49239279,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492393968,"data":"       | We need s = det([rhs, column2]) / det(M), where rhs = [k_x/2, k_y/2].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492395233,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492396406,"data":"       | Thus numerator for s: det([[k_x/2, -k_y], [k_y/2, k_x]]) = (k_x/2)*k_x - (-k_y)*(k_y/2) = (k_x^2)/2 + (k_y^2)/2 = (k_x^2 + k_y^2)/2 = (|K|^2)/2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492397859,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492399003,"data":"       | Thus s = (|K|^2 / 2) / D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492400202,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492401373,"data":"       | Thus Ox = s * w_x = s * (-v_y) = -(v_y) * (|K|^2 / 2) / D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492402641,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492403773,"data":"       | Thus Ox = - (v_y * |K|^2) / (2 D).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492405002,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492406171,"data":"       | Similarly, we could also compute using other component.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492407447,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49240855,"data":"       | Thus ratio expression becomes:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492409745,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492410876,"data":"       | CT = (p+q) - 2 Ox = a - 2 Ox.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492412079,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492413237,"data":"       | BT = 2 Ox.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49241439,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492415535,"data":"       | Thus ratio = ((a - 2 Ox) * NB) / (2 Ox * NE) = (a/(2 Ox) - 1) * NB / NE.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492416851,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49241802,"data":"       | Now NB = distance from B to N = |N| = sqrt(v_x^2 + v_y^2). NE = distance between N and E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492419354,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492420483,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute K and N and E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492421692,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492422852,"data":"       | But we can perhaps express ratio in terms of vectors v and K and E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492425909,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49242712,"data":"       | But we can perhaps simplify further using geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492428415,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492429598,"data":"       | But maybe we can compute ratio directly using these formulas, which may be simpler than computing O_T via solving linear equations each time.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492431009,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492432135,"data":"       | Nevertheless, we still need K coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492433373,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492434513,"data":"       | But maybe we can find K coordinates in terms of known points without heavy algebra.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492435825,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492436992,"data":"       | Recall K is second intersection of circumcircles of ABC and AFE.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492438282,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492439464,"data":"       | There is known relation: The Miquel point of triangle formed by lines AB, AC, and the incircle contact chord EF is the point K. Perhaps K lies on the circumcircle of ABC and also on the circle through A and the touchpoints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492441017,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492442115,"data":"       | Maybe there is known property: K lies on the A-symmedian of triangle ABC? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492443413,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49244474,"data":"       | We can attempt to compute K using intersection of circles as earlier but using analytic formulas (which we already have). That may be manageable.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49244616,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492447324,"data":"       | Thus we can compute K symbolically in terms of p,q,r using the intersection formula we derived earlier (circumcenters O1,O2). That may still involve sqrt, but we can keep.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492448786,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492449929,"data":"       | Thus we can compute ratio expression using sympy and then simplify.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492451234,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492452375,"data":"       | Given p,q are rational (or symbolic), we can substitute p=1, q=phi and then solve for r such that cyclic condition holds (determinant zero). That yields r as algebraic number (maybe degree 4). Then compute ratio expression, which may simplify to p + sqrt(q). Let's try to do this with sympy but focusing on p=1, q=phi.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492454153,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492455302,"data":"       | We'll implement the steps with sympy but using rational approximations for phi? Actually phi is algebraic sqrt(5). So sympy can handle sqrt(5).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492456714,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492457862,"data":"       | We'll compute O1, O2, K using formulas.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492459104,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492460257,"data":"       | Let's attempt to compute K symbolically using the formulas.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49246153,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492462654,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codedef symbolic_K(p_sym, q_sym, r_sym):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492465844,"data":"       |     # define points\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492467062,"data":"       |     B = sp.Point(0,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492468269,"data":"       |     C = sp.Point(p_sym+q_sym, 0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492469489,"data":"       |     a = p_sym+q_sym\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492470711,"data":"       |     I = sp.Point(p_sym, r_sym)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49247196,"data":"       |     # slopes\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492473153,"data":"       |     mB = 2*p_sym*r_sym/(p_sym**2 - r_sym**2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492474384,"data":"       |     mC = 2*q_sym*r_sym/(q_sym**2 - r_sym**2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492475624,"data":"       |     # A\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492476766,"data":"       |     xA = -mC*a/(mB - mC)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492477984,"data":"       |     yA = mB*xA\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492479135,"data":"       |     A = sp.Point(xA, yA)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492480366,"data":"       |     # F\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49248156,"data":"       |     Fx = p_sym*(p_sym**2 - r_sym**2)/(p_sym**2 + r_sym**2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492482805,"data":"       |     Fy = 2*p_sym**2*r_sym/(p_sym**2 + r_sym**2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492484042,"data":"       |     F = sp.Point(Fx, Fy)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492485221,"data":"       |     # E as reflection of F across line AI\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492486433,"data":"       |     line_AI = sp.Line(A, I)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492487616,"data":"       |     E = F.reflect(line_AI)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492488793,"data":"       |     # circumcenter O1 of ABC\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492490015,"data":"       |     xO1 = a/2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49249118,"data":"       |     yO1 = ((xA - xO1)**2 + yA**2 - xO1**2) / (2*yA)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492492408,"data":"       |     O1 = sp.Point(xO1, yO1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492493577,"data":"       |     R1_sq = xO1**2 + yO1**2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492494753,"data":"       |     # circumcenter O2 of AFE\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492496307,"data":"       |     O2 = sp.Point(sp.geometry.Triangle(A, F, E).circumcenter)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492497578,"data":"       |     R2_sq = O2.distance(A)**2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.4924988,"data":"       |     # vector d\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49249999,"data":"       |     dx = O2.x - O1.x\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492501179,"data":"       |     dy = O2.y - O1.y\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492502349,"data":"       |     d_sq = dx**2 + dy**2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492503551,"data":"       |     d = sp.sqrt(d_sq)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492504748,"data":"       |     # a_dist\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492505889,"data":"       |     a_dist = (R1_sq - R2_sq + d_sq) / (2*d)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492507116,"data":"       |     # h_sq\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492508311,"data":"       |     h_sq = R1_sq - a_dist**2\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492510159,"data":"       |     # unit vector u\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492511361,"data":"       |     ux = dx/d\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492513956,"data":"       |     uy = dy/d\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492515165,"data":"       |     # point P\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492516512,"data":"       |     Px = O1.x + a_dist*ux\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492517684,"data":"       |     Py = O1.y + a_dist*uy\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492518902,"data":"       |     # perp vector\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492520089,"data":"       |     perp_x = -uy\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492521389,"data":"       |     perp_y = ux\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492522573,"data":"       |     # K = P + sqrt(h_sq) * perp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492523793,"data":"       |     K = sp.Point(Px + sp.sqrt(h_sq)*perp_x, Py + sp.sqrt(h_sq)*perp_y)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492525084,"data":"       |     return A,B,C,F,E,O1,O2,K\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492526424,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492527558,"data":"       | # test with numeric p,q,r\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492528918,"data":"       | p_sym = sp.Integer(2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492530091,"data":"       | q_sym = sp.Integer(3)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492531322,"data":"       | r_sym = sp.nsimplify(0.7851137277331874)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492532536,"data":"       | A,B,C,F,E,O1,O2,K = symbolic_K(p_sym,q_sym,r_sym)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492533808,"data":"       | K_numer = sp.N(K)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492534999,"data":"       | K_numer\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.492536144,"data":"       | \n"}
