,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.494084605,"data":"       | Thus condition may be expressed as: angle(BN, BK) = angle(BN, reflect(BK, EF)). That implies that line BN bisects the angle between BK and its reflection across EF, i.e., BN is the angle bisector of angle between BK and its mirror across EF. This suggests BN is symmetric w.r.t. EF? Actually if a line reflects a vector across a line, the angle bisector between the original vector and its reflection is the reflecting line itself. Indeed, given a line L (EF) and a vector v (BK), its reflection across L yields v'. The angle between v and v' is twice the angle between v and L. The bisector of angle between v and v' is exactly L. So condition that BN is the angle bisector of angle between BK and K'B (i.e., BK and its reflection) means BN must be collinear with EF. But BN is not collinear with EF generally. Wait: The angle between BK and its reflection across EF is symmetric about EF, so the bisector is EF. Thus if BN is the bisector, BN must be along EF. But BN is not along EF (since N is foot from D to EF, so BN is not along EF). So maybe my interpretation is wrong.\n"}
