,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.495911367,"data":"       | Given the difficulty, perhaps we can accept numeric α and compute p and q via solving p + sqrt(q) = α with p,q rational. Since α is algebraic of degree 2, we can find minimal polynomial of α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.495912891,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.495915932,"data":"       | Let's compute minimal polynomial of α using high precision and sympy's `sp.minpoly` with rational approximation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.495917289,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.495918456,"data":"       | We can compute α using high precision rational approximation: compute α as root of polynomial with integer coefficients using `sp.minpoly` with algebraic numbers? But we need to provide α as algebraic number.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.495920022,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.495921142,"data":"       | We can compute α numerically to high precision and then use `sp.minpoly(sp.nsimplify(alpha, [sqrt(5)]), x)`? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.495922464,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.495923587,"data":"       | But we can attempt to find integer relation for [1, α, α^2] using `mpmath`'s `mp.findmaxroot`? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49592494,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.495926276,"data":"       | We can implement integer relation detection using `sympy.numerics`? Actually sympy has `sympy.numerics import ntheory` not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.495927599,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.495928706,"data":"       | But there is `sympy.numerics import ntheory`? Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.495929927,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.495931046,"data":"       | But there is `sympy.numerics import ntheory`? Not.\n"}
