,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496390166,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse the problem. It's a complex geometry problem with many definitions, but the final answer is a number modulo 99991. The problem reduces to analyzing the maximum possible value of a ratio CT*NB/(BT*NE) for certain triangles satisfying constraints, then find asymptotic bound for a_{2n} as n large, find smallest real number α such that a_{2n} \u003c α for all sufficiently large n. α = p + sqrt(q) for rationals p,q, then compute floor(p^{q^p}) mod 99991.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496392217,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496394831,"data":"       | We need to find p and q rational numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496396057,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496397237,"data":"       | Given the complexity, likely the problem reduces to some known ratio independent of geometry, maybe expressed in terms of side lengths BD, CD, incircle contact distances, etc. The condition that BD = F_n, CD = F_{n+1} (Fibonacci numbers). So BD and CD are consecutive Fibonacci numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496398914,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496400021,"data":"       | Also condition that quadrilateral KNK'B is cyclic. That may impose some relation between K and incircle geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496401344,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496402464,"data":"       | We need to find maximum possible value of CT*NB/(BT*NE) given constraints. Possibly the maximum is attained when triangle is isosceles? But AB != AC, so not isosceles. Might be some limit as n grows large, perhaps ratio tends to a limit.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496404026,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496405139,"data":"       | Goal: find α, the supremum of a_{2n} for large n. Likely α is some constant like φ (golden ratio) plus sqrt(some rational). Let's try to deduce.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496406587,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496407704,"data":"       | First, we need to understand geometry. Let's define triangle ABC with incircle ω touching BC at D, CA at E, AB at F. D is foot of incircle on BC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496409127,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496410241,"data":"       | Let AFE circumcircle intersect circumcircle Ω again at K (other than A). So K is second intersection of circumcircle of triangle AFE with circumcircle of ABC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496411724,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496412827,"data":"       | Reflect K across line EF to K'. So K' is symmetric of K across EF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496414071,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496415171,"data":"       | Let N be foot of perpendicular from D to EF. So N is projection of D onto EF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496416451,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49641773,"data":"       | Circle tangent to line BN and passing through B and K intersects BC again at T ≠ B. So there is a circle through B and K that is tangent to line BN (i.e., BN is tangent to the circle at B? Wait: \"circle tangent to line BN and passing through B and K\" - a circle that passes through B and K and is tangent to line BN. Since it passes through B, being tangent to line BN at B (since BN passes through B). So the circle is tangent to BN at B, passes through B and K, and meets BC again at T (other intersection besides B). So T is second intersection of that circle with BC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49642002,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496421127,"data":"       | We need ratio CT * NB / (BT * NE). NB is distance from N to B. NE is distance from N to E (N is foot from D onto EF). So NE is length of segment N-E along line EF? N is foot from D onto EF, so N lies on EF. So NE is distance from N to E along EF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496422717,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496423831,"data":"       | Goal: maximize CT*NB/(BT*NE) over triangles satisfying BD=F_n, CD=F_{n+1}, and quadrilateral KNK'B cyclic. For each n, define a_n as maximum possible value of ratio.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496426576,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496427719,"data":"       | Then for even n (2n), find limit supremum as n large: α is smallest real number such that eventually a_{2n} \u003c α. So α is essentially the limit supremum of a_{2n} as n→∞.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496429301,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496430414,"data":"       | Thus we need asymptotic behavior of ratio for large n given constraints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496431664,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49643275,"data":"       | Given BD and CD are consecutive Fibonacci numbers, as n large, they become large. So triangle sides become large? Not exactly; BD and CD are distances from B and C to incircle touch point D on BC. So BC = BD + CD = F_n + F_{n+1} = F_{n+2} (since Fibonacci recurrence). So BC length is also Fibonacci number F_{n+2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49643444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496435529,"data":"       | Thus BC = F_{n+2}. So side BC grows like φ^{n+2} / sqrt(5). So large.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496436817,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496437926,"data":"       | Thus triangle's side BC is large; other sides AB, AC are not specified but AB != AC. The incircle radius r is determined by triangle geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496439341,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496440466,"data":"       | We need to consider geometry constraints: incircle contact points D, E, F; K is second intersection of circumcircle of AFE with circumcircle of ABC; K' is reflection across EF; quadrilateral KNK'B cyclic.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496442691,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496444087,"data":"       | Let's analyze the condition that KNK'B is cyclic. Since K' is reflection of K across EF, maybe this condition forces K to lie on some Apollonius circle or something.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49644566,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496446767,"data":"       | We need to compute ratio CT*NB/(BT*NE) in terms of side lengths perhaps.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49644803,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496449121,"data":"       | Let's attempt to derive analytic expressions using coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496450494,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496451587,"data":"       | We can set up coordinate geometry: Place triangle ABC conveniently.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49645283,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496453954,"data":"       | Simplify: Use standard triangle coordinates: Let BC be on x-axis, with B at (0,0), C at (c,0) where c = BC = F_{n+2}. Let incircle ω be tangent to BC at D = (d,0) where d = BD = F_n. So D = (F_n, 0). Since incircle touches BC at D, its center I is at (d, r) where r is incircle radius (distance from BC). So I = (F_n, r). The incircle is tangent to AB and AC as well.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496455871,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496456974,"data":"       | Let AB and AC be lines passing through B and C respectively, tangent to incircle. So we can compute coordinates of A given that incircle touches AB at F and AC at E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496458387,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496459493,"data":"       | Given incircle center I = (d, r). The line AB passes through B (0,0) and is tangent to the circle centered at I with radius r. Similarly, line AC passes through C (c,0) and tangent to same circle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496463047,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496464176,"data":"       | Thus we can find slopes of AB and AC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496465377,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496466487,"data":"       | Given a circle centered at (d, r) radius r. A line through B (0,0) tangent to the circle: The distance from I to line AB must equal r. Since line passes through B, its equation is y = m x (slope m). Distance from point I to line y = m x is |m d - r| / sqrt(m^2 + 1) = r.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49646828,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496469399,"data":"       | Thus condition: |m d - r| = r sqrt(m^2 + 1). Since r\u003e0, we can solve for m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496470684,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496471787,"data":"       | Similarly for line through C (c,0) tangent to circle: line equation y = m' (x - c). Actually passes through (c,0), slope m2. So equation y = m2 (x - c). Distance from I to that line: |m2 (d - c) - r| / sqrt(m2^2 + 1) = r.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496473333,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496474573,"data":"       | We need both lines to intersect at A (some point). The slopes m1 and m2 are determined by geometry (two possible tangents from each point, but one will be interior to triangle). Since incircle is inside triangle, slopes will be such that A is above BC (positive y). So we can take positive slopes perhaps.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496476244,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496477334,"data":"       | We also have AB != AC, meaning slopes m1 != -m2? Actually AB and AC are not symmetric.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496478633,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49647973,"data":"       | Given we have two tangents from B and C to incircle, there are two possibilities each (external vs internal). For triangle, we need the tangents that intersect above BC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496481137,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49648225,"data":"       | We can compute coordinates of A as intersection of lines.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496483479,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496484561,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can use known formula for distances from vertices to touch points: Let side lengths be a = BC, b = CA, c = AB. Let semiperimeter s = (a+b+c)/2. Then BD = s - b, CD = s - c. Indeed, incircle touch point D splits BC into segments BD = s - b, CD = s - c. So BD = s - b = F_n, CD = s - c = F_{n+1}. So we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496486244,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496487336,"data":"       | s - b = F_n\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496488467,"data":"       | s - c = F_{n+1}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496489607,"data":"       | =\u003e b = s - F_n\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496490824,"data":"       | c = s - F_{n+1}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496491969,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496493081,"data":"       | Also a = BC = F_{n+2} = BD + CD = F_n + F_{n+1}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496494301,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496495507,"data":"       | We also know s = (a+b+c)/2 = (F_{n+2} + (s - F_n) + (s - F_{n+1}))/2 =\u003e s = (F_{n+2} + 2s - (F_n + F_{n+1}))/2 =\u003e Multiply both sides by 2: 2s = F_{n+2} + 2s - (F_n + F_{n+1}) =\u003e Cancel 2s both sides, we get 0 = F_{n+2} - (F_n + F_{n+1}) =\u003e 0 = F_{n+2} - F_{n+2} = 0. So identity holds. So any triangle with side a = F_{n+2} and BD, CD as given will have b = s - F_n and c = s - F_{n+1}, but s is unknown. However we can solve for b and c in terms of s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496499224,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496500397,"data":"       | From above: b = s - F_n, c = s - F_{n+1}. So b - c = (s - F_n) - (s - F_{n+1}) = F_{n+1} - F_n = F_{n-1}? Wait Fibonacci: F_{n+1} - F_n = F_{n-1}? Actually F_{n+1} = F_n + F_{n-1}, so F_{n+1} - F_n = F_{n-1}. So b - c = F_{n-1}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49650191,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496503033,"data":"       | Thus side lengths satisfy b - c = F_{n-1}. So AB - AC = b - c = F_{n-1}. Since AB != AC, indeed difference is F_{n-1}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496504347,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496505456,"data":"       | Now we also have a = F_{n+2}. So we have side lengths a,b,c that satisfy:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496506694,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496507813,"data":"       | b - c = F_{n-1}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496509217,"data":"       | b + c = 2s - (F_n + F_{n+1})? Actually from b = s - F_n, c = s - F_{n+1}. So b + c = 2s - (F_n + F_{n+1}).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496510504,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496511633,"data":"       | But we can also compute s: s = (a + b + c)/2 = (F_{n+2} + b + c)/2. So plugging into b = s - F_n gives b = (F_{n+2} + b + c)/2 - F_n =\u003e Multiply 2: 2b = F_{n+2} + b + c - 2F_n =\u003e =\u003e b = F_{n+2} + c - 2F_n =\u003e So b - c = F_{n+2} - 2F_n. But we also have b - c = F_{n-1}. So we get equation:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496513437,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496514531,"data":"       | F_{n+2} - 2F_n = F_{n-1}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496515661,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496516771,"data":"       | Check if this holds for Fibonacci numbers: F_{n+2} = F_{n+1} + F_n. So left side = (F_{n+1} + F_n) - 2F_n = F_{n+1} - F_n = F_{n-1}. Indeed holds. So consistent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496518177,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496519339,"data":"       | Thus side lengths are not uniquely determined; s can vary? Let's solve for b and c explicitly in terms of s. b = s - F_n, c = s - F_{n+1}. So s must be greater than max(F_n, F_{n+1}) to have positive sides. Since b\u003e0, c\u003e0. So s \u003e F_{n+1}. Also s = (a + b + c)/2 = (F_{n+2} + b + c)/2. Plug b,c: s = (F_{n+2} + (s - F_n) + (s - F_{n+1}))/2 =\u003e 2s = F_{n+2} + 2s - (F_n + F_{n+1}) =\u003e Cancel 2s =\u003e 0 = F_{n+2} - (F_n + F_{n+1}) = 0. So any s works? Actually the equation reduces to identity, meaning s can be any value satisfying b = s - F_n \u003e 0 and c = s - F_{n+1} \u003e 0. So s \u003e F_{n+1}. So there is a family of triangles with same incircle touch distances but varying side lengths b,c? Wait but b = s - F_n, c = s - F_{n+1}. So as s varies, b,c vary linearly, but a = BC is fixed. So there is a one-parameter family of triangles with same BC and same incircle touch point D? That seems plausible: moving A along a line parallel to BC while maintaining incircle tangency? Actually incircle radius changes accordingly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496522547,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496523671,"data":"       | But incircle radius r depends on area: r = Δ / s. Since a is fixed, area varies with shape. So there is a continuum of possible triangles satisfying BD, CD fixed, AB != AC. So we can vary ratio.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496525181,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49652804,"data":"       | Now we also have extra condition: quadrilateral KNK'B is cyclic. This imposes a constraint that may fix s (or shape) uniquely for each n. So we need to examine that condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496529519,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496530874,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute K, K', and condition for cyclic quadrilateral.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496532131,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496533234,"data":"       | Given geometry, perhaps the condition reduces to some angle equality leading to a relationship between angles at B and C. Might be something like ∠KBN = ∠K'BN? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496534749,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496535883,"data":"       | We need to find expression for K: second intersection of circumcircle of AFE with circumcircle of ABC. Points A, F, E are contact points of incircle with AB and AC and vertex A. The circumcircle of AFE is known as the \"A-mixtilinear incircle\"? Wait AFE are points: A is vertex, F on AB, E on AC. The circumcircle of AFE is the \"A-excircle\"? Actually there is known configuration: The circumcircle of triangle AEF is the A-mixtilinear incircle? No, the A-mixtilinear incircle is tangent to circumcircle at a point near A and tangent to sides AB and AC. However, the circle through A, E, F is the incircle of the triangle formed by lines AB, AC, and the A-mixtilinear incircle? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496538333,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496539444,"data":"       | But there is known property: The circumcircle of triangle AEF passes through the tangency point of the A-mixtilinear incircle with the circumcircle of ABC. That point is often denoted as T_A. However, here K is defined as second intersection of circumcircle of AFE with circumcircle of ABC. Indeed, the point where the A-mixtilinear incircle touches the circumcircle is known to lie on the circumcircle of AEF. So K is that tangency point (the A-mixtilinear incircle's tangency point). But they didn't mention mixtilinear incircle; they just defined K as intersection of circumcircle of AFE with circumcircle of ABC. So K is that point.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496541697,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49654309,"data":"       | Thus K is the A-mixtilinear touch point. Known properties: K lies on arc BC of circumcircle containing A? Actually the A-mixtilinear incircle touches circumcircle at a point between B and C on the minor arc BC (the arc not containing A). Wait there are two arcs: the arc BC not containing A (minor arc) and the arc BC containing A (major). The A-mixtilinear incircle touches the circumcircle at a point on the arc BC containing A (i.e., near A). Let's recall: The A-mixtilinear incircle is tangent to sides AB and AC and internally tangent to circumcircle. The tangency point with circumcircle lies on the arc BC containing A (the arc opposite B and C). Actually think: The incircle touches AB and AC near A, and the mixtilinear incircle is inside the circumcircle, tangent to AB, AC, and circumcircle. The tangency point with circumcircle is on the arc BC containing A (the arc between B and C that includes A). So K is on arc BC containing A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496545867,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496546974,"data":"       | Thus K is known to have certain angle relations: ∠BKA = 90° + ∠A/2, etc. Actually property: The line AK passes through the exsimilicenter of incircle and circumcircle? Not exactly. There are many known relationships.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496548621,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496549761,"data":"       | Now K' is reflection of K across line EF. Since EF is chord of incircle? Actually E and F are points where incircle touches AC and AB, respectively. So EF is a chord of the incircle? Wait E and F are points of tangency of incircle with sides, so they lie on incircle ω. Indeed incircle touches side AC at E, AB at F. So E and F are points on incircle ω. So EF is a chord of incircle ω. K is a point on circumcircle Ω (circumcircle of ABC). K' is reflection across line EF, which is not necessarily on incircle or circumcircle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496553766,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496555147,"data":"       | Condition that quadrilateral KNK'B is cyclic: Points K, N, K', B lie on a circle. Since K' is reflection of K across EF, maybe this cyclic condition imposes that line BN is tangent to the circle passing through K and K' and B? Actually N is foot from D onto EF, so N lies on EF as well. So N is on line EF. So quadrilateral KNK'B has vertices K (outside), N (on EF), K' (reflection across EF), B (vertex of triangle). Since N lies on EF, and K' is symmetric of K across EF, the circle passing through K, N, K' will be symmetric across EF (since K and K' symmetric). So the center of that circle lies on EF (the perpendicular bisector of KK' is EF). Actually reflection across EF means EF is the perpendicular bisector of segment KK'. So the circle through K, K', and N will have its center on the perpendicular bisector of KK', which is EF, and also passes through N which lies on EF, so the center is on EF and passes through N, so N is at distance equal to radius from center. So the circle's center is some point O' on EF such that O'N = radius = O'K = O'K'.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49655812,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496559237,"data":"       | Thus the circle through K, K', N is symmetric across EF. Now we also require that B also lies on this circle. So B must satisfy O'B = radius. So B is also symmetric across EF? Not necessarily.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496560714,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496561844,"data":"       | Thus condition reduces to: distance from B to EF equals distance from K to EF? Wait O' is on EF, radius = distance from O' to K (perpendicular distance). Since K' is symmetric, O'K = O'K' = distance from O' to K. Also O'N = radius, but N is on EF, so O'N is just |O'N| = distance along EF between O' and N. So radius is also the distance from O' to N (a segment along EF). So we have O'N = O'K. So the distance from O' to K (perpendicular) equals the distance along EF from O' to N.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496563834,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496564934,"data":"       | Thus the condition that B lies on same circle means O'B = O'K = O'N. So B must satisfy that its distance to O' equals that same radius.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496566307,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496567429,"data":"       | Thus the condition may be expressed as: There exists a point O' on EF such that distances O'B = O'K = O'N.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496568757,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496569886,"data":"       | Since O'N is along EF, O'N is simply the absolute difference between O' coordinate and N coordinate along EF. So O' is a point on EF such that its distance to B equals its distance to K and also equals its distance to N.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496571427,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496572531,"data":"       | Thus O' is the circumcenter of triangle BKN? Actually not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496573789,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496575147,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can think of circle through B, K, K' with center O' on EF. Since K and K' are symmetric across EF, the perpendicular bisector of KK' is EF, so any circle through K and K' with center on EF will have EF as perpendicular bisector. So the circle through K and K' with center O' on EF is the unique circle with radius equal to distance O'K. If B also lies on that circle, then O'B = O'K.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496578619,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496579747,"data":"       | Thus condition is that B is equidistant from O' as K (and K').\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496581001,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49658212,"data":"       | Thus O' is intersection of EF with perpendicular bisector of BK? Not necessarily. Actually the perpendicular bisector of BK is a line; O' must lie on EF and also satisfy O'B = O'K. So O' is intersection of EF with the perpendicular bisector of BK. So condition that B, K, K', N are concyclic is equivalent to: The perpendicular bisector of BK meets EF at a point O' such that O' is also equidistant to N (i.e., O'N = O'K). But O'N = O'K is automatically satisfied if O' is intersection of EF with perpendicular bisector of BK? Not automatically; we need also O'N = O'K. So O' must also be such that N lies on the circle with center O' radius O'K. Since N lies on EF, O'N is just distance along EF from O' to N. So O'N must equal O'K.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496584501,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496585609,"data":"       | Thus condition reduces to: There exists a point O' on EF such that O' is equidistant from B and K, and distance from O' to N equals that same distance.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496586997,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49658812,"data":"       | Thus O' is the unique point on EF that is equidistant from B and K (intersection of EF with perpendicular bisector of BK). Then we need O'N = O'K. So O' must be such that N is also at same distance.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496589639,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496590781,"data":"       | Thus condition can be expressed as: Let O' = intersection of EF with perpendicular bisector of BK. Then O'N = O'K. Equivalent to: N is also on the circle centered at O' with radius O'K.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496592251,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496593376,"data":"       | Thus the condition is a relation between distances.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496594614,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49659571,"data":"       | Thus we can compute coordinates and express condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496596907,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496598011,"data":"       | Now we also need N: foot of perpendicular from D onto EF. Since D is (d,0), EF is line segment connecting points where incircle touches AB and AC. Coordinates of E and F can be computed using geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496599513,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49660082,"data":"       | Let's compute coordinates using sympy for general parameters: Let a = BC = F_{n+2}, d = BD = F_n, c = CD = F_{n+1} (distance from C to D). Let r be incircle radius unknown. Let I = (d, r). Then incircle touches AB at F: point on AB where incircle tangent. Since incircle touches AB at a point where line from I to point is perpendicular to AB. So F is foot of perpendicular from I to line AB. Similarly E is foot of perpendicular from I to AC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496602743,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496603887,"data":"       | But we can also compute coordinates of F and E using known distances along sides: The distance from B to point of tangency on AB (i.e., BF) equals s - b? Wait incircle tangency point on AB splits AB into segments: AF = s - a? Actually standard: For triangle ABC, incircle touch point on AB is point F such that AF = s - a? Let's recall: The incircle touch point on side AB divides AB into segments: AF = s - a? Wait side lengths: a = BC, b = CA, c = AB. Then tangency points: On side BC, BD = s - b, CD = s - c. On side CA, CE = s - c? Actually side CA is b. The incircle touches CA at E, splitting CA into segments: CE = s - c? Let's recall: The length from C to touch point on CA (E) is s - c, and from A to E is s - a. Similarly on AB: AF = s - a, BF = s - b. Wait check: On side AB (c), the distances are: AF = s - a, FB = s - b. Yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49660812,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496609248,"data":"       | Thus we have AF = s - a, BF = s - b. Similarly, AE = s - a? Actually side CA (b): CE = s - c, AE = s - a? Let's verify: For side CA (b), the distances from C to touch point E is s - c, from A to E is s - a. So yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496610784,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496611907,"data":"       | Thus we can compute coordinates of F and E using distances along AB and AC from B and C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496613201,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49661434,"data":"       | Given we have coordinates of B (0,0), C (a,0). We need coordinates of A. We can compute using side lengths b = CA, c = AB. Using law of cosines: coordinates of A = (x_A, y_A) where x_A = (c^2 + a^2 - b^2) / (2a) (by projecting onto BC). Since B at (0,0), C at (a,0). Indeed, using standard formula: distance from B to foot of altitude onto BC is (c^2 - b^2 + a^2)/(2a). So x_A = (c^2 - b^2 + a^2) / (2a). y_A = sqrt(c^2 - x_A^2) (positive).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49661625,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496617361,"data":"       | Thus we can compute coordinates of A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49661856,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49661992,"data":"       | Then we can compute coordinates of F: point on AB at distance BF = s - b from B along AB direction. So vector from B to A is (x_A, y_A). The unit vector along BA is (x_A, y_A)/c (since length BA = c). Then F = B + (s - b) * (unit vector). Similarly, E = C + (s - c) * (unit vector from C to A) but direction from C to A is (x_A - a, y_A) with length b. So E = C + (s - c) * ((x_A - a, y_A) / b). Or we can compute using distances along CA: from C to E is s - c.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496621867,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496622977,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can compute using incircle geometry: Since incircle radius r, the foot of perpendicular from I to AB is F. So we can compute F as intersection of AB with line through I perpendicular to AB. That may be easier.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496624544,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496625656,"data":"       | But using distances along sides may be simpler.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496626861,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496627993,"data":"       | Now we need to compute N: foot of perpendicular from D (d,0) to line EF. So N is projection of D onto line EF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496629483,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496630603,"data":"       | Now we need to compute K: second intersection of circumcircle of triangle AFE with circumcircle of ABC. This can be computed as intersection of circles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49663203,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496634881,"data":"       | Circumcircle of ABC: passes through A, B, C. Its center O is circumcenter.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.4966362,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496637317,"data":"       | Circumcircle of AFE: passes through A, F, E.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496638516,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496639627,"data":"       | Their intersection points are A (common) and K (other). So K can be computed as the second intersection of circles defined by points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496640978,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496642097,"data":"       | We can compute K coordinates using sympy solving system of equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496643333,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496644426,"data":"       | Then we can compute K' as reflection of K across line EF: reflect point across line.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496645701,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496647047,"data":"       | Then compute condition that quadrilateral K,N,K',B is cyclic: i.e., points K,N,K',B concyclic. Equivalent to oriented angle condition: ∠KBN = ∠K'BN? Actually for cyclic quadrilateral, opposite angles sum to 180°, or cross ratio condition. But easiest: compute determinant of matrix of distances: For four points, they are concyclic if and only if the pairwise power of points satisfy something. But we can compute using condition that the circumcircle of triangle KBN passes through K'. So we can compute circle through K, B, N and check if K' lies on it.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496649228,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496650333,"data":"       | Thus we can compute condition equation(s) that must hold for parameters.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496651587,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49665269,"data":"       | We have parameters: a = F_{n+2}, d = F_n, c = F_{n+1}, s variable \u003e F_{n+1}. Also b = s - F_n, c_side = s - F_{n+1} (but we used c for side length AB; rename side lengths: side AB = c_side, side AC = b_side). Let's rename:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496654301,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496655414,"data":"       | Let side lengths:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496656564,"data":"       | - a = BC = F_{n+2}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496657734,"data":"       | - b = CA = s - F_n (since CA opposite B)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496658933,"data":"       | - c = AB = s - F_{n+1} (since AB opposite C)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496660134,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496661317,"data":"       | We need to ensure triangle inequality: b + c \u003e a, etc. That will impose constraints on s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496662596,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496663707,"data":"       | Now we can treat s as variable parameter.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496664884,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496665988,"data":"       | We need to impose cyclic condition to find possible s values for each n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496667206,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496668311,"data":"       | Then compute ratio R = (CT * NB) / (BT * NE). Where T is defined via circle through B and K tangent to BN.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49666961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496670801,"data":"       | We need to compute T: intersection of circle (through B and K, tangent to BN at B) with BC again.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496674516,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49667567,"data":"       | Given a circle passing through B and K and tangent to line BN at B: Since it's tangent at B, the line BN is tangent to the circle at B. So the radius to B is perpendicular to BN. So the center O_T of this circle lies on line through B perpendicular to BN. Also O_T lies on perpendicular bisector of BK (since circle passes through B and K). Intersection of these two lines gives O_T. Then T is second intersection of circle with BC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496677521,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496678646,"data":"       | Thus we can compute T coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496679823,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496680943,"data":"       | Thus we can compute ratio R.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496682093,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496683246,"data":"       | Goal: for each n (even maybe), find maximum possible R over s satisfying cyclic condition. Then find asymptotic supremum as n large.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496684571,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496685701,"data":"       | We suspect that as n large, the ratio tends to a limit independent of n, maybe determined by geometry of limit shape as s → ∞? Or as s tends to some value that maximizes ratio.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496687221,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496688317,"data":"       | Thus we need to analyze function R(s) subject to cyclic condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496689588,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496691057,"data":"       | Potentially the cyclic condition may uniquely determine s (maybe there is only one triangle satisfying condition for each n). Then a_n is just the ratio for that triangle. Then we need to find asymptotic limit of that ratio as n large. So a_n would be a sequence; a_{2n} would converge to α from below maybe. Then α is limit supremum.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496692828,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496693951,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute ratio for large n; maybe it converges to constant.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496695211,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49669632,"data":"       | Thus we can compute for large n numerically for several n (even) to guess limit and find expression p + sqrt(q). Then compute floor(p^{q^p}) mod 99991.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49669768,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496698777,"data":"       | Thus we need to implement algorithm to compute ratio for given n (maybe even) by solving for s such that cyclic condition holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496700121,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496701244,"data":"       | But we need to find s that satisfies cyclic condition. There may be multiple s solutions; we need to maximize ratio. But maybe there is unique s; then a_n is that ratio.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496702664,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496703791,"data":"       | We need to test.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496704897,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496705984,"data":"       | Let's implement with sympy numeric solving.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496707147,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496709727,"data":"       | We'll need Fibonacci numbers F_n. Let's generate for n up to maybe 20 to see pattern.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49671101,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496712137,"data":"       | But note that n can be large; but we can compute for moderate n (maybe up to 20) to see ratio approaching limit.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496713486,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496714624,"data":"       | Let's start coding.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496715743,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496716846,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codeimport sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496718056,"data":"       | from math import sqrt\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496719178,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496720276,"data":"       | def fibonacci(n):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496721417,"data":"       |     # returns F_n with F_0=0, F_1=1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49672258,"data":"       |     a,b=0,1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496723734,"data":"       |     for i in range(n):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496724887,"data":"       |         a,b=b,a+b\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496726051,"data":"       |     return a\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496727171,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496728254,"data":"       | def compute_ratio(n):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496729411,"data":"       |     # n is integer \u003e= 2 maybe\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496730636,"data":"       |     # define symbols\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496731834,"data":"       |     s = sp.symbols('s', positive=True)  # semiperimeter\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496733074,"data":"       |     # side lengths\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496734223,"data":"       |     a = fibonacci(n+2)  # BC\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496735391,"data":"       |     d = fibonacci(n)    # BD\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49673654,"data":"       |     c_seg = fibonacci(n+1) # CD\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49673772,"data":"       |     # ensure a = d + c_seg\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496738861,"data":"       |     assert a == d + c_seg\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496740037,"data":"       |     # define side lengths b = CA, c = AB\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496741456,"data":"       |     b = s - d   # CA\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496742656,"data":"       |     c = s - c_seg  # AB\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496743796,"data":"       |     # coordinates\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496744924,"data":"       |     # B = (0,0), C = (a,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496746067,"data":"       |     # compute x_A, y_A\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496747211,"data":"       |     xA = (c**2 - b**2 + a**2) / (2*a)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496748366,"data":"       |     # yA = sqrt(c^2 - xA^2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496749501,"data":"       |     yA = sp.sqrt(c**2 - xA**2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49675066,"data":"       |     # points\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496751817,"data":"       |     B = sp.Point(0,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496752964,"data":"       |     C = sp.Point(a,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496754087,"data":"       |     A = sp.Point(xA, yA)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49675692,"data":"       |     # compute semiperimeter s = (a+b+c)/2 (should hold)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49675817,"data":"       |     # but we treat s as variable; we can enforce equation s - (a+b+c)/2 = 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49675943,"data":"       |     # Actually we have relation: s = (a+b+c)/2. Let's impose that as equation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496760684,"data":"       |     # Let's solve for s using that relation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496761931,"data":"       |     # s = (a + b + c)/2 =\u003e 2s = a + b + c =\u003e 2s = a + (s - d) + (s - c_seg) =\u003e 2s = a + 2s - (d + c_seg) =\u003e cancel 2s =\u003e 0 = a - (d + c_seg) =\u003e 0 = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49676353,"data":"       |     # So identity, no constraint.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496764681,"data":"       |     # So s is free \u003e max(d,c_seg)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496765904,"data":"       |     # compute points F and E using distances along sides\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496767143,"data":"       |     # BF = s - b_side? Actually BF = s - b (where b is side CA). Wait earlier we said BF = s - b (where b = CA). So BF = s - b = s - (s - d) = d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496768571,"data":"       |     # Indeed BF = BD = d! That's a known property: The distance from B to touch point on AB equals BD? Let's verify: BF = s - b = s - (s - d) = d. Yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496769936,"data":"       |     # Similarly, AF = s - a = s - a (a = BC). So AF = s - a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496771166,"data":"       |     # So F lies on AB at distance BF = d from B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496772351,"data":"       |     # Similarly, CE = s - c = s - (s - c_seg) = c_seg = CD.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496773606,"data":"       |     # So CE = CD = c_seg.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496774753,"data":"       |     # And AE = s - a = same as AF.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49677592,"data":"       |     # So E is on AC such that CE = c_seg from C.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496777111,"data":"       |     # Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496778224,"data":"       |     # So we can compute F as point along AB at distance d from B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49677945,"data":"       |     # Unit vector from B to A: (xA, yA)/c\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496780618,"data":"       |     BF = d\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496781771,"data":"       |     # but need to ensure BF \u003c= c (AB length). Since c = s - c_seg, and BF = d, we need d \u003c= s - c_seg =\u003e s \u003e= d + c_seg = a. So s \u003e= a. So semiperimeter must be at least BC. That's plausible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496783501,"data":"       |     # So s must be \u003e= a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496784964,"data":"       |     # We'll keep s variable with constraint s \u003e a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496786207,"data":"       |     # Compute F:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496787353,"data":"       |     F = sp.Point(xA * BF / c, yA * BF / c)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496788508,"data":"       |     # Similarly, E: point on AC at distance CE = c_seg from C towards A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496789743,"data":"       |     # Vector from C to A: (xA - a, yA)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496790917,"data":"       |     CE = c_seg\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49679207,"data":"       |     E = sp.Point(a + (xA - a) * CE / b, 0 + yA * CE / b)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496803264,"data":"       |     # Compute line EF\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496805541,"data":"       |     line_EF = sp.Line(E, F)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49680674,"data":"       |     # Compute D = (d,0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496807887,"data":"       |     D = sp.Point(d, 0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496809034,"data":"       |     # Compute foot N of perpendicular from D to EF\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496810268,"data":"       |     N = line_EF.projection(D)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49681141,"data":"       |     # Compute circumcircle of ABC (Omega)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496812584,"data":"       |     Omega = sp.Circle(A, B, C)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496813824,"data":"       |     # Compute circumcircle of AFE\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496814981,"data":"       |     circle_AFE = sp.Circle(A, F, E)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496816134,"data":"       |     # Compute intersection points of circles\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49681731,"data":"       |     inter = Omega.intersection(circle_AFE)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496818474,"data":"       |     # inter contains A and K (maybe order)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496819657,"data":"       |     # Identify K as point not equal to A\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496820831,"data":"       |     K = None\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496821997,"data":"       |     for pt in inter:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496823158,"data":"       |         if not pt.equals(A):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496824314,"data":"       |             K = pt\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496825457,"data":"       |     if K is None:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49682658,"data":"       |         raise ValueError(\"No K found\")\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496827797,"data":"       |     # Compute reflection of K across line EF =\u003e K'\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496829014,"data":"       |     Kprime = K.reflect(line_EF)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49683016,"data":"       |     # Condition: K, N, K', B are concyclic\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496831351,"data":"       |     # Compute circle through K, N, B and check if K' lies on it (distance equality)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496832631,"data":"       |     # Define circle through K, N, B\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496833817,"data":"       |     circle_KNB = sp.Circle(K, N, B)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496835004,"data":"       |     # Condition: Kprime lies on this circle =\u003e distance from center equals radius\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49683631,"data":"       |     # So equation: (Kprime.distance(circle_KNB.center) - circle_KNB.radius) == 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496837621,"data":"       |     eq_cyclic = sp.simplify(Kprime.distance(circle_KNB.center)**2 - circle_KNB.radius**2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496838891,"data":"       |     # eq_cyclic should be zero for cyclic condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496840147,"data":"       |     # Now compute T: circle through B and K tangent to line BN at B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496841424,"data":"       |     # Tangent condition: line BN is tangent at B, so radius to B is perpendicular to BN.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496842917,"data":"       |     # Center O_T lies on line through B perpendicular to BN.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496845771,"data":"       |     line_BN = sp.Line(B, N)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496847001,"data":"       |     # Perpendicular line through B:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496848181,"data":"       |     perp_B = line_BN.perpendicular_line(B)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496849377,"data":"       |     # Also center lies on perpendicular bisector of BK.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496850571,"data":"       |     mid_BK = sp.Point((B.x + K.x)/2, (B.y + K.y)/2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496851767,"data":"       |     # Slope of BK: line_BK = sp.Line(B, K)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49685295,"data":"       |     line_BK = sp.Line(B, K)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496854126,"data":"       |     perp_BK = line_BK.perpendicular_line(mid_BK)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496855304,"data":"       |     # Intersection gives center O_T\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496856463,"data":"       |     O_T = perp_B.intersection(perp_BK)[0]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496857627,"data":"       |     # Radius = distance O_T to B\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496858778,"data":"       |     r_T = O_T.distance(B)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49685993,"data":"       |     # Circle_T = sp.Circle(O_T, r_T)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496861104,"data":"       |     # Intersection of Circle_T with line BC (y=0) gives points B and T\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496862331,"data":"       |     line_BC = sp.Line(B, C)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496863546,"data":"       |     inter_T = sp.Circle(O_T, r_T).intersection(line_BC)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496864758,"data":"       |     # Should be two points: B and T\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496865924,"data":"       |     T = None\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496867051,"data":"       |     for pt in inter_T:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496868196,"data":"       |         if not pt.equals(B):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496869361,"data":"       |             T = pt\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496870517,"data":"       |     if T is None:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496871636,"data":"       |         # maybe B repeated\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496872836,"data":"       |         T = inter_T[0] if inter_T[0] != B else inter_T[1]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496874048,"data":"       |     # Compute distances for ratio: CT, NB, BT, NE\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496875256,"data":"       |     CT = C.distance(T)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496876404,"data":"       |     NB = N.distance(B)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496877547,"data":"       |     BT = B.distance(T)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496878684,"data":"       |     NE = N.distance(E)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496879844,"data":"       |     # Ratio R = (CT * NB) / (BT * NE)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496881047,"data":"       |     R = sp.simplify((CT * NB) / (BT * NE))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496882267,"data":"       |     # Return expression for R and cyclic condition eq_cyclic\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496883524,"data":"       |     return sp.simplify(eq_cyclic), sp.simplify(R), (A,B,C,D,E,F,N,K,Kprime,T,O_T)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49688481,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.4968859,"data":"       | # Test for a small n, say n=3 (odd) or n=4 (even)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496888747,"data":"       | eq_cyclic, R_expr, _ = compute_ratio(4)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.496889961,"data":"       | eq_cyclic, R_expr\n"}
