,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.498976593,"data":"       | analysisInteresting! Ratio values appear to converge to about 4.236... maybe approaching a limit around 4.23607? That number is maybe φ^2? Let's compute φ = (1+√5)/2 ≈ 1.61803. φ^2 = φ + 1 ≈ 2.61803. Not 4.236. Maybe φ^3 = 4.23607? Let's compute φ^3 = φ^2 * φ = 2.61803 * 1.61803 ≈ 4.23607. Indeed φ^3 = ( (1+√5)/2 )^3 = ( (1+√5)^3 ) / 8 = ?\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.498978667,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.498979855,"data":"       | Compute φ^3 exactly: φ = (1+√5)/2. φ^3 = ( (1+√5)^3 ) / 8 = (1 + 3√5 + 3*5 + 5√5) / 8? Let's compute: (1+√5)^3 = (1 + 3√5 + 3*5 + 5√5)?? Actually (a+b)^3 = a^3 + 3 a^2 b + 3 a b^2 + b^3. With a=1, b=√5: a^3 = 1. a^2 b = 1*√5 = √5, times 3 =\u003e 3√5. a b^2 = 1*(5) = 5, times 3 =\u003e 15. b^3 = (√5)^3 = 5√5. So sum = 1 + 3√5 + 15 + 5√5 = 16 + 8√5. So (1+√5)^3 = 16 + 8√5 = 8 (2 + √5). Divide by 8 gives φ^3 = 2 + √5 ≈ 2 + 2.23607 = 4.23607. Indeed φ^3 = 2 + √5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.498982407,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.498983513,"data":"       | Thus limit appears to be 2 + √5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.498984717,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.498985868,"data":"       | Our ratio values for n even (2,4,6,8,10) are: n=2 (ratio 3), n=4 (4), n=6 (4.2), n=8 (4.230769...), n=10 (4.235294...), approaching 4.23607.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.498987227,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.498988335,"data":"       | Thus α = limit supremum for even n (2n) as n large is likely 2 + √5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49898964,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.498990791,"data":"       | But we need to verify that a_{2n} is maximum ratio for each n, and that ratio tends to that limit from below, so α = 2 + √5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.498992175,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.498993557,"data":"       | Given that a_n is defined as maximum possible value of ratio across all n-tastic triangles. For each n, we found a triangle (the one with s solving cyclic condition) giving ratio values. But we need to ensure that ratio is maximized for that triangle; perhaps there is only one triangle satisfying cyclic condition, thus ratio is unique. So a_n is that ratio.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.498997357,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.49899852,"data":"       | Thus α = sup_{large even n} a_{2n} = limit = 2 + √5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.498999765,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.499000861,"data":"       | Thus α = p + √q with p,q rational. Here α = 2 + √5. So p = 2, q = 5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.499002175,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.499003328,"data":"       | Now compute floor(p^{q^p}) = floor(2^{5^2}) = floor(2^{25}) = 2^{25} = 33,554,432.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.499004602,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.499005699,"data":"       | Now compute remainder of that modulo 99991.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2711.499006855,"data":"       | \n"}
