,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.169449943,"data":"       | Thus the dot product of shift of $\\alpha$ with $\\beta$ yields a function of $n$ that is exactly 1 at two distinct integer points $k,l$, and zero elsewhere.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.169451671,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.169452764,"data":"       | We need to count the number of shifty functions $\\alpha$.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.169454233,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.169456248,"data":"       | Interpretation: For each $\\alpha$ with support in [0,8], there must exist some $\\beta$ (with finite support) and two distinct integers $k,l$ such that the convolution-like condition holds: the inner product of shifted $\\alpha$ with $\\beta$ is 1 at exactly two shift positions, zero elsewhere.\n"}
