,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.169461296,"data":"       | This is reminiscent of cross-correlation: $(\\alpha \\star S_{-n}(\\beta))$? Actually $S_n(\\alpha) \\star \\beta = \\sum_t \\alpha(t+n) \\beta(t) = \\sum_t \\alpha(t) \\beta(t-n) = (\\alpha \\star S_{-n}(\\beta))$ (since shift of beta). So the condition is that the cross-correlation of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ is exactly the indicator of two points $k,l$, each with value 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.16946813,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.169470283,"data":"       | Thus we need to find all $\\alpha$ (supported on [0,8]) such that there exists $\\beta$ (finite support) whose cross-correlation with $\\alpha$ yields exactly two ones at distinct positions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.169472996,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.169474768,"data":"       | We can think of sequences as integer-valued vectors of length maybe infinite but only finite support. The cross-correlation $c_n = \\sum_t \\alpha(t+n) \\beta(t) = 1$ for n=k,l, else 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.169477374,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.169956085,"data":"       | Thus $c_n$ is the convolution of $\\alpha$ reversed? Actually cross-correlation is similar to convolution with reversed sequence: $(\\alpha \\star \\beta)(n) = \\sum_t \\alpha(t) \\beta(t-n)$? Wait typical convolution: $(f * g)(n) = \\sum_t f(t) g(n-t)$. Here we have $\\sum_t \\alpha(t+n) \\beta(t) = \\sum_t \\alpha(t) \\beta(t-n)$ after change of variable $t \\to t-n$? Let's compute: Let $u = t+n$, then $t = u-n$, sum over u: $\\sum_u \\alpha(u) \\beta(u-n)$. So indeed $c_n = \\sum_u \\alpha(u) \\beta(u-n) = (\\alpha * \\tilde{\\beta})(n)$ where $\\tilde{\\beta}(m) = \\beta(-m)$. Actually convolution with reversed index: $(\\alpha * \\beta')(n) = \\sum_u \\alpha(u) \\beta'(n-u)$. So if we define $\\beta'(m) = \\beta(-m)$, then $(\\alpha * \\beta')(n) = \\sum_u \\alpha(u) \\beta'(n-u) = \\sum_u \\alpha(u) \\beta(-(n-u)) = \\sum_u \\alpha(u) \\beta(u-n) = c_n$. So cross-correlation equals convolution with reversed beta.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.169996318,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.169999015,"data":"       | Thus we need to find integer sequences $\\alpha$ (length 9) such that there exists integer sequence $\\beta$ (finite support) with convolution $\\alpha * \\beta'$ equal to indicator of two points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17000128,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.170002867,"data":"       | Thus we can treat this as linear algebra: unknown $\\beta$ (finite support) yields linear equations for each n: sum_{i} \\alpha(i) \\beta(i-n) = c_n where c_n is 1 at k,l else 0. Since $\\alpha$ has support only 0..8, and $\\beta$ is unknown but finite support maybe also limited. The convolution support will be from min support sum to max.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.170004948,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.170006864,"data":"       | Given that $\\alpha$ is zero outside 0..8, we can think of $\\alpha$ as a vector of length 9. Let $\\beta$ have support maybe also within some range (unknown). But we can think of solving for $\\beta$ such that the convolution yields exactly two ones.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.170010085,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.170012758,"data":"       | Observation: Since $\\alpha$ is finite support, the convolution $\\alpha * \\beta'$ will have support that is the sum of supports: if $\\alpha$ supported in [0,8] and $\\beta'$ supported in [a,b], then convolution supported in [0+a, 8+b] = [a, 8+b]. So the output can be nonzero only within that range. For it to be zero outside {k,l}, we need that the support of the convolution is exactly {k,l} (maybe also zeros elsewhere). That means that the output is zero at all other n, but may have zeros inside range as well. So the convolution yields zeros at many positions.\n"}
