,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172260752,"data":"       | Thus we need to know if a is a \"filter\" that can produce such output.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172262319,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172263543,"data":"       | Since a and b are integer sequences, we can treat them as polynomials: define A(x) = sum_{i=0}^8 a_i x^i. Define B(x) = sum_{j} b_j x^j. Then convolution (with reversal) corresponds to product of A(x) and B(x^{-1})? Let's compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172265145,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172266267,"data":"       | Define reversed B: B_rev(x) = sum_j b_j x^{-j}. Then product A(x) B_rev(x) = sum_{i,j} a_i b_j x^{i-j}. The coefficient of x^n is sum_{i-j=n} a_i b_j = sum_i a_i b_{i-n} = c_n. So indeed c_n is coefficient of x^n in A(x) B_rev(x). So we need A(x) B_rev(x) = x^k + x^l (since coefficient 1 at n=k,l). So polynomial equation:\n"}
