,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172274916,"data":"       | Thus we need to find integer polynomials A(x) with degree ≤8 (since a_i zero for i\u003c0, i\u003e8) and integer coefficients a_i, such that there exists integer polynomial B_rev(x) (finite support) and distinct integers k,l with product equal to x^k + x^l.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172279488,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172280801,"data":"       | Thus A(x) must divide x^k + x^l in the ring of Laurent polynomials with integer coefficients? Because B_rev(x) can be any Laurent polynomial (since b_j can be for negative j as well). Indeed B_rev(x) = sum_j b_j x^{-j} is a Laurent polynomial (finite sum of integer powers, possibly negative). So the condition is: there exists integer Laurent polynomial B_rev(x) such that A(x) B_rev(x) = x^k + x^l.\n"}
