,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172292166,"data":"       | Thus A(x) must be a factor (over Z) of x^{d} + 1 multiplied possibly by a monomial factor x^l (which is invertible in Laurent ring). Since A(x) has only nonnegative powers, we can factor out a monomial x^l from the product; that monomial can be absorbed into B_rev(x). Indeed if we have A(x) B_rev(x) = x^l (x^{d} + 1). Then we can write A(x) (x^{-l} B_rev(x)) = x^{d} + 1. Since x^{-l} B_rev(x) is also a Laurent polynomial (shifts powers). So effectively we can ignore monomial factor; we need A(x) to divide x^{d} + 1 in the Laurent ring, i.e., there exists integer Laurent polynomial C(x) such that A(x) C(x) = x^{d} + 1.\n"}
