,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172300021,"data":"       | But note that dividing in the Laurent ring means we can also have negative powers in C(x). However A(x) must be a factor of x^{d} + 1 in the polynomial ring Z[x] after possibly multiplying by a monomial. Actually if A(x) has no negative exponents, we can consider gcd with x^{d}+1. Since x^{d}+1 is a polynomial with integer coefficients, any factor A(x) must be a divisor in Z[x] (up to a unit ±1). However, there is also the possibility that A(x) includes a factor x^m (i.e., monomial) that divides x^{d}+1? But x^{d}+1 has no factor x (since constant term 1). So A(x) cannot have a factor x unless the monomial factor is canceled by the C(x) having negative exponents. Wait: Suppose A(x) = x^m * P(x) where P(0) ≠ 0. Then A(x) divides x^{d}+1 in Laurent ring if there exists C(x) such that x^m P(x) C(x) = x^{d}+1. Then C(x) must have factor x^{-m} times something. Since C(x) can have negative exponents, that's allowed. So A(x) can have a factor x^m (i.e., zero coefficients for low indices) because we can compensate by negative powers in C(x). But A(x) must have support within [0,8]; it can have zeros at low indices, meaning a_0 = 0, etc. That's okay.\n"}
