,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172775277,"data":"       | But we need to verify that each such α indeed satisfies the original condition: there exists β and k,l such that S_n(α)⋆β = 1 for n=k,l else 0. Since we constructed α as x^m * P where P divides x^d+1 (for some d) as product of cyclotomic polynomials, there exists β (Laurent polynomial) such that product equals x^d + 1. Indeed we can set β = x^{-m} * (x^d + 1) / P(x). Since P divides x^d+1, the quotient is integer polynomial (maybe with monomial factor). Then β is integer Laurent polynomial; we can shift to get β in ℱ (finite support). Indeed quotient will be a polynomial (since division yields polynomial). Multiply by x^{-m} yields Laurent polynomial with possibly negative powers, but we can shift to ensure finite support? Actually β must be a function ℤ→ℤ with finite support; that is, a Laurent polynomial (finite sum of integer powers) is allowed, because we can have negative powers as well (since domain ℤ includes negative indices). So β can be any Laurent polynomial with integer coefficients and finite support, which is allowed. So β exists.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172779366,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172780613,"data":"       | Now we also need to ensure that k,l are distinct. Since x^d + 1 = x^l (x^{d-l} + 1) yields two nonzero terms only if d-l = 0? Wait x^d + 1 has exactly two nonzero coefficients (1 at exponent d and 1 at exponent 0). So we can set k = d, l = 0 (or any shift). Actually the product A(x) B_rev(x) = x^k + x^l. So we can set k = d + shift? Let's see: we have A(x) B_rev(x) = x^d + 1. If we multiply both sides by x^{c} maybe? But we can just set k = d and l = 0. Indeed the product yields two terms at exponents d and 0. So k and l are distinct if d != 0. Since d \u003e 0 (since we need two distinct points), we need d\u003e0. The case d = 0 would be x^0 + 1 = 2, not of the form x^k + x^l with k≠l. So we need d\u003e0. In our construction, d is the exponent in x^d + 1. So we need to ensure d \u003e 0. Since we can choose any d that works (the lcm of t's). For the empty subset (P=1), we have A(x) = x^m. Then we need β such that x^m * β = x^d + 1. So β = x^{d-m} + x^{-m}. For β to be integer Laurent polynomial with finite support, we need d-m ≥ 0 (so exponent nonnegative) and -m ≤ 0 (so exponent negative). That's fine. We can pick any d \u003e m to get distinct exponents. So for monomial case we can choose d \u003e m, giving two distinct exponents.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172784366,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172785491,"data":"       | Thus each α we counted yields at least one solution with k,l distinct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172786776,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172787909,"data":"       | Thus answer 133 seems plausible.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172789064,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172790474,"data":"       | But need to double-check that we didn't double count any α that can be represented by different subsets with different shifts. As argued, representation as x^m * P with P having constant term 1 is unique: factor out the maximal power of x dividing A(x). That yields m = smallest index with nonzero coefficient. Then P = x^{-m} A(x). Since P has constant term nonzero, it's unique. So each α corresponds to a unique (m,P). So no double counting.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172792488,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172793615,"data":"       | Now we need to verify that for each such P, indeed there exists integer d such that P divides x^d + 1. Since P is product of distinct Φ_{2t} where t are arbitrary, we can take d = LCM of the t's in the subset. Then x^d + 1 = product_{t|d} Φ_{2t} (since d's divisors). Since the subset S is a subset of the divisors of d, P divides x^d + 1. So yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172808713,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172810585,"data":"       | Thus each subset yields a valid α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172812691,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172814517,"data":"       | Now we need to ensure that we didn't miss any α that are not of this form. Could there be α that are not product of distinct Φ_{2t} times monomial, but still divide some x^d + 1? Since x^d + 1 is squarefree (no repeated cyclotomic factors), any divisor must be product of distinct cyclotomic polynomials. Indeed x^d + 1 factorization into cyclotomic polynomials is product of distinct Φ_{2t} for t|d. So any divisor is product of a subset of those distinct factors. So indeed any divisor of x^d + 1 is of that form. So our enumeration is exhaustive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17281887,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172820055,"data":"       | Thus answer 133.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.1728212,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172822336,"data":"       | But we need to double-check that we counted all subsets with degree ≤8, but also need to consider the case where P could be 1 (empty product). That's included. So total includes monomials.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172823871,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172825002,"data":"       | Now we need to verify with brute force search for all α with support in [0,8] and integer coefficients of limited magnitude maybe up to some bound, but we can't search infinite. However we can search for all α that satisfy the condition by enumerating possible β and k,l within some bounds? That might be huge.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172826773,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.172828315,"data":"       | But we can verify our count by constructing α for each subset and shift, and verifying that there exists β and k,l. Let's implement to generate α polynomials and compute β as quotient.\n"}
