,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173552038,"data":"       | analysisSo tset = [1,2]; lcm = 2. So d=2. Then x^d + 1 = x^2 + 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173555443,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173556715,"data":"       | But does P divide x^2 + 1? No, degree of P is 3 \u003e 2, so not dividing. Indeed our earlier assumption that d = lcm of t's ensures each Φ_{2t} divides x^d + 1, but product may have degree larger than d, thus cannot divide x^d + 1. Wait: For t=1, Φ_2 = x+1 divides x^2 + 1? Let's check: x^2 + 1 divided by x+1 yields remainder? (x^2+1) / (x+1) = x - 1 + 2/(x+1). Not divisible. So my earlier claim that Φ_{2t} divides x^d + 1 for d multiple of t is false for t=1? Let's examine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173558863,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173560294,"data":"       | Take d=2, t=1 =\u003e Φ_{2} = x+1. Does x^2+1 have factor x+1? x^2+1 evaluated at x=-1 gives 2, not zero. So indeed not a factor. Wait condition for Φ_{2t} dividing x^d + 1 is: t|d and (d/t) is odd? Let's derive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173561903,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173563161,"data":"       | Recall: x^d + 1 = ∏_{t|d, (d/t) odd} Φ_{2t}(x). Indeed factorization: x^{2n} - 1 = ∏_{d|2n} Φ_d(x). For x^n + 1, we have x^{2n} - 1 = (x^n - 1)(x^n + 1). So x^n + 1 = ∏_{d|2n, d∤ n} Φ_d(x). So the divisors d of 2n that are not divisors of n. So for n = d (our earlier variable), the factors are Φ_{2t} where t|d and (d/t) is odd. Because 2t divides 2d but not d, which occurs when t|d and (2t)∤ d, i.e., t|d but 2t does not divide d, meaning d/t is odd. So condition is t|d and (d/t) odd.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173565375,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17356658,"data":"       | Thus for t=1, we need d/1 odd =\u003e d odd. So Φ_2 divides x^d + 1 only when d is odd. For d=2 (even), Φ_2 does not divide. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173568042,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173569188,"data":"       | Thus our earlier assumption of any t dividing d is insufficient; need odd quotient.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173570457,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17357161,"data":"       | Thus to ensure product of Φ_{2t} divides x^d + 1, we need to find d such that for each t in S, t|d and (d/t) is odd. That is, d must be a common multiple of t's where each quotient is odd. Equivalent to: d must be a multiple of each t, and for each t, the factor of 2 in d must be exactly the same as in t? Actually we need d/t odd, i.e., d has no extra factor of 2 beyond those in t. So d must be of the form d = t * odd number, but for all t in S, the odd number must be same? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173573638,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173574786,"data":"       | If we have multiple t's, we need a d that works for all: for each t, d/t odd. This means that the 2-adic valuation v2(d) must be equal to v2(t) for each t. Since if t has v2(t) = a, then d must have same v2 as t because otherwise d/t would be even. So all t in S must have same power of 2. So t's must have same v2. Then we can choose d = LCM of the odd parts times the common 2^a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173576587,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173577706,"data":"       | Thus the condition for existence of d is: the set S must have all t with same 2-adic valuation. Then we can take d = LCM of t's (which will have that same 2-adic exponent) and ensures each quotient is odd.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173579237,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173580405,"data":"       | Thus our enumeration must restrict to subsets S where all t have same v2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.173582048,"data":"       | \n"}
