,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174045245,"data":"       | all_good3 = all(verify_alpha_general(alpha) for alpha in alphas2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174046558,"data":"       | all_good3\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17404769,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174048811,"data":"     [Code]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174051596,"data":"       | def verify_alpha_general(alpha_poly):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174052873,"data":"       |     # Determine m and P\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174054015,"data":"       |     monoms = alpha_poly.as_dict()\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174055262,"data":"       |     m = min(exp[0] for exp in monoms)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174056438,"data":"       |     P_expr = sp.expand(alpha_poly.as_expr() / x**m)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174057867,"data":"       |     # Determine tset via factorization\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174059419,"data":"       |     tset = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174060573,"data":"       |     for t in t_list:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174061863,"data":"       |         phi_poly = sp.cyclotomic_poly(2*t, x, polys=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174063108,"data":"       |         if sp.rem(sp.Poly(P_expr, x, domain=sp.ZZ), phi_poly) == 0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174064344,"data":"       |             tset.append(t)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174065509,"data":"       |     # Determine d\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174066678,"data":"       |     if tset:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174067857,"data":"       |         # Need to find d such that each t|d and d/t odd. Since all t have same v2, we can take d = lcm(tset)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174069188,"data":"       |         # But ensure d/t odd for each t; if not, multiply by appropriate odd factor.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174070446,"data":"       |         d_candidate = lcm_list(tset)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174071618,"data":"       |         # Check condition\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17407279,"data":"       |         ok = all((d_candidate // t) % 2 == 1 for t in tset)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174074024,"data":"       |         if not ok:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174075219,"data":"       |             # multiply by odd factor (product of odd parts) to make each quotient odd? Actually if v2 same, lcm will have same v2, so quotient will be odd. So should be ok.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174076719,"data":"       |             # But just in case, we can multiply by product of odd parts of each t? Let's just compute d = lcm of odd parts * 2^v2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174078116,"data":"       |             # Compute v2 common\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17407929,"data":"       |             v = v2(tset[0])\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174080446,"data":"       |             odd_parts = [t // (2**v) for t in tset]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174081646,"data":"       |             d_candidate = (2**v) * sp.ilcm(*odd_parts)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174083297,"data":"       |         d = d_candidate\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17408445,"data":"       |     else:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17408557,"data":"       |         d = m + 1  # any \u003e m\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174086741,"data":"       |     # Compute Q = (x^d + 1) // P\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17408795,"data":"       |     quotient, remainder = sp.div(x**d + 1, P_expr, domain=sp.ZZ)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174089217,"data":"       |     if remainder != 0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174090351,"data":"       |         # division fails\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17409168,"data":"       |         return False\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17409473,"data":"       |     Q_expr = sp.expand(quotient)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174096065,"data":"       |     # Compute convolution check\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174097702,"data":"       |     a = {exp[0]: coeff for exp, coeff in monoms.items()}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174099073,"data":"       |     Q_poly = sp.Poly(Q_expr, x, domain=sp.ZZ)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174100533,"data":"       |     q_coeff = {exp[0]: coeff for exp, coeff in Q_poly.as_dict().items()}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174101948,"data":"       |     max_q_exp = max(q_coeff.keys()) if q_coeff else -1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174103166,"data":"       |     def b(k):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174104325,"data":"       |         exp = m - k\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174105467,"data":"       |         if exp \u003c 0 or exp \u003e max_q_exp:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174106693,"data":"       |             return 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174107818,"data":"       |         return q_coeff.get(exp, 0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174109428,"data":"       |     for n in range(-5, d+6):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174110921,"data":"       |         s = sum(a_i * b(i - n) for i, a_i in a.items())\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17411219,"data":"       |         expected = 1 if n in (0, d) else 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174113378,"data":"       |         if s != expected:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174114529,"data":"       |             return False\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174115718,"data":"       |     return True\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174116884,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174117992,"data":"       | all_good3 = all(verify_alpha_general(alpha) for alpha in alphas2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174119254,"data":"       | all_good3\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174120438,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174121564,"data":"     [Output]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174122688,"data":"       \u003e True\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174123909,"data":"       \u003e \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174125177,"data":"\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174126264,"data":"     [Turn 30]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174127359,"data":"     [Reasoning] (9568 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174128534,"data":"       | analysisGreat! So all alphas generated from subsets2 satisfy condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174129848,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174130941,"data":"       | Thus the number of shifty functions is 80.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174132124,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174133261,"data":"       | But we must verify that there are no other shifty functions beyond those we enumerated. That is, any α that satisfies condition must be of the form x^m * product of distinct Φ_{2t} where t's have same v2 and total degree ≤8. Let's argue.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174134897,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17413606,"data":"       | We derived that condition implies A(x) B_rev(x) = x^k + x^l (two-term polynomial). Up to monomial factor, we can assume k \u003e l, then x^l (x^{k-l} + 1). Multiply both sides by x^{-l} to get A'(x) B_rev'(x) = x^{d} + 1 where d = k - l \u003e 0. So we can assume product equals x^d + 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174139793,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174140994,"data":"       | Thus A'(x) must be a divisor of x^d + 1 in the Laurent ring. Since x^d + 1 is a polynomial with constant term 1, any divisor in Laurent ring can be expressed as monomial times a divisor in polynomial ring (since we can factor out powers of x). So A'(x) = x^m P(x) where P(x) divides x^d + 1 and has nonzero constant term.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174142779,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174144039,"data":"       | Now factorization of x^d + 1 over integers is product of distinct cyclotomic polynomials Φ_{2t} where t|d and (d/t) odd. So any divisor P(x) must be product of a subset of those Φ_{2t}. Moreover, each Φ_{2t} has degree φ(2t). So degree of P is sum of φ(2t) over chosen t.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174146002,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174147127,"data":"       | Thus any shifty α corresponds to some d and subset S of divisors t of d with odd quotient, such that degree ≤8. Also we can multiply by monomial x^m (shift) to get α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174148657,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17415013,"data":"       | Now we need to count distinct α with support in [0,8]. Since α = x^m P(x) where P is product of Φ_{2t} with t dividing d and (d/t) odd, and degree(P) ≤ 8, and m such that m + degree(P) ≤ 8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174151753,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174152895,"data":"       | Now, for each subset S of t's with same v2 (since they must be divisors of the same d with odd quotient condition), there exists a d that works: we can take d = LCM of t's (ensuring each quotient is odd because v2 same). Indeed for each t in S, v2(t) = a constant a. Then LCM of t's will have v2 = a, and odd part = LCM of odd parts. Then for each t, d/t = (odd part LCM)/odd_part(t) which is odd. So condition satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17415491,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174156013,"data":"       | Thus any subset S of t's with same v2 yields a valid d. Conversely, any subset S that appears as divisors of some d with odd quotient must have same v2, because if t1 and t2 have different v2, then any d divisible by both will have v2 = max(v2(t1), v2(t2)), making quotient for the one with smaller v2 even, violating condition. So indeed same v2 is necessary.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174157838,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174158943,"data":"       | Thus the condition for subsets S is exactly: all t in S have same v2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174160219,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174161326,"data":"       | Thus our enumeration of subsets2 (same v2) is correct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174162544,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174163664,"data":"       | Now we must also consider the possibility that P includes a factor Φ_{2t} with degree \u003e8 but combined with monomial shift could reduce degree? No, degree of product is sum of degrees independent of shift. So degree(P) must be ≤8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174165346,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17416646,"data":"       | Thus enumeration of subsets S with sum of φ(2t) ≤ 8 and same v2 gives all possible P.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174167808,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174168906,"data":"       | Now we must also consider the possibility that P could be the constant polynomial 1 (empty subset). That's included; degree 0, v2 not defined but we treat as allowed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174171998,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174173499,"data":"       | Thus total number of α is sum_{S} (9 - deg(P)).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174174775,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174175922,"data":"       | We computed that sum as 80.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174177111,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174178469,"data":"       | Now we need to ensure that α must satisfy also the condition that α(m) = 0 for m\u003c0 and m\u003e8. Our construction ensures that because degree ≤8 and shift m ensures support within [0,8]. Since m≥0 and degree ≤8, the support is within [0,8] indeed. Also we must ensure that α(m) = 0 for m\u003c0, which holds because we have no negative exponents.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174180506,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17418161,"data":"       | Thus all α counted are shifty.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174182837,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17418397,"data":"       | Now we need to verify that there are no other shifty α beyond this enumeration. Our reasoning suggests that any shifty α must be of this form. Let's attempt to brute force search for all α with small coefficient range to see if any extra appear beyond our count. Since coefficients can be large, but maybe there are infinite families? Let's try to generate all α of degree ≤8 with coefficients bounded say -5..5 and test existence of β and k,l via solving linear equations. This may find some that are not captured but maybe they require larger coefficients.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174186229,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.1741875,"data":"       | But we can also test by solving for β and k,l using linear algebra. For each α, we need to find β and k,l such that convolution yields two ones. This is a linear system with unknown β_j (finite support) and unknown k,l. Since k,l can be any integers, but we can restrict to range maybe -10..10 for search because α has support within [0,8], the convolution will be nonzero only within some range. The nonzero positions must be exactly two points. So we can search for k,l within [-10, 10] maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174189707,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174191115,"data":"       | But there could be solutions with large k,l far away, but the shape of convolution is determined by α and β. If β has support far away, the nonzero positions could be far. But we can shift β accordingly. However, we can always shift the whole configuration: if we have a solution (α, β, k,l), we can shift both α and β? Actually α is fixed; we can shift β to produce different k,l? Since condition is S_n(α) ⋆ β = 1 at n = k,l. Shifting β by some amount will shift the positions of ones. So for any α that works, there exist infinitely many (k,l) by shifting β? Let's examine: If β works for k,l, then define β' = S_t(β). Then S_n(α) ⋆ β' = S_{n-t}(α) ⋆ β? Actually compute: S_n(α) ⋆ β' = sum_t α(t+n) β'(t) = sum_t α(t+n) β(t - t0) where t0 shift. That's equal to (S_{n+t0}(α) ⋆ β). Not same. So shifting β changes positions but also changes the pattern relative to α. Might not preserve exactly two ones.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174194546,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174195685,"data":"       | Nevertheless, existence of any k,l is enough.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174196894,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174198013,"data":"       | Thus we can search for α with small coefficients and see if any not in our list appear.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174200944,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17420212,"data":"       | Let's implement brute force enumeration for α with coefficients in small range, maybe -2..2, and check if there exists β (with support maybe limited to some range) and k,l such that condition holds. Since β can have many possible supports, we need to solve linear equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174203844,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174205002,"data":"       | Observation: The condition is linear in β. For a given α and given k,l, we need to find β such that c_n = sum_i a_i b_{i-n} = 1 for n=k,l, else 0. This is a linear system for unknown b_j.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174206553,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174208031,"data":"       | Let the support of β be unknown but we can restrict to range where b_j may be needed. Since a_i are zero outside 0..8, the equation for each n uses b_{i-n} for i=0..8. So for each n, we involve b_{j} for j = i-n, i from 0..8 =\u003e j ranges from -n to 8-n. So for n in [k,l] maybe large, we need b_j for large negative indices. But we can treat β as having infinite support but only finitely many nonzero entries. The linear system may have infinite unknowns but we can restrict to those j that appear in any equation for n in some range. Since we only need to satisfy equations for n where c_n = 0 as well, which are infinite, but for n outside the range where any b_j appears, the equation is automatically satisfied (since sum is empty). So we only need to consider n in range where there is at least one overlapping index between α and β, i.e., where the intervals [0,8] shifted by n intersect support of β. If β has support in some interval [p,q], then the convolution is nonzero only for n such that intervals [0,8] and [p+n, q+n] intersect? Actually c_n = sum_i a_i b_{i-n}. For given n, b_{i-n} is nonzero only if i-n in support of β, i.e., n in i - support(β). So the set of n where c_n may be nonzero is the Minkowski sum of support(α) and support(β) (with sign). More precisely, support of c is {i - j : i∈supp(α), j∈supp(β)}. That's finite.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174211921,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174213064,"data":"       | Thus we can restrict to n in that set. So we can treat unknown β as unknown coefficients for indices j in some finite set; we can choose the set as unknown but we can bound size.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174214544,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174215669,"data":"       | Thus we can solve linear equations: For each n in some finite set (the possible support), we have equation sum_i a_i b_{i-n} = c_n (0 or 1). This is linear constraints on b_j. The number of unknowns is size of support of β (unknown). But we can treat unknowns as all b_j for j in some range covering all needed indices, e.g., j from -M to M for some bound.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174217508,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174218623,"data":"       | We can attempt to solve for β given α and candidate k,l by constructing linear system for unknown b_j for j in range [min_j, max_j] where we need to cover all indices i-n for i=0..8 and n in {k,l} and also for n where we need zero constraints (maybe many). But we can restrict to n within [k-8, l+8] maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174220383,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174221492,"data":"       | But easier: Since we have theoretical classification, we can trust 80. However, we need to double-check that there are no other possibilities where P includes repeated cyclotomic factor? Not possible because x^d + 1 is squarefree (since derivative  d x^{d-1} is not zero mod any factor). Indeed x^d + 1 has distinct roots (primitive 2d-th roots of unity) none repeated. So any divisor is product of distinct cyclotomic polynomials.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174225317,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174226458,"data":"       | Thus classification is complete.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174227642,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174228748,"data":"       | Now we need to compute the count 80.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174229948,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17423106,"data":"       | But we need to ensure that we counted all subsets S of t's with same v2 and degree ≤8. Let's recompute manually to double-check.\n"}
