,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174650523,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse the problem carefully. We have set of finitely supported integer-valued functions on Z (i.e., only finitely many non-zero entries). Define inner product (dot product) α⋆β = Σ_n α(n) β(n). This is a finite sum because each function has finite support.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174652316,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174653432,"data":"       | Define shift operator S_n(α)(t) = α(t+n). So shift left by n? Actually S_n shifts the argument: (S_n α)(t) = α(t+n). So if α is supported on some interval, S_n moves it.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174655007,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174656129,"data":"       | Definition: α is called shifty if α(m)=0 for all m\u003c0 and m\u003e8 (i.e., support within [0,8]) and there exists β∈F and integers k≠l such that for all n∈Z, (S_n α)⋆β = 1 if n∈{k,l} and 0 otherwise. So the inner product of shifted α with β is 1 exactly at two shift positions k,l, zero elsewhere.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174658137,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174659269,"data":"       | Interpretation: α is a vector of length 9 (positions 0..8) with integer entries (maybe zero). β is any finitely supported integer vector (maybe infinite length but finite support). The condition says the convolution (cross-correlation) of α with β yields a function that is zero everywhere except at two points where it's 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.1746611,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174662208,"data":"       | Specifically, define f(n) = (S_n α)⋆β = Σ_t α(t+n) β(t). Since α(t+n) is nonzero only when t+n in [0,8], i.e., t ∈ [-n, 8-n]. So the sum is over t ∈ Z, but effectively finite.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174663839,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174664989,"data":"       | This is like discrete convolution: (α reversed?) Actually (S_n α)⋆β = Σ_t α(t+n) β(t) = Σ_t α(t) β(t-n) after change variable? Let's compute: Let u = t+n =\u003e t = u-n. Then Σ_t α(t+n) β(t) = Σ_u α(u) β(u-n). So f(n) = Σ_u α(u) β(u-n). That's the convolution of α with reversed β? Usually convolution (α * β)(n) = Σ_u α(u) β(n-u). Here we have β(u-n) = β(-(n-u)). So it's like cross-correlation: (α ⋆ β)(n) = Σ_u α(u) β(u-n). Indeed cross-correlation of α and β at lag n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174669388,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174670873,"data":"       | Thus condition: cross-correlation of α and β equals a function that is 1 at exactly two distinct lags k,l, zero elsewhere.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174672315,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174673439,"data":"       | We need to count number of α (with support in [0,8]) for which there exists some β (any finite support) and distinct k,l such that cross-correlation yields exactly two ones.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17467495,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174676058,"data":"       | Essentially we need to find all integer vectors α of length 9 such that there exists integer vector β (maybe infinite length but finite support) whose cross-correlation with α yields a vector that is 0 everywhere except two positions where it is 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174677726,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174678868,"data":"       | This is reminiscent of constructing β as a linear combination of shifted α's to produce a Kronecker delta at two positions? Actually cross-correlation is linear in β: f(n) = Σ_u α(u) β(u-n). For each n, f(n) is linear combination of β values weighted by α. This is like convolution of α with reversed β. So we can think of unknown β as variable; we need to solve linear equations: For each integer n, Σ_u α(u) β(u-n) = f(n) where f(n) is 1 at n=k,l and 0 otherwise.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174681113,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174682215,"data":"       | Since α has support only 0..8, the equation for each n involves β values at indices u-n where u ranges 0..8. So β indices that appear are of the form u-n with u in [0,8] =\u003e β indices in [ -n, 8-n ]. So for each n we have linear combination of β at a contiguous block of length 9.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174684043,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174685381,"data":"       | We can think of β as unknown integer vector, and we have infinite linear equations (but only finitely many nontrivial because α has finite support; for n far away from support of β, the sum may be zero). But we just need existence of integer solution β.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174687086,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174688307,"data":"       | Essentially we need to know for which α there exists β such that cross-correlation yields a vector with exactly two 1's.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174689744,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17469114,"data":"       | Observation: Cross-correlation of α with β is essentially the convolution of α with β reversed: define β_rev(t) = β(-t). Then (α ⋆ β)(n) = Σ_u α(u) β(u-n) = Σ_u α(u) β_rev(n-u). That's convolution (α * β_rev)(n). So we need α * γ = f where γ = β_rev. So we need to find γ (finite support integer) such that α * γ = f, where f is 1 at k,l and 0 elsewhere.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174693368,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174694484,"data":"       | Thus α is a divisor (in convolution algebra) of a function f that is two-point indicator. Since α has support length 9, but may have zeros. The existence of integer γ (finite support) such that convolution yields f.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174697777,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174698932,"data":"       | Thus α must be a divisor of a 0-1 function with exactly two ones.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174700231,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174701404,"data":"       | We can think of polynomial representation: Represent α as polynomial A(x) = Σ_{i=0}^8 a_i x^i (since support only 0..8). Represent γ as polynomial G(x) = Σ_{j} g_j x^j (finite). Convolution corresponds to product of polynomials: (A(x) * G(x)) = Σ_n f_n x^n where f_n are coefficients of f. Since f is 1 at positions k,l, we have F(x) = x^k + x^l. So we need A(x) G(x) = x^k + x^l. Over integers (coeffs integers). So we need to factor x^k + x^l as product of integer-coefficient polynomial A(x) with degree up to 8 (since support up to 8) and G(x) with integer coefficients (any degree). Also A(x) must have coefficients a_i = α(i) (i from 0..8). Also α(i)=0 for i\u003c0 or \u003e8; we already restrict.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174704029,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174705166,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to: Count integer polynomials A(x) of degree at most 8 (allow leading coefficient zero) such that there exist integers k≠l and integer polynomial G(x) with A(x) G(x) = x^k + x^l.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174706718,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174707811,"data":"       | Note: A(x) may have degree less than 8, but its support is within [0,8]; we also require that α(m)=0 for m\u003c0 and m\u003e8, but we don't require that α(0) or α(8) be nonzero. So A(x) can be any integer polynomial of degree ≤8, possibly with gaps.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174709573,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174711014,"data":"       | Also note that G(x) must be a polynomial (finite support) with integer coefficients; it can have negative exponents? Since β is defined on Z, not just nonnegative indices. In polynomial representation, we usually consider Laurent polynomials (allow negative exponents) because β can have support on negative indices. However, we defined γ(t) = β_rev(t) = β(-t). Since β can have any integer indices, γ can have any integer indices as well. So G(x) can be a Laurent polynomial (allow negative exponents). But we can shift: Since A(x) only has nonnegative exponents (0..8), product with G(x) yields F(x) = x^k + x^l (nonnegative exponents). So G(x) must be of the form x^{some shift} times a polynomial with nonnegative exponents? Actually we can allow negative exponents in G(x), but the product must have only nonnegative exponents k,l. Since A(x) has nonnegative exponents, any negative exponent in G(x) would produce negative exponent in product (if A has constant term nonzero). But product must have only nonnegative exponents; we can ensure G(x) has no negative exponents less than -min exponent of A with nonzero coefficient? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174714277,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174715439,"data":"       | Let A(x) = Σ_{i=0}^8 a_i x^i. Suppose G(x) = Σ_{j=m}^{M} g_j x^j where m and M are integers possibly negative. Then product: Σ_{i} Σ_{j} a_i g_j x^{i+j}. Since a_i nonzero only for i∈[0,8]. So exponents i+j range from m+0 to M+8. To have product only at exponents k,l (both nonnegative), we need m+0 ≥ 0? Actually we need no negative exponents in product. So min exponent = m+0 (if a_0 ≠0). If a_0 =0, maybe min exponent could be larger. However, to avoid negative exponents, we need that for any i with a_i ≠0, i+m ≥ 0. So m ≥ -i for all i where a_i ≠0, i.e., m ≥ -min{i | a_i ≠0}. Since i≥0, min i is at least 0; if a_0 ≠0 then m≥0; if a_0=0 but a_1 ≠0 then m ≥ -1 etc. So m can be negative but limited by smallest index of nonzero coefficient.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174720066,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17472144,"data":"       | But we can always shift G(x) by a factor x^t to make m≥0, by absorbing into k,l? Actually we can multiply both sides by x^{-m} to shift. Let's think: Since we can choose k,l arbitrarily, we can always shift G(x) to be a polynomial with nonnegative exponents by adjusting k,l? Wait: Equation A(x) G(x) = x^k + x^l. If we multiply both sides by x^{-m} where m is minimal exponent of G, we get A(x) (x^{-m} G(x)) = x^{k-m} + x^{l-m}. Let G'(x) = x^{-m} G(x) be polynomial with nonnegative exponents. Then we have A(x) G'(x) = x^{k'} + x^{l'} where k' = k - m, l' = l - m. Since k,l are integers, k',l' are integers. So we can always assume G(x) is a polynomial (nonnegative exponents) by adjusting k,l accordingly. Indeed, we can shift G(x) to be a polynomial with nonnegative exponents, and correspondingly shift k,l. Since k,l are not fixed a priori, we can choose them accordingly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174724238,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174725364,"data":"       | Thus we can restrict to G(x) being a polynomial with nonnegative integer exponents (i.e., finite support on nonnegative indices). Then the product A(x) G(x) yields polynomial with nonnegative exponents (makes sense). So we need to factor x^k + x^l as product of two integer polynomials, where one factor A(x) has degree ≤8 and support in [0,8] (i.e., exponents 0..8). The other factor G(x) can be any integer polynomial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174727337,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174728461,"data":"       | But note: A(x) may have zero constant term; that's okay. However, we also have condition that α(m)=0 for m\u003c0 and m\u003e8, which we already satisfied. There's no condition that α is nonzero somewhere? Possibly α could be zero function? Let's check: If α is zero function, then (S_n α)⋆β = 0 for all n regardless of β, cannot produce ones. So α cannot be zero. So we need nonzero A(x).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174730593,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174731731,"data":"       | Thus we need to count number of integer polynomials A(x) with degree ≤8 (allow gaps) such that there exist integers k≠l and integer polynomial G(x) with A(x) G(x) = x^k + x^l.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174733299,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174734433,"data":"       | Essentially we need to count the number of integer divisors of binomials x^k + x^l where the divisor's exponents are within [0,8].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174735801,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174736903,"data":"       | But note that k,l are not bounded; they can be any integers (distinct). However, product A(x) G(x) = x^k + x^l = x^k (1 + x^{l-k}) if we assume k\u003cl. So up to a factor x^k, the polynomial is x^k (1 + x^d) where d = l - k \u003e0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174738513,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174739953,"data":"       | Thus we need A(x) G(x) = x^k (1 + x^d). Since A(x) has nonnegative exponents only up to 8, we can factor out any power of x from A(x) (i.e., its minimal exponent). Let’s denote A(x) = x^r A0(x) where A0 has constant term nonzero (i.e., a_r ≠0 and a_i =0 for i\u003cr). Then r = min{i | a_i ≠0} (i.e., shift). Since α(m)=0 for m\u003c0, r≥0. So A(x) = x^r A0(x) with A0(0) ≠0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174741988,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174743115,"data":"       | Similarly, we can factor x^s from G(x). Then product = x^{r+s} A0(x) G0(x) = x^k (1 + x^d). So we can compare exponents: r+s = k. So we can absorb the factor x^k into shift of product.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174746842,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174748026,"data":"       | Thus we can assume without loss of generality that A0(x) G0(x) = 1 + x^d, where A0(0) ≠0, G0(0) ≠0 (since they have constant term nonzero). This is because we can factor out the x^k factor from both sides.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174749607,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174750721,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to: Find integer polynomials A0(x) with support in [0,8] (i.e., degree ≤8) and with constant term nonzero (since a_r ≠0 and we factor out x^r). Actually A0 may have degree up to 8 - r. But after factoring out x^r, the remaining exponents are within [0,8-r]. However, we can consider A0 as any integer polynomial with nonzero constant term and degree ≤8 (since r can be zero). But we also need that the product A0(x) G0(x) = 1 + x^d for some integer d\u003e0, with G0 integer polynomial. So A0 must be a divisor of 1 + x^d in Z[x].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17475299,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174754119,"data":"       | Thus the count of shifty α corresponds to number of integer divisors A(x) of some cyclotomic-like polynomial 1 + x^d (where d\u003e0) such that deg(A) ≤8 and A has no negative exponents (i.e., it's a polynomial) and its support is within [0,8] (i.e., degree ≤8). Also we must consider the possibility of factoring out x^r shift: the original α may have some leading zeros (i.e., r\u003e0). But factoring out x^r yields A0 with constant term nonzero. So the original α is x^r A0 where A0 is a divisor of 1 + x^d. So the support of α is within [r, r+deg(A0)] ≤ [0,8]; i.e., r+deg(A0) ≤8, r≥0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174756597,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174757723,"data":"       | Thus we need to count all pairs (r, A0) such that r≥0 integer, A0 ∈ Z[x] with constant term nonzero, deg(A0) ≤ 8 - r, and there exists integer d\u003e0 such that A0 divides 1 + x^d in Z[x].\n"}
