,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174764256,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to counting all integer polynomials A(x) (not necessarily monic) with degree ≤8, support in [0,8], nonzero, such that A(x) divides 1 + x^d for some d≥1. Since we can always shift factor x^r, we don't need to separate r; it's already accounted by A(x) itself dividing x^k (1 + x^d) for some k. But we can consider the general condition: there exist integers k,l (distinct) such that A(x) divides x^k + x^l = x^k (1 + x^{l-k}) = x^k (1 + x^d). Since A(x) has no negative exponents, we can factor out any power of x from A(x) to match x^k factor. So the condition reduces to: there exists integer d\u003e0 such that A(x) divides 1 + x^d in Z[x] (up to multiplication by a power of x). Indeed, if A(x) = x^r A0(x) with A0(0) ≠0, then A(x) divides x^{r} (1 + x^d) if and only if A0(x) divides 1 + x^d. Since we can adjust k = r + s, where s is exponent of x factor in G(x). So condition reduces to A0 dividing 1 + x^d.\n"}
