,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174793642,"data":"       | Now we need to understand integer divisors of 1 + x^d. Over Z[x], factorization of 1 + x^d is known: 1 + x^d = ∏_{e|2d, e∤ d} Φ_e(x), where Φ_e are cyclotomic polynomials. Indeed, x^n - 1 = ∏_{d|n} Φ_d(x). Similarly, x^n + 1 = ∏_{d|2n, d∤ n} Φ_d(x). So 1 + x^d = ∏_{e|2d, e∤ d} Φ_e(x). So its irreducible factors over Z are cyclotomic polynomials of order e where e divides 2d but not d. Those are the cyclotomic polynomials for odd e dividing 2d? Actually e must be of the form 2m where m|d and m odd? Let's recall: For d odd, x^d + 1 = Φ_{2d}(x). For d even, x^d + 1 = Φ_{2d}(x) * Φ_{2d/2? Wait: Let's compute: x^n + 1 = (x^{2n} - 1)/(x^n - 1) = ∏_{d|2n} Φ_d(x) / ∏_{d|n} Φ_d(x) = ∏_{d|2n, d∤ n} Φ_d(x). So the set of d such that d|2n but d∤ n. That includes all divisors of 2n that are not divisors of n. Those are exactly the divisors of 2n that have at least one factor of 2 that n lacks. So they are of the form d = 2m where m|n, but also any divisor of n that is odd? Actually if n is odd, then divisors of 2n are divisors of n plus divisors of 2n that are double of divisor of n. Since n odd, any divisor of n is also divisor of 2n, but those are excluded because they divide n. So the remaining divisors are those that have a factor 2 not present in n, i.e., 2 times a divisor of n. So for n odd, x^n + 1 = ∏_{m|n} Φ_{2m}(x). Since 2m divides 2n and does not divide n because n odd, indeed. So factorization for odd n: product over m|n of Φ_{2m}(x). For n even, there are also some odd divisors? Let's examine: Suppose n = 2^a * m where m odd. Then divisors of 2n = 2^{a+1} * m. Divisors of n are those with exponent of 2 up to a. The divisors of 2n that are not divisors of n are those where exponent of 2 is exactly a+1 (i.e., have one extra factor of 2) and any divisor of m. So they are of form 2^{a+1} * d where d|m. So they are all of the form 2 * (divisor of n) where the divisor includes the full power of 2? Actually if n even, then some divisors of 2n not dividing n are those with exponent of 2 equal a+1, i.e., double the highest power of 2 dividing n. So the factorization of x^n + 1 is product over d|m of Φ_{2^{a+1} d}(x). So basically x^n + 1 = ∏_{d|m} Φ_{2^{a+1} d}(x). So the irreducible factors are cyclotomic polynomials whose order is a power of 2 times an odd divisor of n, where the exponent of 2 is one more than the exponent of 2 in n.\n"}
