,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174827955,"data":"       | Thus we need to enumerate all possible B(x) of degree ≤8 (since deg(B) ≤8 after factoring out x^r). But B(x) may have degree up to 8. Also B(0) ≠0, which is automatically true for any divisor of 1 + x^d (since 1 + x^d evaluated at x=0 is 1). Indeed, any divisor of 1 + x^d with constant term nonzero (since 1 + x^d has constant term 1). So B(0) = ±1? Actually constant term of any divisor of 1 + x^d is ±1 because the constant term of product of divisors must be 1 (since constant term of 1 + x^d is 1). Since all cyclotomic polynomials have constant term 1 (or -1?) Let's recall: Φ_n(0) = 1 if n \u003e 1? Actually Φ_n(x) is monic with integer coefficients, and Φ_n(0) = 1 if n \u003e 1 (since the constant term is 1 for n\u003e1). For n=1, Φ_1(x) = x-1, constant term -1. But Φ_1 does not appear in factorization of 1 + x^d because 1 does not divide 2d but not d? Let's check: For n=1, 1 divides 2d, but also divides d, so excluded. So Φ_1 does not appear. So all cyclotomic factors have constant term 1. Therefore any divisor B(x) will have constant term 1 (since product of 1's). So B(0)=1, not -1. So constant term is 1. Good.\n"}
