,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.174844464,"data":"       | But careful: If B(x) = 1 (the constant polynomial), then α(x) = ± x^r. That is a monomial with coefficient ±1 at position r (0≤r≤8). Does such α satisfy the condition? Let's test: α = ± e_r (where e_r is delta at r). Then cross-correlation with β yields? We need to find β such that S_n α ⋆ β = 1 at exactly two n's. Since α is a delta, (S_n α)⋆β = α shifted at n (i.e., delta at position r-n) dot β = β(r-n). So we need to find β such that β(r-n) = 1 for exactly two n values, zero elsewhere. That's equivalent to β being 1 at two positions and zero elsewhere. Indeed choose β to be delta at positions r-k and r-l (with coefficient 1). Then (S_n α)⋆β = 1 when n=k or n=l. So monomial works. So yes, monomials are shifty.\n"}
