,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17560718,"data":"       | analysisWe need to understand the problem: We have functions α: Z→Z with finite support (nonzero only on finitely many integers). The product α⋆β = sum over n α(n) β(n) (dot product). Shift operator S_n: (S_n α)(t) = α(t+n). So S_n α is α shifted left by n? Actually (S_n α)(t) = α(t+n). So if α is supported in [0,8] (since shifty functions have zero outside that interval), then S_n α is supported in [ -n, 8-n ].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175609315,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175610507,"data":"       | Definition: α is called shifty if α(m)=0 for all integers m\u003c0 and m\u003e8 (i.e., support subset of {0,...,8}) and there exists β∈F and integers k≠l such that for all n∈Z, (S_n α)⋆β = 1 if n∈{k,l} and 0 otherwise.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175612299,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175613479,"data":"       | Thus we need to count the number of α (with support within 0..8) for which there exists β and distinct integers k,l such that the dot product of shifted α with β is 1 at exactly two shift positions k,l and zero elsewhere.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175615396,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175616837,"data":"       | Interpretation: We have α as a vector of length 9 (indices 0..8). β is an arbitrary finitely supported integer vector (maybe infinite support but finite nonzero entries). The dot product (S_n α)⋆β = sum_t α(t+n) β(t) = sum_t α(t+n) β(t). Equivalent to convolution: (α ⋆ β_rev?) Actually define convolution (α * β)(n) = sum_t α(t+n) β(t). That's like cross-correlation of α and β. Indeed (S_n α)⋆β = sum_t α(t+n) β(t) = (α ⋆ β)(n) where we treat α as function of integer argument and β as function. This is like discrete convolution where α is reversed? Let's check standard convolution: (f * g)(n) = Σ_t f(t) g(n - t). Here we have Σ_t α(t+n) β(t) = Σ_t α(t+n) β(t) = Σ_u α(u) β(u - n) after substitution u = t+n. So it's Σ_u α(u) β(u - n) = (α * β_rev)(n) where β_rev(u) = β(-u). Actually (α * β_rev)(n) = Σ_u α(u) β_rev(n - u) = Σ_u α(u) β(-(n - u)) = Σ_u α(u) β(u - n). Yes matches. So (S_n α)⋆β = (α * β_rev)(n). So the condition is that the convolution of α with some β_rev (which is also a finitely supported integer function) yields a function that is 1 at exactly two points k,l and 0 elsewhere. So we need to find α (support subset of [0,8]) such that there exists β (finite support) with convolution α * γ (where γ = β_rev) giving exactly two unit spikes at distinct positions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175623123,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175624296,"data":"       | Thus α must be a \"generator\" of a function with support exactly two points (with value 1). Equivalent to: α has a left inverse under convolution? Not exactly; we need existence of γ such that α * γ = δ_{k} + δ_{l} (two Dirac deltas). This is reminiscent of solving linear equations: For each integer n, (α * γ)(n) = Σ_t α(t) γ(n - t) = 0 except at n = k,l where it equals 1. Since α is supported on 0..8, the convolution will be supported on [min(supp α)+min(supp γ), max(supp α)+max(supp γ)]. So we can choose γ with support maybe far away to get spikes at arbitrary k,l.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175626882,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175628349,"data":"       | But we can shift the whole situation: If we have α and we find γ such that α * γ = δ_0 + δ_m (two spikes at positions 0 and m). Then shifting both sides by some integer shift n0 yields α * (S_{n0} γ) = δ_{n0} + δ_{n0+m}. Actually convolution with shift: (α * (S_{n0} γ))(n) = Σ_t α(t) γ(n - t - n0) = (α * γ)(n - n0) = δ_{n0} + δ_{n0+m}. So we can produce any pair of positions spaced m apart by shifting γ accordingly. So the existence of k,l is essentially existence of some m = l - k ≠ 0 such that α * γ = δ_0 + δ_m for some γ. So we can reduce to existence of γ such that α * γ = δ_0 + δ_m, for some nonzero integer m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175631149,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175632283,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to: For which α (supported in [0,8]) does there exist integer m ≠ 0 and γ (finite support integer-valued) such that the convolution α * γ equals δ_0 + δ_m (i.e., equals 1 at n=0 and n=m, zero elsewhere). Count such α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175634045,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175635189,"data":"       | Since α is integer-valued with finite support within [0,8]. No restrictions on β (γ) other than finite support integer-valued.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175636586,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175637743,"data":"       | We need to count number of α functions (i.e., integer vectors of length 9) that can generate a sum of two Dirac deltas via convolution with some integer vector.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17563919,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175640332,"data":"       | Observation: Convolution is linear in γ. For a fixed α, the set of possible convolution results (as functions of n) is the set of integer linear combinations of shifts of α (by varying γ). Indeed (α * γ)(n) = Σ_t α(t) γ(n - t) = Σ_s γ(s) α(n - s) = Σ_s γ(s) (S_{-s} α)(n). So the convolution result is linear combination (with integer coefficients γ(s)) of shifted copies of α. So we need to represent the target function f = δ_0 + δ_m as integer linear combination of shifts of α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175642682,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175643815,"data":"       | Thus we need to find integer coefficients c_s (where s runs over some finite set) such that Σ_s c_s (S_{-s} α) = f. Equivalent to: There exist integer coefficients c_s such that for each n, Σ_s c_s α(n + s) = f(n). Since α is zero outside [0,8], each equation involves only finitely many c_s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175647096,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17564825,"data":"       | Thus we are looking for integer linear combinations of translates of α that produce a function with support exactly at two points (distance m apart). This is reminiscent of the concept of \"linear span\" of translates of α over Z.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17564998,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175651436,"data":"       | Because α has finite support, the set of translates is infinite but each translation shifts the support interval. The linear combination can produce any function whose support is within the union of intervals of the translates used. But we need to produce only two points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17565309,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175654232,"data":"       | Observation: If α has at least two nonzero entries, perhaps we can combine translates to cancel everything except two points. But we need integer coefficients.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17565563,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175656774,"data":"       | Let's think in terms of generating functions: Let A(x) = Σ_{i=0}^8 a_i x^i, where a_i = α(i). Similarly, let G(x) = Σ_{j} g_j x^j, where g_j = γ(j). Then convolution α * γ corresponds to product of generating functions: (A(x) * G(x))? Actually convolution defined as (α * γ)(n) = Σ_t α(t) γ(n - t). That's coefficient of x^n in A(x) G(x). Indeed product of formal Laurent series: A(x) G(x) = Σ_n (Σ_t a_t g_{n - t}) x^n = Σ_n (α * γ)(n) x^n. So convolution corresponds to multiplication of generating functions (Laurent series). Since α has support in [0,8], A(x) is a polynomial of degree ≤8 (with possibly zero constant term). γ can be any Laurent polynomial (finite support). The product A(x) G(x) must equal x^k + x^l (since δ_k + δ_l corresponds to those monomials). So we need to find α (polynomial A(x) with integer coefficients, degree ≤8, support subset of [0,8]) such that there exists G(x) (Laurent polynomial with integer coefficients) and integers k≠l with A(x) G(x) = x^k + x^l.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175660087,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175661229,"data":"       | Thus the condition reduces to: A(x) divides x^k + x^l in the ring Z[x, x^{-1}] (Laurent polynomials with integer coefficients). Since G(x) must be integer-coefficient Laurent polynomial (finite support). So A(x) must be a divisor of a binomial x^k + x^l.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17566281,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17566422,"data":"       | Equivalently, there exists integer m ≠ 0 such that A(x) divides x^0 + x^m = 1 + x^m (by shifting: multiply both sides by x^{-k} to get x^{l - k} + 1). Indeed if A(x) G(x) = x^k + x^l, then multiply both sides by x^{-k}: A(x) x^{-k} G(x) = 1 + x^{l - k}. But x^{-k} G(x) is still a Laurent polynomial with integer coefficients. So A(x) divides 1 + x^m for some nonzero integer m (positive maybe). So condition: There exists m ≠ 0 such that 1 + x^m is divisible by A(x) in Z[x, x^{-1}].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175666282,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17566745,"data":"       | Since A(x) is a polynomial with nonnegative exponents (0..8). Divisibility in Laurent polynomial ring: A(x) must be a factor of 1 + x^m, up to a monomial factor (i.e., multiplication by x^r). But A(x) has only nonnegative exponents; we can allow monomial factor x^r to shift. However A(x) dividing 1 + x^m means there exists integer polynomial B(x) (maybe with negative exponents) such that A(x) B(x) = 1 + x^m. But B(x) can have negative exponents; but we can multiply by a monomial to clear negative exponents: Let B(x) = x^{-r} C(x) where C(x) is a polynomial with nonnegative exponents. Then A(x) x^{-r} C(x) = 1 + x^m =\u003e A(x) C(x) = x^r + x^{m+r}. So we can always shift. So essentially A(x) must be a factor of a binomial x^p + x^q (with p≠q). Since we can absorb monomial factor into shift.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175672476,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17567368,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to counting integer polynomials A(x) with support subset of [0,8] (i.e., degree ≤8, possibly missing some coefficients) such that A(x) divides some binomial x^p + x^q in Z[x, x^{-1}]. Since A(x) is integer polynomial with possibly zeros. Also A(x) must be nonzero (since α is not identically zero? Actually α could be zero? The condition requires existence of β such that S_n α ⋆ β is 1 at two positions; if α is zero, then product always zero; cannot satisfy. So α must be nonzero.)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175675886,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175677364,"data":"       | Thus we need to count all integer polynomials A(x) with degree ≤8, support subset of [0,8], that divide some binomial x^p + x^q. Since we can shift, we can assume p=0, q=m\u003e0 after factoring out monomial x^p. So we need to count all integer polynomials A(x) that divide 1 + x^m for some positive integer m (maybe up to 8+some?). But note that A(x) has degree ≤8, but m can be arbitrarily large because we can shift β to produce spikes far apart. However the factorization of 1 + x^m is known: Over Z, 1 + x^m factors into cyclotomic polynomials: 1 + x^m = ∏_{d|2m, d∤ m} Φ_d(x). Actually x^m + 1 = ∏_{d|2m, d∤ m} Φ_d(x). So the irreducible integer factors are cyclotomic polynomials of order dividing 2m but not m. So any divisor of 1 + x^m is a product of some of these cyclotomic polynomials.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175680162,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175681336,"data":"       | Thus any integer polynomial A(x) that divides some 1 + x^m must be a product of cyclotomic polynomials Φ_d(x) where d is an odd divisor of 2m but not of m (i.e., d is even? Actually for x^m + 1, the divisors d are those where d|2m but d∤ m, which is equivalent to d being of the form 2k where k|m and k odd? Let's recall: x^n - 1 = ∏_{d|n} Φ_d(x). Then x^n + 1 = x^{2n} - 1 / (x^n - 1) = ∏_{d|2n, d∤ n} Φ_d(x). So the d's are divisors of 2n that are not divisors of n; i.e., d such that the highest power of 2 dividing d is greater than that dividing n. So they are the divisors of 2n with an extra factor of 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175683707,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175684879,"data":"       | Thus the divisors d are those where the 2-adic valuation v2(d) \u003e v2(n). So each such d is of the form d = 2^{e} * o where o odd, and e = v2(d) \u003e v2(n). So essentially they are the divisors of 2n that are not divisors of n, i.e., those that have a factor of 2 that n doesn't have.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17568662,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175687756,"data":"       | Thus the cyclotomic polynomials that appear in factorization of x^m + 1 are those with order d where d has a factor of 2 not present in m (i.e., d is even, and m/d' maybe?). In any case, any divisor A(x) of some x^m + 1 must be a product of such cyclotomic polynomials.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175689356,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175690874,"data":"       | Thus the set of possible A(x) (with integer coefficients) is exactly the set of integer polynomials all of whose irreducible factors are cyclotomic polynomials Φ_d(x) where d is even (since they must appear in x^m + 1). Actually Φ_d(x) for odd d also appear in x^m + 1? Let's test: For m=1, x+1 = Φ_2(x). So only Φ_2. For m=2, x^2+1 = Φ_4(x). For m=3, x^3+1 = (x+1)(x^2 - x + 1) = Φ_2(x) Φ_6(x). So Φ_6 appears (d=6). That's even. For m=4, x^4+1 = Φ_8(x) Φ_2(x). Wait x^4+1 = (x^2+1)^2 - (2x^2) =? Actually factorization: x^4+1 = Φ_8(x) Φ_4(x)? Let's check: Φ_8(x) = x^4+1? Actually Φ_8(x) = x^4+1. Indeed, x^4+1 = Φ_8(x). But also there is factor Φ_2? Let's compute: x^4+1 = (x^2+√2 x+1)(x^2-√2 x+1) not integer. So maybe x^4+1 = Φ_8(x). Wait formula: Φ_8(x) = x^4+1. So x^4+1 = Φ_8(x). So only Φ_8. So indeed only even d appear.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17569566,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175696814,"data":"       | Thus any divisor of x^m+1 must be product of cyclotomic polynomials Φ_d where d is even and d|2m but d∤ m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17569821,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175699352,"data":"       | Thus A(x) must be a product of such even-order cyclotomic polynomials.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17570064,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175701794,"data":"       | Now we also require that A(x) has degree ≤8 and its support (i.e., nonzero coefficients) are only for exponents 0..8 (i.e., no negative exponents). Since A(x) is a polynomial with nonnegative exponents, that's automatically satisfied for any product of cyclotomic polynomials (they are monic with integer coefficients). However some cyclotomic polynomials have negative coefficients, but that's okay; α values can be any integer (including negative). There's no restriction on sign.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175703833,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17570537,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to counting the number of integer polynomials A(x) with degree ≤8 that are products of cyclotomic polynomials Φ_d(x) where d is even (i.e., d divisible by 2) and each factor appears at most once? Actually any product of such cyclotomic polynomials, each can appear with exponent 0 or 1 because they are irreducible. However we could also have powers of cyclotomic polynomials? Since divisors of x^m+1 are not necessarily squarefree? Wait cyclotomic polynomials are irreducible over Z, and x^m+1 is squarefree because x^n - 1 is squarefree over Z (since derivative n x^{n-1} not zero at roots of unity except maybe char dividing n). Over Z, x^m+1 has no repeated roots; thus its factorization into cyclotomic polynomials is squarefree (each appears at most once). Indeed x^n - 1 is product of distinct cyclotomic polynomials. So x^m+1 also has distinct cyclotomic factors. So any divisor of x^m+1 is product of a subset of those distinct cyclotomic polynomials (each at most once). So exponent of each factor is either 0 or 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175708406,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175709597,"data":"       | Thus A(x) must be a product of a subset of the set of cyclotomic polynomials Φ_d where d is even and d divides 2m but not m for some m. But the set of such d depends on m. However we can consider all possible even d such that Φ_d has degree ≤8 (since degree of product must be ≤8). Because we can choose m large enough to include any finite set of such d's? Let's check: For a given set of even d's, can we find an m such that each d divides 2m but not m? Equivalent to: For each d in the set, we need v2(d) \u003e v2(m) and the odd part of d divides m. Let's denote d = 2^{e} * o where o odd. Condition: d | 2m but d ∤ m. That means 2^{e} * o | 2m, i.e., 2^{e-1} * o | m. Since d ∤ m, we need e \u003e v2(m). So m must be divisible by 2^{e-1} * o, but not by 2^{e} * o. So we need to choose m such that its 2-adic valuation is exactly e-1 (or less) but at least e-1 to have divisibility. Actually to have d divide 2m, we need m divisible by 2^{e-1} * o. So let m = 2^{e-1} * o * t where t is integer not divisible by 2 (i.e., odd) maybe? Wait t can be any integer. Then v2(m) = e-1 + v2(t). For d ∤ m, we need v2(m) \u003c e, i.e., e-1 + v2(t) \u003c e =\u003e v2(t) \u003c 1 =\u003e v2(t) = 0, i.e., t odd. So m must be of the form m = 2^{e-1} * o * t where t is odd and not divisible by 2. So v2(m) = e-1 exactly. So for each even d = 2^{e} * o, we need to choose m such that its 2-adic valuation is exactly e-1 and its odd part is a multiple of o (i.e., contains o). So we can choose m = 2^{e-1} * o (with t=1). That works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175715869,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175717477,"data":"       | Thus for a given set of even d's, we can choose m as the least common multiple of the numbers 2^{e-1} * o (i.e., each d's half with the same odd part). But we also need to ensure that for each d, v2(m) = e-1? Wait if we take LCM of numbers with possibly different e's, the resulting m may have higher 2-adic valuation than some e-1, causing some d to divide m (since v2(m) might be \u003e= e). To avoid that, we need to choose m such that for each d, v2(m) = e-1 (the minimal among them). If we have d's with different e values, we cannot satisfy all simultaneously because m cannot have multiple 2-adic valuations. However we can circumvent by using product of cyclotomic polynomials with different e's by taking m that is a multiple of each 2^{e-1}*o, but with v2(m) equal to max(e-1) maybe? Let's test: Suppose we have d1 = 2^2 * o1 (e1=2). Then we need v2(m) = 1 (i.e., exactly e1-1) to ensure d1 ∤ m. If we also have d2 = 2^3 * o2 (e2=3). Then we need v2(m) = 2. But cannot be both 1 and 2. So we cannot have both d1 and d2 simultaneously as factors of the same x^m+1? Let's verify with actual example: Consider x^2+1 = Φ_4(x). Contains d=4 (e=2). x^4+1 = Φ_8(x) (d=8, e=3). Are there any m such that x^m+1 is divisible by both Φ_4 and Φ_8? Let's test m=4: x^4+1 = Φ_8(x). Does it also have Φ_4 factor? No. m=8: x^8+1 =? Factorization: x^8+1 = Φ_{16}(x) Φ_{8}(x) maybe? Actually x^8+1 = (x^4+1)^2 - 2x^4 =? The factorization: x^8+1 = Φ_{16}(x) Φ_{8}(x) (since 8 is even, 2-adic valuations). Indeed x^8+1 = (x^4+1)^2 - 2x^4, but irreducible factorization includes Φ_{16} and Φ_8. Not Φ_4. So indeed Φ_4 and Φ_8 cannot appear together in any x^m+1. So the set of cyclotomic factors of a given x^m+1 is such that the 2-adic valuation of the orders is exactly one greater than the 2-adic valuation of m. So all d's have the same 2-adic valuation e = v2(m)+1. So they share the same exponent of 2. Thus we cannot mix cyclotomic polynomials with different powers of 2. So the possible A(x) must be a product of cyclotomic polynomials Φ_d where all d have the same 2-adic valuation (i.e., same exponent of 2). In other words, they are all of the form d = 2^{e} * o where e is fixed and o odd dividing some m' (the odd part of m). So the set of possible A(x) are products of cyclotomic polynomials of orders d with a fixed e (≥1) and odd parts dividing some integer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175723117,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175725254,"data":"       | Thus we need to count all possible products of cyclotomic polynomials Φ_{2^{e} * o} where e≥1 (since d must be even), o odd, such that the total degree ≤8 and the polynomial's support (exponents) is within 0..8 (but any polynomial of degree ≤8 automatically has exponents up to degree). However we also need that the polynomial's coefficients are integer (true) and that the polynomial's minimal exponent is 0? Actually A(x) may have constant term zero? Wait α(m)=0 for m\u003c0 and m\u003e8, but α(0) could be zero. There's no requirement that α(0) ≠ 0. So A(x) may have zero constant term. However the condition that α is supported within 0..8 but not necessarily containing 0. So A(x) may have no constant term, but then A(x) would have factor x? But A(x) is a polynomial with integer coefficients, possibly missing lower-degree terms. But if A(x) has a factor x, then α(0)=0. That's allowed. However does A(x) dividing 1 + x^m require that A(x) be monic? Not necessary. But factorization of 1 + x^m yields monic irreducible factors (cyclotomic polynomials are monic). So any divisor is monic as product of monic polynomials (monic). So A(x) will be monic (leading coefficient 1). But α's leading coefficient is α(max index) maybe not 1. Wait the polynomial A(x) = Σ_{i=0}^8 a_i x^i, with a_i = α(i). If we consider product of cyclotomic polynomials, the leading coefficient is 1 (since each Φ_d is monic). So A(x) will be monic. However α may have leading coefficient not equal to 1 (e.g., 2). But then A(x) wouldn't divide 1 + x^m because leading coefficient would be \u003e1. However we could have factor x^r times a monic divisor? Actually we can have A(x) = x^r * D(x) where D(x) divides 1 + x^m (monic). Since we allowed shift factor x^r (monomial) because we can multiply both sides by x^r? Wait earlier we argued that A(x) dividing 1 + x^m up to monomial factor is sufficient. Indeed we need A(x) G(x) = x^k + x^l. If we factor out x^k from RHS: A(x) G(x) = x^k (1 + x^{l-k}). So A(x) divides x^k (1 + x^{m}) where m = l - k. Since x^k is a monomial, we can allow A(x) to have a factor x^r (i.e., some zeros at low indices). So A(x) can be x^r * D(x) where D(x) divides 1 + x^m. So A(x) can have a factor x^r (i.e., zeros for indices \u003c r). That's allowed because α(m)=0 for m\u003c0, but we can have zeros at the start.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17573278,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175733989,"data":"       | Thus the general form: α corresponds to polynomial A(x) = x^r * D(x) where r ≥ 0 integer, D(x) is a divisor of 1 + x^m for some m\u003e0, and degree of A(x) ≤ 8, i.e., r + deg(D) ≤ 8. Also D(x) monic (since divisor of 1 + x^m). So leading coefficient of A(x) is 1 (since D monic) unless r=0 and D has leading coefficient 1. So A(x) is monic with respect to highest-degree term (coefficient 1). So α(max index) = 1. But is that required? Actually if D is monic, then A's leading coefficient is 1. So α's highest non-zero entry must be 1. The condition does not forbid that; we just need to count functions α that satisfy condition. So any α with leading coefficient not 1 cannot be shifty. But we need to verify if there could be other possibilities: Could D be a divisor of 1 + x^m multiplied by a constant factor c, where c divides 1 + x^m? But 1 + x^m's constant term is 1, leading term is x^m, so any integer factor c dividing both constant and leading coefficient must be ±1. So any integer divisor of 1 + x^m must be monic up to sign. Since coefficients are integer, the only possible constant factor is ±1. So D can be multiplied by -1, giving leading coefficient -1. But we can incorporate sign into γ maybe? Because if D(x) divides 1 + x^m, we could also have -D(x) dividing 1 + x^m? Actually if D(x) divides 1 + x^m, then -D(x) also divides 1 + x^m because -1 is a unit in Z. So we can allow sign. So A(x) could be x^r * (± D(x)). So leading coefficient could be ±1. So α's highest non-zero entry could be ±1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175738104,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175739437,"data":"       | Thus we need to count all polynomials A(x) of degree ≤8 of the form x^r * D(x) where D(x) is a product of distinct cyclotomic polynomials Φ_{2^{e} * o} with same e (≥1) and odd parts dividing some integer (i.e., any set of odd divisors). But D(x) must divide 1 + x^m for some m. For a given e, the set of possible D(x) is any product of cyclotomic polynomials Φ_{2^{e} * o} where o runs over a set of odd numbers dividing some integer N (the odd part of m). However we can choose m arbitrarily large, so we can include any odd numbers as factors as long as the degree constraint is satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175741759,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175742927,"data":"       | Thus the counting reduces to enumerating all subsets of cyclotomic polynomials of the form Φ_{2^{e} * o} (with e fixed) such that total degree ≤8 - r (where r is shift). Additionally, we can also have r from 0 to 8 - deg(D). Also we can choose sign ±.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175746354,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175747539,"data":"       | We also need to ensure that the resulting polynomial A(x) has support only within 0..8, which is automatic if degree ≤8. However we also need to ensure that A(x) has no negative exponents; x^r factor ensures that the minimal exponent is r (≥0). So okay.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175749209,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175750353,"data":"       | Thus the total number of shifty functions equals sum over r=0..8 of (number of possible D(x) with degree ≤ 8 - r) * 2 (for sign) maybe? Wait sign may produce same α? Since α values are integer; if we multiply D(x) by -1, the coefficients change sign, resulting in a different function α (unless D(x) = 0). So sign yields distinct α unless D(x) = 0. So we should count both signs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175752276,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175753424,"data":"       | But we must be careful: The condition requires existence of β, not necessarily that α's leading coefficient is ±1; but we derived that any divisor of 1 + x^m must be monic up to sign. Could there be α with leading coefficient other than ±1 that still works because β may have rational coefficients? No, β must be integer-valued. So the product A(x) G(x) = x^k + x^l must have integer coefficients (the RHS has coefficients 1 at two positions). If A(x) has leading coefficient c ≠ ±1, then G(x) would need to have coefficient 1/c at some term to produce leading coefficient 1. Since G(x) must have integer coefficients, c must divide 1, so c = ±1. So indeed leading coefficient must be ±1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175755974,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175757099,"data":"       | Thus our classification stands.\n"}
