,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175988166,"data":"       | Now note that for each e, the odd part o is n / 2^e. For e=1, o = 1,3,5,7,9,15? Actually compute: n=2 -\u003e o=1; n=6 -\u003e o=3; n=10 -\u003e o=5; n=14 -\u003e o=7; n=18 -\u003e o=9; n=30 -\u003e o=15. So odd o values: 1,3,5,7,9,15. For e=2: n=4 -\u003e o=1; n=12 -\u003e o=3; n=20 -\u003e o=5. For e=3: n=8 -\u003e o=1; n=24 -\u003e o=3. For e=4: n=16 -\u003e o=1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175990297,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175991477,"data":"       | Thus each e corresponds to a set of odd o with certain degrees.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175992704,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175993837,"data":"       | Now the condition for D: product of distinct Φ_{2^e * o} with same e. So for each e, we can select any subset of the available odd o's (including empty) to multiply. The degree sum is sum of φ(2^e * o) = 2^{e-1} φ(o). Since we already have degree values per n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175995497,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175996633,"data":"       | Thus we need to count subsets for each e such that total degree ≤ L (where L = 8 - r). For each e, we also need to consider that we cannot mix different e's in a single D. So D must be built from a single e. However D could be empty (no cyclotomic factor) which is independent of e (or we can treat as any e). So for each e we count subsets of the set of n's with that e.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175998484,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.175999597,"data":"       | But note that D may be empty, which we count once overall (not per e). We'll handle that.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176002664,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176004124,"data":"       | Thus total number of D for a given L is: 1 (empty) + sum_{e≥1} (number of nonempty subsets of the set of n's with v2=n's e such that sum of degrees ≤ L). However note that subsets from different e are distinct; but we must ensure we don't double count empty set across e's. We'll treat empty separately.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176005923,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176007047,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute for each e the number of subsets of the corresponding degree multiset (list of degrees) with total degree ≤ L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176008444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176009584,"data":"       | But also note that for each e, the possible odd o's are limited; we cannot have infinite many because degree bound restricts. Indeed we have only those n with degree ≤8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176011087,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176012191,"data":"       | Thus we can compute for each L (0..8) the number of possible D.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176013436,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176014557,"data":"       | We also need to multiply by 2 for sign and sum over r.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176015777,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176021407,"data":"       | But careful: The sign factor multiplies the entire α = ± x^r D(x). If D is empty (i.e., α = ± x^r), sign still gives two possibilities. So sign factor always 2 regardless of D (including empty). So total number = 2 * Σ_{r=0}^{8} N(L=8-r), where N(L) = number of possible D (including empty) with total degree ≤ L.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176023647,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176024784,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute N(L) for L=0..8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176025993,"data":"       | \n"}
