,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176367678,"data":"       | Now we need to compute N(L) again but also verify that we haven't missed any D formed by product of multiple cyclotomics of same e but with odd parts that are not divisors of a single m? Actually for a given e, the set of possible odd parts o that can appear in D must be such that there exists some m with v2(m) = e-1 and odd part of m divisible by each o. In other words, we need to find a single m such that all o's divide the odd part of m. For e fixed, the odd part of m is some odd integer M. The cyclotomic factors present in 1 + x^m are Φ_{2^e * d} where d divides M (odd). So the set of possible o's is the set of odd divisors of M. So any subset of odd divisors of some M is allowed. But not all subsets of the available o's may be simultaneously realizable with a single M? For example, for e=1, the odd parts are o = 1,3,5,7,9,15 (odd divisors of some M). Can we have a subset like {3,5}? That would require M divisible by both 3 and 5, i.e., M divisible by 15. So choose M=15 (odd). Then m = M (since v2(m)=0). Indeed 1 + x^{15} includes Φ_{2*3}=Φ6, Φ_{2*5}=Φ10, and also Φ_{2*15}=Φ30, and Φ2. So subset {Φ6, Φ10} is allowed. Similarly any subset of the odd divisors of some M is allowed. But does there exist M such that the subset includes 3 and 7 but not 5? M must be divisible by 3 and 7, i.e., 21. Then 1 + x^{21} includes Φ6, Φ14, Φ42? Wait 2*21=42, but 42 \u003e30, degree maybe \u003e8? Actually Φ42 degree φ(42)=12 \u003e8, so not in our list. But we only consider cyclotomics up to degree 8, so we only have odd o up to 15. For M=21, the factor Φ_{2*21}=Φ42 degree φ(42)=12 \u003e8, not in our list; but we could ignore it because we only care about subset of our list; the presence of extra factors of higher degree doesn't affect ability to have subset of lower-degree ones, because D must divide 1 + x^M, and if 1 + x^M contains extra factors of degree \u003e8, that's fine; D can be a divisor ignoring those. So any subset of odd divisors of some M is allowed as long as each factor's degree ≤8 (which holds). So the condition reduces to: there exists an odd M such that the odd parts of D are divisors of M. This is equivalent to requiring that the odd parts are all divisors of their least common multiple (LCM). Indeed we can take M = lcm of odd parts in the subset. So any subset of the odd parts is realizable. So indeed any subset of the available odd o's (within degree bound) is allowed. So our enumeration of all subsets is correct.\n"}
