,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176513197,"data":"       | 1. Translate condition into polynomial language.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17651443,"data":"       | 2. Show that α corresponds to polynomial A(x) = Σ α(i) x^i with degree ≤8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176515811,"data":"       | 3. Define β as polynomial B(x) = Σ β(i) x^i (Laurent polynomial allowed).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176518847,"data":"       | 4. The condition S_n(α)⋆β = 1 iff n∈{k,l} translates to A(x)·B(x) = x^k + x^l.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176520684,"data":"       | 5. Factor out monomial: A(x) = x^r·D(x) where D(0)≠0. Then x^k + x^l = x^r·(1 + x^m) with m = l - k \u003e0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176522173,"data":"       |    Hence D(x) must divide 1 + x^m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176523363,"data":"       | 6. Since 1 + x^m factorization over ℤ is product of cyclotomic polynomials Φ_d where d|2m, d∤m. In particular all such d are even and have the same 2‑adic valuation v2(d)=v2(m)+1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176524951,"data":"       |    Therefore D(x) is a product of distinct cyclotomic polynomials Φ_{2^{e}·o} with a fixed e≥1 and odd o.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176526351,"data":"       | 7. Because deg A ≤8, we only need cyclotomic polynomials of degree ≤8. List them:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176527754,"data":"       |    - e=1: Φ2 (deg1), Φ6 (deg2), Φ10 (deg4), Φ14 (deg6), Φ18 (deg6), Φ30 (deg8).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17652913,"data":"       |    - e=2: Φ4 (deg2), Φ12 (deg4), Φ20 (deg8).\n"}
