,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176586977,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse problem: We have set F of finitely supported integer-valued functions on Z. For alpha, beta in F, define product α⋆β = Σ_{n∈Z} α(n)·β(n). This is just dot product (finite sum). For each integer n, define shift operator S_n: (S_n(α))(t) = α(t+n). So S_n shifts to left? Actually α(t+n) = α shifted left by n? For n positive, S_n(α)(t) = α(t+n) means the function is moved left by n (i.e., original value at t+n moves to t). But fine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176589197,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176590328,"data":"       | Definition: α ∈ F is called shifty if α(m)=0 for all integers m\u003c0 and m\u003e8 (so support is subset of {0,1,...,8}) and there exists β∈F and integers k≠l such that for all n∈Z, S_n(α)⋆β = 1 if n∈{k,l} and 0 otherwise.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176592154,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176593297,"data":"       | Interpretation: There exists β and two distinct shifts k,l such that the dot product of shift of α with β equals 1 exactly for those two shift positions, zero elsewhere. So the convolution-like cross-correlation of α and β yields a function that is 1 at two points, zero elsewhere.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17659682,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176597977,"data":"       | We need to count number of shifty functions α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176599241,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176600711,"data":"       | Essentially we need to find all α with support in [0,8] (i.e., length 9) such that there exists β (finite support) and two distinct integers k,l with cross-correlation condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176602204,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176603341,"data":"       | This looks like we are looking for α such that the set of integer linear combinations of its shifts can produce a vector (function) with exactly two nonzero entries each equal to 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176604807,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176605937,"data":"       | But the condition is: there exists β such that for all n, (S_n(α)⋆β) = Σ_t α(t+n)·β(t) = Σ_t α(t)·β(t-n) (since shift index). This is essentially convolution of α (reversed?) Actually (S_n(α)⋆β) = Σ_t α(t+n) β(t). This is cross-correlation: (α ⋆̃ β)(n) = Σ_t α(t+n) β(t). If we define γ = β reversed? But not needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176608144,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.1766093,"data":"       | Given α fixed, we want to find β such that the cross-correlation function c(n) = Σ_t α(t+n) β(t) equals 1 at two points k,l and zero elsewhere.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176610784,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176611906,"data":"       | Since α has support only 0..8, α(t+n) is nonzero only when t+n ∈ [0,8] =\u003e t ∈ [-n,8-n]. Since β is finitely supported, the sum is finite.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176613426,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176614567,"data":"       | Thus c(n) is defined for all n, but only finitely many n produce nonzero sum because α(t+n) zero for t outside shift.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176615947,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176617057,"data":"       | But we require c(n) = 0 for all n except exactly two positions where it's 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176618336,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176619493,"data":"       | Thus the cross-correlation of α and β is a function with support exactly {k,l} and values 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176620814,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176621937,"data":"       | Goal: find α such that there exists β and distinct k,l achieving this.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176623193,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176624318,"data":"       | Observation: Since α has finite support length 9, its cross-correlation with β is a linear combination of β shifted and weighted by α entries. Let's express: c(n) = Σ_{t} α(t+n) β(t) = Σ_{i} α(i) β(i-n) where i = t+n =\u003e t = i-n. So c(n) = Σ_{i} α(i) β(i-n). This is convolution of α with reversed β: (α * β_rev)(n) where β_rev(m) = β(-m). But again not crucial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176626454,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176627873,"data":"       | Thus we can think of α as a vector a = (a_0,...,a_8) (where a_i = α(i), integer values). β is a vector b with finite support on Z, but we can restrict to some range because only needed for cross-correlation at n = k,l.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17662943,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176630584,"data":"       | We need to find integer vectors a (length 9) not all zero (since need cross-correlation produce nonzero) such that there exists integer vector b (maybe infinite but finite support) and distinct integers k,l where convolution a * b_rev yields vector c with c_k = c_l = 1, all other entries zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17663397,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176635173,"data":"       | Thus we need to find a and b such that a * b_rev = c where c has exactly two 1's.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176636457,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17663759,"data":"       | This is reminiscent of linear diophantine equations: The convolution is linear in b (given a). For each n, c_n = Σ_i a_i b_{i-n}. So for each n, we have linear equation in b entries. Since c is zero except at two positions, we have a system of linear equations with unknowns b_j (integers). We need integer solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176639318,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17664044,"data":"       | Thus for given a, we need to know if there exists integer solution b (finitely many nonzero) to produce c with exactly two 1's.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176641824,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176642974,"data":"       | We can think of b as unknown vector of length maybe up to some range. Since a has support 0..8, the convolution c will have support from min index = min_i (i - max_j) to max_i (i - min_j). Actually if b has support in some interval [p,q], then c will have support in [0 - q, 8 - p] (since i from 0..8). So to have c only at k,l, we need that outside those two indices, the sum zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176644746,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176645874,"data":"       | Thus we can treat unknown b_j for j ranging over some interval; we can choose support of b arbitrarily (finite). So we can treat unknowns b_j for j ∈ Z.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176647306,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17664844,"data":"       | Goal: existence of integer solution to linear system: For each n ∈ Z, Σ_i a_i b_{i-n} = c_n where c_n = 1 if n∈{k,l}, else 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17664985,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176650993,"data":"       | Equivalently, for each shift n, the dot product of a with b shifted by n equals c_n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176652233,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176653353,"data":"       | Thus we need to find two shifts of b that produce dot product 1 with a, and all other shifts produce dot product 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176654691,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176656145,"data":"       | Interpretation: The set of all dot products of a with shifts of b is a function of n; we need that function to be zero except at two points where it's 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176657528,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17665865,"data":"       | Thus we can think of constructing b such that its inner products with a are as needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176659941,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176661075,"data":"       | This is similar to constructing a sequence b such that its cross-correlation with a is a Kronecker delta at two positions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176662421,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176663558,"data":"       | If we think of a as a vector of length 9, we can treat b as an infinite sequence; the cross-correlation yields a linear recurrence relation: The cross-correlation is like convolution of a with reversed b, which is like (a * b_rev). So we need a * b_rev = c where c is two spikes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17666707,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176668265,"data":"       | Thus we can think of solving for b_rev as a convolution inverse: Since a is length 9, we can consider its generating function A(x) = Σ_{i=0}^8 a_i x^i. Let B(x) = Σ_{j} b_j x^j (Laurent series maybe). Then convolution (a * b_rev) corresponds to product A(x) * B(1/x) (or something). Let's derive: c_n = Σ_i a_i b_{i-n}. Define B_rev(m) = b_{-m}. Then c_n = Σ_i a_i B_rev(n - i). That's convolution of a and B_rev: c = a * B_rev. So generating functions: C(x) = Σ_n c_n x^n = A(x) * B_rev(x) where B_rev(x) = Σ_m B_rev(m) x^m = Σ_j b_{-j} x^j = Σ_j b_j x^{-j} = B(1/x). Indeed B_rev(x) = B(1/x). So C(x) = A(x) * B(1/x). We need C(x) = x^k + x^l (since c_n = 1 at k,l). So we need A(x) * B(1/x) = x^k + x^l.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176670794,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176672133,"data":"       | Thus B(1/x) = (x^k + x^l) / A(x). Multiply both sides by x^{?}. Since B(1/x) is a Laurent series with integer coefficients (finite support). So (x^k + x^l)/A(x) must be a Laurent polynomial with integer coefficients, i.e., a finite sum of powers of x with integer coefficients. Moreover, B(1/x) must be a finite Laurent polynomial (i.e., only finitely many nonzero coefficients). That is equivalent to (x^k + x^l) being divisible by A(x) in the ring of Laurent polynomials Z[x, x^{-1}]? Actually we need (x^k + x^l) / A(x) ∈ Z[x, x^{-1}], i.e., the division yields a Laurent polynomial (finite). Since A(x) is a polynomial of degree ≤8 (maybe lower if some leading coefficients zero). For the division to be a Laurent polynomial, A(x) must divide x^k + x^l in Z[x, x^{-1}]; i.e., there exists integer polynomial D(x) (Laurent) such that (x^k + x^l) = A(x) D(x). D(x) corresponds to B(1/x). Since D(x) must have finite support (i.e., polynomial in x and x^{-1}), it's a Laurent polynomial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176675087,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176676207,"data":"       | Thus the condition reduces to: There exists integers k≠l such that A(x) divides x^k + x^l in Z[x, x^{-1}].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176677534,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176678677,"data":"       | Because B(1/x) = D(x) must have integer coefficients (since β values are integers). So D(x) ∈ Z[x, x^{-1}] (Laurent polynomial). Also we need β ∈ F (finite support), which is satisfied if D(x) is a Laurent polynomial (i.e., finite number of terms). So the condition is exactly that A(x) divides x^k + x^l in the Laurent polynomial ring Z[x, x^{-1}].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17668053,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17668165,"data":"       | But note that A(x) may have leading coefficient zero; but we can treat A(x) as polynomial with degree ≤8, maybe not monic.\n"}
