,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176694275,"data":"       | Observation: Since we can multiply A(x) by a unit (±1) and still same α up to sign? Actually α is defined by its coefficients; sign matters. So we need to count distinct integer sequences a_0..a_8 (including zeros) such that there exists k,l with A(x) | (x^k + x^l) in Z[x, x^{-1}].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176695994,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17669741,"data":"       | Simplify: Since x^k + x^l = x^k (1 + x^{l-k}) = x^k (1 + x^d) where d = l - k (nonzero integer). Up to a shift factor x^k, which is a unit in Z[x, x^{-1}] (since x is invertible). So divisibility in Laurent polynomial ring is equivalent to A(x) dividing 1 + x^d (up to multiplication by monomial x^k). Indeed, A(x) divides x^k (1 + x^d) iff A(x) divides 1 + x^d after factoring out x^k (since x^k is a unit). So condition reduces to: there exists nonzero integer d such that A(x) divides 1 + x^d in Z[x, x^{-1}].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17669945,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176700597,"data":"       | Thus we need A(x) to be a divisor of a cyclotomic-like polynomial 1 + x^d (which is x^d + 1). Since A(x) is a polynomial with integer coefficients (degree ≤8). Also A(x) must be monic? Not necessarily; any integer coefficients.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17670213,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176703271,"data":"       | But note: In Z[x, x^{-1}], divisibility by 1 + x^d is same as divisibility in Z[x] because 1 + x^d is a polynomial (no negative powers). However, A(x) may have negative exponents? No, A(x) has only nonnegative exponents 0..8. So divisibility in Laurent ring is same as in Z[x] (since denominator is polynomial). So requirement: A(x) divides 1 + x^d in Z[x] (or equivalently in Z[x]).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176705037,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176706164,"data":"       | Thus we need to count integer polynomials A(x) of degree ≤8, with coefficients possibly zero, such that there exists d ≠ 0 (integer) with A(x) | (1 + x^d) in Z[x].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176707664,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176708774,"data":"       | Also we need α not identically zero, but also α must be zero for negative indices and \u003e8, which is satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176710201,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176711321,"data":"       | Thus problem reduces to counting integer polynomials A(x) of degree ≤8 that divide some 1 + x^d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176712651,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176713808,"data":"       | But note that 1 + x^d is not monic unless d≥0 (monic). For negative d, 1 + x^d = 1 + x^{-d} (Laurent). But we can restrict to positive d because 1 + x^{|d|} times a monomial factor x^{min(k,l)} yields same. Since we already accounted for monomial factor x^k, we can assume d\u003e0. So we need A(x) dividing 1 + x^d for some positive integer d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176715614,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176716728,"data":"       | Thus we need to count integer polynomials A(x) (degree ≤8) that are divisors of some 1 + x^d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176718033,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176720698,"data":"       | Now, 1 + x^d factorization over Z[x] is product of cyclotomic polynomials Φ_{2m}(x) for m dividing d and m odd? Actually x^d + 1 = product_{m|2d, m∤d} Φ_m(x). More precisely, x^n - 1 = ∏_{d|n} Φ_d(x). Then x^n + 1 = ∏_{d|2n, d∤n} Φ_d(x). So the irreducible factors over Z are cyclotomic polynomials of order dividing 2d but not dividing d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17672533,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176726524,"data":"       | Thus any divisor of x^d + 1 is a product of some of those cyclotomic polynomials.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17672783,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176728974,"data":"       | Thus we need to count all integer polynomials A(x) (with degree ≤8) that can be expressed as product of some subset of cyclotomic polynomials Φ_m(x) where m divides 2d but not d, for some d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176730495,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176731734,"data":"       | But note that for each A(x), we can ask: does there exist d such that A(x) divides x^d + 1? Equivalent to: A(x) is a product of cyclotomic polynomials Φ_m where each m is even (since m dividing 2d but not d). Indeed, for x^d + 1, the cyclotomic factors are those with m even and m|2d but not m|d. In other words, m = 2e where e|d, and e odd? Let's recall: x^n + 1 = ∏_{d|2n, d∤n} Φ_d(x). The divisors d of 2n not dividing n are exactly those with d even and n/d odd? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176733997,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176735163,"data":"       | Let n = d (I'll rename variable). The set S = { m : m|2d, m ∤ d }. For each divisor m of 2d, if m does not divide d, then m must contain the factor 2 that d does not have? Because the only way a divisor of 2d fails to divide d is if it has a factor of 2 that d lacks. So m must be of the form m = 2 * t where t|d, and t does not have factor 2? Actually if d is even, then some divisors of 2d that are not divisors of d may have extra factor 2 beyond those present in d. Let's analyze: Let d = 2^a * d_odd. Then 2d = 2^{a+1} * d_odd. Divisors m of 2d that are not divisors of d are those where the exponent of 2 in m is a+1 (i.e., includes the extra factor 2) while the odd part divides d_odd. So m = 2^{a+1} * t where t|d_odd. So m are exactly twice the odd divisors of d (if d odd) or 2^{a+1} times odd divisors of d_odd (if d even). In any case, m are even numbers such that m/2 divides d and m does not divide d (i.e., m has a higher power of 2 than d). So the cyclotomic factors are those with order m where m is even and m/2 | d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176738155,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176739621,"data":"       | Thus the set of cyclotomic polynomials dividing x^d + 1 are those Φ_{2e}(x) where e|d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176740947,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176742147,"data":"       | Thus x^d + 1 = ∏_{e|d} Φ_{2e}(x). Indeed this is known: x^n + 1 = ∏_{d|2n, 2∤d} Φ_d(x)? Wait, there is known identity: x^{2m} - 1 = (x^m - 1)(x^m + 1). And x^m + 1 = ∏_{d|2m, d∤m} Φ_d(x). So for n = d, we have x^d + 1 = ∏_{e|d} Φ_{2e}(x). Let's verify with examples: d=1: x+1 = Φ_2(x). Indeed e|1 =\u003e e=1 =\u003e Φ_{2}(x) = x+1. Works. d=2: x^2+1 = Φ_4(x) = x^2+1. Here e|2 =\u003e e=1,2. Then product Φ_2(x)Φ_4(x) = (x+1)(x^2+1) = x^3 + x^2 + x + 1, not equal to x^2+1. So my formula is wrong. Let's compute x^2+1 factorization: x^2+1 = Φ_4(x). Indeed only Φ_4. So e|2 gave e=1,2; but Φ_{2*1}=Φ_2 = x+1, which does not divide x^2+1. So the condition is not simply e|d. Let's compute using the set S: m divides 2d but not d. For d=2, 2d=4. Divisors of 4: 1,2,4. Those not dividing d=2 are 4 only. So m=4. So factor is Φ_4 only. So indeed m must be divisor of 2d that does not divide d; equivalently m has a factor of 2 that d does not have. So m is of the form 2e where e|d and e odd? Let's test: d=2 =\u003e e|2 =\u003e e=1,2. 2e gives 2,4. Among these, 2 divides d (2), so exclude; 4 does not divide d, include. So condition: m=2e where e|d, and 2e ∤ d. That is, e|d but e is not divisible by the same power of 2 as d? Actually 2e ∤ d means that d is not divisible by 2e, i.e., the exponent of 2 in e is less than exponent of 2 in d? Let's analyze more systematically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176748138,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176749554,"data":"       | Let d = 2^a * d_odd where d_odd odd. Then 2d = 2^{a+1} * d_odd. Divisors m of 2d are of form 2^b * t where 0≤b≤a+1, t|d_odd. m divides d iff b ≤ a. So m does not divide d iff b = a+1 (i.e., includes the extra factor of 2). So m = 2^{a+1} * t where t|d_odd. So m are exactly those numbers of the form 2^{a+1} * t where t is an odd divisor of d (i.e., t|d_odd). So they are even numbers with the highest power of 2 being one more than that of d. So m = 2 * (2^a * t) = 2 * (some divisor of d that contains the maximal power of 2). In other words, m = 2 * (divisor of d that is divisible by 2^a). So m = 2 * (2^a * t) = 2^{a+1} * t where t|d_odd.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176751971,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17675312,"data":"       | Thus the cyclotomic factors of x^d + 1 are Φ_{2^{a+1} * t}(x) where t|d_odd.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176754421,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176755578,"data":"       | Thus for each d, the set of m dividing 2d but not d are exactly those m = 2^{a+1} * t where t|d_odd. So each factor corresponds to a divisor of the odd part of d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176756987,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176758141,"data":"       | Thus x^d + 1 = ∏_{t|d_odd} Φ_{2^{a+1} * t}(x). This matches known formula: x^{2^a * d_odd} + 1 = ∏_{t|d_odd} Φ_{2^{a+1} t}(x). Indeed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17675961,"data":"       | \n"}
