,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176765851,"data":"       | Thus the set of possible m's are even numbers where the exponent of 2 is exactly one more than the exponent of 2 in some integer d. But we can vary d arbitrarily, so essentially any even m can appear? Let's test: For m = 2, can we get it? Need d such that m divides 2d but not d. For m=2, we need 2|2d always true, but 2∤d. So we need d odd. So choose d odd, e.g., d=1. Then x^1 + 1 = x+1 = Φ_2. So Φ_2 appears. So m=2 is possible.\n"}
