,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176786934,"data":"       | Thus any even m yields a cyclotomic factor dividing some x^d+1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176788158,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176789624,"data":"       | Thus the condition that A(x) divides some x^d+1 is equivalent to: all irreducible factors of A(x) are cyclotomic polynomials Φ_m with m even. Moreover, the product of such cyclotomic polynomials must have degree ≤8 (since A(x) degree ≤8). So we need to count all integer polynomials A(x) (with integer coefficients) that are products of distinct (maybe repeated?) cyclotomic polynomials of even order, possibly with multiplicities? Since x^d+1 is square-free? Let's check if x^d+1 has repeated cyclotomic factors? Cyclotomic polynomials are irreducible over Q, and x^d+1 factorization is product of distinct Φ_m (since each m appears only once). So x^d+1 is square-free. Thus any divisor of x^d+1 is also square-free (no repeated factors). So A(x) must be square-free (no repeated cyclotomic factor). So each cyclotomic factor appears at most once.\n"}
