,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176805484,"data":"       | Now we need to count the number of such A(x) (including sign ±?). Since coefficients are integer, we could also multiply by -1 (since -1 is a unit in Z[x]?). If we multiply A(x) by -1, does it still divide x^d+1? Since -1 times A(x) dividing x^d+1 means there exists D(x) such that x^d+1 = (-A(x)) * D(x) =\u003e A(x) * (-D(x)) = x^d+1. So if A(x) divides x^d+1, then -A(x) also divides x^d+1 (since we can absorb sign into D). However, D(x) must have integer coefficients; -D also integer. So yes, sign doesn't affect divisibility. But does α correspond to A(x) up to sign? α's coefficients are the coefficients of A(x). So if we multiply A by -1, we get α' = -α. Does α' also satisfy condition? Yes, because we could take β' = -β (since product S_n(α')⋆β' = ( -α ) ⋆ ( -β ) = α⋆β . Wait cross-correlation uses β directly; if we change sign of α only, we need to adjust β accordingly to maintain c = 1 at two points. Since we can also change β sign, there exists β' = -β such that S_n(α')⋆β' = S_n(-α)⋆(-β) = S_n(α)⋆β = c. So sign of α does not affect existence. So both A and -A correspond to shifty functions. So we need to count both sign variations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176811068,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.176812221,"data":"       | But also we could have A(x) = 0? Not allowed because need cross-correlation produce 1's. So A must be nonzero.\n"}
