,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.177335571,"data":"       | Now, for a given d, v2(d) = a, then allowed m have v2(m) = a+1. So for each a (\u003e=0), we can choose any subset of m's with v2 = a+1, as long as sum of φ(m) ≤8 (since total degree). Since the odd parts must divide odd part of d. However, we also need the odd parts of selected m's to be divisors of the same odd integer (odd part of d). Since we can choose d's odd part to be the lcm of those odd parts? Actually need each odd part e_i to divide odd part of d. So we can choose odd part of d to be a multiple of all e_i. So we can always find such d (choose odd part = product of all e_i). So no restriction beyond that. So any subset of m's with same v2 works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.177339945,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.177341134,"data":"       | Thus the condition reduces to: Choose a nonempty subset of m's all having same v2, such that total degree ≤8. Also we can have empty subset (constant polynomial). Also we can multiply by -1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.177342697,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.177344164,"data":"       | Thus we need to count subsets of each v2 group separately, respecting degree sum ≤8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.177345484,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.177346635,"data":"       | Now we must recompute N accordingly.\n"}
