,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178417023,"data":"     [Reasoning] (29652 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178418193,"data":"       | analysisWe need to parse the problem carefully.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178419387,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178420518,"data":"       | We have $\\mathcal{F}$ = set of integer-valued functions on $\\mathbb{Z}$ with finite support (only finitely many nonzero values). The product $\\alpha \\star \\beta = \\sum_{n\\in\\mathbb{Z}} \\alpha(n) \\beta(n)$. So it's the dot product (convolution? Actually just inner product). Since only finitely many nonzero entries each, sum is finite.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178422595,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17842374,"data":"       | Shift operator $S_n(\\alpha)(t) = \\alpha(t+n)$.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17842506,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178426164,"data":"       | Definition: a function $\\alpha$ is shifty if:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178427444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178428557,"data":"       | - $\\alpha(m)=0$ for all integers $m\u003c0$ and $m\u003e8$. So support of $\\alpha$ is within {0,1,...,8} (maybe also 0? inclusive). So $\\alpha$ is zero outside [0,8] inclusive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178430178,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178431296,"data":"       | - There exists $\\beta\\in \\mathcal{F}$ and integers $k\\neq l$ such that for all $n\\in\\mathbb{Z}$, $S_n(\\alpha) \\star \\beta = 1$ if $n\\in\\{k,l\\}$ and $0$ otherwise.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178433121,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178434265,"data":"       | Interpretation: For each integer shift $n$, the dot product of the shifted $\\alpha$ with $\\beta$ is either 1 (for exactly two shift positions k,l) or 0 otherwise. So the inner product of $\\alpha$ shifted by n with $\\beta$ yields a function of n that is 1 at exactly two positions, zero elsewhere.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178436119,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178437232,"data":"       | We need to count how many such $\\alpha$ exist.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178438516,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178439623,"data":"       | We can treat $\\alpha$ as a vector of length 9 (positions 0..8). Since outside that range it's zero. Similarly $\\beta$ is a finite support integer vector, but we can think of its support anywhere on Z.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178441192,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178442302,"data":"       | Define $\\alpha$ as vector a_i for i=0..8, integer values, not all zero? Actually may be zero for some positions; but support within 0..8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178443732,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178445185,"data":"       | Define $\\beta$ as function with finite support; we can think of its values b_j for j in Z (but only finitely many nonzero). Then the dot product $S_n(\\alpha) \\star \\beta = \\sum_{t\\in Z} \\alpha(t+n) \\beta(t) = \\sum_{t} \\alpha(t+n) \\beta(t)$. Changing variable: let i = t+n, then t = i - n. So sum over i: $\\sum_{i} \\alpha(i) \\beta(i-n)$. Since $\\alpha(i)$ is zero for i outside [0,8], we have sum over i=0..8 of a_i * b_{i-n}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178449305,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178450454,"data":"       | Thus define f(n) = \\sum_{i=0}^{8} a_i b_{i-n}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178451716,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178452817,"data":"       | We require that there exist integers k ≠ l such that f(k) = 1, f(l) = 1, and f(n) = 0 for all other n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178454174,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178455313,"data":"       | Thus f is a function that is zero everywhere except at two points where it's 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17845658,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178457716,"data":"       | Now note that b_j are integers, but we can treat them as unknowns.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178458983,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178460089,"data":"       | The equation f(n) = 0 for all n except k,l yields linear equations in b_j. Since there are only finitely many unknown b_j (nonzero), we can think of constructing a solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178461542,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178462652,"data":"       | But we need existence of some integer-valued beta (finite support) that satisfies these equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178463957,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178465073,"data":"       | We are asked to count number of alpha's for which such a beta exists.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178466321,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178467472,"data":"       | Essentially we need to determine which vectors a (length 9) have the property that there exists a finite support integer vector b (over Z) such that convolution a * b_rev (where reversed?) yields a sequence that is 1 at two points and zero elsewhere.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178469069,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17847019,"data":"       | Specifically f(n) = (a * b_rev)(n) where b_rev(t) = b_{-t}? Wait: Usually convolution (a * b)(n) = sum_i a_i b_{n-i}. Here we have sum_i a_i b_{i-n} = sum_i a_i b_{-(n-i)} = sum_i a_i c_{n-i} where c_{m} = b_{-m}. So it's convolution of a with c where c_{m}=b_{-m}. Since b has finite support, c also has finite support.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178471902,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178473038,"data":"       | Thus we need a convolution of a (length 9) with some finite support integer sequence c (maybe infinite but finite support) to produce a sequence that is 1 at two positions (k,l) and zero elsewhere.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178474533,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178475639,"data":"       | Thus we need to know if there exists integer sequence c (finite support) such that a * c = g where g is a sequence with exactly two 1's.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178477031,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178478313,"data":"       | Now a is a vector of length 9 but we can think of it as a polynomial A(x) = sum_{i=0}^{8} a_i x^i (or maybe x^i). Similarly c corresponds to polynomial C(x) = sum_{j} c_j x^j (finite support). Convolution corresponds to product of polynomials: (A * C)(n) corresponds to coefficient of x^n in A(x) C(x). Indeed coefficient of x^n in product is sum_i a_i c_{n-i}. That's exactly convolution with c as defined (c_{n-i} = b_{i-n}? Wait we have c_{j}=b_{-j}. So coefficient of x^n in A(x)C(x) = sum_i a_i c_{n-i} = sum_i a_i b_{-(n-i)} = sum_i a_i b_{i-n} = f(n). Good.\n"}
