,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178491284,"data":"       | But A(x) is a polynomial with integer coefficients, degree ≤8, and zero for negative exponents. C(x) is also a polynomial (maybe with negative exponents allowed? Since b can have support at any integer positions; c_j = b_{-j} could be nonzero for negative j as well. So C(x) can be a Laurent polynomial (allow negative exponents) but finite support: i.e., C(x) = sum_{j=m}^{M} c_j x^j for some integers m≤M, with integer coefficients.\n"}
