,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178503717,"data":"       | Thus we can think of factoring the polynomial x^{d}+1 (a cyclotomic factorization) into product of A(x) and a Laurent polynomial D(x) (i.e., shift factor). Since we can always factor out a monomial x^{l} from C(x) (or from A(x) but A cannot have negative exponent). So we can rewrite: Let C(x) = x^{l} D(x), where D(x) is a Laurent polynomial with exponent range maybe negative? Wait if C(x) = x^{l} D(x) then product becomes A(x) x^{l} D(x) = x^{l} (A(x) D(x)) = x^{l} (x^{d}+1). So we need A(x) D(x) = x^{d}+1.\n"}
