,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178508769,"data":"       | Thus we can absorb the monomial shift into C(x). So the problem reduces to: does there exist integer polynomial D(x) (maybe Laurent) such that A(x) D(x) = x^{d}+1 for some positive integer d? And D(x) must be a Laurent polynomial (finite support) but we can also think of D(x) as integer polynomial times possibly a monomial factor x^{m} (allow negative exponents). However, if D(x) has negative exponents, we can multiply both sides by appropriate monomial to clear them, but then A(x) would get multiplied, which may break condition of nonnegative exponents. Let's think carefully.\n"}
