,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178512375,"data":"       | We have A(x) is a polynomial with nonnegative exponents (0..8). D(x) may be a Laurent polynomial: D(x) = sum_{j=p}^{q} d_j x^{j} where p may be negative. The product A(x) D(x) = x^{d}+1 is a polynomial with nonnegative exponents (0..d). Since the RHS has only nonnegative exponents, the product must have no negative exponents. This imposes that the minimal exponent of D(x) must be ≥0, because otherwise A(x) D(x) would have negative exponents (since A has nonnegative exponents). Indeed if D has exponent p\u003c0, then the term a_0 d_p x^{p} would be negative exponent unless a_0=0. But a_0 may be zero; but there could be other a_i nonzero and produce negative exponents. However, we can possibly have D with negative exponents if a_0=0 and also a_i=0 for i such that i + p \u003c0. Since A has support only up to 8, we could maybe have D negative exponents as low as -8 and still product nonnegative if a_i zero for those i? But we cannot guarantee; but we can shift negative exponents into monomial factor x^{l} earlier.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17851552,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178516668,"data":"       | Better approach: Since we can choose any k,l, we can shift the product to ensure that D(x) has nonnegative exponents. Indeed we can set l = minimal exponent of product = minimal exponent of A(x)C(x). Since A(x) has minimal exponent 0 (if a_0 maybe zero but we can still have exponent 0 as min). But if a_0 = 0, min exponent of A may be \u003e0 (the smallest i such that a_i ≠0). Let's denote m = min{i | a_i ≠ 0}. Then A(x) = x^{m} A'(x) where A'(x) has constant term nonzero (a_m). Then product A(x) C(x) = x^{m} A'(x) C(x). Since RHS = x^{l}(x^{d}+1), we can set l = m + shift from C maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17851897,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178520096,"data":"       | But we can treat factoring more generally: we need to factor x^{d}+1 into product of two integer Laurent polynomials where one factor A(x) has support within [0,8] (i.e., degree ≤8 and nonnegative exponents). The other factor can be any integer Laurent polynomial (finite support). The shift factor x^{l} can be moved into the other factor.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17852188,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17852326,"data":"       | Thus the existence of such factorization reduces to: does there exist integer polynomial A(x) (nonnegative exponents, degree ≤8) dividing x^{d}+1 in the ring of Laurent polynomials with integer coefficients? Since any factorization in Laurent polynomials can be turned into factorization in ordinary polynomials after multiplying by a monomial to clear negative exponents. But we need A(x) to have nonnegative exponents (i.e., be a polynomial). So we ask: For which integer polynomials A(x) of degree ≤8 does there exist integer polynomial B(x) such that A(x) B(x) = x^{d}+1? (where B(x) may have negative exponents? Actually B(x) must be a Laurent polynomial; but we can multiply both sides by some monomial to get polynomial factorization.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178527433,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178528583,"data":"       | If A(x) divides x^{d}+1 in the ring Z[x] (i.e., there exists integer polynomial B(x) such that A(x) B(x) = x^{d}+1), then we can set C(x) = B(x) (since B has nonnegative exponents). Then we can set l = 0 (k = d). That yields product exactly x^d+1 with two monomials at 0 and d. That's allowed (k and l can be any integers, not necessarily positive). So any A(x) that divides x^{d}+1 for some d\u003e0 qualifies.\n"}
