,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178535995,"data":"       | General condition: There exist integers d\u003e0, t∈Z such that x^{t} (x^{d}+1) is divisible by A(x) in Z[x]; i.e., A(x) | x^{t} (x^{d}+1). Because then we can write x^{t} (x^{d}+1) = A(x) B(x) for some polynomial B(x) with integer coefficients. Then we can set C(x) = x^{l - t} B(x). Since we can choose l appropriately to offset the extra monomial factor. Let's derive systematically.\n"}
