,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178538992,"data":"       | We want A(x) C(x) = x^{k} + x^{l} = x^{l} (x^{d}+1) where d = k-l \u003e0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.1785403,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178541467,"data":"       | Let C(x) be any Laurent polynomial. Write C(x) = x^{p} B(x) where B(x) is a polynomial (nonnegative exponents) and p∈Z (maybe negative). Then product is A(x) x^{p} B(x) = x^{l} (x^{d}+1). So we have A(x) B(x) = x^{l-p} (x^{d}+1). Let t = l-p (integer). So we need A(x) B(x) = x^{t} (x^{d}+1). Since B is polynomial, we need A(x) dividing x^{t} (x^{d}+1) in Z[x]. So condition: there exist integers d\u003e0, t∈Z such that A(x) divides x^{t} (x^{d}+1). Since x^{t} is a monomial, this condition is that A(x) divides (x^{d}+1) up to a factor of x^t; i.e., all prime factors of A(x) are among those of x^{d}+1 or x.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178543825,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178544974,"data":"       | But note that x is irreducible (monomial) and not a unit. So A(x) may contain a factor x (i.e., a_0 = 0). Indeed if a_0 = 0, then A(x) has factor x^m where m = min exponent of nonzero coefficient. So A(x) can be of form x^{m} A'(x) where A'(0) ≠ 0. Then A'(x) must divide x^{d}+1 (since the x^m factor can be absorbed into x^{t}). So the condition reduces to: there exists integer d\u003e0 such that A'(x) divides x^{d}+1. And we also need degree of A ≤8.\n"}
