,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178550053,"data":"       | Thus the classification of shifty functions reduces to integer polynomials A(x) of degree ≤8 with nonnegative exponents (i.e., support within 0..8) such that the primitive part (after factoring out powers of x) divides some cyclotomic polynomial x^{d}+1 (which is not cyclotomic but 2-term polynomial). Actually x^{d}+1 is a sum of two monomials; its factorization over Z is known: x^{d}+1 = ∏_{odd divisors of 2d? Wait: x^{n}+1 factorization: If n is odd, x^{n}+1 = (x+1)Φ_{2n}(x) ... but in general x^{d}+1 = ∏_{r|2d, r∤ d} Φ_r(x). So its irreducible factors over Z are cyclotomic polynomials Φ_{2m} where m|d and m odd? Actually need to recall: x^{n}+1 = ∏_{d|2n, d∤ n} Φ_d(x). So the irreducible factors are cyclotomic polynomials whose indices divide 2n but not n. So they are of form Φ_{2m} where m|n and m odd? Let's verify: For n even, x^{n}+1 = (x^{n/2}+1)^2 - 2x^{n/2} ... Not simple. Let's just treat factorization generically.\n"}
