,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178554625,"data":"       | Thus the condition is that A'(x) must be a divisor of some x^{d}+1. Since d can be arbitrarily large (no bound), the set of possible divisors includes all polynomials whose roots are among the roots of unity of order 2d where d arbitrary. That includes any polynomial whose roots are roots of unity of order a power of 2? Wait: x^{d}+1 = 0 =\u003e x^{d} = -1 =\u003e x^{2d} = 1, but x^{d} ≠ 1 (since -1). So its roots are the 2d-th roots of unity that are not d-th roots of unity (i.e., primitive 2d-th roots of unity of odd order?). Actually the roots are the complex numbers e^{iπ(2k+1)/d} = e^{iπ(2k+1)/d} = primitive 2d-th roots of unity with odd exponent. So they are exactly the primitive 2d-th roots of unity where exponent is odd (i.e., not d-th roots). So the set of all such roots for varying d includes all roots of unity of order a power of 2? Let's examine: For d=1, x+1 roots = -1 (order 2). For d=2, x^2+1 roots = i, -i (order 4). For d=3, roots are e^{iπ/3}, e^{iπ}, e^{i5π/3}? Actually x^3+1 = (x+1)(x^2 - x +1). Roots are -1 (order 2), and primitive 6th roots (order 6). So includes order 6. For d=4, x^4+1 roots are primitive 8th roots (order 8). For d=5, includes primitive 10th roots (order 10). So overall, for any even order 2d, we get primitive 2d-th roots where exponent odd, which includes all primitive roots of even order? Actually any primitive root of order m where m is even can be expressed as a root of x^{m/2}+1? Let's see: Suppose ζ is primitive m-th root of unity, with m even. Then ζ^{m/2} = -1. So ζ is a root of x^{m/2} + 1 = 0. So yes, any primitive root of even order m appears as a root of x^{d}+1 where d = m/2. So the set of all roots of unity of even order (i.e., order divisible by 2) appear. That's all roots of unity except those of odd order. So the union of roots of x^{d}+1 over all d includes all roots of unity of even order. Indeed because for any even m, there exists d=m/2 such that x^{d}+1 has those primitive m-th roots. So the set of possible irreducible factors of some x^{d}+1 are exactly cyclotomic polynomials Φ_m(x) where m is even (i.e., m has factor 2). Because Φ_m(x) divides x^{d}+1 iff m is even and d is a multiple of m/2? Let's verify: For m even, say m = 2r. Then Φ_{2r}(x) divides x^{r}+1? Actually x^{r}+1 = ∏_{d|2r, d∤ r} Φ_d(x). The divisors d of 2r that are not divisors of r are exactly those that have a factor of 2 but not dividing r. Those are the even divisors of 2r that are not divisors of r, i.e., d = 2s where s|r. So the set includes Φ_{2s} for s|r. So indeed Φ_{2r} divides x^{r}+1? Wait, if d = 2r, then d|2r obviously, but does d∤ r? Since 2r does not divide r unless r=0. So yes, Φ_{2r} appears as a factor of x^{r}+1. So any Φ_{2r} divides x^{r}+1. So any cyclotomic polynomial with even index divides some x^{d}+1 (where d = index/2). Good.\n"}
