,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178587175,"data":"       | But there could be many possibilities for D(x) and d. Since coefficients are integers, but divisors of x^{d}+1 have integer coefficients (since cyclotomic polynomials have integer coefficients). However, there are also divisors that are not monic? Actually any divisor of x^{d}+1 in Z[x] must be monic up to unit ±1 (since x^{d}+1 is monic, any divisor can be taken monic). So D(x) can be taken monic (leading coefficient 1) and integer coefficients. Similarly x^{m} factor is monic. So A(x) will be monic (if D monic). However, α coefficients could be any integer, not necessarily monic. Wait, A(x) can have leading coefficient not 1 if we allow factor ±1? Actually divisors of monic polynomial in Z[x] are monic (or have leading coefficient ±1). Because any factorization of monic polynomial yields monic factors (since product of leading coefficients =1). So each factor must have leading coefficient ±1. Since we are dealing with integer coefficients, the leading coefficient must be ±1. So A(x) must be monic up to sign (i.e., leading coefficient ±1). But α can have arbitrary integer coefficients; but if α is not monic, can it still satisfy condition? Let's test: Suppose A(x) = 2x+2 (coeffs 2,2). Then A(x) = 2(x+1). Since x+1 divides x^{d}+1, but factor 2 does not divide x^{d}+1 (since x^{d}+1 is odd constant term? Actually constant term of x^{d}+1 is 1. So 2 does not divide 1, so 2 cannot be factor). So A(x) must be a divisor of x^{d}+1 up to monomial factor, meaning any integer factor beyond ±1 would cause constant term to have factor \u003e1 dividing 1, impossible. So the only possible integer scalar factor is ±1. So A(x) must be monic (or antimonic) with coefficients ±1? Wait monic means leading coefficient 1, but coefficients could be other integers. Example: (x^2 - x + 1) is monic with coefficients 1,-1,1. That's fine. So A(x) can have coefficients not just 0/1, but any integer, as long as polynomial divides x^{d}+1. Since cyclotomic polynomials have coefficients that can be -1,0,1, but some have larger magnitude (e.g., Φ_{105} has coefficients up to 2). However, degree ≤8, so cyclotomic polynomials with degree ≤8 have coefficients limited to -1,0,1 (maybe some have 2?). Let's check. Cyclotomic polynomials up to degree 8: Φ_2 = x+1 (coeffs 1,1). Φ_4 = x^2+1 (coeffs 1,0,1). Φ_6 = x^2 - x + 1 (coeffs 1,-1,1). Φ_8 = x^4 + 1 (coeffs 1,0,0,0,1). Φ_10 = x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1 (coeffs 1,-1,1,-1,1). Φ_12 = x^4 - x^2 + 1 (coeffs 1,0,-1,0,1). Φ_14 = x^6 - x^5 + x^4 - x^3 + x^2 - x + 1 (coeffs 1,-1,1,-1,1,-1,1). Φ_18 = x^6 - x^3 + 1 (coeffs 1,0,0,-1,0,0,1). Φ_20 = x^8 - x^6 + x^4 - x^2 + 1 (coeffs 1,0,-1,0,1,0,-1,0,1). etc. But degree 8 includes up to maybe Φ_30? Actually degree of Φ_n is φ(n). φ(n) ≤8. So n values with φ(n) ≤8: n=1 (Φ_1 = x-1), n=2 (x+1), n=3 (x^2+x+1), n=4 (x^2+1), n=5 (x^4+x^3+x^2+x+1), n=6 (x^2-x+1), n=7 (x^6+x^5+...+1), n=8 (x^4+1), n=9 (x^6+x^3+1), n=10 (x^4-x^3+x^2-x+1), n=11 (x^{10}+...+1), n=12 (x^4-x^2+1), n=13 (x^{12}+...+1), n=14 (x^6-x^5+...+1), n=15 (x^8-x^7+...+1), n=16 (x^8+1), n=17 (x^{16}+...+1), n=18 (x^6-x^3+1), n=20 (x^8-x^6+x^4-x^2+1), n=21 (x^{12} - x^{11} + ... +1), n=22 (x^{10} - x^9 + ...+1), n=24 (x^8 - x^4 + 1), n=26 (x^{12} - x^{11}+...+1), n=28 (x^{12} - x^{11} + ... +1), n=30 (x^8 + x^7 - x^5 - x^4 - x^3 + x + 1). Actually many have degree up to φ(n) which may be up to 8.\n"}
