,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178900615,"data":"       | analysisRemainder 2, not divisible. So Φ_6 does not divide x^6+1. So our earlier reasoning fails: For Φ_{2n} dividing x^{n}+1, we need d such that d/n is odd. For n=3, need d = 3 * odd. d=6 gives ratio 2 even, so not divisible. Indeed x^{6}+1 is not divisible by Φ_6. So we need to choose d = n * (odd). So for n=3, we can choose d=3 (odd multiple 1) -\u003e x^3+1 divisible by Φ_6. But we need same d for all factors. So we need to find d that is a common multiple of all n_i where each quotient d/n_i is odd. This is possible if we take d = L * O where L = lcm of n_i, and O is odd such that for each i, (L*O)/n_i is odd. Since L/n_i may be even for some i. To make product odd, we need O to have appropriate factor of 2 to cancel? Wait O is odd, cannot affect parity. So if L/n_i is even for some i, then (L*O)/n_i will be even regardless of O being odd. So we need L/n_i to be odd for all i. That means that for each i, n_i must contain the highest power of 2 present among all n_i. Because the ratio L/n_i = product of primes to appropriate exponents; parity depends on power of 2. So we need all n_i to have same 2-adic valuation (i.e., the same exponent of 2). Because L includes the maximum exponent of each prime across n_i. If some n_i has lower 2-adic valuation than the max, then L/n_i will have factor 2, making ratio even. So to have all ratios odd, the n_i must have same 2-adic valuation (i.e., all have the same exponent of 2). In other words, the set of n_i must be such that the highest power of 2 dividing any n_i is the same across all n_i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178904623,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178905736,"data":"       | Thus the condition for a product of Φ_{2n_i} to divide some x^{d}+1 is that the n_i have the same 2-adic valuation.\n"}
