,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178908212,"data":"       | Let's verify: Suppose we have n_i = 2^e * m_i where m_i odd. Then Φ_{2n_i} = Φ_{2^{e+1} m_i}. Since 2n_i = 2^{e+1} m_i. The factor divides x^{n_i}+1 = x^{2^e m_i}+1. For d = 2^e m_i * odd = n_i * odd. So the exponent of 2 in n_i is e. So to have a common d, we need all n_i to have same e, else L/n_i will have factor 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178909946,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178911059,"data":"       | Thus the allowed subsets I correspond to sets of cyclotomic polynomials where each n_i has same 2-adic valuation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178912378,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.178913961,"data":"       | But is this condition also sufficient? If all n_i share same 2-adic valuation e, then we can take d = L where L = lcm of n_i (which will have 2-adic valuation e). Since each n_i = 2^e * m_i (odd), L = 2^e * lcm(m_i). Then L/n_i = lcm(m_i)/m_i, which is odd because both numerator and denominator are odd. So each quotient is odd. Hence each Φ_{2n_i} divides x^{L}+1. Therefore product divides x^{L}+1. Good.\n"}
