,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179136381,"data":"       | But we must verify that each such α indeed yields a shifty function, i.e., there exists β. Our condition ensures existence of D dividing x^{d}+1, but we also need to ensure that there exists β with integer coefficients. Since we can set β corresponding to C(x) = x^{l - m} * (x^{d}+1)/D(x). Since D divides x^{d}+1, the quotient is a polynomial with integer coefficients. Then C(x) is a Laurent polynomial (since we may have negative exponent if l - m negative). Choose l = m (or any). Actually we need to produce β such that S_n(α) dot β = 1 at n=k,l. We can set k = m + d (maybe) and l = m (choose). Let's construct explicit β: Let A(x) = x^{m} D(x). Choose d such that D divides x^{d}+1. Let quotient Q(x) = (x^{d}+1)/D(x) ∈ Z[x]. Then define C(x) = x^{l - m} Q(x). Choose l = m (so exponent shift 0). Then C(x) = Q(x). Then product A(x) C(x) = x^{m} D(x) * Q(x) = x^{m} (x^{d}+1) = x^{m} + x^{m+d}. So k = m+d, l = m. Then indeed the product yields two monomials with coefficient 1. So β corresponds to C(x) = Q(x) (which is polynomial). So β has finite support (degree of Q ≤ d). So existence is guaranteed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179139766,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179140857,"data":"       | Thus each allowed (I,m) yields a shifty α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179142114,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179143478,"data":"       | Now we need to verify that there are no other α beyond these. Did we miss possibilities where D is not a product of distinct cyclotomic polynomials with even index? Could D be a divisor of x^{d}+1 that is not a product of cyclotomic polynomials with even index? Actually any divisor of x^{d}+1 over Z[x] is a product of some cyclotomic polynomials Φ_{2r} where r divides d. Since x^{d}+1 = ∏_{r|2d, r∤ d} Φ_r(x). The indices r are divisors of 2d that are not divisors of d. These are exactly the even divisors of 2d (i.e., r = 2s where s|d). So the cyclotomic indices are of the form 2s where s|d. Thus they are indeed even. So any divisor is a product of cyclotomic polynomials Φ_{2s} where s|d. So our classification of D as product of Φ_{2n_i} with n_i dividing d is correct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179147734,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179148946,"data":"       | Thus D must be product of some of those cyclotomic polynomials where each n_i divides d. So the condition that all n_i share same 2-adic valuation is necessary for them to divide a common x^{d}+1. Indeed if we have n_i dividing d, then each n_i must divide d, so they must be divisors of d. Since d is some integer, the set of n_i must be a subset of divisors of d. If d has 2-adic valuation e, then all its divisors have 2-adic valuation ≤ e. However, for each n_i dividing d, we also need that Φ_{2n_i} divides x^{d}+1. The condition for that is that d/n_i is odd, i.e., n_i must have the same 2-adic valuation as d (since if n_i has lower 2-adic valuation, then d/n_i would be even). So indeed all n_i must have the same 2-adic valuation equal to v2(d). So uniform v2 condition is necessary and sufficient.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179151593,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179152774,"data":"       | Thus our enumeration of subsets with uniform v2 is correct.\n"}
