,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179459676,"data":"       | Now we should also verify that there are no other shifty functions beyond these 80 (up to sign). Since α's support limited to 0..8, and coefficients could be any integer, but we enumerated only those that are product of cyclotomic polynomials times monomial. Could there be α that is not of this form but still works? Let's try to brute-force search for all α with coefficients in some bounded range, say -2..2, and check condition via solving for β. Since there are 5^9 = 1,953,125 possibilities (if we restrict to -2..2). That's manageable. For each α, we need to check if there exists β (finite support) and integers k≠l such that dot product property holds. This is a linear system: we need to find β and k,l such that S_n(α)·β = 1 for n=k,l and 0 otherwise.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179464735,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179466301,"data":"       | We can treat unknown β with support limited to some range (maybe -M..M). Since α is zero outside 0..8, the dot product S_n(α)·β depends only on β values at indices where α is nonzero. Specifically, S_n(α)·β = Σ_{i=0}^{8} a_i β_{i-n}. So it's a convolution of a_i with β_j. Since a_i is zero outside 0..8, the output f(n) is determined by β values at indices j such that j = i-n for some i with a_i ≠0 =\u003e j ∈ [n-8, n] maybe. So to get f(n) zero for all n except two positions, we can restrict β's support to some finite interval.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179468763,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179469926,"data":"       | We could search for β with support limited to maybe -8..8 as well? Actually β may need to have support beyond that range because convolution can produce outputs at many n. But we only need f(n) nonzero at two positions, so β could be chosen to have limited support.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179471592,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179472749,"data":"       | We can treat unknown β as vector of length maybe 17 (indices -8..8) and unknown k,l within some range (maybe -8..8). Since α is zero outside [0,8], the convolution f(n) will be zero for |n| large enough (outside some range). Indeed if β has support within [-M,M], then f(n) is zero for n outside [ -M, 8+M] maybe. So we can restrict to some range.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17947454,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179475721,"data":"       | But to guarantee existence of β we might need larger support. However, given α's degree ≤8, we can always choose β as polynomial Q(x) from earlier construction, which has degree d (maybe up to some bound). d can be as large as LCM of n_i, which could be up to maybe 30? Actually n_i up to 15. LCM of some subset may be up to 120? Let's compute worst-case LCM among allowed n_i (with same v2). For v2=0 group: n values 1,3,5,7,9,15. LCM of all is lcm(1,3,5,7,9,15) = lcm(3,5,7,9,15) = lcm(3,5,7,9,15). 9=3^2, 15=3*5. So LCM = 3^2 *5*7 = 315. That's big. So β may have degree up to 315. That's large but still finite.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179478025,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179479657,"data":"       | Thus brute-force search with bounded coefficient range may not capture all possibilities because β could have large degree. But we only need to test α; we can try to solve for β using linear algebra: we need to find β (finite support) and k,l such that convolution yields two ones. This is a linear diophantine system. But we can treat unknown β as infinite but we can restrict to support within some range because only finitely many constraints needed (the convolution must be zero for all n except two). However, there are infinitely many n constraints. But we can use generating function approach: we need A(x) C(x) = x^k + x^l. This is a polynomial identity; C(x) can be any Laurent polynomial. So existence reduces to divisibility condition we derived. So classification is complete.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179482239,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179483379,"data":"       | Thus answer should be 160.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179484535,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179485691,"data":"       | But we must verify that sign factor indeed yields distinct functions: α and -α are distinct functions. The problem asks \"How many shifty functions are there in ℱ?\" So count includes both positive and negative. So answer 160.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179487399,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179490268,"data":"       | We should double-check there is no double counting due to sign equivalence for some α where α = -α (i.e., α = 0). Only zero function equals its negative, but zero is not shifty. So fine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179491907,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179493128,"data":"       | Now we can compute explicit list of α's to verify count 160. We'll generate all polynomials and also their negatives. Then verify that each indeed works by constructing β as described.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179494635,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179495783,"data":"       | Let's produce code to verify for each α (including negative) that there exists β and k,l. We'll compute D and shift m, compute d as LCM of n_i (or any odd multiple). Then compute β polynomial Q(x) = (x^d + 1) / D(x). Then set k = m + d, l = m. Then compute convolution to verify.assistantanalysis to=python codedef construct_beta(alpha_poly):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179497616,"data":"       |     # alpha_poly is sympy expression\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179498806,"data":"       |     # factor out monomial x^m\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179500019,"data":"       |     A_poly = sp.Poly(alpha_poly, x)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179501224,"data":"       |     terms = A_poly.terms()\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17950242,"data":"       |     exps = [exp[0] for exp,coeff in terms if coeff != 0]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179503673,"data":"       |     m_shift = min(exps)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179504816,"data":"       |     D_poly = sp.expand(alpha_poly / x**m_shift)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179506036,"data":"       |     # factor D into cyclotomic polynomials to get n_i\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179507289,"data":"       |     # We'll find n_i by checking which cyclotomic polynomials divide D\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179508554,"data":"       |     n_vals = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179509673,"data":"       |     D = sp.Poly(D_poly, x)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179510875,"data":"       |     # iterate over possible even indices up to 30 (our list)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179512136,"data":"       |     for m,deg,v2 in poly_info:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179513324,"data":"       |         Phi = sp.cyclotomic_poly(m, x)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179514496,"data":"       |         if sp.rem(D_poly, Phi, domain='ZZ') == 0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179515702,"data":"       |             n_vals.append(m//2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179516864,"data":"       |     # compute d as lcm of n_vals (if empty, set d=1)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179518407,"data":"       |     if not n_vals:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179519574,"data":"       |         d = 1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179520723,"data":"       |     else:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179521837,"data":"       |         from math import gcd\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179523078,"data":"       |         def lcm(a,b):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17952419,"data":"       |             return a*b//gcd(a,b)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179525369,"data":"       |         d = n_vals[0]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179526503,"data":"       |         for val in n_vals[1:]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179527634,"data":"       |             d = lcm(d, val)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179530516,"data":"       |     # compute Q = (x^d + 1) / D_poly\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179531723,"data":"       |     Q = sp.expand((x**d + 1) / D_poly)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179532912,"data":"       |     # beta polynomial corresponds to Q (coeffs)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179534158,"data":"       |     beta_poly = Q\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179535312,"data":"       |     k = m_shift + d\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179536478,"data":"       |     l = m_shift\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179537628,"data":"       |     return beta_poly, k, l\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179538796,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179539906,"data":"       | def verify_alpha(alpha_poly):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179541084,"data":"       |     beta_poly, k, l = construct_beta(alpha_poly)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179542265,"data":"       |     # compute convolution f(n) for n in range\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179543484,"data":"       |     # We'll compute using polynomial multiplication\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179544698,"data":"       |     product = sp.expand(alpha_poly * beta_poly)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179545887,"data":"       |     # product should equal x**k + x**l\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.179547067,"data":"       |     expected = x**k + x**l\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.17954821,"data":"       |     return sp.simplify(product - expected) == 0\n"}
