,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.180512364,"data":"       | Thus there are 44 possible α functions (including δ_0). However, we need to verify that each such α indeed satisfies the shifty condition, i.e., there exists β and k,l such that S_n(α)⋆β = indicator_{k,l}. Our analysis suggests that if A(x) divides some 1 + x^d, then we can choose β' = (1 + x^d)/A(x), and then α*β' = 1 + x^d. Then we can set k = 0, l = d (or shift accordingly). Wait, we need S_n(α)⋆β = 1 for n∈{k,l}. Our product A(x)·B(x) = x^k + x^l. If we have A(x)·B'(x) = 1 + x^d, then we can set B(x) = x^k B'(x) where k is chosen arbitrarily? Let's see: Suppose we have A(x)·C(x) = 1 + x^d. Then for any integer k, define B(x) = x^k C(x). Then A(x)·B(x) = x^k + x^{k+d}. So we can set k = any integer; then l = k+d. So we can achieve any pair of positions separated by d. So indeed, existence of C(x) such that A·C = 1 + x^d is sufficient.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.180516941,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.180518094,"data":"       | Thus each A(x) that divides some 1 + x^d yields shifty α. Conversely, if α is shifty, then there exist k,l and β such that A·B = x^k + x^l = x^k (1 + x^{d}) where d = l - k \u003e0. Then dividing both sides by x^k (unit) gives A·B' = 1 + x^d, where B' = x^k B. So A divides 1 + x^d. So the condition is necessary and sufficient.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.180519894,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.180521021,"data":"       | Thus the counting reduces to number of A(x) (non-zero) that divide some 1 + x^d with degree ≤8. We counted 44 subsets of distinct Φ_{2e} factors with total degree ≤8. However, we need to verify that any product of distinct Φ_{2e} with total degree ≤8 indeed divides some 1 + x^d. Since each factor individually divides 1 + x^{e} (since Φ_{2e} divides 1 + x^{e})? Wait, earlier we said 1 + x^d = ∏_{e|d} Φ_{2e}(x). So each Φ_{2e} divides 1 + x^{d} when e divides d. So for a product of several Φ_{2e_i}, we need a d that is a multiple of each e_i (i.e., d is a common multiple). So we can take d = lcm(e_i). Then each Φ_{2e_i} divides 1 + x^{d} (since e_i|d). So the product divides 1 + x^{d}. So yes.\n"}
