,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.180539639,"data":"       | But we should verify via brute-force enumeration: enumerate all integer functions α: Z→Z with support subset of [0,8] and values bounded maybe? But infinite possibilities because coefficients can be arbitrarily large integer. However, condition that α divides 1 + x^d imposes constraints on coefficients; they cannot be arbitrarily large because product with integer polynomial yields 1 + x^d (coeffs 0 or 1). Since 1 + x^d has coefficients 0/1, the coefficients of α must be limited. Indeed, the only possibilities are those divisors with coefficients possibly \u003e1 but still limited. But there might be many possibilities beyond cyclotomic products? Let's test by brute force enumeration for small coefficient ranges (say -2..2) and see if any α not in our set satisfy condition. But infinite possibilities may be large; but we can test up to some bound to gain confidence.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.180545352,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.180546742,"data":"       | But we need to be careful: α could have coefficients larger than 1 but still divide 1 + x^d? For example, consider α(x) = (x+1)^2 = x^2 + 2x + 1. This does not divide 1 + x^d because (x+1)^2 is not squarefree factor of 1 + x^d (since 1 + x^d is squarefree). So not allowed. Similarly, any polynomial with repeated factors cannot divide 1 + x^d. So coefficients must correspond to product of distinct cyclotomic polynomials, which have coefficients 0, ±1, maybe other small integers? Actually some cyclotomic polynomials have coefficients \u003e1 (e.g., Φ_{105} has coefficient 2). But for degree ≤8, the cyclotomic polynomials have coefficients limited to -1,0,1 (I think). Let's check each.\n"}
