,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.181026663,"data":"       | Thus total count = 2 * Σ_{D} (8 - deg(D) + 1) where sum runs over all divisor polynomials D (including D=1). But we must ensure we don't double-count same α from different D,m combos. Could there be overlapping representation? For example, D1 shifted by m1 equals D2 shifted by m2? Could happen if D1 = D2 and m1=m2 obviously same. Could D1 ≠ D2 produce same polynomial after shift? Since D1 and D2 are distinct products of cyclotomic polynomials, they have different factorization; multiplying by monomial cannot change factorization (except adding monomial factor). So they cannot become equal unless they are same up to monomial factor. But D1 and D2 are both monic with constant term 1. Multiplying by monomial x^m yields polynomial with constant term 0 (if m\u003e0). So two different D's cannot produce same polynomial after shift because factorization would differ (monomial factor is the same, but the remaining factor would be D1 vs D2). Since D1 and D2 are distinct irreducible factor sets, they cannot be equal after shift. So no double counting.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.181029699,"data":"       | \n"}
