,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182558175,"data":"       | Now we must verify that there are no additional α beyond these 160 that satisfy condition but are not captured by our classification. We can attempt to brute-force search for α with coefficients limited to some range (e.g., -6..6) and support within 0..8, and test if there exists β, k,l. However, the search space is huge. But we can limit to small coefficient magnitude and see if any extra α appear beyond our set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182560113,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182561259,"data":"       | We can attempt to generate all α with coefficients in [-2,2] maybe and check condition using linear algebra: For a given α, we need to find β (finite support) and distinct k,l such that for all n, S_n α ⋆ β = 1 if n ∈ {k,l}, else 0. This is equivalent to existence of β and d such that α⋆β shifted yields two ones. Equivalent to existence of β' and integer d such that α·β' = 1 + x^d (up to monomial factor). Since α has support within [0,8], we can consider β' with support maybe within some range. But we can treat the equation α·β' = 1 + x^d as polynomial equation: α(x)·β'(x) = 1 + x^d. Since α is known polynomial of degree ≤8, we need to find integer polynomial β'(x) and integer d such that product equals 1 + x^d. This is a Diophantine equation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182564068,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182565406,"data":"       | Given α, we can try to factor α into cyclotomic polynomials times monomial factor. Since α may have arbitrary integer coefficients, factorization may produce irreducible factors not cyclotomic; then it cannot divide 1 + x^d. So we can test for each α (within small coefficient range) whether there exists d such that 1 + x^d is divisible by α in Z[x, x^{-1}]. Equivalent to: there exists integer polynomial γ such that α·γ = 1 + x^d (up to monomial factor). Since α's support is within [0,8], we can compute its content (gcd of coefficients). If content \u003e1, then α cannot divide 1 + x^d (which has coefficients 1). So content must be ±1. So α must be primitive (content 1). Also α must be product of cyclotomic polynomials (since 1 + x^d is product of distinct cyclotomic polynomials). So α must be product of distinct cyclotomic polynomials times a monomial factor.\n"}
