,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182608523,"data":"       | - Represent functions as polynomials with integer coefficients, support in [0,8].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182611425,"data":"       | - Condition translates to existence of β such that α * β = x^k + x^l = x^k (1 + x^d) where d = l - k \u003e 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182612889,"data":"       | - Since monomial factor x^k is a unit, we need α to divide 1 + x^d in Z[x, x^{-1}].\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182614249,"data":"       | - Factorization of 1 + x^d: If d = 2^t·m with m odd, then 1 + x^d = ∏_{f|m} Φ_{2^{t+1}f}(x). Cyclotomic polynomials Φ_{2e} with e = 2^t·f.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182616032,"data":"       | - Hence any divisor of 1 + x^d is product of distinct Φ_{2e} where all e have same 2-adic valuation (the same t). Moreover, any such product with total degree ≤8 can be realized by choosing d = lcm of the e's.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182617607,"data":"       | - So α must be of the form ± x^m·∏_{e∈E} Φ_{2e}(x) where E is a subset of one of the four groups based on v2(e): odd e (v2=0), e≡2 mod 4 (v2=1), e≡4 mod 8 (v2=2), e≡8 (v2=3). The degree of each Φ_{2e} is φ(2e) = φ(e) if e odd, else 2·φ(e) if e even. List degrees: for e=1 (1),2 (2),3 (2),4 (4),5 (4),6 (4),7 (6),8 (8),9 (6),10 (8),12 (8),15 (8).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182619619,"data":"       | - Enumerate subsets within each group whose total degree ≤8. Count: 15 subsets from odd group, 5 from v2=1 group, 3 from v2=2 group, 2 from v2=3 group. Subtract 3 duplicate empty set → total 22 possible divisor polynomials D (including 1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182621199,"data":"       | - For each D of degree d, we may shift by a monomial x^m with 0 ≤ m ≤ 8 - d, and also multiply by -1. This yields (9 - d)·2 distinct α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182622687,"data":"       | - Sum over all D: Σ_{D} 2·(9 - deg(D)) = 2·(9·22 - Σ deg(D)) = 2·(198 - 118) = 160.\n"}
