,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182649443,"data":"       | analysisWe need to understand the problem: We have functions α: Z → Z with finite support (only finitely many nonzero values). The product α ⋆ β = Σ_n α(n)·β(n) is a bilinear form (dot product). Shift operator S_n: (S_n α)(t) = α(t+n). So S_n α is α shifted left by n? Actually S_n α(t) = α(t+n). So if α is supported on [0,8] (since shifty condition: α(m)=0 for m\u003c0 and m\u003e8), then S_n α is supported on [-n, 8-n]. So S_n α ⋆ β = Σ_t α(t+n)·β(t) = Σ_t α(t+n) β(t). Equivalent to Σ_i α(i) β(i-n). It's like convolution: (α ⋆ β) with shift.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182652219,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182653313,"data":"       | Definition: α is shifty if α(m)=0 for all m\u003c0 and m\u003e8 (i.e., support subset of {0,...,8}) and there exists β ∈ F and integers k ≠ l such that for all n ∈ Z, S_n(α) ⋆ β = 1 if n ∈ {k,l} and 0 otherwise. So the cross-correlation between α and β yields a function that is 1 at exactly two distinct shift positions k,l and zero elsewhere.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182655373,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182656483,"data":"       | Thus α is a finite-length integer vector of length up to 9 (positions 0..8). β is also a finite support integer vector (any length). The condition is that the cross-correlation (or convolution) of α and β yields a sequence that is 1 at two positions and zero elsewhere. Equivalent to: define c_n = Σ_{t} α(t+n) β(t) = Σ_i α(i) β(i-n). This is like the discrete cross-correlation (α reversed? but not needed). For each integer shift n, we compute inner product of α shifted by n with β. The condition: c_n = 1 for n = k,l; c_n = 0 otherwise.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182658766,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182659846,"data":"       | Thus α and β are integer sequences with finite support such that the cross-correlation yields a \"two-point\" Kronecker delta (like two spikes) with value 1 each.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18266134,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182662415,"data":"       | We need to count how many α (with support inside 0..8) exist such that there exists some β (any finite support) and some distinct k,l such that the cross-correlation condition holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182663915,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182665032,"data":"       | Essentially we need to know which α can be \"matched\" with some β to produce two unit spikes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18266643,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182667727,"data":"       | Observation: The cross-correlation is a linear functional of β: for each n, c_n = Σ_i α(i) β(i-n). This is like convolution of α with reversed β? Actually define β' where β'(t) = β(-t). Then (α ⋆ β')? Let's not get stuck.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18266943,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182671073,"data":"       | We can think of β as unknown integer vector, and we have linear equations: For each integer n, Σ_i α(i) β(i-n) = δ_{n,k} + δ_{n,l}. Since α has support only 0..8, the sum only involves β at indices i-n where i ∈ [0,8]. So for each n, the sum involves β at indices from (0-n) to (8-n). So each equation is linear combination of β entries with coefficients α(i). This is like a convolution: (α * β_rev)(n) = ... but anyway.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182673156,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182676022,"data":"       | We can consider β as unknown vector with infinite but finite support. The set of equations is infinite, but only finitely many nontrivial equations because α and β have finite support: the cross-correlation will be zero for all n outside some range (the sum of supports). Actually if α supported in [0,8] and β supported in [a,b] (some finite interval), then c_n = Σ_i α(i) β(i-n) will be nonzero only when there exists i in [0,8] and j = i-n in [a,b] =\u003e n = i - j ∈ [0- b, 8 - a]. So c_n is zero outside that interval. So to have c_n = 1 at exactly two positions k,l, we need that the support of c is exactly {k,l}. So the interval [0 - b, 8 - a] must be exactly {k,l} (i.e., the set of n for which there is at least one term). That suggests that the support of β must be such that the possible n values are exactly those two numbers. That seems restrictive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182678913,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182679993,"data":"       | But we can also have cancellations: For some n, there may be contributions from multiple terms that sum to zero. So c_n could be zero even if there are overlapping indices. So support of c may be larger but values zero due to cancellations. So we need to find α for which there exists β giving exactly two unit spikes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182681743,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182683035,"data":"       | We can think of α as a vector a = (a_0, a_1, ..., a_8) with integer entries, not all zero (maybe zero allowed?). The condition: there exists integer vector b (finite support) and distinct integers k,l such that for all n ∈ Z, Σ_{i=0}^8 a_i b_{i-n} = δ_{n,k} + δ_{n,l}. Here b_j denotes β(j). So define convolution: (a * reverse(b))(n) = ... but we can treat as linear equations.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182684907,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182685969,"data":"       | Let’s define b's support maybe also within some range, but we can treat b as infinite sequence with only finitely many nonzero entries.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182687337,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182688436,"data":"       | Define c_n = Σ_{i=0}^8 a_i b_{i-n}. This is like cross-correlation of a and b: c_n = Σ_i a_i b_{i-n} = Σ_i a_i b_{i-n}. Equivalent to (a reversed) * b? Actually define a_rev(i) = a_{-i}. Not needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182689937,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182691049,"data":"       | We can rewrite: Let’s define d_j = b_{-j} (i.e., reversed). Then c_n = Σ_i a_i d_{n-i}. That's convolution: (a * d)(n). Indeed if we set d_j = b_{-j}, then b_{i-n} = d_{n-i}. So c_n = Σ_i a_i d_{n-i} = (a * d)(n). So c = a * d where d is some integer sequence (finite support) (since b finite support =\u003e d finite support). So the condition is: there exists integer sequence d (finite support) such that a * d = δ_{.,k} + δ_{.,l}. So a's convolution with some d yields a sequence that is 1 at positions k,l and 0 elsewhere.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.1826932,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182694267,"data":"       | Thus we need to count number of integer sequences a of length ≤9 (supported on 0..8) for which there exists integer sequence d (finite support) such that a * d = e where e is the sum of two unit impulses at distinct positions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182695779,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182696876,"data":"       | Thus a must be a divisor (in the convolution ring of integer sequences with finite support) of a two-point impulse. Since convolution of finite sequences corresponds to multiplication of polynomials (with integer coefficients) where the index corresponds to exponent. Indeed define polynomial A(x) = Σ_{i=0}^8 a_i x^i. Define D(x) = Σ_{j} d_j x^j (finite). Then convolution a * d corresponds to polynomial multiplication: (A(x) * D(x)) = Σ_n c_n x^n, where c_n = Σ_i a_i d_{n-i}. So indeed c is product polynomial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182700732,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182702092,"data":"       | Thus we need A(x) D(x) = x^k + x^l, where k,l distinct integers (could be negative? Since d can have negative exponents? Wait d_j corresponds to index j (which can be any integer). In polynomial representation, we usually consider exponents integer (could be negative). Since sequences can have support at any integer, not just nonnegative. But a_i only for i between 0 and 8. So A(x) is a polynomial with nonnegative exponents between 0 and 8. D(x) can be a Laurent polynomial (allow negative exponents) because d_j can be for any integer j. However, D(x) must have finite support, so D(x) is a Laurent polynomial (finite sum of integer powers, possibly negative). The product A(x) D(x) must equal x^k + x^l, which is a Laurent monomial sum with two terms (distinct exponents). So we need to factor x^k + x^l as A(x) D(x), where A(x) is a polynomial with degree ≤8, coefficients integer, and D(x) is a Laurent polynomial with integer coefficients.\n"}
