,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182708643,"data":"       | Thus we need to write x^k (1 + x^{d}) = A(x) D(x). Since A(x) has only nonnegative exponents up to 8, we can absorb some power of x into D(x). Indeed D(x) can have negative exponents, so we can shift.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182710157,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182711272,"data":"       | Let’s denote A(x) = Σ_{i=0}^8 a_i x^i. D(x) = Σ_{j=m}^{M} d_j x^j (finite). Then product yields Σ_{n} c_n x^n = Σ_{i,j} a_i d_j x^{i+j}. So the exponents in product are i + j where i in [0,8], j in [m,M]. So the set of exponents is [0+m, 8+M] (contiguous). So the product's support is contiguous interval from min(i+j) to max(i+j). Since a_i may have zeros, but still the range may be smaller; but the sum of intervals of nonzero coefficients may produce gaps if coefficients cancel? However, product of polynomials with integer coefficients cannot have gaps unless cancellations happen. Since we have only two nonzero terms in product, there must be cancellations in intermediate exponents. So we need to find A and D such that product yields exactly two terms.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182713766,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182715002,"data":"       | Thus we need to find factorization of x^k + x^l into product of integer coefficient polynomials where one factor has support limited to exponents 0..8 (nonnegative). This is reminiscent of factoring cyclotomic polynomials? Because 1 + x^d can factor over integers as product of cyclotomic polynomials: 1 + x^d = ∏_{m|2d, m∤d} Φ_m(x). But we also have a factor x^k which is monomial. So A(x) can be any divisor of x^k (1 + x^d) with degree ≤8 and with nonnegative exponents (i.e., polynomial not Laurent). Since A(x) cannot have negative exponents, any monomial factor x^t with t\u003e0 can be part of A(x) or D(x). Similarly, D(x) can have negative exponents to adjust.\n"}
