,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182728529,"data":"       | Since monomial factor x^t is invertible in Laurent ring (its inverse is x^{-t}), but not in polynomial ring. However, we can always move monomial factor between A and D. Since D can have negative exponents, we can absorb any monomial factor into D. So essentially we only need A(x) to divide 1 + x^d in Z[x] (ordinary polynomial) up to a monomial factor. Because we can write x^k + x^l = x^t (1 + x^d). Suppose we choose A(x) = x^s * A0(x) where A0(x) is a polynomial dividing 1 + x^d (with integer coefficients) and s is some integer (≥0 maybe) such that the total degree of A(x) ≤8 and its exponents are nonnegative. Then D(x) = x^{t-s} * (1 + x^d)/A0(x). Since D can have negative exponents if s \u003e t. So any A(x) that is monomial times a divisor of 1 + x^d works.\n"}
