,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182878203,"data":"       | Potential issue: For d even, 1 + x^d = (1 + x^{d/2}) * (1 - x^{d/2})? Actually 1 + x^d factorization includes cyclotomic polynomials for m dividing 2d but not d. So for d even, there are extra factors like Φ_4 = x^2 + 1, etc. So our enumeration for d up to 30 will capture all possibilities.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18287989,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182881183,"data":"       | Now after we have set of base polynomials A0 (including sign), we shift them: for each A0, let deg = degree(A0). For shift s from 0 to 8 - deg, produce polynomial A = x^s * A0. Note: If A0 has constant term 0? Actually A0 divides 1 + x^d, which is monic with constant term 1. So any divisor of it also has constant term dividing 1, so constant term is ±1. Indeed because 1 + x^d evaluated at x=0 gives 1, so any factor evaluated at 0 must be ±1. So constant term of A0 is ±1. So shifting by s yields polynomial with coefficient at exponent s equal to constant term ±1 (i.e., ±1). So α(s) = ±1. So α will have nonzero at the shift start.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182893872,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182895159,"data":"       | Thus α will have at least one nonzero coefficient at the smallest exponent.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182896483,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182897589,"data":"       | Now we need to count distinct α functions (i.e., distinct coefficient vectors). Let's brute-force.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182898957,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182900059,"data":"       | Potential nuance: The shift operator S_n defined as α(t+n). Since α is defined on Z, shifting left or right changes support. For α = x^s A0, the support is from s + min exponent of A0 (which is 0) to s + max exponent of A0 (deg). So indeed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18290169,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182902806,"data":"       | Now we need to ensure that there exist β and distinct k,l such that the convolution condition holds. For α = x^s A0, we can take D(x) = (x^k + x^l) / A(x). Since A divides x^k + x^l in Laurent ring. Since we have A = x^s A0, we can set k,l such that (x^k + x^l) = A(x) D(x). Since we know A0 divides 1 + x^d for some d, we can choose k = s + t, l = s + t + d? Let's see.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182906476,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182907653,"data":"       | If A0 divides 1 + x^d, then there exists D0 such that A0 * D0 = 1 + x^d. Then set A = x^s A0, D = x^{t} D0 where t = something to get x^k + x^l = x^{s+t} (1 + x^d) = x^{k} + x^{l} with k = s+t, l = s+t+d. So we can choose any t integer (including negative) to adjust k,l. Since D can have negative exponents (Laurent), we can always pick t such that D has integer exponents? D = x^{t} D0; D0 is polynomial (nonnegative exponents). If t can be negative, D may have negative exponents, which is allowed. So indeed for any s and any divisor A0, we can find k,l = s + t, s + t + d for any integer t. So condition satisfied. So any α of the form shift of divisor of 1 + x^d works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182910049,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182911186,"data":"       | Thus the counting reduces to counting all distinct α of that form.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182912492,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182913606,"data":"       | Now we need to compute the number of distinct α.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182914873,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182915972,"data":"       | Let's implement brute-force enumeration.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182917563,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182918667,"data":"       | Implementation steps:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182919819,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182920926,"data":"       | - Use sympy's cyclotomic_poly function? Or factor 1 + x**d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182922174,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182923273,"data":"       | - For each d from 1 to maybe 30, factor 1 + x**d over integers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182924523,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182925649,"data":"       | - Get list of irreducible factors (sympy's factor returns product of factors; we can get .args maybe). Actually factor(1 + x**d) returns factorization as product of irreducible polynomials. Use sp.factor(1 + x**d, modulus=None) to factor over integers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18292726,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182928393,"data":"       | - Extract factors: if factor returns something like (x + 1)*(x**2 - x + 1) etc. We can get .as_ordered_factors() maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182929794,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182930894,"data":"       | Simpler: Use sp.factor_list which returns (coeff, [(factor, exponent), ...]).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182932159,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182933272,"data":"       | - For each factor list, collect the distinct irreducible factors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182934529,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182935639,"data":"       | - For each subset of these factors (including empty set) produce product polynomial (multiply). Also include negative sign.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18293701,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18293811,"data":"       | - Keep only those with degree ≤8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182940973,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182942109,"data":"       | - Deduplicate across d.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182943273,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182944426,"data":"       | - Then for each such base polynomial A0 (including sign), generate shifted versions: for shift s from 0 to 8 - deg(A0), produce polynomial A = x**s * A0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182945836,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182946964,"data":"       | - Deduplicate final set of polynomials (coefficient vectors). Count.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182948234,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182949379,"data":"       | Potential issue: Some base polynomial may have degree \u003e8 but after shift maybe still \u003e8; we only keep those with degree ≤8 after shift.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182950912,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182952017,"data":"       | But we also need to ensure that A0's degree ≤8 because after shift deg ≤8 anyway. So we can filter base polynomials with deg ≤8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182953489,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182954556,"data":"       | Now compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182955744,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18295684,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codeimport sympy as sp\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182958057,"data":"       | x = sp.Symbol('x')\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182959217,"data":"       | base_polys = set()\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18296036,"data":"       | # We'll store polynomials as tuple of coefficients (list) from exponent 0 upward.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182961673,"data":"       | def poly_to_tuple(poly):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182962819,"data":"       |     # poly is sympy Poly\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182964029,"data":"       |     coeffs = [int(poly.coeffs()[i]) for i in range(len(poly.coeffs()))]  # this returns descending order\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18296569,"data":"       |     # Actually easier: poly.all_coeffs() returns list from highest degree to constant.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182966984,"data":"       |     coeffs_desc = [int(c) for c in poly.all_coeffs()]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182968263,"data":"       |     # convert to ascending order\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182969412,"data":"       |     coeffs = list(reversed(coeffs_desc))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182970579,"data":"       |     # Remove trailing zeros (should not have)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18297177,"data":"       |     while coeffs and coeffs[-1]==0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182972943,"data":"       |         coeffs.pop()\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18297411,"data":"       |     return tuple(coeffs)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182975286,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182976369,"data":"       | def tuple_to_poly(t):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182977523,"data":"       |     # ascending order list of coefficients\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182978709,"data":"       |     coeffs_desc = list(reversed(t))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182979863,"data":"       |     return sp.Poly(coeffs_desc, x)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182981031,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182982129,"data":"       | max_d = 30\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182985044,"data":"       | for d in range(1, max_d+1):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182986293,"data":"       |     expr = 1 + x**d\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182987496,"data":"       |     coeff, factors = sp.factor_list(expr, modulus=None)  # factor over integers\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182988759,"data":"       |     # factors is list of (factor, exponent)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182989937,"data":"       |     # each factor is sympy Poly or expression\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182991136,"data":"       |     # all exponents are 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182992283,"data":"       |     # Build list of factor Polys\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182993449,"data":"       |     factor_polys = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182994603,"data":"       |     for fac, exp in factors:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182995809,"data":"       |         # convert to Poly\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182996947,"data":"       |         fac_poly = sp.Poly(fac, x, domain='ZZ')\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182998131,"data":"       |         factor_polys.append(fac_poly)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.182999286,"data":"       |     # generate all subsets\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183000443,"data":"       |     n = len(factor_polys)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183001577,"data":"       |     # iterate over subsets via bitmask\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183002737,"data":"       |     from itertools import combinations, chain\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183003911,"data":"       |     # we also consider empty product = 1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18300513,"data":"       |     # generate all subsets\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18300627,"data":"       |     for r in range(n+1):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183007404,"data":"       |         for subset in combinations(factor_polys, r):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183008589,"data":"       |             # product of subset\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183009727,"data":"       |             prod = sp.Poly(1, x, domain='ZZ')\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.1830109,"data":"       |             for f in subset:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183012056,"data":"       |                 prod = prod * f\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183013187,"data":"       |             # include sign +1 and -1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183014391,"data":"       |             for sign in [1, -1]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183015557,"data":"       |                 signed = sp.Poly(sign*prod.as_expr(), x, domain='ZZ')\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183019854,"data":"       |                 deg = signed.degree()\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183021173,"data":"       |                 if deg \u003c= 8:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183022417,"data":"       |                     base_polys.add(poly_to_tuple(signed))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183023866,"data":"       | len(base_polys)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183025377,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183026476,"data":"     [Code]:\n"}
