,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183415617,"data":"       | Now compute final count of shifted polynomials: we got 160. Let's double-check by enumerating all possible α (polynomials of degree ≤8) and testing condition directly: for each α (nonzero polynomial with integer coefficients, support subset of 0..8), check if there exists β (finite support) and k,l such that α ⋆ β spikes at exactly two positions with value 1. This is a more exhaustive search but space is huge because coefficients can be any integer, infinite. But we can restrict to those α that are shift of divisor of 1 + x^d; we have 160. But need to ensure there aren't any α with coefficients beyond those that also satisfy condition but not captured due to some more exotic factorization with cancellations beyond simple divisor-of-1+x^d? Let's think: Suppose A(x) divides x^k + x^l in Laurent ring, but A0 does not divide 1 + x^d? Actually we argued that A0 must divide 1 + x^d. Let's re-derive more formally.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18341858,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183419771,"data":"       | Given A(x) D(x) = x^k + x^l. Let k\u003cl. Then x^k (1 + x^{l-k}) = A(x) D(x). Multiply both sides by x^{-k}: (1 + x^{d}) = x^{-k} A(x) D(x), where d = l - k \u003e0. So A(x) = x^{k} A0(x) where A0(x) = x^{-k} A(x) is a polynomial? Wait A(x) may have minimal exponent m \u003e=0 (since α supported on nonnegative). So we can write A(x) = x^{m} A1(x) where A1(0) ≠ 0 (i.e., not divisible by x). Similarly D(x) may have negative exponents. Then x^{-k} A(x) D(x) = x^{m - k} A1(x) D(x). Since the RHS must be a polynomial (no negative exponents) because LHS 1 + x^d has nonnegative exponents. So we must have m - k + min_exponent(D) \u003e= 0. But we can choose D accordingly. However, the key point is that A1(x) must divide 1 + x^d in Z[x] because D(x) is a Laurent polynomial; we can multiply by appropriate monomial to clear negative exponents.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183422624,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183423766,"data":"       | More concretely, we can write D(x) = x^{t} D0(x) where D0(x) is a polynomial (nonnegative exponents) and t is integer (maybe negative). Then equation becomes A(x) x^{t} D0(x) = x^k (1 + x^d). Write A(x) = x^{s} A0(x) where A0(0) ≠ 0 (i.e., not divisible by x). Then x^{s+t} A0(x) D0(x) = x^k (1 + x^d). Since A0(0) ≠ 0, the left side has minimal exponent s+t. The right side minimal exponent is k. So we must have s+t = k. Then we have A0(x) D0(x) = 1 + x^d. So indeed A0 divides 1 + x^d in Z[x]. So our reasoning holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183427681,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183428836,"data":"       | Thus any shifty α must be of the form x^s A0 where A0 divides 1 + x^d. So enumeration is complete.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183430246,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183431647,"data":"       | Now we need to count distinct α functions. We counted 160. Let's double-check by enumerating all possible α of this form and verifying they are distinct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183433123,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183434253,"data":"       | Also verify that each α indeed yields a valid β (i.e., D0 polynomial). For each base polynomial, compute D0 = (1 + x^d) / A0 (division). Then define β from D = D0 (since we can set shift t = 0). Then α * β = x^s (1 + x^d) = x^{s} + x^{s+d}. So k = s, l = s+d. So condition satisfied.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183436003,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183437093,"data":"       | Thus 160 is answer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18343829,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183439424,"data":"       | But we need to ensure that we counted both signs for each shift. Indeed base_polys includes both signs; shifting preserves sign. So we have both positive and negative versions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183440879,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183442006,"data":"       | Now we must ensure that α must be integer-valued function on Z (i.e., domain all integers). Since our polynomial representation only defines α on nonnegative indices; for negative indices α(m) = 0 (by shifty definition). So we have full function defined.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183443676,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183444757,"data":"       | Thus answer: 160.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183445908,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18344708,"data":"       | But let's double-check that there are no duplicates due to different base polynomials and shifts producing same α. For example, base polynomial (1,) shifted by 0 yields (1,). base polynomial (1,0) shifted by 0 yields (1,0) which is different. But could (1,0) be also obtained from shifting (1,) by 1? That would give (0,1) not same. So distinct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183448907,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183450036,"data":"       | But there could be overlapping: e.g., base polynomial (1,1) shifted by 0 yields (1,1). Could same polynomial be obtained from base polynomial (1) shifted by 0? No. So duplicates unlikely. However, there could be base polynomial (1,0,1) (1 + x^2) and base polynomial (1,0,0,1) shifted by -? Not allowed. So duplicates improbable.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183451764,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183452889,"data":"       | Let's verify that number of distinct α from our enumeration is 160. Also verify that each α is indeed shifty: we can test by constructing β and verifying convolution yields spikes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183454441,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183455773,"data":"       | We'll implement a test for each α: compute A(x) = polynomial, D0 = (1 + x^d) / A0, then compute convolution with β (where β corresponds to D reversed?). Wait we need to check that α ⋆ β (with shift) yields spikes. But we derived using polynomial multiplication: α * D = x^k + x^l. However, α ⋆ β is defined as sum α(n) β(n). The shift condition uses S_n(α) ⋆ β = 1 at k,l. We derived that this corresponds to polynomial product A(x) D(x) = x^k + x^l, where D(x) is β reversed? Actually earlier we defined d_j = β_{-j}, and we got α ⋆ β shifted corresponds to convolution of α with d. Let's verify mapping: For α, define polynomial A(x) = Σ_i α(i) x^i. For β, define polynomial B(x) = Σ_j β(j) x^j. Then S_n(α) ⋆ β = Σ_i α(i+n) β(i) = Σ_i α(i+n) β(i) = Σ_i α(i) β(i-n) (reindex). This is coefficient of x^{?} in product? Let's compute product A(x) * B_rev(x) where B_rev(x) = Σ_j β(j) x^{-j} = Σ_j β(-j) x^{j}. Actually we need to be careful.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183461228,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183462409,"data":"       | Define D(x) = Σ_j β(j) x^{-j}. Then A(x) D(x) = Σ_i Σ_j α(i) β(j) x^{i-j}. The coefficient of x^{n} is Σ_i α(i) β(i-n) = S_n(α) ⋆ β. Indeed coefficient of x^{n} is sum over i of α(i) β(i-n). So S_n(α) ⋆ β = coefficient of x^{n} in A(x) D(x). So the condition that S_n(α) ⋆ β = 1 for n=k,l and 0 otherwise means that A(x) D(x) = x^k + x^l. So D(x) is a Laurent polynomial with integer coefficients (since β integer). So D(x) corresponds to β reversed but with exponents negative.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183464776,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183465888,"data":"       | Thus our earlier mapping is correct: A(x) D(x) = x^k + x^l. So D(x) is the Laurent polynomial we called D. So our construction of D = x^{t} D0 (where D0 polynomial) yields β coefficients as coefficients of D(x) reversed? Actually D(x) = Σ_j β(j) x^{-j}. So β(j) = coefficient of x^{-j} in D(x). So if D(x) = Σ_j d_j x^{j} (with possibly negative exponents), then β(j) = d_{-j}. So D(x) must have integer coefficients, which we have.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183467929,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183469033,"data":"       | Thus our construction yields a valid β.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183470288,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183471637,"data":"       | Now we can test for each α we counted: pick base polynomial A0, find d minimal such that A0 divides 1 + x^d, compute D0 = (1 + x^d) // A0 (polynomial division). Then for shift s, define A = x^s A0, D = D0 (since we can set t = -s? Wait we need A * D = x^k + x^l. We have A = x^s A0, D0 polynomial such that A0 D0 = 1 + x^d. Then A * D0 = x^s (1 + x^d) = x^s + x^{s+d}. So k = s, l = s+d. So D = D0 works (no extra monomial factor). D0 is polynomial with nonnegative exponents. But D(x) must be Laurent polynomial possibly with negative exponents; polynomial is okay (nonnegative exponents). So D0 works.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183473888,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183475014,"data":"       | Thus β can be derived from D0: β(j) = coefficient of x^{-j} in D0. Since D0 has only nonnegative exponents, β will have nonzero values only for j ≤0 (i.e., negative indices). That's allowed because β can be any integer function with finite support (including negative indices). So fine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18347676,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183477866,"data":"       | Thus each α yields a valid β.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183479133,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183480253,"data":"       | Thus answer is 160.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183483188,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183484346,"data":"       | But need to double-check that we didn't miss any α where A0 divides 1 + x^d but A0 is not a divisor of 1 + x^d with integer coefficients? Actually divisors we enumerated include all integer divisors. So fine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183485866,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183486996,"data":"       | Now we should produce final answer: 160.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183488197,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183489331,"data":"       | But before finalizing, let's double-check by brute-force search for α with small coefficient ranges to see if any other α appear. Since coefficients could be large, but we can limit to small absolute values maybe up to some bound, and search for β also within some bound, to see if any α not in our set appear. This is computationally heavy because infinite possibilities, but we can search for α with small coefficient magnitude and see if any satisfy condition but not in our set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18349146,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18349278,"data":"       | Approach: For each α with support subset of 0..8 and coefficients in [-2,2] maybe, we can try to solve for β and k,l using linear algebra. Since condition requires existence of β and k,l such that convolution yields spikes. Equivalent to find integer β and k,l such that A(x) D(x) = x^k + x^l. This is a Diophantine equation in polynomial ring. We can treat unknowns: D(x) unknown Laurent polynomial, but we can limit support to some range, say [-M,M] for some bound. Then we can set up linear equations for coefficients of D to achieve equality. Since we can choose k,l as unknown as well.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183495061,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183496183,"data":"       | But we can also directly compute gcd: For given A(x), compute gcd of A(x) with x^k + x^l? Not known.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183497493,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183498636,"data":"       | Better: For each α, we can compute the set of possible products A(x) * D(x) where D is any Laurent polynomial with integer coefficients (bounded support). This set includes all polynomials that are multiples of A(x) by any Laurent polynomial. So the condition is that there exists a polynomial of the form x^k + x^l (two monomials) in that set. So we need to check if there exist integers k\u003cl such that x^k + x^l is divisible by A(x) in Laurent ring. Equivalent to A(x) divides x^k + x^l. So we can compute the ideal generated by A(x) in Laurent ring: it's all multiples of A(x) by any Laurent polynomial. The condition is that some binomial belongs to this ideal. So we can check if there exists k,l such that A(x) | x^k + x^l.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183501153,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183502277,"data":"       | Thus we can test for each α by checking if there exists d\u003e0 and integer s such that A(x) divides x^s (1 + x^d). Since monomial factor can be absorbed. So we need to check if A(x) divides 1 + x^d after factoring out monomial factor.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183503891,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183505013,"data":"       | Thus we can compute for each α: Let m = min exponent where coefficient nonzero (i.e., smallest i with a_i ≠ 0). Write A(x) = x^m * A0(x) where A0(0) ≠ 0. Then test if there exists d\u003e0 such that A0 divides 1 + x^d. That's exactly our condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183506713,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183507823,"data":"       | Thus we can implement a test: For each α (represented by tuple), compute A0 and test divisibility by 1 + x^d for d up to some bound (maybe up to 30). If any works, α is shifty. Then compare with our set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183511073,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183512223,"data":"       | Let's brute-force all α with coefficients in [-2,2] (excluding all zero) and see if any shifty α not in our set.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183513656,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183514986,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codedef is_shifty(alpha_tuple):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18351625,"data":"       |     # alpha_tuple is coefficients from exponent 0 upward (nonzero at some indices)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183517553,"data":"       |     # find min exponent with nonzero\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183518743,"data":"       |     # ensure support within 0..8\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183519957,"data":"       |     # find m\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183521104,"data":"       |     m = None\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183522257,"data":"       |     for i, coeff in enumerate(alpha_tuple):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183523483,"data":"       |         if coeff != 0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18352467,"data":"       |             m = i\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183525816,"data":"       |             break\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183526946,"data":"       |     if m is None:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183528076,"data":"       |         return False\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183529266,"data":"       |     # factor out monomial x^m\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183530443,"data":"       |     # A0 coefficients: shift left by -m\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183531643,"data":"       |     A0 = tuple(alpha_tuple[i] for i in range(m, len(alpha_tuple)))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183532919,"data":"       |     # ensure A0 constant term nonzero\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183534123,"data":"       |     if A0[0] == 0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183535314,"data":"       |         # if constant term zero, then A0 divisible by x, which contradict minimal m\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183536597,"data":"       |         return False\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183537748,"data":"       |     # Now test divisibility by 1 + x^d for some d up to 30 (should be enough)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183539003,"data":"       |     # Use sympy polynomial division\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183540183,"data":"       |     A0_poly = sp.Poly(poly_from_tuple(A0), x, domain='ZZ')\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183541428,"data":"       |     for d in range(1, 31):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18354265,"data":"       |         expr = 1 + x**d\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183543776,"data":"       |         # compute remainder of expr divided by A0_poly\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183544983,"data":"       |         rem = sp.rem(expr, A0_poly, domain='ZZ')\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183546231,"data":"       |         if rem == 0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183547366,"data":"       |             return True\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183548506,"data":"       |     return False\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183549666,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183550773,"data":"       | # generate all alpha tuples with coefficients in [-2,2] and degree up to 8\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183553744,"data":"       | def generate_alpha_tuples(max_coeff=2):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183554976,"data":"       |     from itertools import product\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183556236,"data":"       |     # length up to 9 (0..8). We'll generate all length 9 tuples; but we can allow trailing zeros.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183557544,"data":"       |     # We'll generate all possible tuples of length 9 with entries in [-2,2] and not all zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183558831,"data":"       |     # Then we will trim trailing zeros to canonical form.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183560083,"data":"       |     all_set = set()\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183561224,"data":"       |     for coeffs in product(range(-max_coeff, max_coeff+1), repeat=9):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183562486,"data":"       |         if all(c==0 for c in coeffs):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.1835637,"data":"       |             continue\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183564851,"data":"       |         # Trim trailing zeros\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183566083,"data":"       |         t = coeffs\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183567576,"data":"       |         while t and t[-1]==0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183568733,"data":"       |             t = t[:-1]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183569879,"data":"       |         all_set.add(t)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183571017,"data":"       |     return all_set\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18357215,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183573273,"data":"       | alpha_set = generate_alpha_tuples(2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18357442,"data":"       | len(alpha_set)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183575568,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18357665,"data":"     [Code]:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18357778,"data":"       | def is_shifty(alpha_tuple):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183578976,"data":"       |     # alpha_tuple is coefficients from exponent 0 upward (nonzero at some indices)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183580243,"data":"       |     # find min exponent with nonzero\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183581414,"data":"       |     # ensure support within 0..8\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183582606,"data":"       |     # find m\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183583743,"data":"       |     m = None\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183584871,"data":"       |     for i, coeff in enumerate(alpha_tuple):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183586063,"data":"       |         if coeff != 0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18358718,"data":"       |             m = i\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183588306,"data":"       |             break\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183589429,"data":"       |     if m is None:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183590553,"data":"       |         return False\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18359175,"data":"       |     # factor out monomial x^m\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183592918,"data":"       |     # A0 coefficients: shift left by -m\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183594139,"data":"       |     A0 = tuple(alpha_tuple[i] for i in range(m, len(alpha_tuple)))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183595386,"data":"       |     # ensure A0 constant term nonzero\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183600337,"data":"       |     if A0[0] == 0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18360154,"data":"       |         # if constant term zero, then A0 divisible by x, which contradict minimal m\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183602824,"data":"       |         return False\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183604043,"data":"       |     # Now test divisibility by 1 + x^d for some d up to 30 (should be enough)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183605346,"data":"       |     # Use sympy polynomial division\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183606553,"data":"       |     A0_poly = sp.Poly(poly_from_tuple(A0), x, domain='ZZ')\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183607803,"data":"       |     for d in range(1, 31):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183608946,"data":"       |         expr = 1 + x**d\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183610087,"data":"       |         # compute remainder of expr divided by A0_poly\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183611288,"data":"       |         rem = sp.rem(expr, A0_poly, domain='ZZ')\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183612513,"data":"       |         if rem == 0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183613641,"data":"       |             return True\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183614814,"data":"       |     return False\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183615974,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183617079,"data":"       | # generate all alpha tuples with coefficients in [-2,2] and degree up to 8\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183618344,"data":"       | def generate_alpha_tuples(max_coeff=2):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183619524,"data":"       |     from itertools import product\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183620728,"data":"       |     # length up to 9 (0..8). We'll generate all length 9 tuples; but we can allow trailing zeros.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183622043,"data":"       |     # We'll generate all possible tuples of length 9 with entries in [-2,2] and not all zero.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183623357,"data":"       |     # Then we will trim trailing zeros to canonical form.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183624623,"data":"       |     all_set = set()\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183626036,"data":"       |     for coeffs in product(range(-max_coeff, max_coeff+1), repeat=9):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183627299,"data":"       |         if all(c==0 for c in coeffs):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183628491,"data":"       |             continue\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183629609,"data":"       |         # Trim trailing zeros\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183630793,"data":"       |         t = coeffs\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183631931,"data":"       |         while t and t[-1]==0:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183633098,"data":"       |             t = t[:-1]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.183634263,"data":"       |         all_set.add(t)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":2937.18363542,"data":"       |     return all_set\n"}
