,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.223918906,"data":"       | analysisWe need to compute product over k=0..12 of (2 - 2 ω^k + ω^{2k}) where ω is a primitive 13th root of unity (i.e., ω^13 = 1, ω ≠ 1). Since ω^k runs over all 13th roots of unity (including 1) as k varies from 0 to 12. So product over all 13th roots ζ of (2 - 2 ζ + ζ^2). So we need product_{ζ^13=1} (ζ^2 - 2 ζ + 2). That's a polynomial evaluated at all roots. This product equals resultant of x^13 - 1 and f(x) = x^2 - 2 x + 2 maybe? Or we can compute as constant term of some polynomial.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.22392323,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.223924463,"data":"       | Specifically, product_{ζ: ζ^13=1} f(ζ) = resultant(x^13 - 1, f(x)). Up to sign maybe? For monic polynomials: resultant(P, Q) = ∏_{P(α)=0} Q(α) = leading_coefficient(Q)^{deg P} ∏_{α} Q(α). Actually resultant(P, Q) = a_n^{m} ∏_{i=1}^n Q(α_i) where a_n is leading coefficient of P (monic =\u003e 1), m = deg Q. Wait formula: resultant(P, Q) = a_n^{m} ∏_{i=1}^n Q(α_i) = (-1)^{nm} b_m^{n} ∏_{j=1}^m P(β_j). Where P monic degree n, Q degree m with leading coefficient b_m. So product_{α_i} Q(α_i) = resultant(P, Q) because a_n = 1. So product = resultant(x^13 - 1, x^2 - 2x + 2). So we can compute resultant.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.223937725,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.223940275,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can compute directly using complex numbers: product over roots of unity of (ζ^2 - 2 ζ + 2). Since ζ runs over all roots, we can factor expression maybe as (ζ - 1)^2 + 1? Let's see: ζ^2 - 2 ζ + 2 = (ζ - 1)^2 + 1. Indeed (ζ-1)^2 = ζ^2 -2ζ +1, add 1 gives ζ^2 -2ζ +2. So f(ζ) = (ζ - 1)^2 + 1 = (ζ - 1)^2 + 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.223942425,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.223943556,"data":"       | But not sure if that simplifies product.\n"}
