,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224170049,"data":"       | Let's denote variable x = ω. Then we consider polynomial f(t) = 2 - 2 t + t^2 = (t^2 - 2t + 2). Evaluate at t = ω^k for k=0..12. So product P = ∏_{k=0}^{12} (ω^{2k} - 2 ω^k + 2). That's like evaluating polynomial f(t) at all 13th roots of unity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224171729,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224172831,"data":"       | We can think of product over all roots of unity of f(t) = ∏_{ζ^13=1} f(ζ). This is reminiscent of evaluating resultant of f(t) and t^{13} - 1 maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.22417427,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224175361,"data":"       | Indeed, product_{ζ: ζ^{13}=1} f(ζ) = resultant(f(t), t^{13} - 1) up to sign maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.2241767,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224177802,"data":"       | Specifically, resultant of two polynomials A(t) and B(t) is ∏_{α: A(α)=0} B(α)^{mult} = leading_coefficient(A)^{deg B} ∏_{β: B(β)=0} A(β)^{mult}. So product_{ζ: B(ζ)=0} A(ζ) = resultant(A,B) / leading_coefficient(A)^{deg B}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224179496,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224180605,"data":"       | Here A(t) = f(t) = t^2 - 2t + 2, degree 2, leading coefficient 1. B(t) = t^{13} - 1, degree 13, leading coefficient 1. So product_{ζ: B(ζ)=0} A(ζ) = resultant(A,B) (since leading coeff of A is 1). So P = resultant(t^2 - 2t + 2, t^{13} - 1).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224182245,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224183331,"data":"       | Thus compute resultant.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224184481,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224185597,"data":"       | Resultant of quadratics and t^{13} - 1 can be computed via evaluating f at roots of unity, but we can compute algebraically using factorization: resultant(A,B) = ∏_{i=1}^{deg A} B(α_i) where α_i are roots of A. So we can compute B evaluated at the two roots of A.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224187249,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224188312,"data":"       | Let α, β be roots of t^2 - 2t + 2 = 0. Solve: t = [2 ± sqrt(4 - 8)]/2 = [2 ± sqrt(-4)]/2 = 1 ± i. So α = 1 + i, β = 1 - i. Indeed t^2 - 2t + 2 = (t - (1+i))(t - (1-i)). So resultant = B(α) * B(β) = (α^{13} - 1)*(β^{13} - 1). Since leading coefficient 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224190042,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224191103,"data":"       | Thus P = ( (1+i)^{13} - 1 ) * ( (1-i)^{13} - 1 ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.22419231,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224193386,"data":"       | Now we need to compute integer value of this product. Since α and β are conjugates, product will be real, indeed integer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224194753,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224195845,"data":"       | Compute (1+i)^{13} and (1-i)^{13}. We can compute using binomial theorem or using complex numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224197129,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224198306,"data":"       | Let’s compute (1+i) in polar form: magnitude sqrt(2), argument π/4. So (1+i)^{13} = (sqrt(2))^{13} * e^{i*13π/4} = 2^{13/2} * e^{i*13π/4}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224201777,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224203009,"data":"       | But we need to compute exactly maybe as integer + i integer? Since (1+i)^n yields integer + i integer. Indeed (1+i)^n = 2^{n/2} * (cos(nπ/4) + i sin(nπ/4)). For n integer, cos and sin values are rational multiples of sqrt(2) maybe but for n multiple of 2? Let's compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224204717,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224205841,"data":"       | 13π/4 = 3π + π/4 (since 12π/4 = 3π). So e^{i*13π/4} = e^{i*3π} * e^{iπ/4} = (-1)^3 * e^{iπ/4} = - e^{iπ/4} = - (cos π/4 + i sin π/4) = - (√2/2 + i √2/2) = -(√2/2) - i (√2/2). Multiply by 2^{13/2} = 2^{6} * sqrt(2) = 64 * sqrt(2). So (1+i)^{13} = 64 * sqrt(2) * ( - √2/2 - i √2/2 ) = 64 * sqrt(2) * ( - √2/2 ) + 64 * sqrt(2) * ( - i √2/2 ) = 64 * ( - (2)/2 ) + 64 * ( - i (2)/2 ) = 64 * ( -1 ) + 64 * ( - i ) = -64 - 64 i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224208188,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224209263,"data":"       | Wait check: sqrt(2) * √2 = 2. So sqrt(2)*(√2/2) = (2)/2 =1. Indeed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224210555,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224211631,"data":"       | Thus (1+i)^{13} = -64 - 64 i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224212794,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224213865,"data":"       | Similarly (1-i)^{13} = conjugate: -64 + 64 i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224215092,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.22421618,"data":"       | Now compute α^{13} - 1 = (-64 - 64 i) - 1 = -65 - 64 i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224217447,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224218505,"data":"       | β^{13} - 1 = (-64 + 64 i) - 1 = -65 + 64 i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224219748,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224220826,"data":"       | Product: (-65 - 64 i) * (-65 + 64 i) = (-65)^2 - (64 i)^2? Actually (a - bi)(a + bi) = a^2 + b^2. But note a = -65, b = 64. So product = (-65)^2 + 64^2 = 65^2 + 64^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.22422222,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224223283,"data":"       | Compute: 65^2 = 4225. 64^2 = 4096. Sum = 8321.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224224443,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224225551,"data":"       | Thus P = 8321.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224226682,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224227747,"data":"       | Now we need remainder when divided by 1000. 8321 mod 1000 = 321.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224228989,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224230064,"data":"       | Thus answer 321.\n"}
