,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224279709,"data":"       | But we must ensure that ω is any 13th root of unity not equal to 1 (i.e., primitive or not). The product over all 13th roots of unity includes all of them regardless of primitive. Since ω can be any root besides 1, the set {ω^k | k=0..12} is still the full set of 13th roots of unity (maybe in different order). Indeed if ω is a primitive root, then its powers generate all roots. If ω is not primitive but not 1, then its order divides 13 (which is prime), so its order must be 13; thus any non-1 13th root of unity is primitive. So indeed ω is primitive.\n"}
