,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224287477,"data":"       | But we need to present reasoning elegantly, perhaps using resultant or using factorization.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224290987,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224292183,"data":"       | Alternative approach: Recognize product = ∏_{k=0}^{12} (ω^{2k} - 2 ω^k + 2). Let y = ω^k. Then product = ∏_{y^13=1} (y^2 - 2 y + 2). This is equal to ∏_{y^13=1} ( (y - 1)^2 + 1 ). Indeed y^2 - 2y + 2 = (y-1)^2 + 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224293792,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224295275,"data":"       | Thus product = ∏_{y^13=1} ((y-1)^2 + 1). Could be expressed as ∏_{y^13=1} ( (y-1) + i ) * ( (y-1) - i ) = ∏_{y^13=1} ( y - (1 - i) ) * ∏_{y^13=1} ( y - (1 + i) ). Each product is ( (1 - i)^{13} - 1 ) maybe with sign? Actually product_{y^13=1} (y - a) = a^{13} - 1 (since polynomial t^{13} - 1 evaluated at a). Indeed t^{13} - 1 = ∏_{y^13=1} (t - y). So plugging t = a gives a^{13} - 1 = ∏_{y^13=1} (a - y) = (-1)^{13} ∏_{y^13=1} (y - a) = -∏_{y^13=1} (y - a). Since 13 odd, product_{y} (y - a) = -(a^{13} - 1). Wait compute: ∏_{y} (t - y) = t^{13} - 1. So set t = a: ∏_{y} (a - y) = a^{13} - 1. But we have product over (y - a) = (-1)^{13} ∏_{y} (a - y) = - (a^{13} - 1). Since 13 odd. So product_{y} (y - a) = -(a^{13} - 1). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224298171,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224299277,"data":"       | Thus product = ∏_{y} ((y - (1-i)) * (y - (1+i))) = (∏_{y} (y - (1-i))) * (∏_{y} (y - (1+i))) = [ - ((1-i)^{13} - 1) ] * [ - ((1+i)^{13} - 1) ] = ((1-i)^{13} - 1) * ((1+i)^{13} - 1). Same as earlier.\n"}
