,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224314689,"data":"       | analysisWe need to compute product over k=0 to 12 of (2 - 2 ω^k + ω^{2k}) where ω is a primitive 13th root of unity (i.e., ω^13 = 1, ω ≠ 1). Since ω is a 13th root of unity, the set {ω^k | k=0,...,12} includes all 13th roots of unity. The product is independent of which primitive root we choose? Because the set of values ω^k cycles through all roots. So the product is symmetric. So we can treat ω as a primitive root.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224316747,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224317839,"data":"       | Goal: compute product P = ∏_{k=0}^{12} (2 - 2 ω^k + ω^{2k}) = ∏_{k=0}^{12} (ω^{2k} - 2 ω^k + 2). This is reminiscent of evaluating polynomial at roots of unity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224319323,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224320603,"data":"       | Define variable x = ω^k, where x runs over all 13th roots of unity (including 1). Then each factor is f(x) = x^2 - 2 x + 2 = (x - 1)^2 + 1. So product over all 13th roots of unity of f(x). So P = ∏_{ζ^13=1} (ζ^2 - 2 ζ + 2). That's the product of evaluating polynomial g(z) = z^2 - 2z + 2 at each 13th root of unity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224324167,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224325283,"data":"       | We can compute this product using properties of cyclotomic polynomials. Let ζ run over roots of unity. Then product_{ζ: ζ^n=1} (z - ζ) = z^n - 1. So product_{ζ} f(ζ) = ?\n"}
