,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224346865,"data":"       | Because each factor product yields 1 - a^{13}. So P = (1 - (1+i)^{13})(1 - (1-i)^{13}).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224353245,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224354405,"data":"       | Now we need remainder of P modulo 1000 (i.e., integer remainder). But P is a complex number? Actually the product should be real, because complex conjugate factors produce conjugates. Indeed (1 - (1+i)^{13}) and its conjugate (1 - (1-i)^{13}) are conjugates, product is real and integer? Let's see. Since (1+i)^{13} and (1-i)^{13} are conjugates (since (1-i) = conjugate of (1+i)). So product is |1 - (1+i)^{13}|^2, which is a non-negative integer (since it's sum of squares). Indeed should be integer because the original product is symmetric with rational coefficients (since polynomial has integer coefficients). So P is integer.\n"}
