,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224475617,"data":"       | We can compute resultant via Euclidean algorithm or using formula: For monic polynomials A(x)=x^n-1, B(x)=x^2 - 2x + 2. The resultant = ∏_{β: B(β)=0} A(β) = ∏_{β: β^2 - 2β + 2 = 0} (β^13 - 1). Since resultant is symmetric: Res(A,B) = a_n^m ∏_{i=1}^n B(α_i) = (-1)^{nm} b_m^n ∏_{j=1}^m A(β_j). Both monic, so a_n=b_m=1. So we can compute product over the two roots β of B of (β^13 - 1). That may be simpler: compute β satisfying β^2 - 2β + 2 = 0 =\u003e β = 1 ± i. Indeed solve: β = (2 ± sqrt(4 - 8))/2 = (2 ± sqrt(-4))/2 = (2 ± 2i)/2 = 1 ± i. So the roots are β1 = 1 + i, β2 = 1 - i.\n"}
