,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224484559,"data":"       | We can compute (1+i)^13 using binomial or polar form: 1+i = sqrt(2) * e^{iπ/4}. Then (1+i)^13 = (sqrt(2))^{13} * e^{i 13π/4} = 2^{13/2} * e^{i (13π/4)}. Since 13π/4 = (12π/4 + π/4) = 3π + π/4 = π/4 + 3π. e^{i 3π} = -1. So e^{i 13π/4} = e^{i π/4} * e^{i 3π} = e^{i π/4} * (-1) = - e^{i π/4} = - (cos π/4 + i sin π/4) = - (√2/2 + i √2/2) = -(√2/2)(1 + i). So (1+i)^13 = 2^{13/2} * ( - (√2/2)(1 + i) ) = - 2^{13/2} * ( √2/2 ) * (1 + i) = - 2^{13/2} * 2^{1/2} / 2 * (1 + i) = - 2^{(13+1)/2} / 2 * (1 + i) = - 2^{14/2} / 2 * (1 + i) = - 2^{7} / 2 * (1 + i) = - 2^{6} * (1 + i) = -64 (1 + i). Wait compute: 2^{13/2} = 2^{6.5} = 2^6 * sqrt(2) = 64 * sqrt(2). Multiply by -(√2/2) = -(sqrt(2)/2). So product = -64 * sqrt(2) * sqrt(2)/2 = -64 * (2)/2 = -64 * 1 = -64. Wait there is also factor (1+i). Let's compute carefully:\n"}
