,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224830136,"data":"       | Now compute (1 ± i)^{13}. Since (1 + i) = sqrt(2) * e^{iπ/4}. So (1 + i)^{13} = (sqrt(2))^{13} * e^{i 13π/4} = 2^{13/2} * e^{i (13π/4)}. Since 13π/4 = 3π + π/4 = (3π) + π/4. e^{i 3π} = -1, so e^{i 13π/4} = e^{i (π/4)} * e^{i 3π} = - e^{i π/4} = - (cos π/4 + i sin π/4) = - (√2/2 + i √2/2) = -(√2/2) - i (√2/2). So (1 + i)^{13} = 2^{13/2} * ( - √2/2 - i √2/2 ) = 2^{13/2} * ( - √2/2 ) * (1 + i?) Wait compute: multiply: 2^{13/2} * ( - √2/2 - i √2/2 ) = -2^{13/2} * √2/2 * (1 + i?) Actually factor -√2/2: - (√2/2) (1 + i). So (1 + i)^{13} = - (√2/2) * 2^{13/2} * (1 + i) = - 2^{13/2} * √2/2 * (1 + i) = - 2^{13/2} * 2^{1/2} / 2 * (1 + i) = - 2^{(13+1)/2} / 2 * (1 + i) = - 2^{7} / 2? Let's compute: 13/2 + 1/2 = 14/2 = 7. So 2^{7} = 128. Then divide by 2 =\u003e 64. So (1 + i)^{13} = -64 (1 + i). Check: 2^{13/2} = sqrt(2^{13}) = sqrt(8192) = 2^{6.5} = 2^{6} * sqrt(2) = 64 * sqrt(2). Multiply by -√2/2 = -(sqrt(2)/2). So product = -64 * sqrt(2) * sqrt(2)/2 = -64 * (2)/2 = -64. Wait we also have factor (1 + i). Let's recompute carefully.\n"}
