,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224957531,"data":"       | Thus product over all ζ of ((ζ - 1)^2 + 1). Since ζ runs over roots of unity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224958853,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224960061,"data":"       | We can think of polynomial g(x) = (x - 1)^2 + 1 = x^2 - 2x + 2. Evaluate product over all roots of unity of g(ζ). This product is equal to resultant of g(x) and x^{13} - 1 (up to sign). Indeed, product_{ζ: ζ^{13}=1} g(ζ) = Res(g, x^{13} - 1) / leading_coefficient(g)^{deg(x^{13} - 1)}? Wait formula: resultant Res(p,q) = a_n^m ∏_{p(α)=0} q(α) = b_m^n ∏_{q(β)=0} p(β). For p(x) = x^{13} - 1 (degree n=13, leading coefficient a_n=1). q(x) = g(x) (degree m=2, leading coefficient b_m=1). Then Res(p,q) = ∏_{p(α)=0} q(α) = ∏_{α^{13}=1} g(α). So product we need equals Res(x^{13} - 1, g(x)). Since leading coefficients are 1, no extra factor.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224962556,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224963629,"data":"       | Thus product = resultant of x^{13} - 1 and x^2 - 2x + 2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224964843,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224965932,"data":"       | Compute resultant. Since both degrees small, we can compute directly: resultant = product_{i=0}^{12} g(ζ_i) where ζ_i are 13th roots. That's what we need.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224967352,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3009.224968586,"data":"       | Alternatively compute via formula: resultant of two polynomials can be computed as det of Sylvester matrix, but easier: compute roots of g(x) and evaluate x^{13} - 1 at them, then product (with sign). Using property: Res(p,q) = b_m^n ∏_{q(β)=0} p(β). Since b_m = 1, n = 13. So compute p(β) for β being roots of g(x). Let β1, β2 be the two roots of g(x). Then product = ∏_{i=1}^{2} (β_i^{13} - 1). Since b_m^n = 1^13 = 1. So product = (β1^{13} - 1)(β2^{13} - 1). This may be easier.\n"}
