,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528954911,"data":"       | analysisWe need to count subsets of {1,...,10} that contain exactly one pair of consecutive integers. That is, among all pairs (i,i+1) with i from 1 to 9, exactly one such pair appears in the subset, and no other consecutive pair appears. Also the subset may contain other elements, but they must not create any other consecutive pair. That is, the subset may contain isolated elements (non-consecutive) and exactly one block of size 2 consisting of two consecutive numbers. Also note that larger blocks of length \u003e2 contain multiple consecutive pairs, e.g., {3,4,5} contains pairs (3,4) and (4,5) two pairs, so not allowed. So we need subsets where the set of consecutive pairs present is exactly one. Equivalent to the subset's elements can be partitioned into isolated points and exactly one adjacent pair (block of size 2). No other adjacent elements.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.52895949,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528960695,"data":"       | Thus we need to count subsets of {1,...,10} that contain a specific pair of consecutive numbers (i,i+1) and do not contain any other consecutive numbers. Also the subset may include other numbers but they must be at distance at least 2 from any other selected number, including the pair's elements. That is, cannot include i-1 (since that would be consecutive with i) nor i+2 (since that would be consecutive with i+1). Also cannot include any other pair j,j+1 for j ≠ i.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528962753,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528963858,"data":"       | Thus we need to count subsets S such that:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528965107,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528966195,"data":"       | - Contains i,i+1 for some i ∈ {1,...,9}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528967431,"data":"       | - Does not contain any other consecutive pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528968688,"data":"       | - Also cannot contain i-1 or i+2 (to avoid extra consecutive pairs). But also cannot contain any other j,j+1 pair. So any other element selected must be isolated (no neighbor selected).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528970173,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528971502,"data":"       | Thus we can think of constructing subsets by selecting a \"special block\" of length 2 at position i, and then selecting any subset of the remaining numbers, subject to the constraint that no two selected numbers are adjacent, and also cannot select numbers adjacent to the block (i-1,i+2). So effectively we remove the block and its immediate neighbors from the pool for selecting isolated elements, and then we need to count subsets of the remaining numbers where no two are adjacent (i.e., independent sets of a path graph). However, note that the remaining numbers are not contiguous; they are the original set minus {i-1,i,i+1,i+2} (where i-1 or i+2 may be out of range at boundaries). The remaining numbers form up to three intervals: left side from 1 to i-2 (if i\u003e=3) and right side from i+3 to 10 (if i\u003c=8). Within each interval, we can select any subset with no consecutive numbers (i.e., independent sets of a path). The intervals are independent (no adjacency across intervals). So the total number for a given i is product of numbers of independent sets in each interval.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528974744,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.52897589,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute for each i, the number of subsets of the left interval of length L = i-2 (if i\u003e=2? Actually left interval from 1 to i-2 inclusive; length L = max(0, i-2).) with no consecutive elements, and similarly right interval of length R = 10 - (i+2) = 8 - i? Wait compute: right interval start = i+3, end =10, length = 10 - (i+3) +1 = 8 - i? Let's check: if i=1, right interval from 4 to 10 length =7 (since 10-4+1=7). 8 - i =7 correct. If i=9, right interval from 12? Actually i+3 =12 \u003e10, so right interval length =0. 8 - i = -1? Wait for i=9, 8-9 = -1 not correct. So formula only works for i ≤ 8. For i=9, right interval length =0. So we need piecewise.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528978373,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528979499,"data":"       | Better: For each i, left length L = i-2 if i≥2 else 0? Actually if i=1, left interval from 1 to -1? none, L=0. So L = max(0, i-2). Right length R = max(0, 10 - (i+2)). Because we exclude i+3 onward? Wait we exclude i+2 (adjacent to block) but we can include i+3 onward. So right interval start = i+3, end =10, length = 10 - (i+3) +1 = 8 - i. If i+3 \u003e10 then length=0. So R = max(0, 8 - i). Indeed for i=9, i+3=12 \u003e10, so length=0. 8 - 9 = -1, max(0,-1)=0. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.52898342,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528984691,"data":"       | Thus L = max(0, i-2). R = max(0, 8 - i). For i from 1 to 9.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528985931,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528987064,"data":"       | Now for a path of length n (i.e., n vertices in a line), the number of subsets with no two consecutive elements (including empty set) is known to be F_{n+2}, where F_1 =1, F_2 =1 (or F_0=0?). Actually number of independent sets of path graph P_n = F_{n+2} (with F_0=0,F_1=1). Let's verify: For n=0 (empty set), there is 1 subset (empty). F_{2}=1? Wait F_2 =1 (if F_0=0,F_1=1). So yes. For n=1, subsets: {} and {1} =\u003e 2. F_{3}=2. For n=2, subsets: {},{1},{2} =\u003e3 (can't have both). F_{4}=3. Works. So formula holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528989173,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528990297,"data":"       | Thus for interval length n, number of subsets with no consecutive elements = F_{n+2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.528991583,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.52899271,"data":"       | Thus for given i, number of valid subsets = (number of independent sets on left interval) * (number on right interval). Because we must include the block (i,i+1) always, and we cannot include any other elements adjacent to the block (i-1,i+2). So we treat left and right intervals as independent.\n"}
