,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.529214135,"data":"       | analysisWe need to find number of subsets of {1,2,...,10} that contain exactly one pair of consecutive integers. That is subsets where there is exactly one pair (i,i+1) both present, and no other consecutive pair present. Also note that a subset can have more than two elements, but only one adjacent pair. Also note that a pair of consecutive integers could be part of a longer run? For example subset {1,2,3} contains pairs (1,2) and (2,3) =\u003e two pairs, not allowed. So any subset with a run of length \u003e=3 would have at least two consecutive pairs, thus not allowed. So allowed subsets must contain exactly one adjacency pair, and all other elements are isolated (no adjacency with any other element). Also the adjacency pair itself cannot be adjacent to any other element in the subset. For example {1,2,4} is okay: pair (1,2) present, and 4 not adjacent to 2 (since 3 missing) and not adjacent to 1 (since 2 already adjacent). So okay.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.529217063,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.529218132,"data":"       | Thus we need to count subsets of {1,...,10} with exactly one adjacency pair, and no other adjacency pairs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.529219486,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.529220629,"data":"       | We can treat the adjacency pair as a \"block\" of size 2, with internal adjacency, and the rest are singletons that cannot be adjacent to each other or to the block's elements. Also the block's elements cannot be adjacent to any other selected element: i.e., if block is {i,i+1}, then we cannot select i-1 or i+2 (if they exist). So we need to count subsets where we choose a block (i,i+1) (i from 1 to 9) and then choose any subset of the remaining numbers (excluding i-1, i, i+1, i+2) such that no two chosen are consecutive (i.e., they form an independent set in the path graph after removing the block and its immediate neighbors). Also we cannot have any other adjacency pairs among the chosen singletons. So we need to count independent sets (no adjacency) of the remaining numbers after removing the block and its adjacent numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.52922334,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.529224673,"data":"       | But careful: The \"remaining numbers\" are the set {1,...,10} \\ {i-1,i,i+1,i+2} (where i-1 or i+2 may be outside 1..10). Among those, we can select any subset with no consecutive numbers (within the original ordering). Since we removed i-1 and i+2, we guarantee no adjacency with the block. But we also need to ensure no adjacency among the selected numbers themselves. So we count independent subsets of a path graph of length N' (the number of remaining vertices) where adjacency is defined by original consecutive integers (i.e., numbers that differ by 1). Since we removed some vertices, the remaining vertices may be broken into up to three intervals (segments) of consecutive numbers: left segment (1..i-2), middle? Actually after removing i-1,i,i+1,i+2, we have left segment from 1 to i-2 (if i-2 \u003e= 1) and right segment from i+3 to 10 (if i+3 \u003c= 10). So at most two segments (since we removed a contiguous block of four numbers). So the remaining numbers are two intervals: [1, i-2] and [i+3, 10]. Within each interval, we can choose any independent set (no two consecutive). The number of independent subsets (including empty) of a path of length L (i.e., L vertices in a line) is known to be F_{L+2}, where F_n is Fibonacci numbers with F_1=1,F_2=1 (or F_0=0,F_1=1). Let's verify: For L=0 (empty set), number of independent subsets = 1 (empty). F_{2}=1? Actually F_{L+2} with F_0=0,F_1=1 =\u003e F_2=1, matches. For L=1, independent subsets: {} and {v} =\u003e 2. F_{3}=2, correct. For L=2, independent subsets: {},{v1},{v2} =\u003e 3, F_4=3, correct. So formula holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.529230667,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.529231831,"data":"       | Thus for each i (block position), we need to compute number of independent subsets of left interval length L1 = i-2 (if i\u003e=3 else L1=0). Right interval length L2 = 10 - (i+3) + 1 = 8 - i? Let's compute: Right interval from i+3 to 10 inclusive, length = 10 - (i+3) + 1 = 8 - i? Actually 10 - i - 3 + 1 = 8 - i. Yes. If i+3 \u003e10, then L2 =0. So L2 = max(0, 8 - i). For i from 1 to 9.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.529233798,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.529235738,"data":"       | Thus total independent subsets for given i = F_{L1+2} * F_{L2+2} (since independent choices in left and right intervals multiply). Because we can independently choose any independent set in each interval.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.529237429,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3044.529238678,"data":"       | But we also need to consider that the block itself is always present (the pair). So each subset counted includes the block. So total subsets with exactly one adjacency pair = sum_{i=1}^{9} F_{L1+2} * F_{L2+2}.\n"}
