,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.114509082,"data":"       | analysisWe need to solve geometry problem: triangle ABC with sides 13,14,15 (scalene). M is midpoint of BC. P is point on circumcircle such that M lies on line AP (i.e., AP passes through M). So P is the second intersection of line AM with circumcircle (other than A). Then there exists unique point Q on segment AM such that angle PBQ = angle PCQ. So Q is point on AM where lines BQ and CQ subtend equal angles from P: i.e., Q is point such that from point P, the rays to B and C make equal angles with lines to Q? Wait angle PBQ is angle between PB and QB; angle PCQ is angle between PC and QC. So we need Q on AM such that angle between PB and BQ equals angle between PC and CQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.114511372,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.114512455,"data":"       | Goal: compute length AQ, expressed as m/sqrt(n) with m,n coprime; find m+n.\n"}
