,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115593945,"data":"       | analysisWe need to solve geometry problem.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115595147,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115596419,"data":"       | We have triangle ABC with side lengths AB=13, BC=14, CA=15. So it's a scalene triangle. M is midpoint of BC. P is point on circumcircle such that M lies on line AP (i.e., AP passes through M). So P is the second intersection of line through A and M with circumcircle (other than A). So P is the antipode of A with respect to the line through M? Not exactly; P is the second intersection of line AM with circumcircle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115598286,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115599464,"data":"       | We then have unique point Q on segment AM such that angle PBQ = angle PCQ. So Q is a point on AM such that lines BQ and CQ subtend equal angles at P? Actually angle PBQ is angle between PB and BQ, and angle PCQ is angle between PC and CQ. So we need Q on AM such that those angles are equal. Equivalent to Q such that Q is on the Apollonius circle? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115601225,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115602327,"data":"       | Goal: find length AQ expressed as m / sqrt(n). Compute m+n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115603538,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115604621,"data":"       | We need to compute coordinates perhaps.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115605811,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115606932,"data":"       | We can set up coordinate geometry. Use triangle coordinates: Let's place B at (0,0), C at (14,0) (since BC=14). Then we need coordinates of A such that AB=13, AC=15. So find point A = (x_A, y_A) with distances to B and C accordingly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115610249,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115611375,"data":"       | Let B=(0,0), C=(14,0). Then AB^2 = x_A^2 + y_A^2 = 13^2 = 169. AC^2 = (x_A-14)^2 + y_A^2 = 15^2 = 225. Subtract: (x_A-14)^2 - x_A^2 = 225-169 = 56. Expand: (x_A^2 -28x_A +196) - x_A^2 = -28x_A +196 =56 =\u003e -28x_A = -140 =\u003e x_A = 5. So x_A=5. Then y_A^2 = 169 - x_A^2 = 169 -25 =144 =\u003e y_A = sqrt(144)=12 (positive for orientation). So A = (5,12). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115613262,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115614363,"data":"       | Thus triangle coordinates: A(5,12), B(0,0), C(14,0). M is midpoint of BC: (7,0).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115615629,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115616709,"data":"       | Now line AM: passes through A (5,12) and M (7,0). Compute equation. Slope = (0-12)/(7-5) = -12/2 = -6. So line equation: y -12 = -6 (x-5) =\u003e y = -6x +30 +12? Wait compute: y-12 = -6(x-5) = -6x+30 =\u003e y = -6x+42. Check at x=5: y = -30+42=12 good. At x=7: y = -42+42=0 good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115618391,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115619481,"data":"       | Now we need intersection of line AM with circumcircle of triangle ABC (other than A). So we need circumcircle equation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115620793,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115621883,"data":"       | Compute circumcenter O of triangle ABC. Use coordinates or formula.\n"}
