,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115723449,"data":"       | Now we need point Q on segment AM (i.e., line y = -6x + 42, with x between 5 and 7) such that angle PBQ = angle PCQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115724739,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115725867,"data":"       | Interpretation: At point B, the angle between PB and BQ equals at point C, angle between PC and CQ. Actually angle PBQ is angle with vertex B? Wait notation: ∠PBQ is angle with vertex B? Usually notation ∠XYZ is angle with vertex at Y formed by XY and YZ. So ∠PBQ is angle between PB and QB at B. Similarly ∠PCQ is angle between PC and QC at C. So we need BQ and CQ such that the angles at B and C formed by lines to P and Q are equal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115727856,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115728979,"data":"       | Thus we need Q such that ∠(PB, QB) = ∠(PC, QC). Equivalent to oriented angle between lines PB and BQ equals oriented angle between PC and CQ. Since Q is on AM, we can treat as unknown point on line.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115730476,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115731566,"data":"       | Goal: find AQ distance.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115732687,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115733887,"data":"       | We can set up using vector dot product: angle equality means cos of angles equal. Use law of cos: For angle at B between vectors BP and BQ. Cosine of angle = (BP·BQ) / (|BP||BQ|). Similarly for angle at C: (CP·CQ) / (|CP||CQ|). So equality of cos values leads to equation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115737053,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.1157383,"data":"       | But note that angles are acute? Might be both acute? Could be ambiguous sign. But we can set equality of the actual angle measures (0 to π). Since Q is on segment AM inside triangle? Actually A is above BC, M is on BC. So AM is a median from A to BC. Q is on AM between A and M, so inside triangle. P is below BC (since y negative). So lines PB and PC go downwards from B and C to P. Q is above BC (y positive). So BQ and CQ are upward lines. So angles at B and C likely are acute? Let's compute approximate to check.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115740347,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115741453,"data":"       | We can solve for Q coordinate (x_Q, y_Q) = (t, -6t+42) with t between 5 and 7. Then we need equality of angles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115742726,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.115743827,"data":"       | Let's solve equation using sympy.\n"}
