,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116294509,"data":"       | analysisWe need to solve geometry problem. Let's restate.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116297314,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116298495,"data":"       | Given triangle ABC with sides AB=13, BC=14, CA=15. So triangle with sides 13-14-15, which is nearly scalene. M is midpoint of BC. P is point on circumcircle of triangle ABC such that M lies on line AP (i.e., A, M, P are collinear). There is a unique point Q on segment AM such that angle PBQ = angle PCQ. Find AQ expressed as m / sqrt(n) with m,n relatively prime positive integers; compute m+n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116300333,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11630142,"data":"       | Goal: compute AQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116302538,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116303627,"data":"       | We need to find geometry relationships.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116304807,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116305918,"data":"       | Let's start by analyzing triangle ABC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116307056,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116308173,"data":"       | First compute coordinates for convenience. Choose coordinate system: Place B at (0,0), C at (14,0) (since BC=14). Then we need coordinates of A such that AB=13, AC=15. So A = (x_A, y_A) with distance to B: sqrt(x_A^2 + y_A^2) = 13, distance to C: sqrt((x_A-14)^2 + y_A^2) = 15.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116309756,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116310874,"data":"       | Solve for x_A, y_A. Use algebra: x_A^2 + y_A^2 = 169. (x_A - 14)^2 + y_A^2 = 225.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116312103,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116313213,"data":"       | Subtract: (x_A -14)^2 - x_A^2 = 225-169=56. Expand: x_A^2 -28x_A +196 - x_A^2 = -28x_A +196 =56 =\u003e -28x_A = 56-196 = -140 =\u003e x_A = 5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116314626,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116315729,"data":"       | Plug into x_A^2 + y_A^2 =169 =\u003e 25 + y_A^2 =169 =\u003e y_A^2 =144 =\u003e y_A = 12 (positive orientation). So A = (5,12). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11631717,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116318502,"data":"       | Thus triangle ABC coordinates: A(5,12), B(0,0), C(14,0). This matches 5-12-13 right triangle? Indeed AB=13, coordinates produce right angle at B? Let's check: AB vector from B to A is (5,12). BC vector from B to C is (14,0). Dot product (5*14 + 12*0) = 70, not zero. So not right at B. But we note that AB=13, AC=15, BC=14. This triangle is not right but close.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116320467,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116321563,"data":"       | Now compute M, midpoint of BC: M = ((0+14)/2, (0+0)/2) = (7,0).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116322781,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116324037,"data":"       | Now line AM: passes through A(5,12) and M(7,0). Compute equation: slope = (0-12)/(7-5) = -12/2 = -6. So line equation: y - 12 = -6 (x -5). So y = -6x +30 +12? Wait compute: y -12 = -6(x-5) =\u003e y -12 = -6x +30 =\u003e y = -6x +42. Check at x=7: y = -6*7+42 = -42+42 =0 good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116325719,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116326839,"data":"       | Now we need point P on circumcircle of triangle ABC such that M lies on line AP (i.e., P is intersection of line AM extended beyond M with circumcircle). So we need second intersection of line AM with circumcircle (first intersection is A). So we can find circumcircle equation and intersect with line.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116328473,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116330917,"data":"       | Let's compute circumcircle of triangle ABC. Use coordinates to find center O (circumcenter) and radius R.\n"}
