,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116977349,"data":"       | analysisWe need to solve geometry problem.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11697853,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116979712,"data":"       | Given triangle ABC with sides 13,14,15. M is midpoint of BC. P is point on circumcircle such that M lies on line AP (i.e., AP passes through M). So P is second intersection of line through A and M with circumcircle (other than A). Then there is unique point Q on segment AM such that angle PBQ = angle PCQ. Q is on AM interior. Find AQ length expressed as m/√n (simplify). Compute m+n.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11698154,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116982649,"data":"       | We need to compute geometry coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116983793,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116984954,"data":"       | Let's set up coordinates: Place triangle ABC conveniently. Since sides 13 (AB), 14 (BC), 15 (CA). This is a scalene triangle. We can place B at (0,0), C at (14,0) (since BC = 14). Then we need coordinates of A such that AB=13, AC=15. So A = (x_A, y_A) with distance to B: sqrt(x^2 + y^2) = 13, distance to C: sqrt((x-14)^2 + y^2) = 15. Solve for x,y.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116986648,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116987754,"data":"       | From equations:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116988873,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116989961,"data":"       | x^2 + y^2 = 169\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116991087,"data":"       | (x-14)^2 + y^2 = 225\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116992191,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116993296,"data":"       | Subtract: (x-14)^2 - x^2 = 225-169 = 56. Expand: (x^2 -28x +196) - x^2 = -28x +196 =56 =\u003e -28x = -140 =\u003e x = 5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116994684,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116995796,"data":"       | Then plug into x^2 + y^2 = 169 =\u003e 25 + y^2 = 169 =\u003e y^2 = 144 =\u003e y = 12 (positive for orientation). So A = (5,12). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11699727,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116998381,"data":"       | Now M is midpoint of BC: B(0,0), C(14,0) =\u003e M = (7,0).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.116999637,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117000877,"data":"       | Line AM passes through A(5,12) and M(7,0). Compute equation: slope = (0-12)/(7-5) = -12/2 = -6. So line equation: y - 12 = -6 (x-5) =\u003e y = -6x +30 +12? Wait compute: y -12 = -6(x-5) = -6x+30 =\u003e y = -6x+42. Check at x=5: y= -30+42=12 correct. At x=7: y = -42+42=0 correct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117004279,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117005405,"data":"       | Now circumcircle of triangle ABC: we need its equation, find intersection of line AM with circumcircle besides A, that is point P.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117006764,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117007874,"data":"       | Circumcircle: find center O (circumcenter) and radius R.\n"}
