,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117010197,"data":"       | Coordinates: Use perpendicular bisectors. Or compute using formula.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117011439,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117012567,"data":"       | Given triangle coordinates: A(5,12), B(0,0), C(14,0). Let's compute circumcenter O.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117013827,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117014921,"data":"       | We can compute using sympy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117016073,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117017222,"data":"       | But also note that triangle with sides 13,14,15 is known to have area: Using Heron: s = (13+14+15)/2 = 42/2=21. Area = sqrt(s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)) = sqrt(21*8*7*6) = sqrt(21*336) = sqrt(7056) = 84. So area =84.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117018743,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117019865,"data":"       | Circumradius R = abc / (4K) = (13*14*15)/(4*84). Compute: 13*14*15 = 2730? Let's compute: 13*14=182; 182*15=2730. 4*84=336. So R = 2730/336 = simplify: divide by 6 =\u003e 455/56 = 455/56 = 8.125? Actually 56*8=448, remainder 7 =\u003e 8 + 7/56 = 8+0.125 = 8.125 = 65/8? Let's compute: 455/56 reduce? gcd(455,56) =? 56*8=448 remainder 7, gcd(56,7)=7 =\u003e divide numerator and denominator by 7 =\u003e 455/7=65, 56/7=8 =\u003e R = 65/8 = 8.125. Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117021933,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117023007,"data":"       | Circumcenter O coordinates? Use formula: For triangle coordinates, we can compute using intersection of perpendicular bisectors.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117024341,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117025442,"data":"       | But we may not need O; we can find intersection of line AM with circumcircle using power of a point or chord property.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117026753,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117027994,"data":"       | Given line through A and M intersects circumcircle at A and P. So AP is chord passing through M (midpoint of BC). So M is interior point of chord AP. We can use power of point M: Power of M relative to circumcircle: MA * MP = MB * MC (since M lies on BC? Actually B and C are points on circle, M is midpoint of BC, not on circle. But power of M relative to circumcircle is product of distances from M to intersection points of any line through M with the circle. For line through M and B, we have intersection points B and C (since line BC passes through M). So MB * MC = power of M.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117030143,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117031271,"data":"       | Thus MA * MP = MB * MC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117032402,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117033479,"data":"       | We know MB = distance from M(7,0) to B(0,0) = 7. MC = distance from M to C(14,0) = 7. So MB*MC = 49.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117036209,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117037365,"data":"       | We can compute MA: distance from M to A: M(7,0) to A(5,12) =\u003e sqrt((5-7)^2 + (12-0)^2) = sqrt((-2)^2 + 144) = sqrt(4+144) = sqrt(148) = 2*sqrt(37). Indeed sqrt(148)=2*sqrt(37). So MA = 2 sqrt(37). Then MP = (MB*MC)/MA = 49 / (2 sqrt(37)) = 49/(2 sqrt(37)). So MP length.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117039013,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117040134,"data":"       | Now we can find coordinates of P along line AM beyond M? Actually M is between A and P? Let's check: M is on line AP. Since MA = 2 sqrt(37) ≈ 2*6.082 = 12.164. MP = 49/(2*6.082) = 49/12.164 ≈ 4.028. So total AP = MA + MP = approx 16.192. Since M is between A and P (makes sense because M is interior to segment AP? Actually line passes through A and M and extends to intersect circle again at P. Since M is inside segment AP? Let's check: If M is interior to segment AP, then distances from A to M and M to P sum to AP. Indeed we have AP = MA + MP = 2 sqrt(37) + 49/(2 sqrt(37)). So M is between A and P.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117042339,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117043463,"data":"       | Thus coordinates of P can be found using param along line from A to M to P. Since we know distances along line, we can compute vector direction from A to M: vector AM = M - A = (7-5, 0-12) = (2, -12). Its length = MA = sqrt(2^2+(-12)^2) = sqrt(4+144)=sqrt(148)=2 sqrt(37). So unit vector from A to M = (2, -12) / (2 sqrt(37)) = (1/ sqrt(37), -6/ sqrt(37)). Indeed because dividing by 2 sqrt(37) yields (1/ sqrt(37), -6/ sqrt(37)). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117045356,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117046489,"data":"       | Thus moving from A to P: AP = MA + MP = 2 sqrt(37) + 49/(2 sqrt(37)). So coordinates of P = A + (unit vector)*(AP) = A + (direction)*(AP). But direction from A to M is same direction as from A to P (since they are collinear). So we can compute:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117048055,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117049175,"data":"       | P = A + (AP)*(unit vector) = (5,12) + (AP)*(1/ sqrt(37), -6/ sqrt(37)). Let's compute AP.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117050481,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11705175,"data":"       | AP = 2 sqrt(37) + 49/(2 sqrt(37)) = (4*37 + 49)/(2 sqrt(37))? Wait compute: 2 sqrt(37) = (4 sqrt(37))/2? Actually let's put over common denominator 2 sqrt(37): 2 sqrt(37) = (4*37?) That's not right. Let's compute numeric: 2 sqrt(37) = 2*sqrt(37). Express as (4*37?) Not needed. Let's keep symbolic: AP = 2 sqrt(37) + 49/(2 sqrt(37)) = (4*37 + 49)/(2 sqrt(37))? Because 2 sqrt(37) = (4*37)/(2 sqrt(37))? Let's verify: (4*37)/(2 sqrt(37)) = (148)/(2 sqrt(37)) = 74/ sqrt(37). But 2 sqrt(37) = 2 sqrt(37). Multiply numerator and denominator: 2 sqrt(37) = (2 sqrt(37) * sqrt(37))/ sqrt(37) = (2*37)/ sqrt(37) = 74/ sqrt(37). Indeed 2 sqrt(37) = 74/ sqrt(37). Good. So we can write both terms with denominator sqrt(37). Let's compute:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117054125,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117055229,"data":"       | 2 sqrt(37) = 74/ sqrt(37). Because sqrt(37)*sqrt(37)=37. Multiply numerator and denominator: 2 sqrt(37) = (2*37)/ sqrt(37) = 74/ sqrt(37). Yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117056609,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117057827,"data":"       | Thus AP = 74/ sqrt(37) + 49/(2 sqrt(37)) = (148 + 49)/ (2 sqrt(37)) = 197/(2 sqrt(37)). Wait check: 74/ sqrt(37) = (148)/(2 sqrt(37)). Add 49/(2 sqrt(37)) = (148+49)/(2 sqrt(37)) = 197/(2 sqrt(37)). So AP = 197/(2 sqrt(37)). Good.\n"}
