,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117660998,"data":"       | analysisMatches.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117662103,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117663327,"data":"       | Thus answer is 247.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117664434,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117665585,"data":"       | But we must ensure there is uniqueness of Q (point on segment AM) such that angle PBQ = angle PCQ. We found one solution s=99/148. The other solution s=197/148 is beyond M, not on segment. So unique.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117667071,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117668289,"data":"       | Now we can produce final answer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117669411,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117670545,"data":"       | But we need to present reasoning elegantly. Provide geometry solution maybe using power of point or angle bisector theorem? Let's attempt to produce a reasoning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117671945,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117673081,"data":"       | We can also attempt to derive using law of sines and perhaps use ratio property.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117674305,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117675504,"data":"       | Let’s attempt to produce a synthetic solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117676691,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11767782,"data":"       | We have triangle ABC with sides 13,14,15. M midpoint of BC. P is second intersection of AM with circumcircle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117679132,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117680256,"data":"       | We need Q on AM such that ∠PBQ = ∠PCQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117681477,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117682583,"data":"       | Observation: Since B, C, A, P are concyclic, we have arcs and inscribed angles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117683812,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117684909,"data":"       | Let’s denote ∠PBQ = ∠PCQ = θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117686154,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117687229,"data":"       | Consider quadrilateral BQPC? Not cyclic generally.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117688403,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117689537,"data":"       | But maybe we can use angle condition to relate ratios of distances using law of sines in triangles PBQ and PCQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117693393,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117694543,"data":"       | In triangle PBQ, angle at B is θ, angle at P is something, angle at Q is something. Similarly for PCQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117695861,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117697001,"data":"       | Alternatively we can use the fact that Q lies on line AM, which is the external symmedian? Not sure.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117698276,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117699382,"data":"       | But we can produce coordinate solution as we did, which is straightforward.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117700654,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117701795,"data":"       | But maybe there is a more elegant approach using the concept of Apollonius circle: Locus of points Q such that ratio of distances to B and C is constant. Because angle equality condition might be equivalent to ratio of distances from Q to B and C being equal to ratio of distances from P to B and C? Let's test.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117703457,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117704619,"data":"       | Given angle PBQ = angle PCQ, we have that Q lies on the internal angle bisector of angle BPC? Wait angle at B vs at C. Not same vertex. However we can use law of sines in triangles PBQ and PCQ: In triangle PBQ, sin(∠PBQ)/PQ = sin(∠P Q B)/PB? Actually law of sines: PB / sin(∠P Q B) = BQ / sin(∠P B Q) = PQ / sin(∠P B Q?). Wait need to be careful.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117706515,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117707638,"data":"       | Let’s denote angles:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117708805,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117709888,"data":"       | In triangle PBQ:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117710995,"data":"       | - At B: ∠PBQ = θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117712263,"data":"       | - At P: ∠BPQ = α (some).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117713507,"data":"       | - At Q: ∠BQP = π - α - θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117714711,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117715779,"data":"       | In triangle PCQ:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117716893,"data":"       | - At C: ∠PCQ = θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117718061,"data":"       | - At P: ∠CPQ = β.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117719215,"data":"       | - At Q: ∠CQP = π - β - θ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117720414,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117721533,"data":"       | But we also have that P, A, B, C are concyclic, so ∠BP A = ∠B C A? Not needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117722855,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117724129,"data":"       | But maybe we can relate α and β because quadrilateral BPC? Actually B, P, C are points on circle (since P is on circumcircle). So ∠BPC is the angle subtended by arc BC. Since B, C, A are on circle, ∠BAC also subtends arc BC. So ∠BPC = ∠BAC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117725862,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117726955,"data":"       | Thus α = ∠BPQ = angle between BP and PQ. Not directly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117728249,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117729343,"data":"       | Nevertheless, we can use ratio of sines to express BQ/CQ in terms of PB/PC perhaps.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117732271,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117733403,"data":"       | From law of sines in triangles PBQ and PCQ:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117734594,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117735673,"data":"       | In triangle PBQ: BQ / sin(∠BPQ) = PB / sin(∠BQP) = PQ / sin(θ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117736985,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117738096,"data":"       | In triangle PCQ: CQ / sin(∠CPQ) = PC / sin(∠CQP) = PQ / sin(θ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117739383,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117740486,"data":"       | Since both have same PQ and sinθ, we can equate:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117741716,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117742823,"data":"       | BQ / sin(∠BPQ) = CQ / sin(∠CPQ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117744004,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117745089,"data":"       | Thus BQ / CQ = sin(∠BPQ) / sin(∠CPQ).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117746281,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117747381,"data":"       | But ∠BPQ and ∠CPQ are adjacent angles at P formed by lines PB, PC, and PQ. Since P, B, C, A are concyclic, we may have relationship between these angles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117748773,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117751017,"data":"       | Specifically, ∠BPQ + ∠CPQ = ∠BPC = ∠BAC (since B, P, C, A concyclic). So they are supplementary? Actually they sum to angle BPC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117752347,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117753382,"data":"       | Thus we can express ratio using sines of two angles that sum to a known angle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117754544,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117755582,"data":"       | Let’s denote x = ∠BPQ, then ∠CPQ = ∠BPC - x = γ - x, where γ = ∠BPC = ∠BAC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117757152,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117758194,"data":"       | Thus BQ / CQ = sin x / sin (γ - x). This is reminiscent of angle bisector theorem: For a point Q on side BC of triangle BPC, the ratio BQ/CQ = (BP/PC) * (sin∠BQP / sin∠CQP). Not exactly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11775961,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117760654,"data":"       | But we have Q on line AM, not on BC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117761725,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117762892,"data":"       | Nevertheless, we could also use law of sines in triangle BPC to relate BP/PC to sin of arcs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117764101,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11776513,"data":"       | Since B,P,C,A are concyclic, we can compute angle BAC using side lengths via law of cosines. Let's compute angle BAC.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117766351,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117767389,"data":"       | But perhaps there is a simpler method: Because Q lies on AM, which is a median, there may be some ratio property.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117768732,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117769763,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can use coordinates as we did; that's acceptable for solution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117770927,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117773339,"data":"       | But we need to produce final answer: m+n = 247.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11777446,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117775495,"data":"       | Now we need to write final answer with reasoning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117776623,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117777626,"data":"       | Let's craft solution:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117778684,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117779725,"data":"       | 1. Place triangle with B=(0,0), C=(14,0), A=(5,12). Compute M=(7,0).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117780891,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117781929,"data":"       | 2. Find circumcenter O using perpendicular bisectors: O = (7,33/8), radius R=65/8.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117783083,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117784114,"data":"       | 3. Line AM equation: param t: (x,y) = (5+2t, 12-12t). Intersection with circumcircle gives second point P (t=197/148). So P = (567/74, -147/37).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11778541,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117786447,"data":"       | 4. Let Q be on AM: Q = (5+2s, 12-12s) with 0 ≤ s ≤ 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117787651,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117788679,"data":"       | 5. Condition ∠PBQ = ∠PCQ translates to equality of cosines:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11778986,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117790929,"data":"       | cos∠PBQ = (BP·BQ)/(|BP||BQ|) and cos∠PCQ = (CP·CQ)/(|CP||CQ|). Set equal.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117792194,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117793217,"data":"       | Simplify leads to equation:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117802546,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117803642,"data":"       | (-1850 s - 495) sqrt(144(s-1)^2 + (2s+5)^2) + (2886 s - 429) sqrt(144(s-1)^2 + (2s-9)^2) = 0.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117804835,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11780588,"data":"       | Solve gives s = 99/148 (and extraneous s = 197/148). Since s ∈ [0,1], we take s = 99/148.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117807107,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117808169,"data":"       | 6. Length AM = √[(2)^2 + (-12)^2] = √148 = 2√37.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117809347,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117810377,"data":"       | Thus AQ = s·AM = (99/148)·2√37 = 99/√148.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117811617,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117814045,"data":"       | 7. Write as m/√n with m,n coprime: m=99, n=148. So m+n = 247.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117815249,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11781629,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can rationalize to show m,n relatively prime.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117817424,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117818503,"data":"       | Thus answer: 247.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117819557,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11782059,"data":"       | Now we must produce final answer.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117821665,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.1178227,"data":"       | But we should also verify that m,n relatively prime: gcd(99,148) = 1 (since 99=3^2*11, 148=2^2*37). So yes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.1178256,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117826664,"data":"       | Thus final answer: 247.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117827724,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117828765,"data":"       | Now produce final message.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.117829842,"data":"       | \n"}
