,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118068002,"data":"       | Now we need Q on segment AM (i.e., t between 0 and 1) such that ∠PBQ = ∠PCQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118069222,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118070296,"data":"       | Interpretation: angle PBQ is angle between PB and QB at vertex B? Wait ∠PBQ: vertex B, sides BP and BQ. So angle between lines BP and BQ. Similarly ∠PCQ: vertex C, sides CP and CQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118071667,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118072714,"data":"       | Thus condition: angle between BP and BQ equals angle between CP and CQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118073858,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118074905,"data":"       | We need to find point Q on line AM (i.e., Q = (5+2s, 12-12s) for some s in [0,1]) such that the condition holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118076115,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11807725,"data":"       | Goal: find AQ length = distance from A to Q = s * length(AM) (since param t is linear). Actually length of AM times s (since t parameter is linear from A to M). Let's compute length AM: distance between A(5,12) and M(7,0) = sqrt((2)^2 + (-12)^2) = sqrt(4+144)=sqrt(148)=2*sqrt(37). Indeed sqrt(148) = 2*sqrt(37). So AQ = s * AM = s * 2*sqrt(37). Since s is between 0 and 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118080528,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11808156,"data":"       | Thus we need s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118082612,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118083684,"data":"       | Condition: ∠PBQ = ∠PCQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118084809,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118085835,"data":"       | We can express using dot product or oriented angle equality.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118086974,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118087974,"data":"       | Let vectors:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118089006,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11809003,"data":"       | At B: vector BP = P - B = P (since B at origin) = (x_P, y_P). Vector BQ = Q - B = Q.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11809121,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118092269,"data":"       | Angle PBQ is angle between vectors BP and BQ. So cos of angle = (BP·BQ) / (|BP||BQ|). Similarly at C: vectors CP = P - C, and CQ = Q - C. Angle PCQ is angle between CP and CQ.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118093646,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11809472,"data":"       | Equality of angles means cos values equal (since angles between 0 and π). However there could be ambiguous sign; but both angles are acute? Not necessarily. But we can solve equation using dot product equality of cos.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118096135,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118097154,"data":"       | Thus condition:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11809819,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118099222,"data":"       | (BP·BQ) / (|BP||BQ|) = (CP·CQ) / (|CP||CQ|).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118100351,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118101372,"data":"       | Cross-multiply: (BP·BQ) * |CP| * |CQ| = (CP·CQ) * |BP| * |BQ|.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118102517,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118103529,"data":"       | But that's messy.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11810459,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118106469,"data":"       | Alternatively we could use oriented angle equality: maybe there is a known property: Q on AM such that PBQ = PCQ means Q is intersection of Apollonius circle? Actually condition that Q sees segment PC under equal angle from B and C? Wait ∠PBQ = ∠PCQ. This is angle at B and C respectively. So Q is such that the ray BQ makes same angle with BP as ray CQ makes with CP. Not symmetrical.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11810823,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118109238,"data":"       | Alternatively we could use law of sines in triangles PBQ and PCQ? Not straightforward.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118110421,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118111477,"data":"       | Better approach: Use analytic geometry: compute Q as unknown point on line AM param s, then compute angle equality using dot product equality of cos. Solve for s.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118112786,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118113912,"data":"       | Let's do that using sympy rational arithmetic.assistantanalysis to=python codes = sp.symbols('s', real=True)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118116932,"data":"       | Q = sp.Point(5+2*s, 12-12*s)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118118017,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118119015,"data":"       | # Vectors\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118120079,"data":"       | BP = sp.Matrix(P)  # P - B\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.11812117,"data":"       | BQ = sp.Matrix(Q)  # Q - B\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118122281,"data":"       | CP = sp.Matrix(P - C)  # P - C\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118123414,"data":"       | CQ = sp.Matrix(Q - C)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118124462,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118125459,"data":"       | def dot(v,w):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118126491,"data":"       |     return (v.dot(w))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118127554,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3102.118128562,"data":"       | def norm(v):\n"}
