,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355156862,"data":"       | Now we need to confirm that we didn't miss rectangles where rectangle sides are formed by chords of same step size (i.e., both pairs are same step size). That would produce a square? Actually if a=b, then opposite sides would be parallel (same step size) and adjacent sides also same step size, meaning rectangle would be a rhombus? But rectangle requires adjacent sides perpendicular, which would require chords of same step size to be perpendicular to each other (i.e., chords of same step size but rotated by 90°). That may be possible for some step size where chords of same step size are perpendicular after rotation (i.e., there exist i and j such that chords (i,i+a) and (j,j+a) are perpendicular). This would produce a rectangle where all four sides are chords of same length (a square). Let's test.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355159495,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355160635,"data":"       | We need to find a such that there exist i,j such that chords of step size a are perpendicular. This would be a rectangle with all sides of same step size (a square). Let's test for a from 1..5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355162181,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355164778,"data":"       | We can try enumeration: choose i for first chord, j for second chord (parallel? Actually we need two chords that are perpendicular, not parallel. But rectangle requires opposite sides parallel, so we need two chords of step size a that are parallel (i.e., opposite chords offset by 6). And the other pair also of step size a but rotated 90° relative to first pair. But that would require two distinct orientations of chords of same step size that are perpendicular. However chords of same step size are rotated by multiples of 30°, so we need orientation difference of 90° modulo 180°, i.e., 90° difference. Since the step size chords rotate by 30°, we need to find integer m such that 30° * m ≡ 90° (mod 180°). That means 30m ≡ 90 (mod 180) =\u003e 30m = 90 + 180k =\u003e m = 3 + 6k. So m = 3 mod 6. So m = 3,9,... But modulo 12 (since 12 possible rotations), m=3 or 9 produce 90° difference. So chords of same step size a, when rotated by 90°, correspond to shifting starting vertex by 3 steps (i.e., 90°). So chords (i,i+a) and (i+3,i+3+a) are perpendicular.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35516893,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355170092,"data":"       | Now we need to see if we can form rectangle with opposite sides being chords (i,i+a) and (i+6,i+6+a) (parallel) and the other pair being chords (i+3,i+3+a) and (i+9,i+9+a) (parallel). That would be a rectangle (maybe a square) formed by four chords all of same step size a, offset by 3 steps.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355176155,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355177342,"data":"       | But we need to ensure that these chords intersect inside polygon and produce rectangle. Let's test for each a.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355178681,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355179802,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codedef rectangle_same_a(i,a):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355181085,"data":"       |     # chords: A1 = (i,i+a); A2 = (i+6,i+6+a); B1 = (i+3,i+3+a); B2 = (i+9,i+9+a)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355182418,"data":"       |     A1 = chord_points(i,a)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355183732,"data":"       |     A2 = chord_points(i+6,a)\n"}
