,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355814938,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can consider the rectangle's sides must be parallel to each other in opposite pairs. In a regular polygon, chords are symmetric; many chords are parallel to each other. Indeed, chords connecting vertices i and j have slope (or direction) determined by the difference of indices modulo n. Two chords are parallel if the arcs they subtend have the same length (i.e., same chord length) and are symmetric about the center. Actually chords connecting vertices i and j have direction angle equal to the average of the angles of the two vertices (the line's direction). For a regular n-gon inscribed in a circle centered at origin, the chord from vertex k to vertex l has direction angle equal to (θ_k + θ_l)/2 + π/2? Wait the line connecting two points on a circle: The chord's perpendicular bisector passes through the center. The chord's direction is orthogonal to the radius at the midpoint angle. The chord's slope is determined by the average of the two vertex angles plus π/2. More concretely, the chord connecting vertices i and j (with angles 2π i/n and 2π j/n) has direction angle (θ_i + θ_j)/2 + π/2 (i.e., perpendicular to the radius at angle (θ_i + θ_j)/2). So chords with same sum of indices (i + j) modulo n have the same direction (parallel). Indeed, chord direction depends on the average angle; chords connecting vertices whose average angle is the same are parallel.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355820735,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355822225,"data":"       | Thus chords can be grouped by the sum of indices modulo n. For n=12, there are 12 possible sums modulo 12 (i + j mod 12). However, chords are undirected; chord (i,j) same as (j,i), sum modulo n is same. So each direction class corresponds to a set of chords with same sum modulo n. For n even, chords connecting opposite vertices (i and i+6) have sum 2i+6 ≡ 2i+6 mod12; they are diameters, direction angle is (θ_i+θ_{i+6})/2 + π/2 = (θ_i + (θ_i+π))/2 + π/2 = θ_i + π/2 + π/2 = θ_i + π = opposite direction? Actually diameters are lines through center, direction is same as radius direction, not perpendicular. Wait need to compute: For diameters, chord direction is same as line connecting opposite points, which passes through center. Its direction angle is the angle of the line itself, which is the angle of the radius of one endpoint (or the average angle? Actually the line connecting points at angles α and α+π passes through the center, direction is α (or α+π). The average angle is α+π/2, but the line direction is α (or α+π). So the earlier formula (average + π/2) fails for diameters because the chord is a line through center, not perpendicular to radius at midpoint? Let's derive: Coordinates: (cos α, sin α) and (cos(α+π), sin(α+π)) = (-cos α, -sin α). The line connecting them is the line through origin with direction vector (cos α, sin α). So direction angle is α (i.e., average angle - π/2?). Actually average of α and α+π is α+π/2. Adding π/2 gives α+π, which is opposite direction. So direction of chord is average angle minus π/2 (or plus π/2) depending on orientation. So chords are parallel if (i + j) mod n is same or opposite? Let's compute for non-diameter chords: chord connecting i and j, with angles θ_i = 2π i/n, θ_j = 2π j/n. The direction vector is difference of coordinates: (cos θ_i - cos θ_j, sin θ_i - sin θ_j). This vector is 2 sin((θ_j - θ_i)/2) * ( - sin((θ_i+θ_j)/2), cos((θ_i+θ_j)/2) ) (using trig identity). So direction angle = (θ_i+θ_j)/2 + π/2 (or -π/2). Indeed, direction is perpendicular to radius at angle (θ_i+θ_j)/2. So chord direction depends on sum of indices modulo n (i+j). For diameters, sin((θ_j - θ_i)/2) = sin(π/2) = 1, but the direction vector reduces to ( - sin((θ_i+θ_j)/2), cos((θ_i+θ_j)/2) ) times factor. But for diameters, (θ_j - θ_i) = π, so sin((θ_j - θ_i)/2) = sin(π/2) = 1. The direction vector becomes ( - sin((θ_i+θ_j)/2), cos((θ_i+θ_j)/2) ). But note that (θ_i+θ_j)/2 = θ_i + π/2. So direction vector is ( - sin(θ_i+π/2), cos(θ_i+π/2) ) = ( - cos θ_i, - sin θ_i ) = -(cos θ_i, sin θ_i). So direction is opposite to (cos θ_i, sin θ_i). So indeed direction is α+π (i.e., opposite). So direction still depends on sum (i+j) mod n. So chords are parallel if i+j ≡ k+l (mod n). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355831511,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355832952,"data":"       | Thus we can group chords into n/2 families of parallel lines? Actually for n even, there are n/2 distinct directions because sum modulo n and sum modulo n plus n correspond to same direction but opposite orientation (i.e., same line direction). Since direction is determined by sum modulo n up to sign, there are n/2 distinct directions. For n=12, there are 6 distinct directions (parallel families). Let's verify: Sums modulo 12: 0,1,...,11. But chord (i,j) with sum s and chord (i',j') with sum s' are parallel if s ≡ s' (mod 12) or s ≡ -s' (mod 12)? Actually direction vector for sum s is ( - sin(π s/12), cos(π s/12) ) times factor sin(π (j-i)/12). Changing s to s+12 flips sign? Since sin(π (s+12)/12) = sin(π s/12 + π) = - sin(π s/12). Cos also flips sign? cos(π (s+12)/12) = cos(π s/12 + π) = - cos(π s/12). So direction vector becomes ( - sin(π(s+12)/12), cos(π(s+12)/12) ) = ( - (- sin(π s/12)), - cos(π s/12) ) = ( sin(π s/12), - cos(π s/12) ) = - ( - sin(π s/12), cos(π s/12) ). So direction flips sign, i.e., same line direction (parallel). So sums s and s+12 give same direction (parallel). Since modulo 12, s and s+12 are same anyway. So there are 12 possible sums, but direction for s and (12 - s) maybe same? Let's test: s' = 12 - s. Then sin(π (12 - s)/12) = sin(π - π s/12) = sin(π s/12). Cos(π (12 - s)/12) = cos(π - π s/12) = - cos(π s/12). So direction vector ( - sin(π (12 - s)/12), cos(π (12 - s)/12) ) = ( - sin(π s/12), - cos(π s/12) ) = - ( - sin(π s/12), cos(π s/12) ). So again opposite direction. So s and 12 - s produce opposite direction, same line orientation. So there are 6 distinct directions (since s and 12 - s give same). Indeed families correspond to s = 0,1,2,3,4,5 (mod 12). So there are 6 families of parallel chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355837838,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355838966,"data":"       | Thus each direction family consists of chords with a given sum s (mod 12). For each s, we have chords connecting vertex i to vertex j where i + j ≡ s (mod 12). Since i and j are distinct vertices (0 ≤ i \u003c j ≤ 11). For each s, how many chords belong to that family? Let's compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355840783,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355841948,"data":"       | Given s (mod 12), the pairs (i,j) with i\u003cj and i+j ≡ s (mod 12). Since i+j can range from 1 to 23 (max 11+12?). Actually i and j in {0,...,11}, i\u003cj. i+j can be from 1 to 21? Actually max sum = 10+11=21. So i+j modulo 12 can be any value 0-11. For each s, we need to count number of unordered pairs (i,j) with i+j ≡ s (mod 12). This is essentially number of chords in that direction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355843995,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355845338,"data":"       | We can compute via combinatorics: For each i, j = s - i (mod 12). Since j must be \u003e i and distinct. So each unordered pair counted twice if we consider both i and j. So number of unordered pairs = (1/2) * number of ordered pairs (i,j) with i≠j and i+j ≡ s (mod12). For each i (0..11), there is exactly one j (mod12) satisfying j ≡ s - i (mod12). That j may equal i (if 2i ≡ s mod12) leading to i=j which is not allowed. So for each i, we have a partner j. The mapping i ↔ j is involution: if j = s - i mod12, then i = s - j mod12. So each unordered pair corresponds to two ordered pairs (i,j) and (j,i). So number of unordered pairs = (12 - f)/2 where f is number of i such that i = s - i mod12 (i.e., 2i ≡ s mod12). Solutions exist if s is even modulo 12. For each s, number of fixed points f = number of i solving 2i ≡ s (mod12). Since modulo 12, 2i runs over even residues 0,2,4,...,10. So if s is even, there are solutions; if s is odd, none. For s even, equation 2i ≡ s mod12 has exactly 2 solutions? Let's solve: 2i ≡ s (mod12). Since gcd(2,12)=2, there are 2 solutions modulo 12 if s is divisible by 2. Indeed number of solutions = 2. For s odd, no solutions. So for s even, there are f=2 fixed points (i=j) which correspond to degenerate chords of length zero? Actually i=j not allowed, so those are excluded. So number of unordered pairs = (12 - 2)/2 = 5 for s even. For s odd, f=0, number of unordered pairs = 12/2 = 6. Wait check: For s odd, each i maps to distinct j ≠ i, so there are 12 ordered pairs, each unordered counted twice, so 6 unordered pairs. For s even, there are 2 i where j=i (i.e., i = s/2 mod6?), those correspond to i=j, not allowed, so we have 12-2=10 ordered pairs with i≠j, giving 5 unordered pairs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355851595,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355853079,"data":"       | Thus each direction family for odd s (1,3,5,7,9,11) has 6 chords; for even s (0,2,4,6,8,10) has 5 chords. However note that s=0 corresponds to chords where i+j ≡ 0 mod12. This includes pairs like (0,0) (excluded) and (1,11), (2,10), (3,9), (4,8), (5,7). That's 5 chords (including diameters? Actually (0,0) excluded; (6,6) excluded). The chords are (0,0) not allowed, (6,6) not allowed. So we have chords connecting opposite vertices? Wait (0,0) is not a chord, (6,6) not a chord. The diameters correspond to pairs (0,6), (1,7), (2,8), (3,9), (4,10), (5,11). Their sums are 6 mod12, not 0. Indeed diameters have sum 6 mod12 (since i + (i+6) = 2i+6 ≡ 6 mod12). So s=6 family corresponds to diameters (6 chords). Indeed s=6 is even, but we predicted even s have 5 chords. Wait our count gave 5 chords for even s. But s=6 has 6 chords (the diameters). Let's double-check. For s even, there are 2 fixed points solutions to 2i ≡ s (mod12). For s=6, equation 2i ≡ 6 mod12 =\u003e i ≡ 3 mod6 =\u003e i=3,9? Actually i=3 gives 2*3=6, i=9 gives 2*9=18 ≡ 6 mod12. So i=3,9 are fixed points (i=j). But i=j would require j = s - i mod12 = 6 - i mod12. For i=3, j = 6 - 3 = 3, yes fixed. For i=9, j = 6 - 9 = -3 ≡ 9 mod12. So indeed there are two self-pairs (3,3) and (9,9) which are not chords. So we have 12-2=10 ordered pairs with i≠j, giving 5 unordered chords. But we know there are 6 diameters. Let's list diameters: (0,6) sum 6, (1,7) sum 8? Wait 1+7=8, not 6. Oops I made mistake: diameters are vertices opposite each other (difference 6). Their sum i + (i+6) = 2i+6. Mod12, this sum varies with i: For i=0, sum=6; i=1, sum=8; i=2, sum=10; i=3, sum=0; i=4, sum=2; i=5, sum=4; i=6, sum=6 again? Actually i+6 for i from 0 to 5 produce sums 6,8,10,0,2,4. So diameters belong to six different sum classes (0,2,4,6,8,10). Indeed each sum even class contains exactly one diameter (i,i+6). So each even sum class has 5 chords, one of which is the diameter. So there are 5 chords per even sum class, including the diameter. That matches our count: each even s has 5 chords (including the diameter for s ≡ 0,2,4,6,8,10). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355858238,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355859401,"data":"       | Thus we have 6 direction families: s=0,2,4,6,8,10 (even) each with 5 chords; s=1,3,5,7,9,11 (odd) each with 6 chords. However note that s=0 and s=6 correspond to chords that are perpendicular? Actually direction families are determined by s modulo 12 up to sign; s and 12-s are same direction. So we have 6 families: s=0 (same as s=12) direction; s=1 (same as s=11); s=2 (same as s=10); s=3 (same as s=9); s=4 (same as s=8); s=5 (same as s=7). So we can choose representatives 0-5.\n"}
