,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355873848,"data":"       | Now a rectangle is formed by two pairs of parallel chords: opposite sides must be parallel, i.e., belong to same direction families. So rectangle consists of two chords from one direction family (forming opposite sides) and two chords from another direction family (the other pair of opposite sides), with the chords intersecting appropriately to form a rectangle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355875625,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355876819,"data":"       | Thus we need to count the number of rectangles formed by choosing two distinct direction families (say families A and B) and then selecting a pair of chords from family A and a pair from family B such that they intersect to form a rectangle (i.e., the four chords intersect pairwise at four vertices, forming a rectangle). In the arrangement of chords, any two chords from distinct families intersect (unless they share a vertex). Actually chords from different direction families are not parallel, so they intersect at some point (unless they share an endpoint). If they share a vertex, they intersect at that vertex, which would be a rectangle vertex at a polygon vertex. That's okay. However, for rectangle we need four distinct vertices (maybe some could be polygon vertices). The rectangle's sides are subsegments of chords; the vertices are intersection points of chords (including polygon vertices). So we need to consider all possible selections of two chords from family A and two chords from family B such that they form a rectangle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355879785,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355881156,"data":"       | We must ensure that the four chords are arranged such that each chord from family A intersects each chord from family B at distinct points, and that the intersection points form a rectangle (i.e., each chord from family A is intersected by both chords from family B, giving two vertices on that chord, and similarly for chords from family B). This essentially corresponds to selecting two chords from family A that are not intersecting each other (i.e., they are parallel, so they don't intersect unless they share a vertex). Since chords from same family are parallel, they either are distinct non-intersecting chords (if they don't share a vertex) or they intersect at a vertex (if they share a vertex). For rectangle, opposite sides must be parallel and distinct, not overlapping. So we need to pick two distinct chords from same family that are not the same line. They could share a vertex? If they share a vertex, then the rectangle would have a zero side? Actually if two opposite sides share a vertex, they would intersect, not parallel. But they are parallel lines, so they cannot intersect except at infinity. If they share a vertex, they would be the same line (since they both go through that vertex and are parallel, they must be identical). So two distinct chords from same family cannot share a vertex (since if they share a vertex, they'd be the same chord). Let's verify: Suppose chords (i,j) and (i,k) with i common. Their direction depends on sum i+j and i+k. For them to be parallel, we need i+j ≡ i+k mod12 =\u003e j ≡ k mod12 =\u003e j=k, so same chord. So indeed distinct chords from same direction family are disjoint (no common vertices). So they are parallel and non-intersecting.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355885415,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355886871,"data":"       | Thus for rectangle, we need to select two distinct parallel chords from family A (they will be opposite sides) and two distinct parallel chords from family B (other opposite sides). The four chords intersect pairwise at four points (forming rectangle). The intersection of a chord from A with a chord from B is a point (maybe a vertex of polygon if they share a vertex). Since chords from different families are not parallel, they intersect at a unique point (unless they share a vertex). But if they share a vertex, then that vertex would be a rectangle corner where two sides meet at right angle? Actually rectangle sides meet at right angle at vertices. In rectangle, adjacent sides are not parallel, they intersect at a vertex. So if a chord from family A and a chord from family B share a vertex (i.e., both incident to same polygon vertex), then that vertex would be a corner of rectangle. That's allowed. However, we need to ensure that each vertex of rectangle is formed by intersection of one chord from A and one chord from B, and each chord from A meets both chords from B at two distinct points (the two vertices of that side). If a chord from A shares a vertex with both chords from B, that would mean both chords from B also share that vertex, which would imply they intersect at that vertex, which would be impossible because chords from B are parallel and distinct, cannot share a vertex. So each chord from A can share at most one vertex with a chord from B. So rectangle vertices may be polygon vertices (if one chord from A and one from B both incident to same vertex). That would happen when we choose chords that have endpoints that are vertices of the rectangle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355892728,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355893873,"data":"       | Thus counting rectangles reduces to counting unordered pairs of direction families (A,B) and unordered pairs of chords from each family such that the four chords are distinct and form a rectangle.\n"}
