,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355899229,"data":"       | Essentially we need to count all rectangles formed by the arrangement of lines determined by chords (including sides). This arrangement yields a set of lines (each chord defines a line segment; but the infinite line extends beyond segment). However rectangle sides must be subsegments of chords, not necessarily entire lines. But if we consider the infinite lines through chords, rectangle sides will be subsegments of those lines, bounded by intersection points with the other pair of lines. So rectangle is determined by four lines (two from each direction family). As long as the lines intersect appropriately, we get a rectangle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355901611,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355903276,"data":"       | Thus we can count rectangles as number of ways to choose two distinct direction families (A,B) and then choose two distinct lines from each family (i.e., chords) such that the four lines are not concurrent (i.e., no three intersect at a point) and they form a convex quadrilateral with right angles (since lines are perpendicular? Actually for rectangle, adjacent sides must be perpendicular, not just any angle. Wait we missed condition: rectangle sides must be perpendicular, not just intersect. In Euclidean geometry, rectangle is a quadrilateral with four right angles. For a quadrilateral formed by two pairs of parallel lines, it is a parallelogram. To be a rectangle, the adjacent sides must be perpendicular, i.e., the two direction families must be orthogonal (i.e., the lines are at right angles). Indeed a rectangle is a parallelogram with right angles. So the two families of parallel chords must be perpendicular. So we must select two direction families that are orthogonal. In the regular dodecagon, direction families correspond to sums s. The direction angle of family s is (π s/12 + π/2) (mod π). So direction angle = (π/2) + (π s/12). Two families s and t are perpendicular if direction angles differ by π/2 (i.e., 90°). So we need (π/2 + π s/12) - (π/2 + π t/12) = π (s - t)/12 ≡ π/2 (mod π). So (s - t)/12 * π ≡ π/2 (mod π) =\u003e (s - t)/12 ≡ 1/2 (mod 1) =\u003e s - t ≡ 6 (mod 12). So families are perpendicular if their sum indices differ by 6 modulo 12. So direction families s and s+6 (mod12) are orthogonal. But note that s and s+6 correspond to the opposite direction (i.e., same line orientation rotated by 90°). Since we have 6 families (0-5), the perpendicular partner of s is s+3 (mod6) because adding 6 modulo 12 corresponds to adding 3 modulo 6. Indeed s and s+6 correspond to families with opposite sums, but they are distinct families? Wait s runs 0..5 (representative). Adding 6 gives s+6 which is in the other set (6..11) which is same direction as s (since s and 12-s are same direction). Actually earlier we said s and 12-s correspond to same direction. So s+6 is not same direction; it's perpendicular. Let's test: For s=0 direction angle = π/2 (vertical). s+6=6 direction angle = π/2 + π*6/12 = π/2 + π/2 = π (horizontal). So indeed s=0 vertical, s=6 horizontal (perpendicular). But s=6 is same as s=0? No, s=6 is not same as s=0 because s=0 and s=12 are same, but s=6 is distinct. So we have 12 possible sums, but direction families are identified modulo 12 up to sign (i.e., s and 12-s same). So we have 6 direction families: {0,6}, {1,11}, {2,10}, {3,9}, {4,8}, {5,7}. Actually each family includes two sums that are opposite (s and 12-s). So the families can be labeled by the unordered pair {s,12-s}. For example, family A: {0,12} = {0}, {6}? Wait 12-s for s=0 is 12, which is equivalent to 0. So family for s=0 includes sum 0 only? But we said s and 12-s produce same direction. So direction families correspond to pairs (s,12-s) where s=0..5. So we can label families by s (0..5). So s=0 family includes chords with sum 0 or 12 (same). s=1 family includes sum 1 or 11. s=2 includes 2 or 10. s=3 includes 3 or 9. s=4 includes 4 or 8. s=5 includes 5 or 7.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355914828,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355916001,"data":"       | Now perpendicular families: s and t such that direction angles differ by π/2. Since direction angle for family s is (π/2) + π s/12 (mod π). So difference = π (s - t)/12. Set equal to π/2 modulo π =\u003e (s - t)/12 ≡ 1/2 (mod 1) =\u003e s - t ≡ 6 (mod 12). Since s,t are in 0..5, we can consider s - t = 6 or -6 mod 12. But s,t in 0..5, difference cannot be 6 unless we consider modulo 12. So s and t are opposite families: s and t such that s ≡ t+6 (mod12). Since s,t in 0..5, adding 6 yields a number in 6..11, which corresponds to the same direction family as the complement? Wait we need to map to families. Let's compute direction angles for families s=0..5:\n"}
