,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.355966542,"data":"       | Thus direction families correspond to sum s modulo 12, but s and 12-s give same direction (parallel). So there are 6 families: s=0 (parallel to s=12), s=1 (parallel to s=11), s=2 (parallel to s=10), s=3 (parallel to s=9), s=4 (parallel to s=8), s=5 (parallel to s=7). The family s=6 is actually parallel to s=0? Let's check: s=0 direction angle = π/2 (vertical). s=6 direction angle = π (horizontal). They are perpendicular, not parallel. So s=6 is not parallel to s=0. But s=6 is not in the set {0,...,5} because s=6's direction is distinct. However, s=6 is parallel to s=6? Actually s=6 is its own family; but we already have 6 families from s=0..5. But there are 12 sums, each direction family includes s and s+6? Wait s and s+6 produce directions that differ by 90°, not parallel. So they are distinct families. So we have 12 sums, but direction families are determined modulo 12 up to sign (i.e., s and -s produce same direction). Since -s ≡ 12-s. So we have 6 families: {0,0}, {1,11}, {2,10}, {3,9}, {4,8}, {5,7}. The sum 6 is its own negative? Because -6 ≡ 6 (mod12). So 6 belongs to a family {6} (self). So there are actually 7 families? Wait we need to consider s and -s (mod12) equivalence classes. For s=0, -0=0 =\u003e class {0}. For s=6, -6=6 =\u003e class {6}. For s=1, -1=11 =\u003e class {1,11}. Similarly for 2,10; 3,9; 4,8; 5,7. That's total 1 + 1 + 5 = 7 classes. Indeed there are 7 direction families: s=0, s=6, and s=1,2,3,4,5 each paired with its complement.\n"}
