,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.356000078,"data":"       | - s=1 (105°) -\u003e +90° = 195° ≡ 15° (since modulo 180°, 195° - 180° = 15°). 15° corresponds to angle = 15°, which is direction of s? Which s gives angle 15°? Let's find s such that angle = π/2 + π s/12 ≡ 15° (π/12). Solve: π/2 + π s/12 ≡ π/12 (mod π). Subtract π/12: π/2 + π s/12 - π/12 = π/2 + π(s-1)/12 ≡ 0 (mod π). So π/2 + π(s-1)/12 = kπ. For k=0: π/2 + π(s-1)/12 = 0 =\u003e impossible. For k=1: π/2 + π(s-1)/12 = π =\u003e divide by π: 1/2 + (s-1)/12 = 1 =\u003e (s-1)/12 = 1/2 =\u003e s-1 = 6 =\u003e s = 7. But s=7 corresponds to same direction as s=5 (since s and 12-s are same). Indeed s=5 direction angle = 165°, which is 180° - 15° = 165°. That's not 15°, but direction lines have orientation modulo 180°, so 165° is same as -15° (i.e., 165° = 180° - 15°). So direction 15° is same as direction 165° (since lines are undirected). So s=5 family (or s=7) is perpendicular to s=1. So s=1 and s=5 are perpendicular.\n"}
