,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.356079908,"data":"       | However, not every such selection yields a rectangle: the four chords must intersect to form a quadrilateral, i.e., the two chords from family s must intersect each chord from family s+6. Since chords from distinct families intersect unless they share a vertex. If they share a vertex, intersection point is at that vertex. That's okay; rectangle vertices can be at polygon vertices. However, we must ensure that the four intersection points are distinct (i.e., the rectangle is not degenerate). If a chord from family s shares a vertex with both chords from family s+6, then that vertex would be intersection of three chords, which would cause overlapping? But chords from same family are parallel, cannot share a vertex. So each chord from family s can share at most one vertex with each chord from family s+6. So at most one vertex of rectangle may be a polygon vertex. That's fine.\n"}
