,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.356937586,"data":"       | Thus my assumption that any two chords from perpendicular families produce rectangle is false. The rectangle condition also requires that the two chords from each family be \"separated\" such that the intersection points form a convex quadrilateral with right angles and equal opposite sides. For vertical/horizontal families, any two distinct vertical lines and any two distinct horizontal lines produce rectangle because vertical lines are all parallel and horizontal lines are all parallel, and the distances between the two vertical lines measured along any horizontal line is constant (the horizontal distance). However, that holds for any pair of families of parallel lines: distance between two parallel lines is constant, independent of which line from the perpendicular family you intersect. So why does it fail for s=1 and s=7? Let's examine geometry: The lines L1 and L2 are parallel (s=1). The lines M1 and M2 are parallel (s=7) and perpendicular to L1/L2. The distance between M1 and M2 measured along direction perpendicular to them (i.e., along L1) is constant. So the rectangle formed by L1, L2, M1, M2 should have equal side lengths. So why is the shape not rectangle? Perhaps because L1 and L2 are not infinite lines but chords (segments) of finite length. The rectangle side must be a subsegment of the chord, but the chord may not extend far enough to intersect both lines M1 and M2. In our example, L1 is side (0,1) which is short; it intersects M1 at vertex 0 (point at one endpoint) and M2 at vertex 1 (other endpoint). So the segment of L1 between those intersection points is exactly the side of the polygon (short). That's fine. However, the distance between M1 and M2 measured along L1 is the length of segment of L1 between its intersection points with M1 and M2, which is the side length. Meanwhile, the distance between M1 and M2 measured along L2 is the length of segment of L2 between its intersection points with M1 and M2, which may be longer (since L2 is longer chord). Wait but the distance between two parallel lines is defined as the perpendicular distance between them, not measured along a particular line. The distance between M1 and M2 is constant (the perpendicular distance). However, the segment of L2 between its intersections with M1 and M2 is not necessarily equal to that perpendicular distance; it's the length of the segment of L2 that lies between the two lines M1 and M2. Since L2 is not perpendicular to M1/M2 (actually L2 is parallel to L1, which is perpendicular to M1/M2). So L2 is perpendicular to M1/M2, thus the segment of L2 between M1 and M2 is exactly the perpendicular distance between M1 and M2, which is same as the length of segment of L1 between M1 and M2 (since both are perpendicular). So they should be equal. But L2 may not intersect both M1 and M2 within its segment (i.e., the intersection points may lie outside the chord segment). Indeed chord (2,11) is long, but does it intersect both M1 and M2 within its segment? Let's check: chord (2,11) endpoints are vertices 2 (60°) and 11 (330°). The line extends beyond the segment but the segment is limited between those vertices. The intersection of line (2,11) with M1 (0,7) might be within the segment? Let's compute intersection point coordinates we got: (0.7499999999999998, -0.06698729810778072). Is this point between vertices 2 and 11? Let's compute param t along chord from vertex 2 to 11. Compute dot product etc.\n"}
