,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357771797,"data":"       | Thus a rectangle can be formed by choosing two distinct lines (one for each pair of opposite sides) that intersect at four points forming a rectangle. The two lines must be parallel (since opposite sides of rectangle are parallel). So we need two parallel lines each being a side or diagonal of the dodecagon. And they must intersect two other parallel lines (also sides or diagonals) that are perpendicular to the first pair (or at least intersect at right angles). Actually rectangle sides must be perpendicular, not just parallel pairs. So we need a set of four lines: two parallel lines L1 and L2 (forming opposite sides) and two parallel lines M1 and M2 (forming the other opposite sides), with L1 perpendicular to M1 (and M2). In Euclidean geometry, a rectangle is defined by four right angles. So we need L1 ⟂ M1. However, if the rectangle is not axis-aligned with the coordinate axes, we still need right angles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357774628,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357775752,"data":"       | Thus we need to find pairs of lines (sides or diagonals) that intersect at right angles. Then choose two distinct parallel lines from each family.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357777169,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357778318,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can think of selecting four vertices of the dodecagon (or intersection points) that form a rectangle. But the rectangle's sides must lie on sides or diagonals, not just any chord. So the rectangle's vertices must be points where two such lines intersect. That includes dodecagon vertices (where two sides meet) and interior intersection points of diagonals.\n"}
