,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357783764,"data":"       | Given a regular 12-gon inscribed in a circle. Let's assign coordinates to vertices for easier analysis. Suppose the dodecagon is centered at origin, radius 1 (or any). Vertex k at angle θ_k = 2πk/12 = πk/6. So coordinates: (cos πk/6, sin πk/6). Let's compute all lines formed by connecting any pair of vertices (including sides and diagonals). Among these lines, we need to consider their intersection points (including vertices). Then we need to count rectangles formed by four intersection points such that each side of rectangle lies on one of the lines (i.e., each side is a subsegment of a line connecting two vertices). Equivalent: choose two pairs of parallel lines (each from the set of lines connecting vertices) that intersect at right angles, and then the rectangle is determined by the intersection points of these four lines, provided those intersection points are within the dodecagon.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357786614,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357787746,"data":"       | But we also need to ensure that each side of rectangle lies entirely within the dodecagon (i.e., subsegment of the line that lies inside). Since the dodecagon is convex, any segment connecting two interior points of the dodecagon lies inside. So if we have four lines as described, the intersection points will be inside the dodecagon (or on its boundary). The rectangle formed will be inside.\n"}
