,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357925467,"data":"       | Given a rectangle inside the dodecagon, each side of rectangle lies on a chord of the dodecagon. Since each side is a subsegment of a chord, the chord containing that side is uniquely determined: it's the line through the side that connects two vertices of the dodecagon (the chord). However, there could be multiple chords that contain that side? For a given side segment, there may be multiple chords that contain that segment (i.e., chords that share the same line). For a regular polygon, any line passing through two vertices is a chord. If a side segment lies on a line that passes through two vertices, there could be multiple pairs of vertices that define the same line if the line passes through more than two vertices (i.e., if the line is a diameter passing through opposite vertices). For a regular 12-gon, lines through opposite vertices (diameters) pass through two vertices (i and i+6). That's the only case where a line contains more than two vertices? Actually a line could also pass through three vertices if they are collinear, but in a regular polygon, only diameters have this property (two opposite vertices). So each line (chord) is uniquely defined by its two endpoints (except for diameters where there are exactly two vertices). So each side of rectangle lies on a unique chord (the line connecting the two vertices that define that line). However, there could be multiple chords that share the same line if the line passes through more than two vertices (i.e., if there are multiple pairs of vertices collinear). In a regular even n-gon, each diameter passes through two opposite vertices; there are n/2 diameters, each defined by a pair of opposite vertices. So each diameter is a chord connecting opposite vertices, unique.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357931168,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357932437,"data":"       | Thus each rectangle side corresponds to a unique chord. So the rectangle is uniquely determined by the set of four chords (its sides). Conversely, given four chords (two parallel from family s, two parallel from family s+6) that intersect appropriately, we get a rectangle. So each rectangle corresponds to exactly one unordered set of four chords (two from each family). However, there is also the possibility that the rectangle's sides could be subsegments of longer chords (i.e., the rectangle side may be a subsegment of a chord that extends beyond the rectangle). But the chord is still defined by its endpoints (the vertices of the polygon). So the rectangle side is a subsegment of that chord.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357934822,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357935951,"data":"       | Thus each rectangle corresponds uniquely to a choice of two chords from family s and two chords from family s+6. So counting such selections should give the number of rectangles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357937428,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357938531,"data":"       | Now we need to count selections where each chord from s intersects each chord from s+6 inside polygon. Let's compute.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357939893,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357941023,"data":"       | We need to consider chords (i,j) with i\u003cj. Let's define sum s = (i+j) mod 12. For each s, collect chords. Then for each pair of chords L in family s and M in family s+6, check if they intersect inside polygon. Then we need to count quadruples where all four intersections are inside. However, if each L intersects each M inside, then automatically each pair of L and M intersect inside. But we also need to ensure that L1 and L2 intersect M1 and M2 inside. That's the same condition for each pair. So we can test for each pair of L chords (L1, L2) and each pair of M chords (M1, M2) that all four intersections are inside. Equivalent to requiring that for each L in {L1, L2} and each M in {M1, M2}, the intersection is inside.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357943475,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357944609,"data":"       | Thus we can enumerate all possible quadruples and test.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357945835,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357946935,"data":"       | Let's implement in Python.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357948122,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357949226,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codeimport itertools, math\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357952715,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357953862,"data":"       | n = 12\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357955006,"data":"       | vertices = [(math.cos(2*math.pi*k/n), math.sin(2*math.pi*k/n)) for k in range(n)]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357956328,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357957439,"data":"       | def chord_intersection_inside(i,j,k,l):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357958655,"data":"       |     # returns True if chords (i,j) and (k,l) intersect inside polygon (including at vertices)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357959962,"data":"       |     # chords are defined by vertices indices i\u003cj, k\u003cl.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357961422,"data":"       |     # Intersection inside polygon if they share a vertex (i==k or i==l or j==k or j==l) -\u003e on boundary (acceptable)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357962819,"data":"       |     # else if endpoints interleaved cyclically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357964032,"data":"       |     # We'll treat polygon vertices cyclic order 0..n-1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357965241,"data":"       |     # Determine if chords intersect inside polygon (including boundary).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357966497,"data":"       |     # Use orientation test: For convex polygon, chords intersect if and only if the intervals (i,j) and (k,l) cross when considered on the circle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357967897,"data":"       |     # Represent as cyclic intervals: i\u003cj, k\u003cl.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357969204,"data":"       |     # They intersect if i\u003ck\u003cj\u003cl or k\u003ci\u003cl\u003cj.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357970683,"data":"       |     # However need to handle wrap-around intervals (if j-i \u003e n/2?). But chords are defined as straight lines; the interval notion works for convex polygon if we consider the order around the circle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357972228,"data":"       |     # For chords that are not adjacent? Let's implement generic check using orientation of points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357973559,"data":"       |     # Use segment intersection test for line segments between vertices on circle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357974834,"data":"       |     # Compute intersection of line segments (i,j) and (k,l). If they intersect at a point that lies inside the polygon (including boundary), we return True.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35797624,"data":"       |     # Use robust method: compute orientation of triples.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357977454,"data":"       |     def orient(a,b,c):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357978616,"data":"       |         # cross product (b-a) x (c-a)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357979797,"data":"       |         ax, ay = vertices[a]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357980975,"data":"       |         bx, by = vertices[b]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357982177,"data":"       |         cx, cy = vertices[c]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35798339,"data":"       |         return (bx-ax)*(cy-ay) - (by-ay)*(cx-ax)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357984619,"data":"       |     # Check if segments intersect (including endpoints)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357985847,"data":"       |     # Using general segment intersection test for convex polygon (no collinearities except at vertices).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357987166,"data":"       |     # Since polygon is convex and vertices are in general position (no three collinear except diameters maybe).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35798999,"data":"       |     # We'll treat intersection at endpoints as True.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357991258,"data":"       |     # Use orientation signs\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357992456,"data":"       |     o1 = orient(i,j,k)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357993639,"data":"       |     o2 = orient(i,j,l)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357994838,"data":"       |     o3 = orient(k,l,i)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35799599,"data":"       |     o4 = orient(k,l,j)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357997408,"data":"       |     # Intersection if o1 and o2 have opposite signs (or zero) and o3 and o4 opposite signs (or zero)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.357998724,"data":"       |     # Use \u003c=0 for inclusive of collinear (endpoint)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358000044,"data":"       |     def sign(x):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35800119,"data":"       |         if abs(x) \u003c 1e-9:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358002401,"data":"       |             return 0\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358003559,"data":"       |         return 1 if x\u003e0 else -1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358004876,"data":"       |     s1, s2, s3, s4 = map(sign, (o1,o2,o3,o4))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358006084,"data":"       |     # General case\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358007239,"data":"       |     intersect = (s1*s2 \u003c= 0) and (s3*s4 \u003c= 0)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358008593,"data":"       |     # Need also to ensure that intersection point lies inside polygon (including boundary)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358009889,"data":"       |     # For convex polygon, if segments intersect, the intersection point lies inside polygon (or on boundary) because both endpoints are on boundary.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35801133,"data":"       |     # However, there is case where they intersect outside polygon if chords are extended lines? But we consider segments only.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358012713,"data":"       |     # Actually two chords (segments) can intersect outside polygon only if they share no interior points? In convex polygon, any two chords (segments) intersect either inside polygon or not at all (or at a vertex). They cannot intersect outside because the polygon is convex and chords lie inside polygon. Wait: chords are inside polygon (including boundary). So any point on a chord is inside polygon. Intersection of two chords must be inside polygon as well. However, there is case where chords intersect outside polygon if we consider lines extended beyond segment endpoints, but not segments.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358014921,"data":"       |     # So if segments intersect, the intersection point is inside polygon (including boundary).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358016268,"data":"       |     return intersect\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358017446,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358018565,"data":"       | # Test function on some known cases\n"}
