,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358165503,"data":"       | analysisWe have families of size 5 or 6 chords. For odd sums (1,3,5,7,9,11) there are 6 chords each. For even sums (0,2,4,6,8,10) there are 5 chords each. Indeed, chords with even sum correspond to chords where i+j even, which includes diameters (i+j ≡ 0 mod 12? Actually diameters have i+j ≡ 0 or 6? Let's check: opposite vertices i and i+6: sum = 2i+6 ≡ 6 (mod 12) if i even? Wait compute: i + (i+6) = 2i+6. Mod 12, 2i+6 can be any even number? Since 2i mod 12 can be 0,2,4,6,8,10. Adding 6 gives 6,8,10,0,2,4. So diameters belong to families 0,2,4,6,8,10 (even). So each even family includes some diameters plus other chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358167863,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358169045,"data":"       | Now families of size 5 (even sums) and size 6 (odd sums). Let's verify counts: total chords = n*(n-1)/2 = 12*11/2 = 66. Sum of sizes: 6 families of size 6 =\u003e 36, 6 families of size 5 =\u003e 30, total 66. Good.\n"}
