,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358172386,"data":"       | Now we need to consider each pair of perpendicular families: (s, s+6). For s from 0..5, we have pairs: (0,6), (1,7), (2,8), (3,9), (4,10), (5,11). Let's compute rectangles count for each pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358173876,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358175049,"data":"       | Approach: For each s, let A = chords in family s, B = chords in family s+6. For each unordered pair of distinct chords a1,a2 from A, and unordered pair b1,b2 from B, check if each a_i intersects each b_j (i=1,2; j=1,2) inside polygon. If yes, count rectangle. Since ordering of pairs doesn't matter, we count each rectangle once. So number of rectangles for pair (A,B) = number of unordered pairs of chords from A times number of unordered pairs from B, but only those where all intersections are inside. However, it's possible that some chord pairs (a,b) do not intersect inside (maybe they intersect outside). So we need to filter.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358177326,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358178448,"data":"       | Let's compute via brute force enumeration.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.358179625,"data":"       | \n"}
