,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359534881,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35953602,"data":"       | Let's check OEIS maybe: This could be formula (n/2)*(n/2-1)*(n/2-2)*(n/2-3)/? Let's compute for n=12 (m=6). Evaluate m*(m-1)*(m-2)*(m-3) = 6*5*4*3 = 360. Our count 315 is 360 - 45. For n=10 (m=5): m*(m-1)*(m-2)*(m-3) = 5*4*3*2 = 120. Our count 125 is 5 more. So not simple.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359537579,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359538671,"data":"       | Let's compute difference between count and C(m,2)^2 = (m choose 2)^2 = (m(m-1)/2)^2.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359539955,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359541255,"data":"       | For m=6, C(6,2)=15, square=225. Count 315 = 225 + 90. For m=5, C(5,2)=10, square=100, count 125 = 100 + 25. For m=4, C(4,2)=6, square=36, count 40 = 36 + 4. For m=3, C(3,2)=3, square=9, count 9 = 9 + 0. For m=2 (n=4), C(2,2)=1, square=1, count 1 = 1. So additional term seems to be (m-2)^2? Let's test: For m=6, (m-2)^2 = 16, but we need 90. Not.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35954296,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359544075,"data":"       | Maybe count = C(m,2)^2 + C(m,4)*something? Let's compute C(m,4) = m choose 4.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359546881,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359548005,"data":"       | For m=6, C(6,4)=15. Additional 90 = 6*15? Actually 6*15=90. So count = C(m,2)^2 + (m-?)*C(m,4)? Let's compute for m=5: C(5,2)^2 = 100. C(5,4)=5. Additional 25 = 5*5. So additional = (m?) * C(m,4)? For m=5, m=5, C(5,4)=5, product = 25 matches. For m=6, m=6, C(6,4)=15, product = 90 matches. For m=4, C(4,2)^2 = 36. C(4,4)=1. Additional 4 = 4*1 matches. For m=3, C(3,2)^2 = 9. C(3,4)=0, additional 0 matches. For m=2, C(2,2)^2 = 1, C(2,4)=0, additional 0 matches.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.3595499,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359550967,"data":"       | Thus formula appears to be:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359552124,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359553224,"data":"       | Number of rectangles = C(m,2)^2 + m * C(m,4), where m = n/2 (half the number of vertices). Let's test for m=7 (n=14). Compute formula: C(7,2)=21, square=441. C(7,4)=35, m*C(m,4)=7*35=245. Sum = 441+245=686. Indeed matches count for n=14 (686). For m=8 (n=16): C(8,2)=28, square=784. C(8,4)=70, m*C(m,4)=8*70=560. Sum=1344 matches. Good! So formula holds.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359554922,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359556006,"data":"       | Thus number of rectangles for regular 2m-gon is:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359557155,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359558223,"data":"       | R(m) = (C(m,2))^2 + m*C(m,4) = (m(m-1)/2)^2 + m * (m choose 4).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359559417,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359560529,"data":"       | Simplify: R(m) = [m(m-1)/2]^2 + m * [m(m-1)(m-2)(m-3)/24] = (m^2 (m-1)^2)/4 + m^2 (m-1)(m-2)(m-3)/24.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359561829,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359562881,"data":"       | Factor m^2 (m-1) maybe.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359563973,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359565043,"data":"       | Compute: R(m) = m^2 (m-1)^2 /4 + m^2 (m-1)(m-2)(m-3)/24 = m^2 (m-1) [ (m-1)/4 + (m-2)(m-3)/24 ] = m^2 (m-1) [ (6(m-1) + (m-2)(m-3))/24 ] = m^2 (m-1) [ (6m -6 + m^2 -5m +6)/24 ] = m^2 (m-1) [ (m^2 + m)/24 ] = m^2 (m-1) * (m(m+1))/24 = m^3 (m-1)(m+1)/24.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359566571,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359567656,"data":"       | Check: m^3 (m-1)(m+1)/24 = m^3 (m^2 -1)/24 = (m^5 - m^3)/24. Let's test for m=6: (6^5 - 6^3)/24 = (7776 - 216)/24 = 7560/24 = 315. Yes! For m=5: (3125 - 125)/24 = 3000/24 = 125. Good! So formula simplifies to:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359569113,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359570167,"data":"       | R(m) = (m^5 - m^3)/24 = m^3 (m^2 - 1)/24 = m^3 (m-1)(m+1)/24.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359571371,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359572619,"data":"       | Thus for n = 2m, number of rectangles formed by sides and diagonals of regular n-gon is (m^5 - m^3)/24.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359573897,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359574934,"data":"       | Now for n=12, m=6, we get (6^5 - 6^3)/24 = (7776 - 216)/24 = 7560/24 = 315.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359576147,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359577208,"data":"       | Thus answer 315.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359578297,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359579353,"data":"       | Now we can produce solution using combinatorial reasoning deriving this formula.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359582181,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359583275,"data":"       | Let's attempt to derive formula elegantly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35958446,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359585531,"data":"       | We can approach as follows:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359586659,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359587763,"data":"       | Let the regular dodecagon have vertices labeled 0,...,11. A chord (i,j) has sum s = i+j (mod 12). Two chords are parallel iff they have same sum modulo 12. Two chords are perpendicular iff their sums differ by 6 (mod 12). So rectangle sides must be chosen from two families of chords whose sums differ by 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359589417,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35959051,"data":"       | Thus we have 6 pairs of perpendicular families: (0,6), (1,7), (2,8), (3,9), (4,10), (5,11). For each pair, we need to count the number of rectangles formed using chords from those families.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359591953,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359593035,"data":"       | Now we need to count for a given pair (s, s+6) the number of K_{2,2} subgraphs in the bipartite graph where vertices are chords from each family and edges correspond to intersecting chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35959451,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359595699,"data":"       | We can analyze combinatorially using the labeling.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35959689,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359598151,"data":"       | Let m = n/2 = 6. For a given pair (s, s+6), the chords in family s correspond to pairs (i, s-i) for i = 0,...,m-1 (mod n). Among them, there is exactly one diameter if s even, otherwise none. So we have two types of families: even s (size m-1 = 5 chords, one of which is a diameter) and odd s (size m = 6 chords, all non-diameters). Similarly the perpendicular family has opposite parity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359599921,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359601139,"data":"       | We can count rectangles by considering three cases based on whether the chosen pair of parallel chords includes a diameter.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359602455,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359603817,"data":"       | Case 1: Both families consist of non-diameter chords (i.e., s odd). Then each family has m chords. For any two chords from the first family, the number of chords in the perpendicular family intersecting both equals 2,4, or 6 depending on the relative positions. Counting yields total rectangles = (m choose 2)*(m-2 choose 2) + (something). Actually we can derive formula.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359605563,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359606761,"data":"       | But perhaps easier: Use formula derived earlier: total rectangles = m^3 (m-1)(m+1)/24.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359607986,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359609214,"data":"       | We can present derivation:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359610348,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359611554,"data":"       | Consider all unordered pairs of parallel chords (choose two). There are two types: (i) pairs of chords that are diameters? Actually there is at most one diameter per family. So we can treat separately.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359613014,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35961418,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can count rectangles directly: Choose any four vertices of the dodecagon that are the endpoints of two diameters (i.e., choose two diameters). This yields rectangles with all four vertices on polygon: C(m,2) = 15.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359617354,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359618599,"data":"       | Next, consider rectangles with exactly two vertices on polygon. Choose a diameter (m choices). Choose a second chord parallel to that diameter but not sharing its endpoints (so choose a chord from same family that is not the diameter and not sharing endpoints). There are (m-2) such chords? Let's compute: For a given diameter, the family (even) has m-1 chords total (including the diameter). Among the remaining chords, some share an endpoint with the diameter (i.e., chords that have one endpoint same as one of the diameter's vertices). Let's count those: The diameter connects vertices a and a+m (mod n). Any chord from same family that shares a vertex must be of form (a, a+k) where k ≡ s - a (mod n). But since s = sum of diameter endpoints = a + (a+m) = 2a + m ≡ m (mod n) because 2a mod n cancels? Actually sum of diameter endpoints is (a + a+m) = 2a + m ≡ m (mod n) because 2a ≡ 0 (mod n) only if n divides 2a, which is true when n=2m and a integer? Wait 2a mod n may not be zero unless a multiple of m. But for any a, 2a mod n may be not zero. However, sum s = a + (a+m) = 2a + m. Mod n, this is (2a + m) mod 2m. Since m = n/2, 2a + m mod 2m = (2a mod 2m) + m. So s can be any odd number? Actually m is even? For n=12, m=6, s = 2a + 6 mod 12. Since 2a mod 12 is even, s is even. So s even. So family is even.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359622038,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359623375,"data":"       | Now chords in same family have sum s. So any chord (i,j) with i+j ≡ s (mod n). If we fix a diameter (a, a+m), the other chords in same family are those with i+j ≡ s (mod n). Among them, some share vertex a or a+m. Let's count.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359624949,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359626053,"data":"       | Given a fixed diameter endpoints a and a+m. For any other vertex x ≠ a, a+m, there is a unique chord in the family that includes x: the chord (x, s - x mod n). Since s = a + a+m = 2a + m, the chord containing x will be (x, s - x). Does this chord share a vertex with the diameter? It will share a vertex if s - x ≡ a (mod n) or s - x ≡ a+m (mod n). That gives x ≡ s - a or s - (a+m). Compute s - a = (2a + m) - a = a + m = a+m (mod n). So x = a+m, which is excluded. Similarly s - (a+m) = (2a + m) - (a+m) = a (mod n). So x = a, excluded. So any chord containing a vertex other than a or a+m does not share endpoints with the diameter. So chords from same family that are not the diameter are all disjoint from it (no shared vertices). Wait but earlier we saw chords (2,10) and (3,9) share vertex? Actually (3,9) is diameter, (2,10) does not share vertices with (3,9). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359628737,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359629784,"data":"       | Thus for a given diameter, the other chords in its family are all disjoint from the diameter. So they can be used as opposite side of rectangle with exactly two vertices on polygon (the endpoints of the diameter). Good.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359631265,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35963238,"data":"       | Thus number of choices for opposite side (parallel chord) is (m-2) (since total chords in family = m-1, subtract the diameter itself). For m=6, that's 5-1 = 4? Wait m-2 = 4. Indeed there are 4 chords parallel to a given diameter that are not the diameter.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359633949,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359635029,"data":"       | Now, after choosing the two parallel chords (diameter and another chord), we need to choose two chords from the perpendicular family that intersect both of them. The perpendicular family (odd sum) has m chords (no diameters). Among these, which chords intersect both selected parallel chords? Let's analyze.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359638319,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359639629,"data":"       | Given a diameter D (a, a+m) and a parallel chord C (i, j) (i+j = s). We need chords from the perpendicular family (sum s+6) that intersect both D and C. Since D is a diameter, any chord from the perpendicular family that is not parallel to D (which they aren't) will intersect D at exactly one of its endpoints? Actually D is a diameter; a chord from perpendicular family will intersect D at a point interior to D (unless it shares an endpoint). Since D's endpoints are a and a+m. A chord from perpendicular family may share one of these vertices (if its sum is s+6). Let's check: For a given vertex a, there is a chord in the perpendicular family that includes a: chord (a, t - a) where t = s+6. Since s = a + a+m = 2a + m, then t = s+6 = 2a + m + 6 = 2a + (m+6). Since n=12, m+6 = 12 = n, so t ≡ 2a (mod n). So chord (a, 2a - a) = (a, a) not valid. Wait compute properly: t = s+6 mod n. Since n=12, s+6 ≡ (2a + m + 6) mod 12 = 2a + (m+6) mod 12. Since m=6, m+6 = 12 ≡ 0, so t ≡ 2a (mod 12). So the chord from perpendicular family that contains vertex a would be (a, t - a) = (a, 2a - a) = (a, a) which is not a chord. So there is no chord in the perpendicular family that contains a. Similarly for a+m. So chords from perpendicular family do not share endpoints with the diameter. So they intersect D at interior points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359643055,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359644147,"data":"       | Now we need chords from perpendicular family that intersect both D and C. Since D and C are parallel, they are separated by some distance. The chords from perpendicular family that intersect both will be those that pass between the two parallel lines. For a given pair of parallel chords, the number of perpendicular chords intersecting both is equal to the number of chords whose endpoints lie on opposite sides of both chords (i.e., intersecting the strip). This number depends on the relative positions of the chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359646134,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359647315,"data":"       | We can compute combinatorially: For a given pair of parallel chords (a, a+m) and (i,j) with i+j = s, the number of chords from perpendicular family intersecting both equals the number of vertices k such that the chord (k, t - k) intersects both. Equivalent to counting k such that k lies in the interval between the two parallel chords? Let's think.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359649035,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359650161,"data":"       | Better approach: Use the labeling method to compute the number of chords from perpendicular family intersecting both given chords. This can be expressed in terms of the number of vertices between certain arcs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359651639,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359652725,"data":"       | But perhaps there is a more global counting approach: Count all rectangles by counting K_{2,2} subgraphs using combinatorial formulas for bipartite graph defined by intersecting chords. This graph is known as \"intersection graph of chords of a circle\" restricted to families with sums differing by 6. This graph might have a regular structure that yields formula.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359654556,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359655639,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can count rectangles by considering the possible sets of four vertices of the dodecagon that can serve as rectangle vertices, plus additional interior vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359658858,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359659962,"data":"       | But we already have formula derived from data: R(m) = (m^5 - m^3)/24. Let's derive combinatorially.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359661228,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359662325,"data":"       | One approach: Consider all unordered pairs of parallel chords (choose two parallel chords). There are two types: both from families with even sum (size m-1 each) and both from families with odd sum (size m each). Actually we need to consider each pair of families (s, s+6). For each such pair, we need to choose two chords from each family that intersect each other appropriately.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359664093,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359665203,"data":"       | We can compute total number of K_{2,2} subgraphs in a complete bipartite graph K_{a,b} is C(a,2) * C(b,2). But our bipartite graph is not complete: not all chords intersect. However, for families (odd, even) the intersection pattern is regular: each chord from one family intersects exactly (some number) chords from the other family.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359666893,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359667978,"data":"       | Let's compute degrees.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359669074,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359670315,"data":"       | For a given chord from family s (size a), how many chords from family t = s+6 intersect it? Let's compute for even s (size a = m-1) and odd s (size a = m). Use Python to compute degree distribution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35967177,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359672873,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codedef degree_distribution(s):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359674049,"data":"       |     A = family[s]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359675185,"data":"       |     B = family[(s+6)%n]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359676363,"data":"       |     degs = []\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359677474,"data":"       |     for a in A:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359678599,"data":"       |         deg = sum(1 for b in B if chord_intersection_inside(a[0],a[1],b[0],b[1]))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359679841,"data":"       |         degs.append(deg)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359680992,"data":"       |     return degs\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359682104,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359683156,"data":"       | for s in range(6):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359684269,"data":"       |     print(s, degree_distribution(s))\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359685438,"data":"       | \n"}
