,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359713801,"data":"     [Reasoning] (8876 chars):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359714993,"data":"       | analysisFor s even (0,2,4), degrees are [3,5,5,5,3] i.e., two chords intersect 3 chords from opposite family, three chords intersect 5 chords. The chords with degree 3 are the diameters? Let's check: For s=0, chords: (1,11) (non-diameter) degree 3? Actually earlier we saw (1,11) had common neighbor count 3? That was for pair (1,11) with (2,10) etc. Let's identify which chords have degree 3: (1,11) and (5,7) maybe? Those are the chords that are \"most extreme\" near the ends of the family.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359717023,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359718113,"data":"       | For s odd (1,3,5), degrees are [2,4,6,6,4,2] (two chords degree 2, two degree 4, two degree 6). So distribution symmetrical.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359719409,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359720501,"data":"       | Thus each chord from odd family intersects 2,4,6 chords from even family depending on its position.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35972174,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359722961,"data":"       | Now we need to count K_{2,2} subgraphs. There is known formula: Number of K_{2,2} subgraphs in a bipartite graph can be computed as sum over unordered pairs of vertices on left of C(|N(u) ∩ N(v)|, 2). That's what we used.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359724486,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359725592,"data":"       | We can compute the intersection sizes using combinatorial reasoning: For families with sizes a and b, and given the cyclic ordering, the intersection size for a pair of chords depends on the distance between them.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359727047,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35972812,"data":"       | We can derive formulas for even families (size m-1). Let's attempt to derive general formulas for m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359729373,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359730515,"data":"       | General case: n = 2m. Families with sum s even have size m-1 (including a diameter). Families with sum s odd have size m (no diameters). Let's denote families: even families (E) size m-1, odd families (O) size m.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35973205,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359734659,"data":"       | Now consider a pair of chords from an even family E. There are three types of pairs: (i) both chords are non-diameters, (ii) one is the diameter and the other is a non-diameter, (iii) both are non-diameters but on opposite sides? Actually we need to compute intersection size of common neighbors in the opposite odd family O.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359736448,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35973759,"data":"       | Let's define labeling for even family E_s: chords are (i, s-i) for i = 0,...,m-1 (mod n). Among them, the diameter corresponds to i = (s - (i+m))? Actually the diameter occurs when i and s-i differ by m (i.e., s-i = i+m mod n). Solve for i: s - i ≡ i + m (mod n) =\u003e 2i ≡ s - m (mod n). Since s even, s - m ≡ s - m (mod 2m). Since n=2m, we can find unique solution i = (s - m)/2 (mod m). So there is exactly one i giving a diameter. So we can label chords by i from 0..m-1, with i = d being the diameter.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359739804,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359740914,"data":"       | Now consider two chords from E: (i, s-i) and (j, s-j) with i\u003cj (mod m). Their sum is s each.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359742268,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359743399,"data":"       | Now we need to count chords from O family (sum t = s+ m) that intersect both. O chords are (k, t - k) for k = 0..m-1 (since t odd). They are all non-diameters.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359744826,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359745955,"data":"       | Intersection condition: chord (i, s-i) intersects (k, t - k) if and only if k lies between i and s-i (cyclic) but not both? Let's derive.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359747352,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359748606,"data":"       | Define orientation: chord (i, s-i) splits the circle into two arcs: from i clockwise to s-i (excluding endpoints), and the other arc from s-i clockwise to i. For a chord (k, t - k) to intersect (i, s-i), exactly one endpoint k must lie in the first arc and the other endpoint t - k must lie in the second arc. Since t = s + m, we have t - k = s + m - k (mod n). So condition: k in interval (i, s-i) (clockwise) XOR (t - k) in that interval. Equivalent to k in interval (i, s-i) but t - k not in that interval.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359750652,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359751753,"data":"       | Thus intersection condition can be expressed in terms of positions.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359753017,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35975413,"data":"       | Now for two chords (i, s-i) and (j, s-j), we need chords (k, t - k) that intersect both. This means k must be in both intervals (i, s-i) and (j, s-j) (or one of the endpoints in each interval). Actually for each chord, we need exactly one endpoint inside the interval. So for both chords simultaneously, we need k to be in the intersection of the intervals for which the condition holds? Let's examine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359755983,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359757123,"data":"       | Let I_i = interval of vertices strictly between i and s-i clockwise (excluding endpoints). Similarly I_j = interval between j and s-j clockwise. For a chord (k, t - k) to intersect both chords, we need k ∈ I_i XOR (t - k) ∈ I_i, and similarly for j. Since t - k = s + m - k, note that if k ∈ I_i, then t - k is not in I_i because t - k is opposite side? Actually not necessarily. But due to geometry, if k is in I_i, then the other endpoint is not in I_i because the chord is not parallel to the first chord.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359760752,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359761886,"data":"       | Thus condition reduces to k being in the intersection of I_i and I_j, or k being in the complement of both intervals? Let's test with examples.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359763276,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359764405,"data":"       | Let's compute for m=6, s=0, i=2 (diameter?), j=4? Actually i=2, j=4 produce chords (2,10) and (4,8). Intersection counts with O family (t=6) gave common=5. Let's compute intervals.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359765887,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359767017,"data":"       | Indices modulo 12. For chord (2,10): i=2, s-i = -2 mod 12 = 10. Interval I_i = vertices 3,4,5,6,7,8,9 (clockwise from 2 to 10). That's 7 vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359768416,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359769635,"data":"       | For chord (4,8): interval I_j = vertices 5,6,7 (clockwise from 4 to 8). That's 3 vertices.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359770945,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359772062,"data":"       | Intersection of I_i and I_j = vertices 5,6,7 (3 vertices). But common neighbor count is 5, not 3. So my condition is not just k in intersection.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359773515,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359775528,"data":"       | Let's compute chords from O family (odd sum) that intersect both. O chords are (k, 6 - k) (since t = s+6 = 6). Actually for s=0, t=6. So O chords are (k, 6 - k) mod 12. Let's list them: (0,6) (diameter? Wait sum 6 even; but O family is odd sum? Actually t=6 is even, but O family should have odd sum. Wait s=0 even, s+6 = 6 even. But earlier we said families differ by 6 are perpendicular regardless of parity. So both families can be even or odd. Actually for n=12, s even leads to t = s+6 also even (since 6 is even). So families can both be even. Wait earlier we said families with sum parity differ? Let's check: s even =\u003e s+6 even (since 6 even). So both families have same parity. Indeed families (0,6) both even. Similarly (1,7) both odd. So each perpendicular pair consists of same parity families. So earlier classification of even vs odd families is not about perpendicular families but about each family individually. So for s even, both families are even; for s odd, both families are odd. So we need to consider both cases.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359778573,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359779943,"data":"       | Thus for s=0, both families are even: A (family 0) size 5 (including diameter), B (family 6) also size 5 (including diameter). Actually family 6 also has a diameter (since sum 6). Indeed chords with sum 6 include diameters (0,6), (1,5), (2,4), (3,3?) Wait (3,3) not allowed. So there are 5 chords: (0,6) diameter, (1,5), (2,4), (7,11)?? Actually (7,11) sum 18 ≡ 6 mod 12. So there are 5 chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359781833,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.35978305,"data":"       | Thus for s even, both families have size m-1 = 5.\n"}
