,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.359917146,"data":"       | The central chords are those that are \"close to the diameter\". The diameter is one chord. The chords adjacent to the diameter (i.e., chords whose indices differ by 1 from the diameter) are two chords. Then the pair of those two adjacent chords also have common = m-1. So central pairs: (diameter, chord adjacent left), (diameter, chord adjacent right), and (adjacent left, adjacent right). That's 3 pairs, independent of m? For m \u003e 3, there will be exactly 3 central pairs? Let's test for larger m (e.g., n=14, m=7). Let's compute distribution for even s families for n=14.\n"}
