,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362190782,"data":"       | Actually we can think of the polygon's interior as a convex region; the lines of each family cut the polygon into strips. For each family, the polygon's intersection with each line yields a segment (or point). The lines are ordered; the outermost lines intersect polygon at its boundary. The interior region of the polygon is bounded by the outermost lines of each family (the extreme lines). The rectangle formed by lines L_i, L_j (i\u003cj) from family A and M_k, M_l (k\u003cl) from family B will be inside polygon if and only if the rectangle's vertices are inside polygon. Since the polygon is convex, this is equivalent to L_i and L_j being not beyond the polygon's boundary relative to family A, and similarly for B. But all lines are chords of polygon, so each line lies entirely inside polygon (except possibly outside beyond polygon? Actually a chord line extends beyond polygon, but the segment of the line inside polygon is the chord segment. The line outside the polygon is outside region. So for a rectangle formed by two lines from each family, the rectangle's vertices are intersection points of those lines; those points lie inside polygon if they lie on the chord segments (i.e., inside). Since each line intersects polygon in a segment (the chord). The intersection point of two lines will be inside polygon if it lies on both chord segments, i.e., within the polygon. So we need to ensure that the intersection point lies between the two vertices of each chord.\n"}
