,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362228142,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362229314,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to counting axis-aligned rectangles formed by grid lines (vertical lines at u_i and horizontal lines at v_k) that lie within a convex polygon (the transformed dodecagon). This is reminiscent of counting rectangles in a grid inside a convex region.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362230932,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362232095,"data":"       | Thus we can compute number of axis-aligned rectangles inside a convex polygon defined by set of vertical lines (U) and horizontal lines (V). The polygon is centrally symmetric and its boundary intersects the grid lines at certain points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362233665,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362234825,"data":"       | The number of axis-aligned rectangles inside a convex region can be counted by combinatorial formula: For each pair of vertical lines (u_i\u003cu_j), the allowed horizontal lines (v) must be such that the segment between u_i and u_j lies within polygon for the given v range. Equivalent to for each vertical strip between u_i and u_j, the polygon's intersection with that strip is an interval in v (maybe varying). Then we need to count pairs of horizontal lines (v_k\u003cv_l) that are both within that interval.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362236951,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362238092,"data":"       | Thus total rectangles = sum over i\u003cj of C( number of v values that lie within the vertical strip between u_i and u_j , 2). Where number of v values is count of horizontal lines whose v coordinate lies within the intersection of polygon with the vertical strip.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362239717,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362240845,"data":"       | Thus we can compute counts for each direction pair using geometry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362242089,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362245007,"data":"       | But perhaps easier: we can compute using coordinates of lines: For each direction pair, compute u-values (signed distance from origin) for lines in group α, and v-values for group β. Then compute count using above formula.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362246618,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362247752,"data":"       | Let's attempt to compute analytically to verify 51 and 54 counts.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362248975,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362250137,"data":"       | But for final answer we just need number 315. However we may want to provide reasoning in solution: use symmetry and counting of rectangles formed by families of parallel chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362251581,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362252704,"data":"       | Let's attempt to derive counts.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362253857,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362254974,"data":"       | First, identify the six direction pairs:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362256139,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362257247,"data":"       | - Pair A: horizontal (0) and vertical (π/2) directions (both even groups). Each has 5 lines.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362258554,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362259669,"data":"       | - Pair B: direction 0.2618 rad (15°) and 1.8326 rad (105°) (both odd groups). Each has 6 lines.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362260995,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362262134,"data":"       | - Pair C: direction 0.5236 rad (30°) and 2.0944 rad (120°) (both even groups). Each has 5 lines.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362263458,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36226457,"data":"       | - Pair D: direction 0.7854 rad (45°) and 2.3562 rad (135°) (both odd groups). Each has 6 lines.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362265874,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362266977,"data":"       | - Pair E: direction 1.0472 rad (60°) and 2.61799 rad (150°) (both even groups). Each has 5 lines.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362268279,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362269397,"data":"       | - Pair F: direction 1.30899 rad (75°) and 2.87979 rad (165°) (both odd groups). Each has 6 lines.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362270724,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36227188,"data":"       | Thus three even-even pairs (size5 each) and three odd-odd pairs (size6 each).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36227311,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362274225,"data":"       | Now we need to count rectangles for each pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362275432,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362276549,"data":"       | Let's compute the u-values for each line group.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362277717,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362278857,"data":"       | Define coordinate axes aligned with the directions: For each pair, we can rotate coordinate system so that one direction is horizontal (x-axis) and the other vertical (y-axis). Then the lines in first group are vertical lines (x = constant) and lines in second group are horizontal lines (y = constant). The constants correspond to distances from origin.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.3622806,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362283317,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute the set of x-coordinates of vertical lines (i.e., distances of lines of direction α from origin) and y-coordinates of horizontal lines (distances of lines of direction β). Then count axis-aligned rectangles formed by these lines that lie within polygon.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362285004,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362286125,"data":"       | The polygon after rotation is still a regular dodecagon oriented differently, but we can compute its projection onto x and y axes.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362287501,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362288655,"data":"       | We can compute the x-coordinates of vertical lines (i.e., for each chord line of direction α, compute its signed distance from origin along the normal direction). Since direction α is perpendicular to the normal direction (the direction of x-axis). For vertical lines (parallel to y-axis), x-coordinate is constant.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362290404,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362291517,"data":"       | Thus we need to compute the set of x-values for lines in group α, and similarly y-values for group β.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362292867,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362294007,"data":"       | Then the rectangle defined by x_i \u003c x_j and y_k \u003c y_l is inside polygon if for all four corners (x_i,y_k), (x_i,y_l), (x_j,y_k), (x_j,y_l) are inside polygon. Since polygon is convex and symmetric, we can check condition that for each vertical line pair (x_i,x_j), the allowed y-values must be within the vertical strip.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362295797,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362296947,"data":"       | The polygon's interior in uv-plane is intersection of half-planes defined by its sides (rotated accordingly). Since polygon is regular, its boundary is given by lines at angles multiples of 30°, after rotation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362298501,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362299921,"data":"       | Thus we can compute for each pair (x_i,x_j) the min and max y allowed: the polygon's intersection with vertical strip is a convex shape whose y-interval at a given x is defined by top and bottom edges of polygon. Since polygon is convex, the y-interval is a single interval [y_min(x), y_max(x)], where y_min and y_max are piecewise linear functions of x (since polygon edges are lines). For each vertical strip between x_i and x_j, the set of y-values that are inside polygon for all x in [x_i,x_j] is the intersection of intervals across x, which is [max_{x∈[x_i,x_j]} y_min(x), min_{x∈[x_i,x_j]} y_max(x)]? Actually we need points (x_i,y_k) and (x_j,y_k) to be inside polygon. So for a given y_k, we need both (x_i,y_k) and (x_j,y_k) inside polygon. Since polygon is convex, if both endpoints of a horizontal segment at y=y_k are inside polygon, then the whole segment between them is inside polygon. So the condition reduces to: y_k must be between y_min(x_i) and y_max(x_i), and also between y_min(x_j) and y_max(x_j). So y_k must be ≤ min(y_max(x_i), y_max(x_j)) and ≥ max(y_min(x_i), y_min(x_j)). So the allowable y-values for a given vertical strip are those y values that lie in the intersection of the intervals at x_i and x_j.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362303369,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362304502,"data":"       | Thus for each pair of x-values (x_i\u003cx_j), the allowable y-values are those in the interval I_{ij} = [max(y_min(x_i), y_min(x_j)), min(y_max(x_i), y_max(x_j))]. Since y-values are from set of horizontal line constants V, we need to count how many of those lie within I_{ij}. Then number of rectangles for that pair is C(k,2) where k = number of y-values inside I_{ij}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36230872,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362309882,"data":"       | Thus total rectangles = sum_{i\u003cj} C(k_{ij}, 2).\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362311169,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362312289,"data":"       | Thus we can compute analytically by evaluating y_min and y_max at each x coordinate.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362313564,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36231467,"data":"       | Now we can compute for each direction pair using coordinates.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362315924,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362317061,"data":"       | But we can also derive formulas using symmetry: For even groups (size5), the x-values are symmetric: there is a central line at x=0 (through center) and two pairs at ±a, ±b (with a\u003cb). Similarly for odd groups (size6), there are three pairs at ±c, ±d, ±e (no zero). The values correspond to distances from center.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362318924,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362320044,"data":"       | Thus we can compute these distances.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362321225,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362322384,"data":"       | Let's compute for each direction pair the distances.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362323747,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36232486,"data":"       | We'll rotate coordinates so that direction α is vertical (i.e., lines are vertical). That is, we rotate the polygon such that the direction α's normal aligns with x-axis. Equivalent to rotate polygon by -α (so that lines of direction α become vertical). Then compute x-coordinates of lines.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362326581,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362327881,"data":"       | Simplify: For each chord line defined by two vertices, compute its signed distance from origin along normal direction n = unit vector perpendicular to line direction (i.e., direction α). The distance d = n·p for any point p on line (since line passes through p). Since n is unit, d is signed distance.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362329607,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362330755,"data":"       | Thus for group α, we compute distances d_i (i=1..m). Similarly for group β, compute distances e_j.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362332142,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362333254,"data":"       | Now we need to compute y_min(x) and y_max(x) for the rotated polygon. Since the polygon is regular, the shape in rotated coordinates is still a regular dodecagon rotated; its boundary lines are at angles multiples of 30° relative to rotated axes. So we can compute y_min(x) and y_max(x) piecewise.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362334907,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362336037,"data":"       | But perhaps we can compute the intervals I_{ij} directly by checking which y-values are inside polygon for both x_i and x_j.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362337395,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36233851,"data":"       | Thus we can compute count for each pair using algorithmic enumeration of distances, which yields 51 and 54 as we computed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362339877,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362341019,"data":"       | But we need to produce a solution that is combinatorial and not rely on brute force.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362345189,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362346357,"data":"       | Nevertheless, we can present solution: Use symmetry to reduce to counting rectangles for three types of direction pairs: those formed by chords of odd step lengths (6 lines each) and those formed by chords of even step lengths (5 lines each). For each type, compute number of rectangles using analysis of distances.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362348055,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362349198,"data":"       | Let's attempt to compute counts manually.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362350409,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362351517,"data":"       | First, let's compute distances for each group.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362352705,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362353857,"data":"       | We'll compute for each direction group the signed distances from center.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362355119,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362356248,"data":"       | assistantanalysis to=python codedef line_distance(pair, normal):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362357528,"data":"       |     # normal is unit vector (nx,ny) perpendicular to line direction (i.e., normal points outward)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362358857,"data":"       |     # compute signed distance from origin to line defined by pair (i,j)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362360117,"data":"       |     i,j=pair\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362361247,"data":"       |     x1,y1=verts[i]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362362425,"data":"       |     x2,y2=verts[j]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362363627,"data":"       |     # line passes through point (x1,y1). distance = dot(normal, point)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362364894,"data":"       |     nx,ny=normal\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362366029,"data":"       |     d = nx*x1 + ny*y1\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362367421,"data":"       |     # Since line passes through both points, distance same for either.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362368717,"data":"       |     return d\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362369841,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36237096,"data":"       | def distances_for_group(ang):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362372137,"data":"       |     # direction angle (line direction) = ang\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362373354,"data":"       |     # normal vector is unit vector at angle ang+pi/2 (perpendicular)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362374641,"data":"       |     nx = math.cos(ang+math.pi/2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362375817,"data":"       |     ny = math.sin(ang+math.pi/2)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362377017,"data":"       |     dists = [line_distance(pair, (nx,ny)) for pair in dir_groups[ang]]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362378277,"data":"       |     # sort\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362379405,"data":"       |     dists.sort()\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362380564,"data":"       |     return dists\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.3623817,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362382824,"data":"       | # compute distances for each group\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362383977,"data":"       | dist_by_angle={}\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362385107,"data":"       | for ang in sorted(dir_groups.keys()):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362386274,"data":"       |     dist_by_angle[ang]=distances_for_group(ang)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.362389037,"data":"       |     print(ang, dist_by_angle[ang])\n"}
