,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363556674,"data":"       | analysisAngles from 0 to π inclusive. Actually angle 0 corresponds to chords that are horizontal (east-west direction). 0.2618 rad = 15°, 0.5236 = 30°, 0.7854 = 45°, 1.0472 = 60°, 1.3089969 = 75°, π/2 = 90°, etc. So we have directions at multiples of 15° up to 180° (including 0 and 180). Indeed 12-gon has vertices at multiples of 30°, so chords can have direction angles at multiples of 15°, because chords connecting vertices separated by k steps produce direction angles that are (θ_i + θ_j)/2 + π/2? Actually direction of chord is perpendicular to the line connecting the midpoint of the chord to the center. The chord's direction is orthogonal to the radius at its midpoint. Since the midpoint of chord lies at angle halfway between the two vertices, the chord direction is at that angle + 90°. So direction angles are (θ_i + θ_j)/2 + 90°, which yields multiples of 15° indeed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363559615,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36356077,"data":"       | Thus we have 13 possible direction families (including 0° and 180° same as 0). Actually direction modulo π yields 0°, 15°, 30°, 45°, 60°, 75°, 90°, 105°, 120°, 135°, 150°, 165°, 180° (which is same as 0). So there are 13 distinct direction families (including both extremes). However 0° and 180° are same direction modulo π, but we treat 0 and π as same; but we have both 0 and π as separate keys due to rounding? Actually angle 0 and π are both direction families (horizontal). Since we map to [0,π), we should have 0 included but π excluded. However we got both 0 and π (3.14159). That's because some chords have direction angle exactly π (i.e., vector pointing left). But we map to [0,π) by subtracting π if \u003e=π. Did we? In direction_angle we subtracted π if \u003e=π. Actually we have:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36356369,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363564804,"data":"       | if ang \u003c 0: ang += 2π\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363566026,"data":"       | if ang \u003e= π: ang -= π\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363567242,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36356836,"data":"       | Thus angles should be in [0,π). So angle π should become 0 after subtraction. But we got π as a key due to rounding tolerance maybe? Let's check angle for chord (0,6) which is a diameter (through center). The direction vector from vertex 0 to vertex 6 is opposite direction; angle is π (since dx = -2cos0 = -2?). Actually vector from 0 to 6 is (cos π - cos 0, sin π - sin 0) = (-1 - 1, 0 - 0) = (-2,0). atan2(0,-2) = π (or -π). Our code: if ang \u003c 0: add 2π (makes π). Then if ang \u003e= π: subtract π, so π becomes 0. Indeed for chord (0,6) we should get 0. Let's test.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363572352,"data":"       | \n"}
