,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36382201,"data":"       | analysisThus each direction family has either 5 or 6 chords. Let's verify pattern: For step sizes (difference in vertex indices) k, there are n chords of that step (since each chord of step k can start at any vertex i, but chords (i,i+k) and (i+k,i) are same; so number of distinct chords of step k is n/2 if k != n/2 else n/2? Actually for n=12, step k (1 ≤ k ≤ 5) produce chords of length k (i.e., connecting vertices i and i+k). For each k, there are 12 chords (i from 0 to 11) but each chord counted twice (i,i+k) and (i+k,i) produce same unordered pair, so there are 12/2 = 6 chords for each k (since n is even). For step k=6 (diameter), there are 6 chords (pairs of opposite vertices). So each step size yields 6 chords. However we have only 5 chords for some direction families. Why? Because some chords of same step produce same direction? Actually direction families correspond to angle of chord direction, which is (θ_i + θ_j)/2 + π/2 mod π. For step k, the direction angle depends on i (starting vertex) as (θ_i + θ_{i+k})/2 + π/2 = (θ_i + θ_i + k*Δθ)/2 + π/2 = θ_i + k*Δθ/2 + π/2. Since θ_i increments by Δθ = 2π/12 = π/6 (30°). So direction angle for chords of step k varies with i, giving a set of possible directions. For each step k, as i varies, direction angle changes by Δθ = 30° increments. So there are 12 possible direction angles for each step k, but modulo π, there are only 6 distinct directions (since adding π yields same direction). So each step yields 6 chords but direction families may have 5 chords for some angles because some chords of different steps share same direction angle. Let's examine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363829017,"data":"       | \n"}
