,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363832748,"data":"       | But for rectangle counting, we just need to know which chords are parallel (same direction). So we have groups of chords of size either 5 or 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363834088,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363835418,"data":"       | Now we need to count rectangles: choose two parallel chords from group A (size m_A) and two parallel chords from group B (size m_B) such that A and B are perpendicular direction families (i.e., angle difference = π/2). Additionally, each chord from group A must intersect each chord from group B interiorly. So we need to count number of unordered pairs of chords (a1,a2) from group A and (b1,b2) from group B such that all four intersections exist interiorly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36383734,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363838472,"data":"       | Thus rectangle count = sum over unordered perpendicular direction pairs (A,B) of number of ways to choose two intersecting chords from A and two intersecting chords from B such that each chord from A intersects each chord from B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36383995,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363841048,"data":"       | But we need to ensure that the rectangle formed is inside polygon. If chords intersect interiorly, the rectangle will be inside because the intersection points are interior. However, there could be cases where the intersection points produce a self-intersecting quadrilateral (i.e., not rectangle). But if chords are parallel/perpendicular, the quadrilateral formed by intersection points is a rectangle (maybe degenerate if chords intersect at same point). Since chords are distinct and intersect interiorly, they intersect at distinct points. The shape formed by intersecting lines will be a rectangle (since lines are parallel/perpendicular). So we just need to ensure each chord from A intersects each chord from B interiorly (i.e., they cross). If any pair does not intersect (i.e., they are disjoint or intersect outside polygon), rectangle not formed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.3638436,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363844674,"data":"       | Thus we need to count for each pair of perpendicular direction families (A,B) the number of unordered pairs of chords (a1,a2) from A, (b1,b2) from B such that each a_i intersects each b_j interiorly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363846107,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363847204,"data":"       | We also need to ensure that the rectangle's sides lie on the chords, i.e., the segment of a_i between its intersections with b1 and b2 is part of the chord. Since chords are straight lines, the segment between intersection points lies on the chord. So fine.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363848747,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363849844,"data":"       | Thus count = sum_{perp direction pairs} C_{A,B}, where C_{A,B} = number of unordered pairs of chords from A and B satisfying crossing condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363851169,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363852496,"data":"       | Now we need to compute C_{A,B}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363854438,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363856955,"data":"       | We can brute force enumerate all rectangles via computational search to get answer, then attempt to derive combinatorial reasoning.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363858478,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363859564,"data":"       | Let's brute force compute all rectangles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363860842,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363861958,"data":"       | Enumeration approach:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363863188,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363864314,"data":"       | - For each unordered pair of chords (c1, c2) that are parallel and intersect each other? Actually parallel chords do not intersect (except maybe at infinity). So they don't intersect. So we need to find pairs of chords that are parallel but distinct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363865947,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363867034,"data":"       | - For each unordered pair of parallel chords (a1,a2) and each unordered pair of perpendicular chords (b1,b2) such that a1 intersects b1, a1 intersects b2, a2 intersects b1, a2 intersects b2 interiorly.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363868621,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363869694,"data":"       | - Count each rectangle once. The rectangle defined by (a1,a2,b1,b2) is the same as (a2,a1,b1,b2) etc. So we need to consider unordered pairs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363871174,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.363872302,"data":"       | Thus we can iterate over all unordered pairs of chords (a1,a2) that are parallel, all unordered pairs of chords (b1,b2) that are perpendicular to the direction of a1, and test crossing condition.\n"}
