,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364436231,"data":"       | We can approach combinatorially: There are 12 vertices. There are 66 chords (including sides). Each chord direction is determined by its midpoint angle + 90°, resulting in 12 possible directions (multiples of 15°). For each direction, there are either 5 or 6 chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364437856,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364438955,"data":"       | Specifically, for a regular 12-gon, chords of step k (distance in vertices) produce direction angles: for k odd (1,3,5) we have 6 chords each; for k even (2,4,6) maybe 5 chords? Let's compute distribution.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364440438,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364441506,"data":"       | Let's list chords by step size.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364442636,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364443846,"data":"       | But we can derive: For step size k (1 ≤ k ≤ 5) and k=6 (diameter). The direction angle of chord (i,i+k) is θ_i + k*Δθ/2 + π/2. Since i varies over 0..11, direction angles for a given k will be θ_i + constant offset. Since θ_i increments by 30°, direction angles for step k will be a set of 12 angles spaced by 30°, but modulo π they reduce to 6 distinct angles (since adding π corresponds to same direction). So each step yields 6 chords (since each chord appears twice for i and i+k). However, some chords may have same direction as chords from another step size; thus the total number of chords per direction may be 5 or 6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364446202,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364447329,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can count directly: For each direction angle (multiple of 15°), there are chords of two possible step sizes (k and 12 - k). Actually direction angle for step k and step (12 - k) are same? Let's check: For step k and step n - k, direction angle offset? Since chord (i,i+k) and (i,i - k) are same line but reversed orientation? Actually (i,i+k) and (i,i-k) are chords of different lengths (k vs n-k) but they are symmetric across the center; they are not the same chord unless k = n/2. However, direction angle of step k is θ_i + k*Δθ/2 + π/2; direction angle of step n - k is θ_i + (n - k)*Δθ/2 + π/2 = θ_i + (n/2 - k/2)Δθ + π/2 = θ_i + (π - k*Δθ/2) + π/2 = θ_i + π/2 + π - k*Δθ/2 = (θ_i + π/2 - k*Δθ/2) + π. Modulo π, this is θ_i + π/2 - k*Δθ/2 (mod π). Which is direction angle of step k but with sign reversed (i.e., opposite direction). So step k and step n - k give opposite directions (i.e., same line but reversed). So they belong to same direction family (since we consider direction modulo π). So each direction family corresponds to a step size k (1 ≤ k ≤ 5) and also includes chords of step n - k (i.e., 11,10,9,8,7). However, chords of step n - k are same as chords of step k but reversed orientation; but as unordered chords they are the same set. Wait, chord (i,i+k) is same as (i+k,i) which is step n - k when considered from vertex i+k to i (since difference modulo n is n - k). So each unordered chord appears exactly once for each step size k ≤ n/2 (i.e., k=1..6). So each chord belongs to exactly one step size k (the smaller of the two arcs). So step size classification is unique.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364453342,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364454624,"data":"       | Thus direction families correspond to step size k and starting vertex i, but modulo π we have 6 distinct angles for each k, but chords of step k produce 6 chords each direction? Actually there are 12 chords of step k (since each i yields chord (i,i+k) but each chord counted twice). So there are 6 distinct chords of step k (since each chord appears twice). Wait, for n=12, step k yields chords connecting vertices i and i+k for i = 0..11. Since chord (i,i+k) = (i+k,i) (same unordered pair), each unordered chord appears twice (once for i, once for i+k). So total distinct chords of step k is 12/2 = 6. Indeed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364456798,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364457891,"data":"       | Thus each step k yields exactly 6 chords. So there are 6 chords for each k = 1..5, and for k=6 (diameter) also 6 chords (since each vertex pairs with opposite vertex; there are 6 diameters). So total chords = 6*6 = 36? Wait 6 steps times 6 chords each = 36, but we have 66 chords. Something off.\n"}
