,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36454798,"data":"       | Thus each direction family has either 6 chords (odd families) or 5 chords (even families). There are 6 odd families (15°,45°,75°,105°,135°,165°) each with 6 chords. There are 6 even families (0°,30°,60°,90°,120°,150°) each with 5 chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364549678,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36455098,"data":"       | Now rectangle formation: we need to pick a pair of perpendicular direction families. Since directions are spaced 15°, the perpendicular pairs are (0°,90°), (15°,105°), (30°,120°), (45°,135°), (60°,150°), (75°,165°). Each pair consists of one even family and one odd family (since 0° even, 90° even? Actually 90° is even (since step 6? Wait 90° is direction of step 2? Let's check: 90° direction corresponds to vertical lines. Which chords have vertical direction? Those are chords connecting vertices with step 2? Let's compute: direction 90° is angle π/2; chords in that direction are of step 2 and 4? Actually vertical chords correspond to chords connecting vertices that are symmetric across vertical axis. For dodecagon oriented with vertex at angle 0°, vertical chords are those connecting vertices with angles 30° and 150°, etc. Those are step 2 chords? Let's not worry.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364553958,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36455508,"data":"       | But each perpendicular pair includes one direction from even group and one from odd group? Let's check: Pair (0°,90°): both are even families (0° and 90°). Wait 90° is also even? Actually 90° is 6*15°, which is even multiple of 15°, but classification as even vs odd families based on step parity? Let's compute: direction 90° (π/2) is 90°, which is multiple of 30°? Actually 90° is 3*30°, but not multiple of 15°? Wait 90° = 6*15°, which is even multiple of 15°, but classification earlier: even families correspond to angles 0°,30°,60°,90°,120°,150° (multiples of 30°). Odd families correspond to 15°,45°,75°,105°,135°,165° (odd multiples of 15°). Indeed 0°,30°,60°,90°,120°,150° are multiples of 30°, which correspond to even families (step parity even). So perpendicular pairs: (0°,90°) both even; (15°,105°) both odd; (30°,120°) both even; (45°,135°) both odd; (60°,150°) both even; (75°,165°) both odd. So each perpendicular pair is either both even or both odd.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364558686,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364559808,"data":"       | Thus for each perpendicular pair, the groups have sizes (5,5) for even pair, and (6,6) for odd pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364561064,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364562375,"data":"       | Now we need to count rectangles formed by picking two chords from each group such that they intersect.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364563684,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364567172,"data":"       | Observation: For a given pair of perpendicular direction families, any chord from one family intersects any chord from the other family interiorly? Not necessarily; some chords may be \"non-crossing\" (i.e., they may be disjoint within polygon). Let's test.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.364568848,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36456993,"data":"       | Take even families (0°,90°). There are 5 chords in each. Do all chords from 0° intersect all chords from 90°? Let's test with Python.\n"}
