,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.365123067,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.365124171,"data":"       | - A rectangle must have its opposite sides parallel, thus its sides belong to two perpendicular directions. The perpendicular direction pairs are (0°,90°), (15°,105°), (30°,120°), (45°,135°), (60°,150°), (75°,165°). Thus there are three even-even pairs and three odd-odd pairs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36512584,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.365126964,"data":"       | - For a fixed pair of perpendicular directions, we consider the bipartite graph whose vertices are the chords of the first direction and chords of the second direction, with an edge when the two chords intersect interiorly (i.e., their endpoints are interleaved). A rectangle corresponds to a complete bipartite subgraph K_{2,2} (two chords from each family that all intersect each other). Counting rectangles reduces to counting K_{2,2} subgraphs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.365128814,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.365129936,"data":"       | - For an even direction family (size 5), the pattern of intersections with the perpendicular family (also size 5) is as follows: the diameter chord intersects all 5 chords of the other family; each of the two step‑4 chords intersects three chords (the central chord and the two step‑4 chords); each of the two step‑2 chords intersects only the central chord. Consequently, the adjacency matrix yields exactly three unordered pairs of chords in the first family that have at least two common neighbours: the diameter together with each of the two step‑4 chords (each gives 3 common neighbours) and the two step‑4 chords together (again 3 common neighbours). Each of those three pairs contributes \\(\\binom{3}{2}=3\\) rectangles, giving \\(3\\cdot 3 = 9\\) rectangles for each even‑even perpendicular pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36513478,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.365136056,"data":"       | - For an odd direction family (size 6), the chords consist of two of each step size 1,3,5. In a perpendicular pair, the two step‑5 chords intersect all four chords of the opposite family that have step 3 or 5; the two step‑3 chords intersect exactly the two step‑5 chords; the two step‑1 chords intersect none. Hence the adjacency pattern yields one pair of chords (the two step‑5 chords) that have four common neighbours, giving \\(\\binom{4}{2}=6\\) rectangles, and three pairs consisting of a step‑5 chord with a step‑3 chord, each having two common neighbours, giving \\(3\\cdot\\binom{2}{2}=3\\) rectangles. Altogether each odd‑odd perpendicular pair yields \\(6+3=11\\) rectangles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.365138925,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.365140013,"data":"       | - There are three even‑even pairs and three odd‑odd pairs, so the total number of rectangles is\n"}
