,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366207262,"data":"       | analysisSympy enumeration is slower and timed out after 6 seconds. Let's try to optimize: we can compute intersections using rational arithmetic but still heavy. However 975 combos is not many; the slowdown may be due to sympy's simplification of many expressions with sqrt(3). Let's try to compute using rational approximations but with high precision to avoid floating errors, maybe using decimal with high precision. But we already got 315 using floats; we need to verify that 315 is correct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366209105,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366210203,"data":"       | Let's double-check with a more robust algorithm: compute intersection points using analytic geometry with exact rational slopes maybe using integer coordinates after scaling. Since vertices are on unit circle, coordinates involve sqrt(3)/2, etc. But we can also embed the dodecagon in a coordinate system with vertices at complex numbers e^{iπk/6}. Multiply by 2 to avoid fractions: coordinates (2cosθ, 2sinθ) will be integers plus sqrt(3). Actually cos(π/6) = √3/2, sin(π/6)=1/2. Multiply by 2: coordinates become (√3,1). So coordinates are of the form (a√3 + b, c√3 + d) where a,b,c,d are integers. So we can treat them as elements of Q(√3). Represent each coordinate as pair (p,q) representing p + q*√3. Then we can compute intersection using rational arithmetic in this field. That may be more efficient.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366212762,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366213742,"data":"       | But maybe we can trust the floating enumeration.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366214835,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366216007,"data":"       | Let's also compute using integer scaling to avoid floating errors: Use coordinates (cosθ, sinθ) with high precision (like 50 decimal digits) and test inside polygon using tolerance 1e-12. That should be reliable.\n"}
