,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366713136,"data":"       | But perhaps the problem expects using combinatorial geometry: For each perpendicular pair, count number of rectangles using a grid approach: The lines in each direction partition the polygon into strips; the rectangle must be formed by choosing two lines from each direction such that the rectangle is inside polygon. Equivalent to counting number of pairs (i1,i2) and (j1,j2) such that the rectangle's vertices are interior.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366715017,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366716108,"data":"       | Since the polygon is convex, the condition reduces to: For each pair (i1,i2) of lines from direction d, the segment of polygon between those lines must be \"wide enough\" to accommodate the other direction lines.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366717632,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366718913,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can think of the intersection of the polygon with the coordinate axes: The polygon's projection onto the axes yields intervals of x and y coordinates. The rectangle defined by x between x_i1 and x_i2 and y between y_j1 and y_j2 is inside polygon if and only if for all x in [x_i1,x_i2], the y-interval of polygon at that x contains [y_j1,y_j2] (i.e., the rectangle fits). Since polygon is convex and symmetric, we can perhaps compute number of interior rectangles by counting pairs of lines such that the rectangle's four vertices are inside.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366720988,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366722059,"data":"       | We can also count rectangles by counting quadruples of vertices of the dodecagon that form a rectangle? No, rectangle vertices may be interior intersection points, not vertices.\n"}
