,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366741806,"data":"       | Now we can produce a solution explanation: Use combinatorial reasoning: There are 12 directions (indexed by sum of vertex indices modulo 12). For each direction, there are either 5 or 6 lines (parallel chords). Perpendicular directions are pairs (d,d+6). For each such pair, we need to count rectangles formed by picking two lines from each family such that the rectangle lies inside the dodecagon. For each pair we can count using a grid approach: Represent the lines as equally spaced parallel lines; the interior of the dodecagon corresponds to a convex region; the number of rectangles equals sum over i1 \u003c i2 of number of j1 \u003c j2 such that the rectangle's vertices are inside. Equivalent to counting lattice points inside a certain shape.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366744274,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366745425,"data":"       | We can compute counts manually perhaps using combinatorial reasoning: For each pair of perpendicular families, we can label the lines in order from one side of the polygon to the other. Then the rectangle formed by lines i1,i2 and j1,j2 is inside if the four corner points (i1,j1), (i1,j2), (i2,j1), (i2,j2) are inside. Because of convexity, this is equivalent to requiring that the four points (i1,j1) and (i2,j2) (the \"lower-left\" and \"upper-right\" corners) are inside; the other two will then be inside as well.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366747629,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366748762,"data":"       | Thus we can count number of pairs (i1,i2) and (j1,j2) such that both (i1,j1) and (i2,j2) are interior points of a certain lattice region defined by the polygon's projection onto the grid. This region is a convex polygon with vertices at the intersection points of the outermost lines. For each pair of families, we can draw a grid of m lines each direction, forming a lattice of (m x m) points (the intersections). The points inside the dodecagon correspond to a set of lattice points; rectangles correspond to choosing two points (i1,j1) and (i2,j2) with i1 \u003c i2, j1 \u003c j2 such that both are interior points. So number of rectangles equals number of unordered pairs of interior lattice points that are not in same row or column? Actually each rectangle corresponds to choosing two interior lattice points that are opposite corners of a rectangle aligned with the grid. So the count equals number of unordered pairs of interior lattice points where the two points are not in same row or column, i.e., they differ in both coordinates. Equivalent to total number of unordered pairs of interior points minus those that share same x or same y coordinate.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366751936,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366754081,"data":"       | Thus we can compute number of interior lattice points for each grid, then compute rectangle count.\n"}
