,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36691111,"data":"       | analysisThus 138 unordered pairs of points not sharing row or column. Dividing by 2 yields 69 rectangles. But enumeration gave 51. So some of those pairs correspond to rectangles that are not fully inside polygon, maybe because the rectangle formed by those opposite corners extends beyond polygon even though the two opposite corners are inside. Indeed, having opposite corners inside does not guarantee the whole rectangle inside for convex polygon? Actually for convex polygon, if both opposite corners are inside, the rectangle may still have other corners outside if the rectangle is not axis-aligned to polygon's sides? Wait rectangle is axis-aligned with respect to the grid (i.e., sides parallel to chosen directions). For convex polygon, if both opposite corners are inside, the rectangle is inside because the polygon is convex and contains the entire line segment between any two interior points; but the rectangle includes points beyond the line segment connecting opposite corners? Actually rectangle is convex hull of its four vertices. If the polygon is convex and contains the four vertices, then it contains the convex hull (the rectangle). So if all four vertices are inside, rectangle is inside. But we only know two opposite vertices are inside. The other two vertices may be outside even if opposite corners inside. For convex shape, containing two opposite corners does not guarantee containing the other two corners. Example: a convex shape like a diamond shape; two opposite corners of a rectangle may be inside but the rectangle may extend outside. So we need to ensure all four vertices inside.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366916313,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366917372,"data":"       | Thus counting based on opposite corners is insufficient.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366918601,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366919636,"data":"       | Thus enumeration is needed.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366920773,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366921869,"data":"       | Now we can attempt to compute count analytically by analyzing which rows/columns produce interior points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366923166,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36692439,"data":"       | For each pair of lines (i1,i2) and (j1,j2), rectangle vertices are (i1,j1), (i1,j2), (i2,j1), (i2,j2). All four must be interior points. So we need to count quadruples of interior points forming a rectangle. Equivalent to counting pairs of rows (i1,i2) and columns (j1,j2) such that all four intersection points are interior. So we need to know for each row i and column j whether the point (i,j) is interior. Then rectangle count = sum over i1\u003ci2, j1\u003cj2 of indicator that all four points interior.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366926444,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366927543,"data":"       | Thus we can treat interior points as a binary matrix of size m x m (m lines each direction). Then rectangle count is number of axis-aligned rectangles of interior cells (i.e., submatrices) where all four corners are 1. This is a known combinatorial problem: count number of submatrices with all corners 1.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366929183,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366930302,"data":"       | Given binary matrix A (size m x m), number of rectangles = sum over pairs of rows (i1,i2) of C(k,2) where k is number of columns where both rows have 1 at that column. Because for a given pair of rows, a rectangle is determined by choosing two columns where both rows have 1 (i.e., corners). So we can compute count efficiently.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36693197,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36693303,"data":"       | Thus we can compute counts analytically for each pair (d,d+6) by constructing binary matrix of interior points.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366934307,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36693538,"data":"       | Let's compute for (0,6) matrix.assistantanalysis to=python codedef interior_matrix(d):\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366936627,"data":"       |     lines_d = lines_by_d[d]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36693776,"data":"       |     lines_perp = lines_by_d[(d+6)%12]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366938922,"data":"       |     m = len(lines_d)\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366940028,"data":"       |     matrix = [[0]*len(lines_perp) for _ in range(m)]\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.366941198,"data":"       |     for i, L in enumerate(lines_d):\n"}
