,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.367838143,"data":"       | We'll enumerate all unordered pairs of chords from each family, and then for each pair of chords from A and B, check if they intersect (i.e., each chord from A intersects each chord from B). But we need rectangle formed by two chords from A (a1,a2) and two chords from B (b1,b2). For rectangle to exist, we need a1 intersect b1, a1 intersect b2, a2 intersect b1, a2 intersect b2. Since chords are convex, if a1 intersect b1 and a1 intersect b2, then a1 intersects both B chords; similarly for a2. However, we need to ensure that the four intersection points are distinct (i.e., not degenerate). This will be true unless some chords share endpoints leading to overlapping vertices. But rectangle could be degenerate if a1 and a2 intersect at same point with b1? That would happen if a1 and a2 intersect each other, but they are parallel, so they don't intersect. So intersection points will be distinct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.367840806,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.367841916,"data":"       | Thus we can simply check that each chord from A intersects each chord from B. Since chords from A are parallel, they cannot intersect each other; same for B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.367843307,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.367844469,"data":"       | Thus rectangle condition reduces to: For given unordered pair (a1,a2) from A and (b1,b2) from B, check if intersect_segments(a1,b1) and intersect_segments(a1,b2) and intersect_segments(a2,b1) and intersect_segments(a2,b2). If all true, rectangle exists.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.367845989,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.367847116,"data":"       | Let's compute total count.\n"}
