,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.367920617,"data":"       | But we need to double-check that all rectangles counted indeed have sides lying on chords (including possibly subsegments). Since we only used chords as sides, not subsegments of chords beyond endpoints. However, rectangle sides may be subsegments of chords that are not full chords but part of chord between intersection points. That's exactly what we have: each side is a segment of a chord between intersection points with perpendicular chords. So we are counting all rectangles formed by intersecting chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.367922579,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.367923689,"data":"       | Now we need to ensure there are no rectangles formed by chords where one side is a subsegment of a chord but the rectangle's vertices are not intersection points of chords from the two families? Actually any rectangle formed by chords must have each vertex at intersection of a chord from each family (since each vertex belongs to one side from each family). So vertices are intersection points of a chord from each orientation. So our counting method captures them.\n"}
