,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368112193,"data":"       | Now we need to ensure that there are no rectangles missed by this method. Could there be rectangles whose sides are not formed by chords but by subsegments of chords that are not full chords? Our method uses full chords as sides lines; any rectangle side is a subsegment of a chord, which lies on the same line as the chord. So we considered all chords as possible lines for sides. However, there might be rectangles where a side lies on a chord but the side's endpoints are not intersection points of chords from the other orientation, but rather a vertex of the polygon (which is also intersection of two chords: a side and a diagonal). But that is still intersection of a chord from each orientation: the vertex belongs to chords of many orientations (all chords incident to that vertex). So our method includes that because intersection of a chord from A with a chord from B at a vertex counts as intersect_segments returning True (since we treat endpoints as intersection). So rectangle vertices at polygon vertices are included.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368116694,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368117793,"data":"       | Thus we likely captured all rectangles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368118941,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368120074,"data":"       | Now we need to ensure that we didn't double count rectangles that are squares counted multiple times due to symmetry? Our counting is based on chord sets, each rectangle corresponds to unique unordered pair of chords from each family. There is no double counting.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36812162,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368122686,"data":"       | Thus answer 315.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368123769,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36812492,"data":"       | But we should verify by enumerating all rectangles as sets of four points (coordinates) and counting unique sets of points irrespective of orientation. Let's compute all intersection points of chords (including vertices) and then find all rectangles formed by four points such that each side lies on a chord (i.e., the line through each side is a chord). This may be more complex but we can attempt to verify that number of distinct rectangles (by vertices) equals 315.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368126834,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368128146,"data":"       | We'll generate all intersection points of chords (including vertices). For each pair of chords that intersect, we have a point. Then we need to find all rectangles formed by four such points where each side is a segment of a chord. However, we can also directly compute rectangles as we did; each rectangle corresponds to chords a1,a2,b1,b2. The vertices are intersection points of those chords. So each rectangle is uniquely identified by the set of four chords (a1,a2,b1,b2). However, there could be multiple sets of chords that produce same rectangle? For example, if a rectangle side lies on a chord that contains another chord collinear (i.e., same line). But chords are defined by pairs of vertices; there may be multiple chords lying on same line? In a regular polygon, chords of same orientation are parallel but not collinear (except diameters which are collinear with each other? Actually diameters all pass through center, but they are not collinear; they intersect at center, but each diameter is a line through center at a different angle. So no two chords share the same line except if they share both endpoints (i.e., same chord). So each line is defined by a unique unordered pair of vertices (except maybe chords that are sides of polygon share same line as a longer diagonal? For example, side (i,i+1) is not collinear with any other chord because any other chord connecting other vertices will not be collinear with that side unless they are parallel but not same line. So each line is unique to a chord.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368131813,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368132869,"data":"       | Thus rectangle uniquely determined by the four chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368135676,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368136756,"data":"       | Thus 315 is correct.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368137907,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368139034,"data":"       | Now we should double-check by enumerating all rectangles via combinatorial reasoning to confirm 315.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368140276,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368141331,"data":"       | Let's attempt to derive formula.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368142467,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368143586,"data":"       | We have 6 orthogonal orientation pairs: 3 even pairs (size 5 each) and 3 odd pairs (size 6 each). For each pair, we need to count rectangles formed by chords from each family.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368144963,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368146056,"data":"       | Thus total rectangles = 3 * f(5) + 3 * f(6), where f(m) = number of rectangles formed by two families each of size m (parallel chords) with intersection condition.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368147419,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368148533,"data":"       | Our computed counts: for even pair (size 5) we got 51 rectangles per pair; for odd pair (size 6) we got 54 rectangles per pair.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368149829,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368150881,"data":"       | Thus total = 3*51 + 3*54 = 153 + 162 = 315.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368152009,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368153063,"data":"       | Now we can attempt to compute f(m) analytically.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368154203,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368155469,"data":"       | Let A and B be families of m parallel chords each. Not all chords intersect each other; we need to count number of unordered pairs (a1,a2) from A and (b1,b2) from B such that each a intersects each b.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368156883,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368158019,"data":"       | Define intersection graph: bipartite graph between chords of A and chords of B where edge indicates intersection. Then rectangle corresponds to a complete bipartite subgraph K_{2,2} (i.e., a pair of chords from each side all intersect). So we need to count number of K_{2,2} subgraphs in this bipartite graph.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368159643,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368160721,"data":"       | Thus f(m) = number of K_{2,2} in the bipartite intersection graph.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368161909,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368162997,"data":"       | Thus we can compute adjacency matrix and count K_{2,2}.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368164153,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368165261,"data":"       | For m=5 (even pair), we can compute adjacency matrix and count K_{2,2} = sum_{i\u003cj} C(deg_{i,j},2) where deg_{i,j} = number of common neighbors of chords a_i and a_j in B. Equivalent formula: number of unordered pairs of chords in A that share at least two common intersecting chords in B.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368166883,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368167997,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can compute using combinatorial reasoning about endpoints.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36816921,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368170343,"data":"       | But perhaps easier: we can compute f(m) by analyzing pattern of intersection between families.\n"}
