,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36832024,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368321369,"data":"       | Now we can attempt to derive f(m) formula for m=5 and m=6 based on combinatorial properties of chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368322657,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368323893,"data":"       | But perhaps we can produce a solution that counts rectangles directly using combinatorial reasoning, not brute force.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368325183,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368326231,"data":"       | Let's attempt to reason.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368327333,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368328464,"data":"       | We have a regular 12-gon. The chords can be classified by direction index r = (2i + k) mod 12, where i is starting vertex and k = j-i (mod 12). There are 12 direction families (parallel families). Each family has either 5 or 6 chords: families with even r have 5 chords (including diameters), odd r have 6 chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368330119,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368331246,"data":"       | Thus there are 6 orthogonal pairs (r, r+6). For each pair, we need to count rectangles formed by chords from each family.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368332519,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368333608,"data":"       | Now we need to count for a given orthogonal pair (size m chords each) the number of rectangles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368334839,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368336077,"data":"       | Observation: For chords of same orientation (parallel), they are ordered by distance from the center (i.e., their distance from the center). In a regular polygon, chords of same orientation are parallel and have different distances from the center (i.e., they are at different offsets). For each orientation family, the chords can be ordered from closest to the center to farthest (i.e., side vs diagonal vs diameter). For even families (size 5), the chords are: one diameter (through center), two pairs of symmetric chords (one pair of longer diagonals, one pair of shorter diagonals?), plus maybe a side? Actually even families correspond to chords with even residues. Let's list chords for r=0 (even). Print them.\n"}
