,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368371241,"data":"       | analysisThese are chords: (0,1) side, (2,11) diagonal across 9 steps? Actually (2,11) step 9 (i.e., 12-3). So includes side and other chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368372566,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368373771,"data":"       | Thus each orientation family consists of chords that are \"parallel\" and arranged at different distances from center.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368375129,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368376233,"data":"       | Now for a given orthogonal pair, the intersection graph between families is determined by which chords intersect. Since chords are parallel, they intersect if they are not too far apart (i.e., they cross). For two families of perpendicular chords, each chord from A intersects many chords from B, but some pairs may be disjoint (non-intersecting). The pattern is that chords that are \"far\" from each other (i.e., both close to center or both far from center) may not intersect.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368378226,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368379291,"data":"       | We can analyze intersection condition in terms of distances from center.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368380526,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368381613,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can treat chords as lines with equations. For each orientation family, chords are parallel lines at distinct distances from the center. For orthogonal families, the lines form a grid of intersection points (like a rectangular lattice) but some intersections lie outside the polygon (i.e., beyond the chord segments). The rectangle exists only when the intersection points are within the chord segments, which corresponds to chords intersecting.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368385157,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368386266,"data":"       | Thus the problem reduces to counting the number of rectangles formed by a grid of lines where some intersections are missing due to chord lengths.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.3683876,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368388679,"data":"       | Thus we can compute number of rectangles in a grid of m parallel lines in each direction, where some intersections are missing.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368389983,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368391056,"data":"       | Specifically, for each orthogonal pair, we have m parallel lines (chords) in each direction. The rectangle count is number of ways to choose two lines from each direction such that the four intersection points exist (i.e., each pair of lines intersect). This is exactly the number of K_{2,2} subgraphs in the bipartite intersection graph.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368392751,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368393823,"data":"       | Thus we can compute f(m) by analyzing the intersection pattern.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368395013,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368396247,"data":"       | Let's compute the intersection pattern for even families (size 5). Let's label chords A_i (i=1..5) sorted by distance from center (maybe). Similarly B_j (j=1..5). Intersection matrix we saw: rows (a) vs columns (b) where True indicates intersection.\n"}
