,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368406586,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368407667,"data":"       | Thus pattern symmetrical: the outermost chords (i.e., first and last) intersect only with middle three chords of B (and not with the outermost B chords). So the intersection graph is like a \"cross\" shape: the middle three chords intersect all chords; the outer chords intersect only middle three.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368409333,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368410399,"data":"       | Thus we can compute f(5) combinatorially: There are 5 chords each side. Let's label A1 (outermost), A2 (next), A3 (middle), A4 (next), A5 (outermost). Similarly B1..B5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368411799,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368412871,"data":"       | Intersections: A1 intersects B2,B3,B4 (not B1,B5). A5 same pattern. A2 intersects all B1..B5. A4 same. A3 intersects all B1..B5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368414171,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368415236,"data":"       | Thus adjacency: outer chords intersect middle three B chords; inner chords intersect all B chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368416466,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.368417547,"data":"       | Now we need to count number of rectangles = number of ways to choose two A chords and two B chords such that all four intersections exist. Let's compute combinatorially.\n"}
