,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369702082,"data":"       | Goal: Provide solution explanation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369703199,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369705865,"data":"       | We need to count rectangles formed by intersection of chords. Approach:\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369707148,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369708249,"data":"       | - Represent chords by unordered vertex pairs (i,j). The direction of chord depends on sum i+j modulo 12.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369709507,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369710603,"data":"       | - There are 12 possible directions (orientations). For each direction d, chords are those with i+j ≡ d (mod 12). The number of chords in direction d is N_d = 5 if d even, 6 if d odd.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369712059,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369713276,"data":"       | - Opposite directions differ by 6 (mod 12). Two directions are perpendicular if they differ by 6 (since direction angle α = π/2 + π(i+j)/12). So each direction d is perpendicular to direction d+6.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369714777,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369715869,"data":"       | - Thus rectangles correspond to choosing two chords from direction d and two chords from direction d+6 such that each chord from one family intersects each chord from the other family.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369717321,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36971839,"data":"       | - Not all chords from perpendicular families intersect; we need to count intersecting pairs.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369719633,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369720717,"data":"       | - For a given direction d (say odd), each chord intersects exactly 5 chords of the perpendicular direction (d+6) (maybe?). Let's compute intersection counts.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369722085,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369723155,"data":"       | Let's analyze intersection patterns.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369724305,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.36972541,"data":"       | Given chord (i,j) with i+j ≡ d (mod 12). For a chord (p,q) with p+q ≡ d+6 (mod 12), they intersect iff the vertices are interleaved around the circle: i \u003c p \u003c j \u003c q (or p \u003c i \u003c q \u003c j) in cyclic order. Equivalent to the intervals crossing.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369727163,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369728222,"data":"       | Thus intersection condition: The endpoints of the two chords are alternating around the circle.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369729481,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369730543,"data":"       | Thus for given chord (i,j), the number of chords of opposite orientation intersecting it can be determined combinatorially.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369731844,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369732901,"data":"       | We can compute for each orientation parity.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369734059,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369735127,"data":"       | Let's attempt to compute combinatorially: For a chord (i,j) with step length k = j-i (mod 12) (choose 1..11). Its orientation d = i + j mod 12. The opposite orientation chords have sum ≡ d+6. For a chord (p,q) to intersect (i,j), we need p and q to be on opposite sides of the line segment: i \u003c p \u003c j \u003c q (mod 12) in cyclic order.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369736919,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369737975,"data":"       | Given i and j, we can count number of chords (p,q) with sum ≡ d+6 that cross (i,j). This depends on step length k.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369741011,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.369742093,"data":"       | Let's compute counts for each chord type. Use enumeration to see distribution.\n"}
