,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370000407,"data":"       | analysisSo among the C(5,2)=10 pairs of chords in orientation 0, 7 pairs have 3 common intersecting chords, and 3 pairs have 5 common intersecting chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370003552,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370004668,"data":"       | Thus rectangles contributed: sum over pairs of C(m,2). For m=3, C(3,2)=3 each; for m=5, C(5,2)=10 each. So total rectangles = 7*3 + 3*10 = 21 + 30 = 51. Indeed matches earlier.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370006076,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370007199,"data":"       | Now we can try to find combinatorial reasoning for these numbers.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370008398,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370009711,"data":"       | We can classify chords in orientation 0 as \"outer\" chords (those near polygon boundary) vs \"inner\" chords (more central). Actually chords (2,10), (3,9), (4,8) intersect all five chords of opposite orientation; chords (1,11) and (5,7) intersect only three chords. So there are 2 \"short\" chords (step length 2? Let's compute step lengths: (1,11) step = (11-1) mod 12 = 10 (or -2). Actually step length min(k,12-k) = 2 (since connecting vertices 1 and 11 are adjacent? Wait vertices 1 and 11 are adjacent? Vertex indices 0..11 around circle. Vertex 1 and 11 are adjacent (since difference 2 mod 12? Actually 1 and 11 are two apart around the circle? Let's compute: vertices 1 and 11 are separated by 2 edges (going backward). Indeed, they are adjacent? The polygon is regular 12-gon; vertices are numbered around. Vertex 0 adjacent to 1 and 11. So vertices 1 and 11 are also adjacent? Let's check: Vertex 1 adjacent to 0 and 2. Vertex 11 adjacent to 10 and 0. So 1 and 11 are not adjacent; they are separated by two edges (via 0). So chord (1,11) is a side of length 2 (skip one vertex). That's step length 2 (i.e., connecting vertices with one vertex between them). Similarly (5,7) is also step length 2. So chords of step length 2 are the \"short\" chords that intersect only three chords of perpendicular orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370013255,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370014406,"data":"       | Chords (2,10), (3,9), (4,8) are step length 4? Let's compute: (2,10) difference 8 (or 4). That's step length 4 (skip three vertices). So those chords intersect all five chords of opposite orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370015833,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370016957,"data":"       | Thus classification: For even orientation, there are 2 chords of step 2 (short) that intersect only 3 chords of perpendicular orientation; 2 chords of step 4 (medium) intersect all 5 chords; and 1 diameter (step 6) intersects all 5 chords? Let's check adjacency for diameter (0,6) belongs to opposite orientation, not in orientation 0. In orientation 0, there is no diameter; orientation 0 chords are all of step 2 or 4? Actually orientation 0 chords are (1,11) step 2, (2,10) step 4, (3,9) step 6? Wait (3,9) difference 6 (diameter). Indeed (3,9) is a diameter (since 3+9=12). So orientation 0 includes a diameter (3,9). Similarly (4,8) step 4? Actually (4,8) difference 4 (step 4). (5,7) step 2. So orientation 0 includes chords of step lengths 2,4,6 (diameter). So there is one diameter in each orientation.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.37001945,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370020789,"data":"       | Thus orientation even includes: 2 chords of step 2 (short), 2 chords of step 4 (medium), 1 diameter (step 6). The diameter intersects all chords of opposite orientation? Let's check adjacency for diameter (3,9) in orientation 0: In adjacency list above, (3,9) intersects all 5 chords of opposite orientation (0,6), (1,5), (2,4), (7,11), (8,10). Indeed, it intersects all 5.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370022511,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370024957,"data":"       | Thus classification: For even orientation, chords are of three types: short (step 2) intersect 3 chords of opposite orientation; medium (step 4) intersect 5 chords; diameter (step 6) intersect 5 chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370029697,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370030833,"data":"       | Now we need to compute number of rectangles contributed by each combination of chord types.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370032079,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370033183,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can compute the number of K_{2,2} subgraphs directly combinatorially.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370034406,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370035544,"data":"       | But we can produce a solution that enumerates possibilities based on step lengths.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370036782,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370037859,"data":"       | We can also approach by counting rectangles based on the distances between parallel sides measured in terms of \"layers\" of chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.37003926,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370040355,"data":"       | Observation: The chords of a given orientation form a set of parallel lines that partition the polygon interior into parallel strips. For even orientation, there are 5 chords: two short, two medium, one diameter. They are arranged in order from one side of the polygon to the opposite side. The intersection of chords from the perpendicular orientation yields a grid of rectangles. The number of rectangles formed by a grid of m parallel lines intersecting n parallel lines is C(m,2)*C(n,2). However, here the lines are segments, not infinite lines, and they may not intersect all others. But the arrangement is like a \"grid\" where some lines are shorter and don't intersect all lines of the other family.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370042718,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370043807,"data":"       | Thus we need to count rectangles in such a \"partial grid\".\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370045063,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370046652,"data":"       | We can think of the arrangement of chords of a given orientation as a set of parallel segments of varying lengths. The longest is the diameter (covers whole width). The shorter chords are near the boundary and intersect only a subset of the perpendicular chords.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370048195,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370049281,"data":"       | Thus we can compute the number of rectangles by considering the positions of chords along the perpendicular direction.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370050551,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370051619,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can compute using combinatorial approach: For each pair of chords from orientation d, count the number of chords from orientation d+6 intersecting both. That yields rectangles.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.37005306,"data":"       | \n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370054159,"data":"       | Thus we can compute for each pair type.\n"}
,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370055286,"data":"       | \n"}
