,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370178437,"data":"       | Intersection pattern: A chords intersect only A' chords? Actually from adjacency above, (0,1) (type A) intersects chords (0,7) and (1,6). Those chords (0,7) and (1,6) are also type A (step 1) in orientation d+6? Let's verify: orientation 7 chords list: (0,7) step 7? Actually (0,7) difference 7 (or 5). Wait step length = min(7, 12-7=5) = 5. So (0,7) is step 5 (type C). (1,6) difference 5 (or 7) also step 5. So A chords intersect only C' chords? Let's compute classification: orientation 7 chords: (0,7) step 5, (1,6) step 5, (2,5) step 3, (3,4) step 1, (8,11) step 3? Actually (8,11) difference 3 (step 3), (9,10) step 1. So orientation 7 includes 2 chords of each step length: step 1: (3,4) and (9,10); step 3: (2,5) and (8,11); step 5: (0,7) and (1,6). So indeed A chords (step 1) intersect only C' chords (step 5). Similarly, B chords (step 3) intersect A' and B' and C'? Let's examine adjacency for (2,11) (type B) intersects (0,7) (C'), (1,6) (C'), (2,5) (B'), (8,11) (B'). So B chords intersect C' and B' chords but not A' chords. Similarly, C chords (step 5) intersect all three types A', B', C' (since they intersect 6 chords). Indeed, (3,10) (type C) intersects (0,7) (C'), (1,6) (C'), (2,5) (B'), (3,4) (A'), (8,11) (B'), (9,10) (A'). So C chords intersect all.\n"}
