,{"stream_name":"stdout","time":3398.370265406,"data":"       | Alternatively, we can give a more systematic approach: For each pair of perpendicular families, we can treat chords as lines dividing the polygon into strips. The number of rectangles formed equals sum over all pairs of parallel chords of the number of common intersecting chords from the other family. Then compute using combinatorial classification.\n"}
